"use strict";

var CONCEPTS = [
{
  "id": "kin-scalars-vectors",
  "group": "u1",
  "prereqs": [],
  "source": "CED 1.1",
  "title": "Scalars, vectors, and the sign that carries direction",
  "analogy": "A bank statement records +40 and −25 in the same column of the same ledger. Nobody writes '25, but the leaving kind' — the minus sign already says it. One axis, one sign convention, and direction stops being a word you have to remember and becomes part of the number you are already carrying.",
  "plain": "Some quantities are finished as soon as you give a number and a unit. A stopwatch reading of 4.2 s. A distance of 30 m. A speed of 12 m/s. Nothing is missing from any of those. Quantities like this are **scalars**.\n\nOther quantities are not finished until you also say *which way*. If a drone moved 30 m, you still do not know where it ended up. If it moved 30 m east, you do. Quantities that need a direction as well as a size are **vectors**. Position, displacement, velocity and acceleration are vectors. Distance and speed are not.\n\nThe standard picture of a vector is an arrow. It points the way the quantity points, and it is drawn with a length proportional to the magnitude — so a 20 m/s velocity gets an arrow twice as long as a 10 m/s velocity on the same diagram. That proportionality is the whole reason the picture is useful: you can compare two vectors by looking.\n\nIn one dimension you do not need arrows. Choose an axis, decide which way counts as positive, and then a plus or minus sign in front of the number carries all the direction there is. Writing $v_x = -12$ m/s is complete: twelve metres per second, in the negative direction.",
  "depth": "The CED separates two notations deliberately. A vector *as a vector* is written with an arrow over the symbol, as in $\\vec{v} = \\vec{v}_0 + \\vec{a}t$. A **component** of that vector along a chosen axis is written without the arrow and with a subscript naming the axis, as in $v_x = v_{x0} + a_x t$. The component is a signed number, not a vector, so the arrow would be wrong — and in one dimension the sign of that component describes the direction completely.\n\nThat is why every kinematic equation on the provided equation sheet is printed in component form: $v_x = v_{x0} + a_x t$, not the arrow version. The sheet is telling you how it expects you to work. You will resolve the motion onto an axis, and from then on all the vector information lives in the signs.\n\nAdding vectors in one dimension is then just signed addition. Two displacements along the same line, one $+30$ m and one $-50$ m, sum to $-20$ m. Opposite directions are opposite signs, and the arithmetic does the geometry for you. This only works once you have committed to an axis and a positive direction, and it breaks the moment you change that convention halfway through a problem.\n\nMagnitude and direction can then be read back off the signed result: $-20$ m means a magnitude of 20 m pointing in the negative direction. Magnitude is never negative. If your final answer is a magnitude and it came out negative, you have reported the component and called it a magnitude.\n\nSee [[kin-vector-components]] for what happens when the motion is not confined to one line.",
  "formula": "scalar     : magnitude only            e.g. distance d = 30 m, speed v = 12 m/s\nvector     : magnitude AND direction   e.g. displacement, velocity, acceleration\n\nvector form     :  v(vec) = v0(vec) + a(vec) t\ncomponent form  :  v_x    = v_x0     + a_x t        <- sign = direction\n\n1-D vector sum  :  (+30 m) + (-50 m) = -20 m  =  20 m in the -x direction",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A cyclist on a straight bike path rides 30 m east, stops, then rides 50 m west. Taking east as the positive x direction, find (a) the total distance travelled and (b) the vector sum of the two displacements, stated as a magnitude and a direction.",
    "given": "Leg 1: 30 m east. Leg 2: 50 m west. Positive x is east.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Write down the axis and the positive direction before touching the numbers: $+x$ is east.",
        "why": "Every sign after this point is meaningless without it. Fixing the convention first is what lets you stop thinking about directions in words."
      },
      {
        "do": "Distance travelled $= 30 + 50 = 80$ m.",
        "why": "Distance is a scalar. It counts path length and has no direction to cancel, so the two legs add even though they point opposite ways."
      },
      {
        "do": "Write the two displacements as signed components: $\\Delta x_1 = +30$ m and $\\Delta x_2 = -50$ m.",
        "why": "West is the negative direction under the convention just chosen, so the second leg gets a minus sign. This is the step where the geometry becomes arithmetic."
      },
      {
        "do": "Sum them: $\\Delta x = (+30) + (-50) = -20$ m.",
        "why": "Opposite directions are opposite signs, so a one-dimensional vector sum is ordinary addition of signed numbers."
      },
      {
        "do": "Convert the signed component back into magnitude and direction: magnitude 20 m, direction west.",
        "why": "The question asked for a magnitude and a direction, and a magnitude is never negative. The minus sign is not part of the size; it is the direction, and it should be spoken rather than written once you say 'magnitude'."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "(a) distance $= 80$ m. (b) vector sum $= -20$ m, i.e. 20 m west of the start.",
    "check": "Limiting case: if the second leg had also been 30 m, the sum would be $(+30)+(-30) = 0$ — the cyclist is back where they started, displacement zero, while the distance is still 60 m. The formalism gives the answer you already know is right, which is evidence the sign convention was applied consistently. Also note $80 \\ne 20$: any method that produced the same number for both parts has confused a scalar with a vector."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Signs are used instead of words because they survive being fed into an equation. '50 m west' cannot be substituted into $x = x_0 + v_{x0}t + \\frac{1}{2}a_x t^2$; $-50$ m can. The convention costs you one line at the top of the page and buys you an entire problem in which direction takes care of itself.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "The tempting alternative is to keep every number positive and track direction in words at the side — '20 m/s, moving left; slowing at 3 m/s², so subtract'. It works for one step and then fails, because deciding when to subtract *is* the physics you were trying to avoid doing. The sign convention makes that decision once, in advance, and correctly.",
  "trap": "Two failures, both common. First, dropping a minus sign because the answer 'ought to be positive' — a velocity component is allowed to be negative, and forcing it positive throws away exactly the information the vector was carrying. Second, calling speed a vector. Speed is the magnitude of velocity, so it is a scalar and never negative; if you have written a negative speed, you meant a velocity component."
},
{
  "id": "kin-position-displacement",
  "group": "u1",
  "prereqs": [
    "kin-scalars-vectors"
  ],
  "source": "CED 1.2",
  "title": "Position, displacement, and why distance is a different question",
  "analogy": "Your phone's step counter and a map pin answer different questions. The counter says how much walking happened; the pin says where you are now. Walk a full loop of the block and the counter reads 800 steps while the pin has not moved at all. Neither is wrong. They are distance and displacement.",
  "plain": "Before you can talk about motion you need somewhere to measure from. Choose an origin and an axis, and every location on that axis gets a number: its **position**, written $x$. Position is a vector quantity — it has a direction relative to the origin, carried by its sign.\n\n**Displacement** is the change in position: where you ended minus where you started, $\\Delta x = x - x_0$. It depends only on those two positions. Everything that happened in between — the stops, the doubling back, the scenic route — leaves no trace in it.\n\n**Distance** is the other question: how much path was covered. It is a scalar, it never decreases as the motion continues, and it is equal to the magnitude of the displacement only when the object never reverses direction.\n\nPhysics also does something quietly drastic here. When we say 'the object', we throw away its size, its shape and everything going on inside it, and treat it as a single point that still has properties like mass. That is the **object model**, and it is what makes it legal to say a car is 'at $x = 40$ m' when the car is four metres long.",
  "depth": "Position is defined relative to an origin you choose, so the *value* of $x$ is a convention while $\\Delta x$ is not. Slide the origin three metres and every position in the problem changes by three metres, but every displacement is untouched, because the shift subtracts out of $x - x_0$. This is worth knowing before you set up a problem: you may put the origin wherever it makes the arithmetic cleanest, and it cannot change a displacement, a velocity or an acceleration.\n\nThe relation $\\Delta x = x - x_0$ is not printed on the provided equation sheet. Neither is any definition of distance. The sheet gives you the three constant-acceleration equations and expects the definitions of the quantities appearing in them to be yours already. That is a small but real thing to carry into the exam.\n\nFor a trip with a reversal, split it at the turning point. On each leg the object moves one way, so on each leg the distance covered equals $|\\Delta x|$ for that leg. Add the signed $\\Delta x$ values to get the total displacement; add the unsigned ones to get the total distance. The two sums differ exactly when some leg is negative — which is another way of saying that distance and displacement agree if and only if the motion never turns round.\n\nThe object model has limits the course cares about later: a system with internal structure is handled by tracking its centre of mass rather than pretending it is a point. For Unit 1 the point treatment is always available.",
  "formula": "position       x        (measured from a chosen origin, sign = which side)\ndisplacement   dx = x - x0        vector; can be +, -, or zero\ndistance       d  = total path length      scalar; never negative, never decreases\n\nd = |dx|  ONLY if the object never reverses direction",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A trolley on a straight track starts at $x_0 = +2.0$ m, rolls forward to $x = +9.0$ m, then rolls back and stops at $x = +4.0$ m. Find its total displacement and the total distance it travelled.",
    "given": "Start $+2.0$ m; furthest point $+9.0$ m; finish $+4.0$ m. Positive x is forward.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Note that the motion reverses at $x = +9.0$ m, and split the trip there into two legs.",
        "why": "Distance and displacement only differ because of a reversal. Splitting at the turning point is what makes each leg one-directional, and on a one-directional leg the two quantities agree."
      },
      {
        "do": "Total displacement: $\\Delta x = x - x_0 = 4.0 - 2.0 = +2.0$ m.",
        "why": "Displacement needs only the endpoints, so the trip to $+9.0$ m does not enter the calculation at all. Resisting the urge to use the 9.0 is the whole skill here."
      },
      {
        "do": "Leg 1 distance: $|9.0 - 2.0| = 7.0$ m. Leg 2 distance: $|4.0 - 9.0| = 5.0$ m.",
        "why": "Each leg is one-directional, so its path length is the magnitude of its own displacement. The absolute value is what stops the return leg from cancelling the outward leg."
      },
      {
        "do": "Total distance $= 7.0 + 5.0 = 12$ m.",
        "why": "Distance is a scalar: legs add, they never subtract, regardless of direction."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "Displacement $= +2.0$ m (2.0 m forward). Distance $= 12$ m.",
    "check": "Two independent checks. (1) Distance must be at least the magnitude of displacement, and $12 \\ge 2.0$ holds. If it had come out smaller, something was subtracted that should not have been. (2) Move the origin: measure from the start instead, so the positions become $0$, $+7.0$, $+2.0$. Displacement is still $2.0 - 0 = +2.0$ m and the distance is still $7.0 + 5.0 = 12$ m. Both answers are origin-independent, as they must be."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Displacement is defined by the endpoints rather than the path because that is what the rest of mechanics can use. Velocity, acceleration and later the work done by a constant force are all built on changes between states. A path-length quantity would not slot into any of them — you cannot write a useful equation of motion in terms of how scenic the route was.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "Defining 'how far it went' as the path length and using that everywhere feels more honest, and for a fuel bill it is. But then two objects that end in the same place would have different 'positions', and the idea of a position would be gone. Distance is kept as a separate, clearly labelled scalar instead of being allowed to contaminate the vector.",
  "trap": "On a problem with a turning point, the marks go to whoever noticed the reversal. It is easy to report the distance when the question asked for displacement, or use the furthest position as the final position. Read the last sentence of the question again and ask: does it want *where it ended up*, or *how much ground it covered*?"
},
{
  "id": "kin-average-velocity",
  "group": "u1",
  "prereqs": [
    "kin-position-displacement"
  ],
  "source": "CED 1.2",
  "title": "Average velocity, average speed, and the instantaneous limit",
  "analogy": "A car journey of 120 km in 2 hours 'averages 60 km/h' even if the car never once sat at 60. The average is a summary of the whole interval, and a summary is allowed to describe a value that never actually occurred.",
  "plain": "**Average velocity** is displacement divided by the time it took: $\\vec{v}_{avg} = \\frac{\\Delta\\vec{x}}{\\Delta t}$. Because displacement is a vector and time is a positive scalar, average velocity is a vector pointing the same way as the displacement.\n\n**Average speed** is the other one: total distance divided by total time. It uses the path length, so it is a scalar and it is never negative. The two are computed from different numerators, which is why an object that returns to its starting point has an average velocity of exactly zero and an average speed that is nothing of the sort.\n\nNeither average tells you anything about the middle of the interval. Both only look at initial and final states. If you want to know how fast the object was going at one instant, you have to shrink the interval: compute the average over a smaller and smaller stretch of time around that moment, and the value you get closes in on the **instantaneous velocity**.\n\nSo instantaneous velocity is not a different kind of thing from average velocity. It is what average velocity becomes when the interval gets short enough that the motion has no time to change during it.",
  "depth": "The defining relation $\\vec{v}_{avg} = \\frac{\\Delta\\vec{x}}{\\Delta t}$ is **not on the provided equation sheet**. The sheet prints the three constant-acceleration equations and assumes you know what average velocity means. Carry it in your head.\n\nThis matters because average velocity is the more general quantity. It is true for any motion whatever — accelerating, decelerating, reversing, jerking — since it is a definition, not a model. The constant-acceleration equations in [[kin-constant-a-equations]] are the ones with a condition attached.\n\nOne special case is worth memorising because it is the bridge between the definition and the equations: when acceleration is constant, and only then, the average velocity over an interval equals the mean of the initial and final velocities, $v_{avg} = \\frac{v_0 + v}{2}$. That equation is also not on the sheet. Under constant acceleration the velocity is a straight line against time, and the average height of a straight line is its midpoint value — which is exactly why the rule fails when the graph is curved.\n\nThe instantaneous limit is stated in the CED as a practical rule rather than a derivative: the average over a very small interval is very close to the instantaneous value. Graphically that is the statement that a short enough chord is indistinguishable from the tangent, which is the content of [[kin-graphs-slope]]. Algebra-based physics does not ask you to take the limit formally; it asks you to know that a tangent slope and a very short average are the same measurement.",
  "formula": "average velocity   v_avg = dx / dt          vector; sign from dx\naverage speed      s_avg = (path length) / dt      scalar; >= 0\n\nconstant acceleration ONLY:   v_avg = (v0 + v) / 2\n\ninstantaneous v = the value v_avg approaches as dt -> 0\nNONE of these three are printed on the AP equation sheet.",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "The trolley of the previous example starts at $x_0 = +2.0$ m, reaches $+9.0$ m after 3.5 s, then returns to $+4.0$ m at $t = 6.0$ s. Find its average velocity and its average speed over the full 6.0 s.",
    "given": "$\\Delta x = +2.0$ m; total distance $= 12$ m; $\\Delta t = 6.0$ s.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Use the displacement, $+2.0$ m, for average velocity — not the 12 m.",
        "why": "The definition has displacement in the numerator. The 3.5 s split is given to tempt you into treating the legs separately; the whole-interval average does not care where the turning point fell."
      },
      {
        "do": "$v_{avg} = \\frac{+2.0}{6.0} = +0.33$ m/s.",
        "why": "Dividing a vector by a positive scalar keeps the direction, so the sign of the displacement carries straight through to the answer: the trolley finished forward of where it started."
      },
      {
        "do": "$s_{avg} = \\frac{12}{6.0} = 2.0$ m/s.",
        "why": "Average speed takes the path length instead, which is the whole of the difference between the two quantities. Same interval, same object, different question."
      },
      {
        "do": "State both with their character: velocity $+0.33$ m/s (forward), speed 2.0 m/s (no direction).",
        "why": "Reporting a direction on the speed, or omitting the sign on the velocity, loses the distinction the question was testing."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "Average velocity $= +0.33$ m/s, i.e. 0.33 m/s forward. Average speed $= 2.0$ m/s.",
    "check": "Average speed can never be less than the magnitude of average velocity, because path length can never be less than $|\\Delta x|$ over the same interval. Here $2.0 > 0.33$, consistent. An independent order-of-magnitude check: the trolley covered 7.0 m in the first 3.5 s, so it was moving at roughly 2 m/s on the way out — the average speed of 2.0 m/s sits right there, while the average velocity is much smaller because most of the outward trip was undone."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Average velocity is defined with displacement rather than distance so that velocities combine the way displacements do. Two successive stages of a journey have displacements that add, so their contributions to overall velocity add too. Build the definition on path length instead and that additivity is gone, along with any hope of relating velocity to a change in position.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "Treating 'average velocity' as the mean of the velocities you observed along the way — read the speedometer ten times and take the mean — sounds equivalent and is not. It weights each *reading* equally instead of each *second*, so ten readings taken during a brief fast burst would drag the mean up. The definition weights by time, which is why it uses total displacement over total time.",
  "trap": "$v_{avg} = \\frac{v_0 + v}{2}$ is only legal when the acceleration is constant. Students apply it to a two-stage trip — half an hour at 30 km/h, half an hour at 90 km/h — and get lucky when the *times* are equal, then apply it to equal *distances* and get it wrong. If the stages are equal distances rather than equal times, go back to total displacement over total time."
},
{
  "id": "kin-acceleration",
  "group": "u1",
  "prereqs": [
    "kin-average-velocity"
  ],
  "source": "CED 1.2",
  "title": "Acceleration, and the sign trap that follows from it",
  "analogy": "Braking hard in reverse is still 'accelerating' in physics. The word in a physics problem means the velocity is changing; it does not mean going faster, and it does not carry the everyday flavour of a foot on a pedal.",
  "plain": "**Average acceleration** is the change in velocity divided by the time it took: $\\vec{a}_{avg} = \\frac{\\Delta\\vec{v}}{\\Delta t}$. It is built out of velocity in exactly the way velocity is built out of position — a rate of change of the thing below it.\n\nAn object is accelerating whenever the **magnitude or the direction** of its velocity is changing. That second half is easy to miss. A car going round a bend at a steady 40 km/h is accelerating, because the direction of its velocity is changing even though the speedometer is not moving.\n\nNow the trap. Acceleration is a vector, so it has a sign, and that sign says which way the acceleration points — not whether the object is speeding up. Speeding up and slowing down is decided by comparing the sign of the acceleration with the sign of the velocity:\n\n- same signs: the object is speeding up\n- opposite signs: the object is slowing down\n\nSo an object moving in the negative direction with a positive acceleration is slowing down. There is nothing paradoxical about it once you stop reading 'positive acceleration' as 'going faster'.",
  "depth": "$\\vec{a}_{avg} = \\frac{\\Delta\\vec{v}}{\\Delta t}$ is **not on the provided equation sheet**, in the same way the average-velocity definition is not. What the sheet does print is $\\vec{F}_{net} = \\frac{\\Delta\\vec{p}}{\\Delta t} = m\\frac{\\Delta\\vec{v}}{\\Delta t} = m\\vec{a}$, which contains the definition inside it — a useful place to recover it from if it deserts you under pressure.\n\nThe numerator $\\Delta\\vec{v} = \\vec{v} - \\vec{v}_0$ is a vector subtraction. In one dimension it is signed subtraction, and the classic sign error lives in the double negative: a velocity going from $-20$ m/s to $-8$ m/s has $\\Delta v = (-8) - (-20) = +12$ m/s, a *positive* change, even though the object is moving backwards throughout. The acceleration is positive; the speed fell from 20 to 8. Both statements are true at once.\n\nThe instantaneous acceleration is the same limiting story as for velocity: shrink the interval until the average stops changing, and graphically that is the tangent slope on a velocity–time graph, which is [[kin-graphs-slope]].\n\nConstant acceleration is a special case, not the general one. AP Physics 1 will not ask you to compute anything about non-uniform acceleration, but it will ask you to describe it, sketch it, and reason about it qualitatively — the CED says so explicitly. So 'the acceleration is changing' is a sentence you may have to write, even though it is never a sentence you have to integrate.",
  "formula": "average acceleration   a_avg = dv / dt = (v - v0) / (t - t0)      [not on the sheet]\n\naccelerating  <=>  speed changing  OR  direction changing\n\nsign(a) = sign(v)   ->  speeding up\nsign(a) = -sign(v)  ->  slowing down\na = 0               ->  constant velocity (constant speed AND straight line)",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A car is reversing down a straight driveway at 20 m/s when the driver brakes. Four seconds later it is reversing at 8 m/s. Taking forward as the positive direction, find the average acceleration, and state whether the car is speeding up or slowing down.",
    "given": "$v_0 = -20$ m/s, $v = -8$ m/s, $\\Delta t = 4.0$ s. Forward is $+x$.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Write both velocities as signed components: $v_0 = -20$ m/s, $v = -8$ m/s.",
        "why": "Reversing is the negative direction under the stated convention, so both velocities are negative. Skipping this step and using 20 and 8 is where most of the marks are lost on this problem."
      },
      {
        "do": "$\\Delta v = v - v_0 = (-8) - (-20) = +12$ m/s.",
        "why": "Final minus initial, always in that order. Subtracting a negative is what makes the change positive here — the velocity moved *up the number line*, from $-20$ towards zero."
      },
      {
        "do": "$a_{avg} = \\frac{+12}{4.0} = +3.0$ m/s$^2$.",
        "why": "Dividing by a positive time keeps the sign, so the acceleration points forward — which is the direction the brakes push the car, and that is the physical sanity check."
      },
      {
        "do": "Compare signs: $v$ is negative, $a$ is positive, so they oppose. The car is slowing down.",
        "why": "The sign of $a$ alone never answers 'speeding up or slowing down'. Only the comparison does. Here a positive acceleration is unambiguously a deceleration, because the motion is negative."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$a_{avg} = +3.0$ m/s$^2$ (magnitude 3.0 m/s$^2$, directed forward). The car is slowing down.",
    "check": "Independent check by magnitudes only: the *speed* dropped from 20 m/s to 8 m/s, a drop of 12 m/s over 4.0 s, which is 3.0 m/s per second. The size agrees with the signed calculation, which means no arithmetic slipped. And the direction agrees with physics that has nothing to do with kinematics: a braking force on a backwards-moving car points forwards, and by $\\vec{F}_{net} = m\\vec{a}$ so does the acceleration."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Acceleration is defined as the rate of change of the velocity *vector*, not of the speed, because that is the version Newton's second law needs. Force is a vector; if acceleration only tracked speed, then a force perpendicular to the motion — the one that turns a car on a bend — would produce no acceleration at all, and the second law would be false for every curved path.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "The everyday definition, 'acceleration means getting faster', would make deceleration a separate phenomenon needing its own equation, and would have nothing to say about an object turning at constant speed. One signed vector quantity covers speeding up, slowing down and turning, and connects to force. The everyday word does none of that.",
  "trap": "'Negative acceleration means slowing down' is the single most expensive false belief in Unit 1, and it survives because it is right roughly half the time. It is only right when the velocity is positive. Whenever an exam question hands you a negative velocity — a falling ball after it has turned round, a cart rolling back down a ramp — check the two signs against each other rather than reading the acceleration alone."
},
{
  "id": "kin-graphs-slope",
  "group": "u1",
  "prereqs": [
    "kin-acceleration"
  ],
  "source": "CED 1.3",
  "title": "Reading motion graphs: what a slope means",
  "analogy": "A graph of your bank balance against time does not show your salary anywhere as a height — the salary is how steeply the balance climbs. The rate is in the steepness, not in the value. Motion graphs work exactly this way, twice over.",
  "plain": "Motion can be represented as a diagram, a table, an equation, a sentence or a graph, and the exam moves between these constantly. The graphs are where most of the marks live, and they obey one rule you can apply to all three of them.\n\nOn a **position–time** graph, the slope is the velocity. Steep means fast. Flat means stationary. A downward slope means a negative velocity — moving towards smaller positions.\n\nOn a **velocity–time** graph, the slope is the acceleration. So the same steepness idea appears again, one level up.\n\nIf the graph is a straight line, the slope is the same everywhere and one calculation of rise over run gives you the answer. If it is curved, the slope is different at every point, and 'the velocity at $t = 3$ s' means the slope of the **tangent** to the curve at that point. A curved position–time graph is exactly what accelerating looks like.",
  "depth": "The two statements are the graphical form of the two definitions. Instantaneous velocity is the rate of change of position, so it is the tangent slope on an $x$–$t$ graph; instantaneous acceleration is the rate of change of velocity, so it is the tangent slope on a $v$–$t$ graph. Drawing a chord between two points on the curve and taking its slope gives you the *average* over that interval — which is why the CED's remark that a short-enough average approaches the instantaneous value is the same claim as 'a short enough chord lies along the tangent'.\n\nReading the shape is a skill in itself. On an $x$–$t$ graph: a straight line means constant velocity and therefore zero acceleration; a curve bending upwards means the slope is increasing, so the velocity is increasing; a maximum or minimum means the slope is momentarily zero, so the object is instantaneously at rest — this is where a thrown ball turns round.\n\nA caution about that last point. At the top of the throw the position graph has a horizontal tangent, so $v = 0$ there. The *acceleration* is not zero there: the velocity–time graph is passing through zero with a steep slope, not flattening out. Zero velocity and zero acceleration are different features of different graphs, and reading one off the other is a standard exam trap.\n\nUnits are a free consistency check. Slope on an $x$–$t$ graph has units of metres per second; slope on a $v$–$t$ graph has metres per second per second. If your rise-over-run produced something else, you divided the wrong way round. See [[kin-graphs-area]] for the other direction of travel between these graphs.",
  "formula": "x-t graph :  slope = velocity          [m/s]\nv-t graph :  slope = acceleration      [m/s^2]\na-t graph :  slope = (not assessed quantitatively in AP Physics 1)\n\nstraight line -> slope constant -> one rise/run does it\ncurve         -> slope varies   -> tangent at the instant asked for\nchord slope   -> the AVERAGE over that interval",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A cart's position–time graph is made of three straight segments. From $t = 0$ to $t = 3.0$ s the position rises from $+4.0$ m to $+10.0$ m. From $t = 3.0$ s to $t = 5.0$ s it stays at $+10.0$ m. From $t = 5.0$ s to $t = 8.0$ s it falls from $+10.0$ m to $-2.0$ m. Find the velocity in each segment, and sketch in words what the velocity–time graph looks like.",
    "given": "Three straight segments, endpoints as stated.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Recognise that every segment is straight, so within each one the velocity is constant and a single rise-over-run is exact.",
        "why": "Straightness is the licence to use one calculation for a whole interval. On a curve the same arithmetic would only give the average, and the question would need a tangent instead."
      },
      {
        "do": "Segment 1: $v = \\frac{10.0 - 4.0}{3.0 - 0} = +2.0$ m/s.",
        "why": "Rise over run, with rise in metres and run in seconds, so the units come out as velocity automatically. The sign is positive because the position increased."
      },
      {
        "do": "Segment 2: $v = \\frac{10.0 - 10.0}{5.0 - 3.0} = 0$.",
        "why": "A horizontal line on an $x$–$t$ graph is not 'no graph' — it is the statement that the object is parked at $+10.0$ m. Zero slope is a real reading, not a missing one."
      },
      {
        "do": "Segment 3: $v = \\frac{-2.0 - 10.0}{8.0 - 5.0} = \\frac{-12.0}{3.0} = -4.0$ m/s.",
        "why": "The cart is now moving towards smaller positions, so the velocity component is negative. It also passes through the origin on the way, which changes nothing — the origin is not a special place."
      },
      {
        "do": "Describe the $v$–$t$ graph: a horizontal line at $+2.0$ m/s for 3.0 s, a jump down to 0 held for 2.0 s, then a jump down to $-4.0$ m/s for 3.0 s.",
        "why": "Each straight segment of $x$–$t$ becomes a horizontal segment of $v$–$t$, because constant slope means constant velocity. The vertical jumps are an artefact of the idealised straight-line graph: a real cart would need a short interval to change velocity."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$v_1 = +2.0$ m/s, $v_2 = 0$, $v_3 = -4.0$ m/s. The velocity–time graph is three horizontal steps at $+2.0$, $0$ and $-4.0$ m/s.",
    "check": "Independent check via the whole-trip definition rather than the graph: the cart went from $+4.0$ m to $-2.0$ m in 8.0 s, so its average velocity must be $\\frac{-6.0}{8.0} = -0.75$ m/s. Rebuilding that from the segments, $\\frac{(2.0)(3.0) + (0)(2.0) + (-4.0)(3.0)}{8.0} = \\frac{6.0 + 0 - 12.0}{8.0} = -0.75$ m/s. The two agree, so the three slopes are mutually consistent — an error in any one of them would have broken this."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Slope is used rather than the height of the graph because rate of change is what the definitions are made of. Velocity is *defined* as a change in position over a change in time, and rise over run is that ratio drawn. This is also why the same rule reappears one level up on the velocity graph without needing a new idea.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "Reading the velocity off the *height* of the position–time graph is the most common wrong method, and it is seductive because 'higher up means further along' feels like 'faster'. It fails immediately on a horizontal line high on the graph, where the object is far from the origin and completely stationary.",
  "trap": "Mistaking a maximum on an $x$–$t$ graph for a maximum speed. It is the opposite: at a peak the tangent is horizontal, so the object is instantaneously at rest. Also watch which graph you are handed — a horizontal line means 'not moving' on an $x$–$t$ graph but 'moving at a steady speed' on a $v$–$t$ graph. Always read the vertical axis label before saying anything about the shape."
},
{
  "id": "kin-graphs-area",
  "group": "u1",
  "prereqs": [
    "kin-graphs-slope"
  ],
  "source": "CED 1.3",
  "title": "Reading motion graphs: what an area means",
  "analogy": "If you drive at 20 m/s for 30 s you cover 600 m, and on a graph of speed against time that is literally the rectangle 20 tall by 30 wide. The area is not a metaphor for the distance; multiplying the height by the width *is* the calculation.",
  "plain": "Slopes take you down the chain, from position to velocity to acceleration. Areas take you back up it.\n\nThe area under a **velocity–time** graph, between the curve and the horizontal axis, over some interval, is the **displacement** during that interval.\n\nThe area under an **acceleration–time** graph over an interval is the **change in velocity** during that interval.\n\nThe areas are signed. Where the graph is below the axis the area counts as negative, because the object is moving in the negative direction and its position is going down. Add the positive and negative pieces with their signs and you get displacement. Add their magnitudes instead and you get distance travelled. That single choice is where most of the marks on graph questions are won or lost.\n\nFor the shapes the exam uses — rectangles, triangles, trapezia — you do not need anything beyond the geometry on the equation sheet: $A = bh$ for a rectangle and $A = \\frac{1}{2}bh$ for a triangle.",
  "depth": "Why an area, rather than something else? Over a very short interval $\\Delta t$ the velocity is effectively constant, and the displacement in that sliver is $v \\Delta t$ — the height of the graph times its width, a thin rectangle. The whole displacement is what you get by laying those slivers side by side, which is the area. The same argument with $a$ and $\\Delta v$ gives the acceleration-graph rule.\n\nWhen a velocity graph crosses the axis, the object has reversed direction, and the crossing point is where the two questions split: displacement wants the signed sum, distance wants the sum of magnitudes. Find the crossing time first, before computing any areas, and the rest is arithmetic. This is the same reversal that separated distance from displacement in [[kin-position-displacement]], seen on a graph.\n\nThe area rule holds for any shape of velocity graph, including curved ones — it is a consequence of the definition, not of constant acceleration. What changes for a curved graph is only that you may not be able to compute the area exactly with rectangles and triangles. AP Physics 1 keeps quantitative work to shapes you can decompose, and asks for qualitative reasoning when the acceleration is non-uniform.\n\nNote what the area rule does **not** give you. The area under a velocity–time graph is the *change* in position, not the position. To get the actual position you need to know where the object started and add: $x = x_0 + (\\text{area})$. Every graph question that asks 'where is it at $t = 6$ s' rather than 'how far did it move' is testing whether you noticed.",
  "formula": "area under v-t  =  displacement dx        [m/s * s = m]\narea under a-t  =  change in velocity dv  [m/s^2 * s = m/s]\n\nbelow the axis  =>  negative area\nsigned sum of areas   -> displacement\nsum of |areas|        -> distance travelled\n\nposition needs the start:  x = x0 + (signed area)\nrectangle A = bh          triangle A = (1/2)bh     [both on the equation sheet]",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A cart's velocity–time graph is $+6.0$ m/s, constant, from $t = 0$ to $t = 4.0$ s; it then decreases along a straight line to $-2.0$ m/s at $t = 8.0$ s. Find the cart's displacement over the 8.0 s, the distance it travelled, and its acceleration during the second phase.",
    "given": "$v = +6.0$ m/s on $0 \\le t \\le 4.0$ s; straight line from $(4.0, +6.0)$ to $(8.0, -2.0)$.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Find where the velocity graph crosses the axis. The second phase has slope $\\frac{-2.0 - 6.0}{8.0 - 4.0} = -2.0$ m/s$^2$, so $v = 0$ when $6.0 - 2.0(t - 4.0) = 0$, giving $t = 7.0$ s.",
        "why": "The crossing is where displacement and distance stop agreeing, so it has to be located before any area is computed. Doing this first also hands you the acceleration for free, since the slope of a $v$–$t$ graph is the acceleration."
      },
      {
        "do": "Area of phase 1: a rectangle, $(6.0)(4.0) = +24$ m.",
        "why": "Height times width, with the height being a velocity and the width a time, so the product is a displacement. Positive because the graph is above the axis."
      },
      {
        "do": "Area from 4.0 s to 7.0 s: a triangle, $\\frac{1}{2}(3.0)(6.0) = +9.0$ m.",
        "why": "The velocity is still positive here, falling linearly to zero, so the region is a triangle with base 3.0 s and height 6.0 m/s. The cart is still moving forwards, just less and less."
      },
      {
        "do": "Area from 7.0 s to 8.0 s: a triangle, $\\frac{1}{2}(1.0)(2.0) = 1.0$ m in size, counted as $-1.0$ m.",
        "why": "This piece lies below the axis: the velocity is negative, so the cart is moving backwards and its position is decreasing. The geometry gives the size; the position of the region relative to the axis gives the sign."
      },
      {
        "do": "Displacement $= +24 + 9.0 - 1.0 = +32$ m. Distance $= 24 + 9.0 + 1.0 = 34$ m.",
        "why": "Displacement is the signed sum because the backwards metre and a bit undoes part of the forward travel. Distance counts every metre of path regardless of direction, so the same three numbers are added as magnitudes."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "Displacement $= +32$ m. Distance $= 34$ m. Acceleration in phase 2 $= -2.0$ m/s$^2$.",
    "check": "Independent check on the displacement, using average velocity instead of areas: $v_{avg} = \\frac{32}{8.0} = 4.0$ m/s. That must lie between the extreme velocities $-2.0$ and $+6.0$ m/s, and it does, sitting nearer the top because the cart spent most of the interval fast and forwards. A second check on the split: distance minus displacement is $34 - 32 = 2.0$ m, which should be exactly twice the backwards travel, $2 \\times 1.0 = 1.0 \\times 2 = 2.0$ m. It is — the backwards leg is subtracted once from the displacement and added once to the distance."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Displacement is read as an area rather than from the endpoints of the velocity graph because velocity has to be summed over time, not sampled. Two motions can start and end at the same velocity and cover wildly different ground, depending on what happened in between — and 'what happened in between' is precisely what an area accumulates and an endpoint ignores.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "A tempting shortcut is to take the final velocity times the total time, or the average of the first and last velocities times the time. The second is not always wrong: it works whenever the velocity graph is a single straight line, because then the trapezium's area really is the mean height times the width. It fails here, because the graph is a rectangle joined to a slope, and $\\frac{6.0 + (-2.0)}{2} \\times 8.0 = 16$ m — nowhere near 31 m.",
  "trap": "Forgetting that area below the axis is negative, and reporting 34 m when the question asked for displacement. The two answers differ by twice the backwards travel, so this is never a small error. The other half of the trap is answering 'where is the cart' with the area alone: the area is the *change* in position and must be added to the starting position."
},
{
  "id": "kin-constant-a-equations",
  "group": "u1",
  "prereqs": [
    "kin-acceleration",
    "kin-graphs-area"
  ],
  "source": "CED 1.3",
  "title": "The three constant-acceleration equations, and when they are legal",
  "analogy": "A recipe that says 'bake at a steady 180°C' stops being a recipe if someone keeps turning the oven dial. The three equations are the same kind of promise: they are exact, and they are exact only while the acceleration is being held still.",
  "plain": "When the acceleration is constant, three equations describe the motion completely. All three are printed on the AP equation sheet, in component form:\n\n- $v_x = v_{x0} + a_x t$ — velocity after a time $t$\n- $x = x_0 + v_{x0}t + \\frac{1}{2}a_x t^2$ — position after a time $t$\n- $v_x^2 = v_{x0}^2 + 2a_x\\left(x - x_0\\right)$ — velocity after a displacement, with no $t$ in it\n\nBetween them they connect five quantities: initial velocity, final velocity, acceleration, displacement and time. Each equation is missing exactly one of the five. That is how you choose. Write down which three you know and which one you want, and then pick the equation that does not contain the fifth — the one you neither know nor want.\n\nThey are written with $x$ subscripts because they apply to one axis at a time. The CED says explicitly that they work in any single direction, so the same three equations, with $y$ in place of $x$, handle vertical motion.\n\nThe condition is not decoration. If the acceleration changes during the interval, all three are simply wrong, and no amount of care with the arithmetic will rescue them.",
  "depth": "The equations are not independent facts to memorise separately; they are the same content in three forms. The first is the definition of constant acceleration rearranged. The second is the area under a straight-line velocity graph: a rectangle $v_{x0}t$ plus a triangle $\\frac{1}{2}(a_x t)(t)$ — which is why [[kin-graphs-area]] comes before this concept rather than after it. The third is the first two with $t$ eliminated between them, and its value is precisely that: it answers questions in which time is neither given nor wanted.\n\nA reliable procedure. (1) Draw the axis and fix the positive direction. (2) Choose an origin, and prefer the one that makes $x_0 = 0$. (3) List $v_{x0}$, $v_x$, $a_x$, $\\Delta x$, $t$ and mark which are known. (4) Pick the equation missing the one you neither know nor want. (5) Substitute *with signs*. Nearly every lost mark in this topic is a sign that went in unsigned at step 5.\n\nSome consequences worth having ready. If $a_x = 0$ the second equation collapses to $x = x_0 + v_{x0}t$, constant velocity — the constant-velocity case is not a separate model, it is this one with a zero in it. Starting from rest, $v_{x0} = 0$, displacement grows as $t^2$: double the time, quadruple the distance. And $v_x^2 = v_{x0}^2 + 2a_x\\Delta x$ contains a squared velocity, so it hands you a magnitude and leaves the direction to you: taking the square root gives two signs, and the physical situation decides which.\n\nThe boundary the CED draws is sharp. Non-uniform acceleration will not be analysed quantitatively in AP Physics 1, but you will be asked to describe it, sketch graphs of it, and reason about it. So the honest answer to 'use the kinematic equations to find the speed of a falling coffee filter' is that you cannot, and saying why earns the mark that using them anyway would lose.",
  "formula": "CONDITION: acceleration constant over the whole interval\n\n  v_x   = v_x0 + a_x t                        [missing: displacement]\n  x     = x0 + v_x0 t + (1/2) a_x t^2         [missing: final velocity]\n  v_x^2 = v_x0^2 + 2 a_x (x - x0)             [missing: time]\n\nAll three ARE printed on the AP Physics 1 equation sheet.\nSame three equations work along y; swap the subscript.\n\nchoose by: list v0, v, a, dx, t -> find the one you neither know nor want\n           -> use the equation that does not contain it",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A go-kart starts from rest and accelerates uniformly at 2.0 m/s$^2$ along a straight track. How fast is it moving after it has covered 36 m, and how long did that take?",
    "given": "$v_{x0} = 0$, $a_x = +2.0$ m/s$^2$, $\\Delta x = +36$ m. Take the direction of travel as $+x$ and the start as the origin.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Set the origin at the start, so $x_0 = 0$ and $x = +36$ m, with $+x$ along the direction of travel.",
        "why": "Putting the origin at the start makes $x_0$ vanish from every equation. Nothing physical depends on where the origin is, so you may as well take the version with fewer terms to mis-copy."
      },
      {
        "do": "List what is known: $v_{x0} = 0$, $a_x = +2.0$, $\\Delta x = +36$. Wanted: $v_x$. Neither known nor wanted: $t$.",
        "why": "This is the selection step. Identifying the unwanted fifth quantity is what picks the equation, rather than trying each one and hoping."
      },
      {
        "do": "Use the equation without $t$: $v_x^2 = v_{x0}^2 + 2a_x(x - x_0) = 0 + 2(2.0)(36) = 144$.",
        "why": "Choosing this one avoids solving a quadratic in $t$ and then substituting back. It is the same answer either way, but one route has half the places to slip."
      },
      {
        "do": "$v_x = +12$ m/s, taking the positive root.",
        "why": "The square root has two signs; the kart is moving in the $+x$ direction, so the positive one is the physical solution. The equation cannot know that, and this is where you supply it."
      },
      {
        "do": "Now find $t$ from $v_x = v_{x0} + a_x t$: $12 = 0 + 2.0t$, so $t = 6.0$ s.",
        "why": "With $v_x$ now known, the first equation is the shortest route to the time. Reusing a result you have just computed is fine as long as you are confident in it — and the check below is what makes that confidence earned."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$v_x = 12$ m/s after 36 m, reached at $t = 6.0$ s.",
    "check": "Independent check by area, not by re-substitution. Under constant acceleration the velocity graph is a straight line from 0 to 12 m/s, so the average velocity is $\\frac{0 + 12}{2} = 6.0$ m/s, and the displacement is the area of that triangle, $(6.0)(6.0) = 36$ m. That reproduces the given 36 m, using a different relationship from either equation used above. A dimensional check as well: $2a_x\\Delta x$ has units of (m/s$^2$)(m) $=$ m$^2$/s$^2$, which is a velocity squared, as $v_x^2$ must be."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "There are three equations rather than one because three of the five quantities are usually known and different problems withhold different ones. The alternative — a single relation plus algebra every time — is exactly what the third equation saves you from, since eliminating $t$ by hand under exam pressure is a reliable source of error. The set is small enough to memorise and, more importantly, it is printed for you.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "Using $v_{avg} = \\frac{\\Delta x}{\\Delta t}$ for everything is the general, always-true approach, and it is worse here. It is true for any motion, but it gives you no way to bring the acceleration in, so it can only ever tell you about an interval you already know both ends of. The constant-acceleration equations trade generality for the ability to predict.",
  "trap": "Using these equations when the acceleration is not constant. The commonest cases are a stated changing force, a graph of acceleration against time that is not horizontal, and any situation with air resistance that the question has not told you to ignore. The second commonest error is unit or sign inconsistency inside one substitution — mixing a positive displacement with a negative acceleration is often correct, but only if the axis was fixed first and both signs came from it."
},
{
  "id": "kin-free-fall",
  "group": "u1",
  "prereqs": [
    "kin-constant-a-equations"
  ],
  "source": "CED 1.3",
  "title": "Free fall: constant acceleration, whichever way the object is moving",
  "analogy": "Throw a ball straight up and it is in exactly the same condition on the way up, at the top, and on the way down: nothing but gravity acting. The motion changes character at the top; the physics does not change at all.",
  "plain": "Near Earth's surface, an object with only gravity acting on it has a downward acceleration that is constant. The CED gives its magnitude as approximately $10$ m/s$^2$ for any problem needing a number, while the equation sheet prints the more precise $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$; you are not penalised for using $9.8$ or $9.81$.\n\nBecause the acceleration is constant, the three equations of [[kin-constant-a-equations]] apply, with $y$ in place of $x$ and $a_y = -g$ if you have called upwards positive.\n\nThe part that costs marks is the top of the flight. A ball thrown upwards slows, stops for an instant, and comes back. At that instant its velocity is zero — but its acceleration is still $g$ downwards. If the acceleration were zero there, the ball would stay parked in the air.\n\nNote also that the acceleration is downward the whole time, including while the ball is going up. Rising and falling are not two phases with two different accelerations. They are one motion with one acceleration, and the velocity happens to change sign in the middle of it.",
  "depth": "Set up the axis once, at the start, and do not change it. Upwards positive is the usual choice, giving $a_y = -g \\approx -10$ m/s$^2$ for the entire flight, upwards and downwards alike. The alternative — downwards positive, $a_y = +g$ — is equally valid and sometimes tidier for a pure drop, but a problem with both a throw and a fall is easier to keep straight with upwards positive.\n\nThree consequences worth having ready. Time to the highest point from a launch speed $v_{y0}$ is $t = \\frac{v_{y0}}{g}$, from setting $v_y = 0$ in the first equation. Maximum height gained is $\\frac{v_{y0}^2}{2g}$, from the third equation with $v_y = 0$. And for a projectile that lands back at its launch height, the flight is symmetric: it takes as long to come down as to go up, and it arrives with the same speed it left with, in the opposite direction. None of these three are on the equation sheet; they are one line of algebra each from equations that are.\n\nTwo idealisations are doing work. Air resistance is assumed negligible unless the problem says otherwise — that is a stated exam convention, not something you may assume in the laboratory. And the value of $g$ is treated as constant over the heights involved. Both fail for a real coffee filter or a real skydiver, which is exactly why those appear in questions asking you to describe non-uniform acceleration rather than compute with it.\n\nWatch the difference between height above the ground and displacement. A ball thrown from a ledge and landing below its launch point has a negative $\\Delta y$ even though it went up first, and a maximum height above the ground that is neither of those numbers. Deciding what the origin is, and saying so, resolves all three.",
  "formula": "only gravity acting  ->  a_y = -g  (with UP positive), constant, all the way\ng = 9.8 m/s^2 on the equation sheet;  g ~ 10 m/s^2 is what AP uses numerically\n\nat the highest point:  v_y = 0   BUT   a_y = -g,  NOT zero\n\ntime up   t_up  = v_y0 / g              [derived, not on the sheet]\nrise      h_max = v_y0^2 / (2g)         [derived, not on the sheet]\nsymmetry: launch and return at the same height => equal times, equal speeds",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A ball is thrown straight up at 15 m/s from the edge of a ledge 20 m above flat ground, and misses the ledge on the way down. Using $g \\approx 10$ m/s$^2$, find (a) how high above the ledge it rises, (b) how long it is in the air before it hits the ground, and (c) how fast it is moving when it lands.",
    "given": "$v_{y0} = +15$ m/s, $a_y = -10$ m/s$^2$, ground at $y = -20$ m if the origin is the ledge.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Take upwards as positive and put the origin at the ledge, so $y_0 = 0$, the ground is at $y = -20$ m, and $a_y = -10$ m/s$^2$ throughout.",
        "why": "One axis for the whole problem, including the part below the launch point. Putting the origin at the ledge rather than the ground makes $y_0 = 0$ and turns the landing condition into a single negative number, which is easier to get right than carrying $y_0 = 20$."
      },
      {
        "do": "(a) At the highest point $v_y = 0$. Use the equation without $t$: $0 = (15)^2 + 2(-10)(\\Delta y)$, so $\\Delta y = \\frac{225}{20} = 11.25$ m.",
        "why": "The rise is asked for without any mention of time, so the third equation is the direct route. Setting $v_y = 0$ is the mathematical statement of 'highest point' — that substitution is the physics, and the rest is arithmetic."
      },
      {
        "do": "(b) For the landing, use $y = y_0 + v_{y0}t + \\frac{1}{2}a_y t^2$ with $y = -20$: $-20 = 15t - 5t^2$.",
        "why": "The whole flight is one interval with one constant acceleration, so it can be done in a single equation. Splitting it into 'up' and 'down' phases is legal but doubles the work and the number of chances to lose a sign."
      },
      {
        "do": "Rearrange to $5t^2 - 15t - 20 = 0$, divide by 5 to get $t^2 - 3t - 4 = 0$, factorise as $(t - 4)(t + 1) = 0$, so $t = 4.0$ s.",
        "why": "The quadratic has two roots and the negative one, $t = -1$ s, is the time the ball would have left the ground had it been thrown from there — a real solution of the equation, but not of the problem, which starts at $t = 0$. Rejecting it is a deliberate step, not an oversight."
      },
      {
        "do": "(c) Use $v_y = v_{y0} + a_y t = 15 + (-10)(4.0) = -25$ m/s, so the landing speed is 25 m/s.",
        "why": "The minus sign says downwards, which is the direction check: a positive answer here would mean the ball landed while still travelling upwards. Speed is the magnitude, so the reported answer is 25 m/s."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "(a) 11.25 m above the ledge. (b) 4.0 s in the air. (c) landing at 25 m/s downwards.",
    "check": "Independent check on (c) using the equation without time: $v_y^2 = v_{y0}^2 + 2a_y\\Delta y = (15)^2 + 2(-10)(-20) = 225 + 400 = 625$, giving $|v_y| = 25$ m/s. This uses the displacement rather than the time, so it would not reproduce the same number if the quadratic in step (b) had been solved wrongly. A symmetry check on (b) as well: the ball takes $\\frac{15}{10} = 1.5$ s to rise and, by symmetry, 1.5 s to return to the ledge at 15 m/s downwards — leaving 1.0 s for the drop to the ground, over which it falls $15(1.0) + 5(1.0)^2 = 20$ m. That is the ledge height, and the times sum to 4.0 s."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Upwards is taken as positive by convention rather than necessity, and the reason to fix it before writing anything is that $g$ then appears exactly once, as $a_y = -g$, and never has to be re-signed. Problems that go wrong here almost always went wrong because the sign of $g$ was decided separately in each equation, on the basis of whether the object happened to be rising at the time.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "The natural-looking alternative is to treat the flight as two problems: a rise with 'deceleration' $g$, then a fall with 'acceleration' $g$. It gives the right answer if executed perfectly, and it teaches the wrong idea — that something changes at the top — while requiring two sets of substitutions instead of one. The single-interval treatment is both shorter and truer.",
  "trap": "Writing $a = 0$ at the highest point because $v = 0$ there. Velocity and acceleration are independent at any instant; the ball is momentarily at rest and being accelerated downwards at $g$, which is exactly why it does not stay at rest. The runner-up trap is assuming the flight time up equals the flight time down when the object lands at a *different* height from the one it left — the symmetry argument needs equal heights, and the worked example above is a case where it does not apply to the full flight."
},
{
  "id": "kin-reference-frames",
  "group": "u1",
  "prereqs": [
    "kin-average-velocity",
    "kin-scalars-vectors"
  ],
  "source": "CED 1.4",
  "title": "Reference frames and relative velocity in one dimension",
  "analogy": "On a moving train, the person walking down the aisle passes you at a stroll and passes a cow in the field at 130 km/h. Nobody is mistaken. 'How fast' is not a property of the walker alone; it is a property of the walker and whoever is watching.",
  "plain": "A **reference frame** is the point of view a measurement is made from. Choosing a different frame changes the numbers you get for position and velocity, and can change their direction too — a car that is moving forwards relative to the road may be moving backwards relative to a faster car beside it.\n\nMeasurements in one frame can be converted into another. In one dimension the rule is signed addition: what an observer measures is the object's velocity combined with the velocity of the observer's own frame. In symbols, with all velocities along the same axis,\n\n$$v_{AC} = v_{AB} + v_{BC}$$\n\nwhere $v_{AC}$ means the velocity of A as measured in frame C. The inner subscripts match up and cancel, which is a useful way to check you have combined them the right way round. Reversing a pair flips the sign: $v_{BA} = -v_{AB}$.\n\nOne thing does not change between frames. All inertial observers measure the **same acceleration** for the same object, because they disagree by a constant velocity and a constant makes no contribution to a rate of change.\n\nAP Physics 1 keeps relative velocity to one dimension, so you never need to add vectors at an angle to answer one of these.",
  "depth": "Nothing in the conversion is on the equation sheet. What the sheet does state, as an exam convention, is that the frame of any problem may be assumed inertial unless you are told otherwise — an inertial frame being one that is not itself accelerating.\n\nThe practical method is to write every velocity in the same frame first, usually the ground, with a consistent axis. Then any relative velocity is a subtraction: the velocity of A as seen from B is $v_{AB} = v_{Ag} - v_{Bg}$, both measured relative to the ground. This is the same rule as the addition above, since $v_{AB} = v_{Ag} + v_{gB}$ and $v_{gB} = -v_{Bg}$.\n\nTwo objects moving the same way subtract to a small relative velocity; two moving oppositely subtract to a large one, because one of them is negative. That sign bookkeeping is the entire difficulty of the topic, and it disappears if you never write an unsigned velocity down.\n\nThe invariance of acceleration is the reason frames matter later. If observer B moves at a constant $v_{Bg}$, then A's velocity in B's frame differs from A's velocity in the ground frame by that same constant at every instant, so the two velocity–time graphs are vertical shifts of each other, with identical slopes — see [[kin-graphs-slope]]. Identical slopes means identical acceleration, which means $\\vec{F}_{net} = m\\vec{a}$ gives the same forces to both observers. Physics that changed with the observer's speed would not be much of a physics.",
  "formula": "v_AC = v_AB + v_BC        (inner subscripts match and cancel)\nv_BA = -v_AB\n\nin practice, from a common frame (the ground, g):\n   v of A as seen from B  =  v_Ag - v_Bg      [signed]\n\nacceleration is the SAME in all inertial frames\nAP Physics 1: relative velocity is restricted to ONE dimension\nNone of this is printed on the equation sheet.",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "On a straight two-lane road, car A travels north at 25 m/s and car B travels north at 15 m/s. A passenger inside car B walks towards the back of the bus-sized vehicle at 1.5 m/s relative to it. Taking north as positive, find (a) the velocity of A as measured by the driver of B, (b) the velocity of B as measured by the driver of A, and (c) the velocity of the walking passenger relative to the road.",
    "given": "$v_{Ag} = +25$ m/s, $v_{Bg} = +15$ m/s, $v_{PB} = -1.5$ m/s. North is $+$.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Fix the axis: north is $+$. Write both cars in the ground frame: $v_{Ag} = +25$ m/s, $v_{Bg} = +15$ m/s. The passenger walks towards the back, so relative to B, $v_{PB} = -1.5$ m/s.",
        "why": "Everything goes into one frame with one axis before any combining happens. 'Towards the back' is a direction word; converting it into a sign now is what stops it being converted wrongly later."
      },
      {
        "do": "(a) $v_{AB} = v_{Ag} - v_{Bg} = 25 - 15 = +10$ m/s.",
        "why": "Subtracting the observer's own velocity is what it means to watch from inside B. The result is positive, so from B's seat car A is pulling ahead northwards — which matches the fact that A is the faster car."
      },
      {
        "do": "(b) $v_{BA} = -v_{AB} = -10$ m/s.",
        "why": "Swapping which object and which observer reverses the sign, and nothing else. You could recompute $15 - 25$; the reversal rule is the same statement and is one place fewer to make an arithmetic error."
      },
      {
        "do": "(c) $v_{Pg} = v_{PB} + v_{Bg} = (-1.5) + (+15) = +13.5$ m/s.",
        "why": "The inner subscripts match, so the combination is valid: the passenger's motion relative to B, plus B's motion relative to the ground, gives the passenger relative to the ground. The vehicle carries the passenger along even while they walk the other way, which is why the answer is still northwards."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "(a) $+10$ m/s (A moves north at 10 m/s as seen from B). (b) $-10$ m/s (B moves south at 10 m/s as seen from A). (c) $+13.5$ m/s north.",
    "check": "Independent check on (a) by re-deriving it inside B's frame from scratch. In B's frame, B is at rest and the road runs backwards at $-15$ m/s. Car A, which the road sees moving at $+25$ m/s, therefore appears at $25 + (-15) = +10$ m/s. Same answer from a different starting point. A sanity check on (c): the passenger must be slower than B relative to the road, since they are walking backwards inside it, and $13.5 < 15$. Had the answer come out as $16.5$ m/s, the sign of the walking velocity was dropped."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Velocities are combined by signed addition, rather than by any rule involving speeds, because velocity is a vector and vectors add. The 'inner subscripts cancel' notation is worth adopting even though it is bookkeeping: it converts a question about points of view, which is easy to get backwards, into a pattern-match, which is not.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "The intuitive rule 'same direction means add, opposite means subtract' is a memory aid that inverts under pressure, and it is genuinely backwards for the relative velocity of two objects: two cars going the same way have their speeds *subtracted*. Signing every velocity from the axis and then always subtracting the observer's velocity never has to be remembered, because it is the same operation every time.",
  "trap": "Two failures. First, adding when the objects move the same way, which produces 40 m/s for the closing speed of two cars that are barely separating. Second, forgetting that acceleration is frame-independent, and reporting a different acceleration for an observer on a constant-velocity train — the velocities differ, but the rate at which they change does not."
},
{
  "id": "kin-vector-components",
  "group": "u1",
  "prereqs": [
    "kin-scalars-vectors"
  ],
  "source": "CED 1.5",
  "title": "Resolving a vector into perpendicular components",
  "analogy": "Giving directions as 'three blocks east, four blocks north' is more useful than 'five blocks, that way', because the streets run east and north. Components are the same move: describe the vector along the axes the problem is actually built on.",
  "plain": "Any vector can be rebuilt as the sum of two perpendicular vectors, one along each axis. Those two are its **components**, and finding them is called resolving the vector.\n\nYou choose the axes. Once chosen, a vector of magnitude $v$ at an angle $\\theta$ measured from the $x$ axis has components\n\n$$v_x = v \\mathrm{cos} \\theta$$\n\n$$v_y = v \\mathrm{sin} \\theta$$\n\nand you can go back the other way with Pythagoras and the tangent: $v = \\sqrt{v_x^2 + v_y^2}$, with the direction from $\\mathrm{tan} \\theta = \\frac{v_y}{v_x}$.\n\nWhich function goes with which component depends on where the angle is measured from, not on a rule you can memorise. The component **next to** the angle uses cosine; the component **across from** it uses sine. Draw the right triangle, label the angle, and read it off — this takes five seconds and removes the single most common source of error in two-dimensional problems.\n\nThe reason this matters is that once resolved, the two components can be handled separately, with all the one-dimensional machinery you already have.",
  "depth": "The right-triangle relations $\\mathrm{sin}\\theta = \\frac{a}{c}$, $\\mathrm{cos}\\theta = \\frac{b}{c}$, $\\mathrm{tan}\\theta = \\frac{a}{b}$ and $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$ are printed in the geometry block of the provided equation sheet, along with a table of sine, cosine and tangent for $0°$, $30°$, $37°$, $45°$, $53°$, $60°$ and $90°$. The 37–53 pair is there because it gives the exact 3–4–5 triangle: $\\mathrm{sin} 37° = \\frac{3}{5}$ and $\\mathrm{cos} 37° = \\frac{4}{5}$, with the roles swapped at $53°$. When an exam problem hands you 37° or 53°, it is telling you the arithmetic is meant to come out clean.\n\nChoosing the axes is a real decision with real consequences. For projectile motion the sensible choice is horizontal and vertical, because gravity then lies entirely along one axis and has no component along the other. For an object on a ramp the sensible choice is along and perpendicular to the surface, because the motion then lies entirely along one axis. Both choices are made for the same reason: put an axis along something that is already zero in one direction, and half the algebra never appears.\n\nComponents add independently. If two velocity vectors are to be summed, resolve both, add the $x$ components to get the resultant's $x$ component, add the $y$ components to get its $y$ component, then recombine. Perpendicular components never mix — that independence is not a convenience, it is the property that makes the whole method work and it is what [[kin-projectile]] rests on.\n\nTwo checks on any resolution. Each component must be no larger in magnitude than the vector itself, since sine and cosine never exceed 1. And the two components must satisfy Pythagoras with the original magnitude. Both take a few seconds and catch a swapped sine and cosine immediately when the angle is not 45°.",
  "formula": "v_x = v cos(theta)      v_y = v sin(theta)     [theta measured from the +x axis]\nv   = sqrt(v_x^2 + v_y^2)      tan(theta) = v_y / v_x\n\nwhich function? the side NEXT to the angle -> cos;  ACROSS from it -> sin\n\non the equation sheet: sin = a/c, cos = b/c, tan = a/b, a^2 + b^2 = c^2\nand the trig table:  37 deg -> sin 3/5, cos 4/5, tan 3/4\n                     53 deg -> sin 4/5, cos 3/5, tan 4/3\n\n|component| <= |vector|   always",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A ball leaves a launcher at 25 m/s, aimed 37° above the horizontal. Find the horizontal and vertical components of its initial velocity, and verify that they recombine to the original vector.",
    "given": "$v_0 = 25$ m/s, $\\theta = 37°$ above the horizontal. Sheet values: $\\mathrm{cos} 37° = \\frac{4}{5}$, $\\mathrm{sin} 37° = \\frac{3}{5}$.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Choose axes: $x$ horizontal in the direction of launch, $y$ vertically upwards. Sketch the velocity as the hypotenuse of a right triangle with the 37° angle at the launch point.",
        "why": "Horizontal and vertical are chosen because gravity will act along $y$ only. Sketching the triangle is what turns 'which is sine' from a memory question into a looking question."
      },
      {
        "do": "The horizontal component is adjacent to the 37° angle, so $v_{0x} = v_0 \\mathrm{cos} 37° = 25 \\times \\frac{4}{5} = 20$ m/s.",
        "why": "Adjacent takes cosine, straight off the labelled triangle. Using the sheet's exact fraction rather than a decimal keeps the arithmetic clean and signals that the problem was designed around the 3–4–5 triangle."
      },
      {
        "do": "The vertical component is opposite the angle, so $v_{0y} = v_0 \\mathrm{sin} 37° = 25 \\times \\frac{3}{5} = 15$ m/s.",
        "why": "Opposite takes sine, from the same triangle. Both components are positive because the ball is launched forwards and upwards under the axes just chosen."
      },
      {
        "do": "Recombine: $\\sqrt{20^2 + 15^2} = \\sqrt{400 + 225} = \\sqrt{625} = 25$ m/s.",
        "why": "This is the definition of components run backwards, and it is worth doing every time until it is automatic, because it fails loudly if sine and cosine were swapped in a way the individual numbers would not reveal."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$v_{0x} = 20$ m/s horizontally, $v_{0y} = 15$ m/s vertically upwards; magnitude $\\sqrt{20^2+15^2} = 25$ m/s as required.",
    "check": "Independent check on the direction, not just the magnitude: $\\mathrm{tan} \\theta = \\frac{v_y}{v_x} = \\frac{15}{20} = 0.75$, and the sheet's table gives $\\mathrm{tan} 37° = \\frac{3}{4} = 0.75$. The recovered angle is the one we started with, so both components are right — a swapped pair would have given $\\frac{20}{15} = 1.33$, which is $\\mathrm{tan} 53°$, and the error would have been visible immediately. Sense check as well: 37° is below 45°, so the launch is more horizontal than vertical, and indeed $20 > 15$."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Perpendicular components are used rather than any other pair of directions because perpendicular components do not influence each other. Resolve onto two axes at 60° apart and the 'components' would each contain part of the other, so changing one would change the other, and the independent one-dimensional treatment that follows would be invalid.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "Memorising 'horizontal is always cosine' works until an exam gives the angle from the vertical — a ramp, a hanging string, a launch described as 20° from the upright — and then it is exactly backwards. The triangle is drawn instead, every time, because the drawing is right whichever way the angle was quoted.",
  "trap": "Swapping sine and cosine when the angle is measured from an unexpected axis. Also, treating components as things you add in magnitude: a vector of 20 and one of 15 perpendicular give 25, not 35, and the difference is Pythagoras. If any component of yours came out bigger than the vector it came from, stop — that is impossible, and the error is in the trigonometry, not in the arithmetic after it."
},
{
  "id": "kin-projectile",
  "group": "u1",
  "prereqs": [
    "kin-vector-components",
    "kin-free-fall"
  ],
  "source": "CED 1.5",
  "title": "Two-dimensional motion and projectiles: two problems sharing a clock",
  "analogy": "Fire a bullet horizontally and drop one from the same height at the same moment, and they land together. The sideways motion of the first has no opinion about how fast it falls. Two independent stories, one stopwatch.",
  "plain": "Motion in two dimensions is handled by resolving it into components and then treating each axis as its own one-dimensional problem. This works because perpendicular components are independent: what happens along $x$ does not change what happens along $y$.\n\n**Projectile motion** is the special case where the acceleration is zero along one axis and constant and non-zero along the other. With only gravity acting:\n\n- horizontally, $a_x = 0$, so the horizontal velocity never changes and $x = v_{0x}t$\n- vertically, $a_y = -g$, so the vertical motion is exactly the free fall of [[kin-free-fall]]\n\nThe one thing the two axes share is the time. That is the whole method: solve whichever axis gives you $t$, then carry that $t$ across to the other one. The vertical axis usually goes first, because gravity is there and the landing condition is a statement about height.\n\nA consequence worth stating on its own: how long a projectile stays in the air does not depend on how fast it was thrown horizontally. Two balls leaving the same height at the same instant, one dropped and one thrown sideways, hit the ground at the same moment.",
  "depth": "Set the problem up as two columns, one per axis, and never let a quantity cross between them except $t$. In the $x$ column: $a_x = 0$, so the only equation you need is $x = x_0 + v_{0x}t$. In the $y$ column: $a_y = -g$ and all three constant-acceleration equations are available. The vertical column is where the interesting physics is; the horizontal column is bookkeeping.\n\nFor a projectile launched at speed $v_0$ and angle $\\theta$ from the horizontal, $v_{0x} = v_0 \\mathrm{cos} \\theta$ and $v_{0y} = v_0 \\mathrm{sin} \\theta$, from [[kin-vector-components]]. If it lands at the same height it left, the flight is symmetric, so the time of flight is twice the time to the top: $t = \\frac{2v_0 \\mathrm{sin} \\theta}{g}$, and the horizontal range follows as $R = v_{0x}t$. Neither of those is on the equation sheet, and neither should be memorised as a formula — both are one substitution away from equations that are, and the memorised version is wrong the moment the landing height differs from the launch height.\n\nAt the highest point of the flight the *vertical* velocity is zero. The velocity itself is not zero: the projectile is still moving horizontally at $v_{0x}$, and its speed there is its minimum for the flight. The acceleration at that point is still $g$ downwards, as it is everywhere else along the path.\n\nThe independence claim can be tested rather than asserted, which is why it appears in experimental questions. Roll a ball off a table at several launch speeds and record the fall time each run: the horizontal distance changes and the time does not. That is a one-variable experiment whose result is a physics claim, and it is the kind of design an Experimental Design and Analysis question asks for.",
  "formula": "resolve, then run two 1-D problems that share ONLY t\n\n  x-axis (a_x = 0):     x = x0 + v_0x t                v_x stays constant\n  y-axis (a_y = -g):    y = y0 + v_0y t - (1/2) g t^2\n                        v_y = v_0y - g t\n                        v_y^2 = v_0y^2 - 2 g (y - y0)\n\nlaunch at v0, angle th:  v_0x = v0 cos(th),  v_0y = v0 sin(th)\nlevel ground only:  t_flight = 2 v0 sin(th) / g,  R = v_0x * t_flight   [derived]\n\nat the top: v_y = 0, v_x = v_0x (not zero), a = g downward (not zero)",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A ball is launched from ground level at 25 m/s, 37° above the horizontal, over flat ground. Using $g \\approx 10$ m/s$^2$, find its time of flight, its horizontal range, and its maximum height.",
    "given": "$v_0 = 25$ m/s, $\\theta = 37°$, $g \\approx 10$ m/s$^2$, launch and landing both at $y = 0$.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Resolve the launch velocity: $v_{0x} = 25 \\mathrm{cos} 37° = 20$ m/s, $v_{0y} = 25 \\mathrm{sin} 37° = 15$ m/s. Take up and forwards as positive, origin at the launch point.",
        "why": "Nothing else can be done until the single 25 m/s is split into two numbers that live on separate axes. The 37° is a signal that the sheet's exact 3–4–5 values were intended."
      },
      {
        "do": "Work the vertical axis first. The ball lands at $y = 0$, so $0 = 15t - 5t^2 = t(15 - 5t)$, giving $t = 0$ or $t = 3.0$ s.",
        "why": "Vertical first, because the landing condition is a statement about height and so only the $y$ column can supply the time. The root $t = 0$ is the launch instant — a true solution of the equation and the wrong answer to the question."
      },
      {
        "do": "Carry the time across to the horizontal axis: $x = v_{0x}t = (20)(3.0) = 60$ m.",
        "why": "Time is the only quantity allowed to cross between columns, and this is the step it crosses. The horizontal equation has no acceleration term because $a_x = 0$, which is what makes projectile motion a special case rather than a general two-dimensional one."
      },
      {
        "do": "Maximum height: at the top $v_y = 0$, so $0 = (15)^2 + 2(-10)\\Delta y$, giving $\\Delta y = \\frac{225}{20} = 11.25$ m.",
        "why": "'Maximum height' is a vertical question and is stated mathematically as $v_y = 0$. Using the equation without $t$ avoids having to find the time to the top first."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "Time of flight $3.0$ s, range $60$ m, maximum height $11.25$ m.",
    "check": "Independent check by symmetry and free fall, not by re-substitution. The ball is at the top halfway through the flight, at $t = 1.5$ s. From rest vertically, it should then fall $\\frac{1}{2}g t^2 = \\frac{1}{2}(10)(1.5)^2 = 11.25$ m in the second half — which matches the maximum height computed from a different equation. A second check: the range must be less than $v_0 t = (25)(3.0) = 75$ m, the distance if the whole launch speed had been horizontal, and $60 < 75$. And a limiting case: at $\\theta$ near zero the predicted flight time goes to zero, which is right — a ball rolled horizontally along the ground never leaves it."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "The motion is split along horizontal and vertical rather than any other pair of perpendicular axes because gravity then has a component along only one of them. Tilt the axes and both columns acquire an acceleration term, both become constant-acceleration problems, and the horizontal simplification $x = v_{0x}t$ is lost. The answer would still be right; the work would roughly double.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "Treating the projectile as moving along its curved path at a single speed, and using the kinematic equations on the path length, is a plausible-looking approach and it is wrong. The acceleration is not along the path, and it is not constant *relative to the path*, so the constant-acceleration equations do not apply to distance measured along the trajectory. They apply to each perpendicular component separately, which is exactly what the component method uses.",
  "trap": "Three that recur. Using $g$ in the horizontal equation — there is no horizontal acceleration, so no $\\frac{1}{2}at^2$ term. Saying the velocity is zero at the top, when only its vertical component is. And applying the level-ground flight-time formula $t = \\frac{2v_0 \\mathrm{sin} \\theta}{g}$ to a projectile launched from a cliff or a table, where the landing height differs from the launch height and the symmetry argument behind that formula does not hold."
},
{
  "id": "dyn-system",
  "group": "u2",
  "prereqs": [],
  "source": "CED 2.1",
  "title": "Systems and the system boundary",
  "analogy": "Packing a suitcase, you can weigh the whole case or weigh each shirt. Both are honest answers to different questions. Once you have decided to weigh the case, the way the shirts push against each other inside stops mattering — they cannot change what the scale reads.",
  "plain": "A **system** is whatever collection of objects you decide to treat as one thing. It is a choice you make, not something the problem hands you. A single block can be a system. Two blocks joined by a rope can be one system. A block, a rope, a pulley and the Earth can be one system.\n\nOnce you have drawn the boundary, every force falls into one of two boxes. **Internal forces** act between objects that are both inside the boundary. **External forces** are exerted on the system by something outside it. Only the external forces can change how the system as a whole moves.\n\nThat is the whole reason the choice matters. Pick the boundary well and an unknown force becomes internal, which means it disappears from your equation and you never have to find it. Pick it badly and you are solving for quantities you did not want.\n\nIf the details inside do not matter for the question you are asking, the whole system can be modelled as a single object located at its [[dyn-center-of-mass]].",
  "depth": "Newton's third law is what makes internal forces cancel. Every internal force comes in a pair, equal in magnitude and opposite in direction, and both members of the pair are inside the boundary — so when you add up all forces on the system, they sum to zero. Internal interactions therefore cannot change the motion of the system's centre of mass. See [[dyn-third-law]].\n\nSystems are not sealed. A boundary can let energy or mass cross it. It can also be redrawn mid-problem: a standard two-step method is to take the whole system first, get the acceleration from the external forces only, then take one piece as its own system and use that acceleration to find the internal force you now want.\n\nTwo more things the course expects you to say. First, the parts of a system can behave differently from each other and from the system as a whole — a rolling wheel's centre of mass moves steadily while every point on the rim speeds up and slows down. Second, the internal structure of a system affects how you analyse it, and changing something outside the system can change that internal structure.\n\nThere is no equation on the provided equation sheet for 'choose a system'. It is a modelling move, and it is the move that most often decides whether a dynamics problem takes two lines or two pages.",
  "whyThisChoice": "Why treat several objects as one? Because the quantity you are asked for is often a property of the whole — the acceleration of a train, not of carriage four. Taking the whole as the system removes every coupling force between the carriages from the algebra in a single stroke.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "The tempting alternative is to always analyse each object separately and never think about systems. It works, and it is never wrong, but for a chain of $n$ connected objects it produces $n$ simultaneous equations where one would have done. Under exam time pressure that difference is the mark.",
  "formula": "Internal forces sum to zero, so for the system as a whole only external forces\nappear in Newton's second law:\n$$\\vec{a}_{\\mathrm{sys}} = \\frac{\\sum \\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{external}}}{m_{\\mathrm{sys}}}$$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "Two blocks sit on a frictionless horizontal table, joined by a light string. Block A has mass 2.0 kg and block B has mass 4.0 kg. A horizontal force of 18 N pulls block B directly away from block A, so the string is taut and A is dragged along behind. Find the acceleration of the pair and the tension in the string.",
    "given": "$m_A = 2.0$ kg, $m_B = 4.0$ kg, applied force $F = 18$ N, frictionless surface, ideal (massless, inextensible) string.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Choose system 1 = both blocks and the string together. Write $F = (m_A + m_B)a$.",
        "why": "The tension is exerted by the string on A and by A on the string — both inside this boundary. As an internal force it cannot appear in the equation for the system as a whole, so the only horizontal external force left is the 18 N pull. One unknown, one equation."
      },
      {
        "do": "$a = \\dfrac{18}{2.0 + 4.0} = 3.0$ m/s$^2$.",
        "why": "Vertical forces (gravity and the normal force from the table) balance, so there is no vertical acceleration and no need to write the vertical equation at all."
      },
      {
        "do": "Now redraw the boundary. Choose system 2 = block A alone.",
        "why": "Tension is what the question asks for, so it has to be made external. The only boundary that does that puts the string outside and one block inside."
      },
      {
        "do": "For block A the only horizontal force is the tension: $T = m_A a = (2.0)(3.0) = 6.0$ N.",
        "why": "A is inextensible-linked to B, so A has the same acceleration as the system — that is the piece of information carried across from step 2, and it is why the two-step method works."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$a = 3.0$ m/s$^2$ in the direction of the applied force; $T = 6.0$ N.",
    "check": "Redo the last step with block B as the system instead. Block B feels the applied 18 N forward and the string pulling backward with tension $T$, so $18 - T = (4.0)(3.0) = 12$, giving $T = 6.0$ N. Two different system choices produce the same tension, which they must. Also note $T < F$, as it has to be: the string only has to accelerate the 2.0 kg block, not all 6.0 kg."
  },
  "trap": "Writing tension into the equation for the combined system. Students often set up $F + T = (m_A + m_B)a$ or $F - T = (m_A + m_B)a$ and then wonder why they have two unknowns. Once the boundary encloses both ends of the string, the tension is internal and is not allowed to appear. The discipline is: say out loud what your system is *before* you write the equation, and then only forces from outside it go in."
},
{
  "id": "dyn-center-of-mass",
  "group": "u2",
  "prereqs": [
    "dyn-system"
  ],
  "source": "CED 2.1",
  "title": "Centre of mass",
  "analogy": "Balance a spanner across one finger. There is exactly one place along it where it does not tip, and it is not the middle — it is nearer the heavy end. That balance point is the centre of mass.",
  "plain": "The **centre of mass** is the mass-weighted average position of a system. It is the single point that stands in for the whole system when you are asking how the system moves.\n\n'Mass-weighted' is the important word. You do not average the positions. You multiply each position by its mass, add those up, and divide by the total mass. A heavy object pulls the centre of mass towards itself.\n\nIf the mass is spread symmetrically, the centre of mass lies on the lines of symmetry — the centre of a uniform sphere, the middle of a uniform rod. That shortcut removes most of the arithmetic when it applies.\n\nThe centre of mass need not be inside any object. For a doughnut it is in the hole; for two balls joined by a rod it is somewhere on the rod.",
  "depth": "Along one axis, the definition is\n\n$$\\vec{x}_{cm} = \\frac{\\sum m_i \\vec{x}_i}{\\sum m_i}$$\n\nand **this equation is on the provided equation sheet**. To locate a centre of mass in two dimensions, apply it once to the $x$ coordinates and once to the $y$ coordinates. There is no separate two-dimensional formula to learn.\n\nThe result depends on where you put the origin, but the physical point does not. Put the origin at the position of one of the masses and that term contributes zero, which is usually the least arithmetic.\n\nThe reason this particular point is singled out: a system can be modelled as a single object located at its centre of mass, and it is the centre of mass whose acceleration obeys Newton's second law for the system. Internal forces cannot move it. Gravity on a system can be treated as acting there. A thrown spanner tumbles chaotically, but its centre of mass traces a clean parabola.\n\nThe course boundary is explicit: you are expected to calculate a centre of mass for systems of **five or fewer particles** in a two-dimensional arrangement, or for systems that are highly symmetrical. You will not be asked to integrate over a continuous irregular body.",
  "formula": "$$\\vec{x}_{cm} = \\frac{\\sum m_i \\vec{x}_i}{\\sum m_i}$$\nOn the equation sheet. Apply once per axis for a 2-D arrangement.",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "Three small blocks lie along a straight line. A 2.0 kg block is at $x = 0$, a 3.0 kg block is at $x = 0.40$ m, and a 5.0 kg block is at $x = 1.00$ m. Find the $x$ coordinate of the centre of mass of the three-block system.",
    "given": "$m_1 = 2.0$ kg at $x_1 = 0$; $m_2 = 3.0$ kg at $x_2 = 0.40$ m; $m_3 = 5.0$ kg at $x_3 = 1.00$ m.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Keep the origin at the 2.0 kg block.",
        "why": "Its $m_i x_i$ term is then zero, so one of the three products vanishes. The answer would be the same physical point with any origin; this choice only reduces arithmetic and the chance of a sign slip."
      },
      {
        "do": "Total mass: $\\sum m_i = 2.0 + 3.0 + 5.0 = 10.0$ kg.",
        "why": "Computing the denominator first gives you a number to sanity-check against; if the numerator later exceeds $10.0 \\times 1.00$ you have made an error, because the centre of mass cannot lie beyond the outermost block."
      },
      {
        "do": "Weighted sum: $\\sum m_i x_i = (2.0)(0) + (3.0)(0.40) + (5.0)(1.00) = 0 + 1.20 + 5.00 = 6.20$ kg$\\cdot$m.",
        "why": "Each position is multiplied by its own mass. Averaging the three positions instead would give $0.47$ m, which ignores that the 5.0 kg block is half the system."
      },
      {
        "do": "$x_{cm} = \\dfrac{6.20}{10.0} = 0.62$ m.",
        "why": "Divide by total mass, not by the number of particles. Dividing by 3 here would give $2.07$ m, outside the system entirely — a result that is visibly impossible."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$x_{cm} = 0.62$ m from the 2.0 kg block, measured towards the 5.0 kg block.",
    "check": "Two independent checks. (i) The answer must lie between the outermost blocks, $0$ and $1.00$ m, and it does. (ii) The 5.0 kg block is half the total mass, so the centre of mass must sit past the midpoint $0.50$ m on the heavy side, and $0.62 > 0.50$. If the answer had come out below $0.50$ m, the mass weighting was applied the wrong way round."
  },
  "trap": "Averaging positions instead of mass-weighting them. The giveaway is that the answer does not shift when you change one of the masses. A second trap is dividing by the number of particles rather than the total mass — dimensionally that leaves kg$\\cdot$m, not m, so units catch it if you write them."
},
{
  "id": "dyn-force-interaction",
  "group": "u2",
  "prereqs": [],
  "source": "CED 2.2",
  "title": "A force is an interaction, not a possession",
  "analogy": "A handshake is not something one hand has. It is something two hands do. You cannot describe it without naming both. Forces are the same: name two objects or you have not named a force.",
  "plain": "A force is a push or pull that arises when two objects interact. It is a vector, so it has a size and a direction. The part students skip is the two objects.\n\nEvery force on an object is exerted **by some other object**. So every force has a correct name of the form *force of X on Y*: the gravitational force of Earth on the ball, the normal force of the table on the book, the tension force of the rope on the crate.\n\nWrite forces that way and a whole class of mistakes becomes impossible. If you cannot fill in the X, you have not found a force — you have found a motion, an effect, or a habit of speech.\n\nAn object cannot exert a net force on itself. You cannot lift yourself by pulling on your own belt.",
  "depth": "Forces divide into contact and non-contact. **Contact forces** — normal force, friction, tension, the push of a spring, the drag of air — happen where surfaces touch, and they are the macroscopic result of electric forces between atoms in the two surfaces. That is why a normal force exists at all: the atoms of the table repel the atoms of the book.\n\n**Non-contact forces** act across a gap. In AP Physics 1 the only non-contact force in play is gravity; electric and magnetic interactions are the business of Physics 2. That is a useful narrowing: if two objects are not touching and one is not an astronomical body, there is probably no force between them in your problem.\n\nSome named quantities that look like forces are not forces:\n\n- **Inertia** is a property of an object, measured by its mass. It is not a force and never appears on a free-body diagram.\n- **Momentum** is a property of motion, not a push.\n- **Centripetal force** is a *role* played by real forces, not an extra force. See [[dyn-circular-motion]].\n- The **'force of the throw'** stops existing the instant the hand loses contact with the ball. A ball in flight has gravity on it, and nothing else if air resistance is negligible.\n\nNo equation on the sheet corresponds to this idea. It is a naming discipline, and it is what makes a free-body diagram checkable.",
  "whyThisChoice": "Why insist on the two-subscript naming when 'gravity' and 'normal' are shorter? Because the third law is stated in exactly that notation, $\\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{A\\ on\\ B}} = -\\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{B\\ on\\ A}}$, and a student who has written every force with both objects can read a third-law pair straight off the page by swapping the subscripts. A student who wrote 'gravity' and 'normal' cannot.",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A crate is being pushed at steady speed across a rough horizontal warehouse floor by a worker's hands. A student lists the forces on the crate as: gravity, the normal force, friction, the push, the force of motion forwards, and inertia. Audit that list. Which entries are real forces, what are their proper two-object names, and which entries must be struck out?",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Take each entry and try to complete the sentence 'the force of ___ on the crate'.",
        "why": "This is the whole test. A real force has an identifiable other object. It converts a vague word into something you can check."
      },
      {
        "do": "Gravity survives: the gravitational force of **Earth** on the crate, directed down.",
        "why": "Earth is a real second object, and gravity is the one non-contact force allowed in this course."
      },
      {
        "do": "Normal force survives: the normal force of the **floor** on the crate, directed perpendicular to and away from the floor.",
        "why": "The floor is touching the crate, and its atoms repel the crate's atoms. Note that this fixes the direction too — normal means perpendicular to the surface, which is not always vertical."
      },
      {
        "do": "Friction survives: the kinetic friction force of the **floor** on the crate, directed opposite to the crate's motion relative to the floor.",
        "why": "Same contact, different component. The normal force is the perpendicular component of what the floor exerts; friction is the parallel component."
      },
      {
        "do": "The push survives: the contact force of the **worker's hands** on the crate.",
        "why": "Hands are touching. This one is real for as long as contact lasts, and no longer."
      },
      {
        "do": "Strike out 'the force of motion forwards'. There is no object exerting it.",
        "why": "Motion does not need a force to continue — that is Newton's first law. This entry is the belief that a moving thing carries a forward push, and it is the single most common wrong arrow on a free-body diagram."
      },
      {
        "do": "Strike out 'inertia'. It is a property of the crate, measured by its mass in kilograms.",
        "why": "Inertia is measured in kg, not newtons. A quantity with the wrong units cannot be added to a sum of forces."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "Four real forces: gravitational force of Earth on the crate; normal force of the floor on the crate; kinetic friction force of the floor on the crate; contact push of the hands on the crate. 'Force of motion' and 'inertia' are struck out.",
    "check": "The crate moves at steady speed, so by Newton's first law the four surviving forces must cancel in pairs: the push balances friction horizontally, and the normal force balances gravity vertically. Four forces, two balanced pairs, consistent. Had 'force of motion' been kept, the horizontal forces could not sum to zero and the crate would have to be speeding up, which contradicts the problem statement."
  },
  "trap": "Listing 'the force of the throw' or 'the force of motion' on an object that has already left the thrower's hand. Once contact ends, that force ends. A ball rising after being thrown straight up has exactly one force on it — gravity — and its acceleration is downwards the whole way up, at the top, and on the way down."
},
{
  "id": "dyn-free-body",
  "group": "u2",
  "prereqs": [
    "dyn-force-interaction",
    "dyn-center-of-mass",
    "kin-vector-components"
  ],
  "source": "CED 2.2",
  "title": "Free-body diagrams done properly",
  "analogy": "A free-body diagram is a witness statement from one object. It records only what was done *to* that object, by whom, and from which direction. What that object did to everything else belongs in somebody else's statement.",
  "plain": "A **free-body diagram** shows one object — or one system — as a dot, with an arrow for each force exerted **on** it by its environment. Nothing else goes on it.\n\nThe rules are narrow, and following them exactly is worth marks:\n\n- The object is drawn as a dot, standing for its centre of mass.\n- Each arrow starts **on the dot** and points in the direction of that force.\n- Only forces exerted **on** the object appear. Forces the object exerts on other things do not.\n- Velocity, acceleration and momentum are not forces and do not appear.\n- Two forces in the same direction are drawn side by side, not stacked on top of each other, so a marker can see there are two.\n\nThe diagram is not decoration. It is the thing you read your equations off. Every arrow becomes a term; anything with no arrow contributes no term.",
  "depth": "After the arrows come the axes, and the axis choice is a real decision. Put one axis **parallel to the direction of the acceleration**. Then the acceleration has a component on one axis only, the perpendicular equation reduces to a statement that forces balance, and you have halved the algebra.\n\nFor an object on an incline that means tilting the axes: $x$ down the slope, $y$ perpendicular to the slope. The block accelerates along the slope, so with tilted axes only the gravitational force needs resolving, whereas with horizontal and vertical axes you would have to resolve both the normal force and the friction force, and the acceleration as well.\n\nResolving gravity on an incline of angle $\\theta$ measured from the horizontal:\n\n$$F_{g,\\parallel} = mg\\sin\\theta \\qquad F_{g,\\perp} = mg\\cos\\theta$$\n\nNeither of these is on the equation sheet — the sheet gives you the right-triangle relations and a table of sine, cosine and tangent for 0°, 30°, 37°, 45°, 53°, 60° and 90°, and you are expected to do the resolving yourself.\n\nOne course boundary is worth memorising because it decides marks: on the AP exam you draw the **forces**, not their components. Do not draw $mg\\sin\\theta$ and $mg\\cos\\theta$ as arrows on the free-body diagram alongside $mg$ — that is the same force counted twice. Do the resolving in the algebra, off to the side, after the diagram is drawn.",
  "whyThisChoice": "Why draw the object as a dot at all? Because the free-body diagram is about translation, and for translation a system behaves as though all its mass sits at the centre of mass. Drawing the object's shape invites you to worry about where along the object each force acts, which matters for rotation (Unit 5) and not here.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "Keeping horizontal and vertical axes on an incline is not wrong, and it gives the same answer. But it forces you to write two coupled equations with the acceleration split across both axes, and to remember that the block's vertical acceleration is not zero even though it stays on the surface. Most errors on incline problems trace back to that last point.",
  "formula": "On an incline of angle $\\theta$ from the horizontal, with axes tilted:\n$$\\sum F_{\\parallel} = mg\\sin\\theta - f = ma$$\n$$\\sum F_{\\perp} = F_N - mg\\cos\\theta = 0$$\nNeither line is on the equation sheet; both are read off the diagram.",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A 4.0 kg block is released from rest on a frictionless ramp inclined at 30° to the horizontal. Draw the free-body diagram in words, choose axes, and find the magnitude of the normal force and the acceleration of the block.",
    "given": "$m = 4.0$ kg, $\\theta = 30$°, frictionless, $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$. From the equation sheet's trigonometry table, $\\sin 30° = 1/2$ and $\\cos 30° = \\sqrt{3}/2 \\approx 0.866$.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Draw a dot. Two arrows only: the gravitational force of Earth on the block, straight down, magnitude $mg$; and the normal force of the surface on the block, perpendicular to the ramp surface, pointing away from it.",
        "why": "Frictionless removes the third arrow. There is no 'force down the slope' — that is a component of gravity, not a separate interaction, and drawing it as its own arrow double-counts."
      },
      {
        "do": "Choose axes tilted with the ramp: $+x$ down the slope, $+y$ perpendicular to the slope.",
        "why": "The block accelerates down the slope and not through the surface, so with this choice $a_y = 0$ exactly and the perpendicular equation becomes a balance. With horizontal and vertical axes, both components of the acceleration would be non-zero."
      },
      {
        "do": "Resolve gravity onto those axes: $F_{g,x} = mg\\sin 30°$, $F_{g,y} = -mg\\cos 30°$. The normal force lies wholly along $+y$.",
        "why": "Only gravity is off-axis, which is exactly the saving the tilted axes bought. The angle between the gravitational force and the perpendicular axis equals the ramp angle, which is why the cosine goes with the perpendicular direction."
      },
      {
        "do": "Perpendicular equation: $F_N - mg\\cos 30° = 0$, so $F_N = (4.0)(9.8)(0.866) = 34$ N.",
        "why": "The block neither sinks into the ramp nor lifts off, so the perpendicular acceleration is zero and the perpendicular forces must balance. This is the step that shows $F_N \\ne mg$ here."
      },
      {
        "do": "Parallel equation: $mg\\sin 30° = ma$, so $a = g\\sin 30° = (9.8)(0.5) = 4.9$ m/s$^2$ down the slope.",
        "why": "The mass cancels, which is the signature of a gravity-driven frictionless problem and a useful thing to notice before substituting numbers."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$F_N \\approx 34$ N, directed perpendicular to and away from the ramp surface; $a = 4.9$ m/s$^2$ directed down the slope.",
    "check": "Take two limiting cases the algebra must reproduce. As $\\theta \\to 0$ the ramp becomes flat: $F_N \\to mg = 39$ N and $a \\to 0$, which is a block sitting still on a table. As $\\theta \\to 90$° the ramp becomes a cliff: $F_N \\to 0$ and $a \\to g$, which is free fall. Both limits come out right, and note that $F_N = 34$ N sits below $mg = 39$ N as it must for any tilt."
  },
  "trap": "Two traps live here. First, assuming $F_N = mg$ on an incline — it is $mg\\cos\\theta$, and the cosine is less than one for every real ramp. Second, drawing the components $mg\\sin\\theta$ and $mg\\cos\\theta$ as arrows on the diagram while the full $mg$ arrow is still there. That is three gravitational arrows for one interaction, and on the AP exam the diagram is marked on whether it shows forces rather than components."
},
{
  "id": "dyn-third-law",
  "group": "u2",
  "prereqs": [
    "dyn-force-interaction",
    "dyn-free-body"
  ],
  "source": "CED 2.3",
  "title": "Newton's third law",
  "analogy": "Push against a wall on roller skates and you move backwards. The wall did not decide to shove you; your push on it and its push on you are the same single interaction, described from the two ends.",
  "plain": "If object A exerts a force on object B, then B exerts a force on A that is equal in magnitude and opposite in direction. The two forces are the two halves of one interaction.\n\nThe sentence that does the work: **the two forces act on different objects.** Because they act on different objects, they can never cancel each other out. Cancellation only happens between forces on the *same* object.\n\nThe pair is always equal in magnitude. Not usually — always. A lorry hitting a bicycle pushes the bicycle exactly as hard as the bicycle pushes the lorry. What differs is the *effect*, because the same force divided by a much smaller mass gives a much larger acceleration.\n\nThe two members of a pair are also always the same kind of force. Gravity pairs with gravity, a normal force with a normal force, friction with friction.",
  "depth": "In symbols,\n\n$$\\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{A\\ on\\ B}} = -\\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{B\\ on\\ A}}$$\n\nand this expression is **not on the provided equation sheet**. You carry it.\n\nThe two-subscript naming from [[dyn-force-interaction]] makes finding a pair mechanical: swap the subscripts. The partner of 'the normal force of the table on the book' is 'the normal force of the book on the table'. The partner of 'the gravitational force of Earth on the book' is 'the gravitational force of the book on Earth', which is a real force that really does accelerate the Earth, by an unmeasurably small amount.\n\nContrast that with a book resting on a table, where the gravitational force of Earth on the book and the normal force of the table on the book are equal in magnitude and opposite in direction. They are **not** a third-law pair. They act on the same object, they are different types of force, they come from different interactions, and their equality is a *consequence* of the book not accelerating — put the book in a lift and it stops being true. See [[dyn-normal-apparent-weight]].\n\nThe third law is also why internal forces vanish for a system: both members of every internal pair lie inside the boundary, so they cancel in the sum. Internal interactions cannot change the motion of a system's centre of mass. That is the formal justification for the whole-system shortcut in [[dyn-system]].",
  "formula": "$$\\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{A\\ on\\ B}} = -\\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{B\\ on\\ A}}$$\nNot on the equation sheet. Equal magnitudes, opposite directions,\nsame type of force, and acting on DIFFERENT objects.",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A 4000 kg lorry collides head-on with a 1500 kg car. During the collision the lorry exerts a force of magnitude $3.0 \\times 10^4$ N on the car. Find the magnitude of the force the car exerts on the lorry, and the magnitude of each vehicle's acceleration at that instant. Which vehicle is more damaged, and why?",
    "given": "$m_{\\mathrm{lorry}} = 4000$ kg, $m_{\\mathrm{car}} = 1500$ kg, $F_{\\mathrm{lorry\\ on\\ car}} = 3.0 \\times 10^4$ N.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "$F_{\\mathrm{car\\ on\\ lorry}} = 3.0 \\times 10^4$ N, opposite in direction.",
        "why": "The third law fixes this with no calculation and no reference to the masses or the speeds. Mass appears nowhere in the statement of the law, so nothing about the lorry being heavier can change it."
      },
      {
        "do": "Car: $a = \\dfrac{3.0 \\times 10^4}{1500} = 20$ m/s$^2$.",
        "why": "Newton's second law is applied to the car alone, so the car's own mass is the one that divides. The collision force is the dominant horizontal force during the impact."
      },
      {
        "do": "Lorry: $a = \\dfrac{3.0 \\times 10^4}{4000} = 7.5$ m/s$^2$.",
        "why": "Same numerator, different denominator. This is where the asymmetry lives — in the second law, not the third."
      },
      {
        "do": "Answer the last part: the car occupants suffer more, because equal forces produce unequal accelerations when the masses differ.",
        "why": "The question invites the wrong answer 'the lorry hit harder'. Stating the correct mechanism — equal force, unequal mass, unequal acceleration — is what earns the justification mark."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "The car exerts $3.0 \\times 10^4$ N on the lorry. The car accelerates at 20 m/s$^2$, the lorry at 7.5 m/s$^2$. The car and its occupants experience the larger acceleration, and so the larger injury, despite the forces being equal.",
    "check": "The ratio of accelerations should be the inverse ratio of the masses: $20/7.5 = 2.67$ and $4000/1500 = 2.67$. They match, which they must if equal forces were used for both vehicles. Had the accelerations come out equal, or in the same ratio as the masses, the third law would have been applied backwards."
  },
  "trap": "Naming the gravitational force on an object and the normal force on that same object as a third-law pair. They act on one object, so they are a *balanced* pair, and they are only equal while that object has no vertical acceleration. A third-law pair is equal always, and never acts on one object. Test any candidate pair with one question: are these two forces on the same thing? If yes, it is not a third-law pair."
},
{
  "id": "dyn-tension",
  "group": "u2",
  "prereqs": [
    "dyn-third-law",
    "dyn-system"
  ],
  "source": "CED 2.3",
  "title": "Tension, ideal strings and ideal pulleys",
  "analogy": "A tug-of-war rope is not doing something of its own. It relays each team's pull to the other. If the rope is light, nothing is lost in the relay, and the pull that arrives is the pull that was sent.",
  "plain": "**Tension** is the force a string, rope, cable or chain exerts along its own length. It is the net result of every segment of the string pulling on the segments next to it, in response to something pulling on the ends.\n\nStrings pull. They never push. A tension arrow on a free-body diagram always points away from the object, along the string.\n\nThe exam assumes strings are **ideal** unless told otherwise: negligible mass, and no stretching under tension. Two consequences follow, and they are the two facts you use constantly.\n\n- Because the string is massless, the tension is the same at every point in it.\n- Because it does not stretch, objects joined by a taut string have the same magnitude of acceleration.",
  "depth": "An **ideal pulley** has negligible mass and turns on its axle with negligible friction. Its job is to change the direction of the tension without changing its magnitude. So a string over an ideal pulley with a block on each end carries one tension, not two, and the block on one side accelerates upwards at the same rate as the block on the other accelerates downwards.\n\nA real string with mass behaves differently, and the course expects you to describe this **qualitatively only**. In a heavy hanging chain, each link must support the weight of everything below it, so the tension is largest at the top and smallest at the bottom. In an ideal string that gradient vanishes because there is no weight to support.\n\nTension is not a new equation to memorise — there is nothing for it on the provided equation sheet. You get it by applying $\\vec{a}_{\\mathrm{sys}} = \\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{net}}/m_{\\mathrm{sys}}$ to one object at a time, with the string as an external force on each. Choosing [[dyn-system]] boundaries well is what makes a tension problem short: take the whole system to get the acceleration, then one object to get the tension.\n\nSign convention matters in connected-body problems. Define positive as 'the direction the system actually accelerates', which for two blocks over a pulley means down for the heavy block and up for the light one. Then both equations carry the same $a$ with the same sign, and you can add them.",
  "formula": "Ideal string: one tension throughout, and equal acceleration magnitudes\nfor connected objects. For an Atwood pair with $m_2 > m_1$:\n$$a = \\frac{(m_2 - m_1)g}{m_1 + m_2} \\qquad T = m_1(g + a) = m_2(g - a)$$\nNeither is on the equation sheet; both come from Newton's second law\napplied to each block.",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "Blocks of mass 3.0 kg and 5.0 kg hang from the two ends of a light string that passes over an ideal pulley. The system is released from rest. Find the magnitude of the acceleration and the tension in the string.",
    "given": "$m_1 = 3.0$ kg, $m_2 = 5.0$ kg, ideal string and pulley, $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Define positive as the direction of motion: the 5.0 kg block moves down, the 3.0 kg block moves up.",
        "why": "With this convention both blocks have the same signed acceleration $a$, because the inextensible string ties their speeds together. Using 'up is positive' for both would give one block $+a$ and the other $-a$, which works but invites a sign error."
      },
      {
        "do": "Take the whole system (both blocks and the string). The tension is internal and drops out: $(m_2 - m_1)g = (m_1 + m_2)a$.",
        "why": "The string's pull on the 5.0 kg block and its pull on the 3.0 kg block are both inside this boundary, so neither appears. The only external forces along the direction of motion are the two weights, and they oppose each other."
      },
      {
        "do": "$a = \\dfrac{(5.0 - 3.0)(9.8)}{3.0 + 5.0} = \\dfrac{19.6}{8.0} = 2.45$ m/s$^2$.",
        "why": "One equation, one unknown, because of the system choice. Solving block-by-block first would have meant two simultaneous equations in $a$ and $T$."
      },
      {
        "do": "Now take the 3.0 kg block alone: $T - m_1 g = m_1 a$, so $T = m_1(g + a) = (3.0)(9.8 + 2.45) = 36.75$ N.",
        "why": "Redrawing the boundary makes the tension external, so it reappears in the equation. The lighter block accelerates upwards, so the tension must exceed its weight — the sign of $a$ carries that automatically."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$a = 2.45$ m/s$^2$ (5.0 kg block down, 3.0 kg block up); $T \\approx 37$ N.",
    "check": "Get the tension a second way, from the 5.0 kg block: $m_2 g - T = m_2 a$, so $T = (5.0)(9.8 - 2.45) = 36.75$ N. The two independent routes agree. A further check on magnitude: the tension must lie between the two weights, $29.4$ N and $49$ N, and $36.75$ N does. If tension had come out above both weights or below both, the signs were wrong."
  },
  "trap": "Writing $T = mg$ for a hanging block that is accelerating. That is only true in equilibrium. In the worked example, $T = 36.75$ N is neither $3.0g = 29.4$ N nor $5.0g = 49$ N. A second trap is inventing two different tensions, $T_1$ and $T_2$, on the two sides of an ideal pulley — with a massless string and a frictionless massless pulley there is exactly one."
},
{
  "id": "dyn-first-law",
  "group": "u2",
  "prereqs": [
    "dyn-free-body"
  ],
  "source": "CED 2.4",
  "title": "Newton's first law and translational equilibrium",
  "analogy": "A puck slid across smooth ice keeps going in a straight line at the same speed until something touches it. The ice is not keeping it moving; nothing is. Motion is the default state, and it takes an interaction to change it.",
  "plain": "The **net force** on a system is the vector sum of every force exerted on it. Newton's first law says: if that sum is zero, the system's velocity does not change.\n\nRead 'velocity does not change' carefully. It covers two cases, not one. The system may be at rest and stay at rest, or it may be moving and keep moving at the same speed in the same straight line. A parachutist falling at a steady 12 m/s is in **translational equilibrium** every bit as much as a book on a table.\n\nSo no force is needed to keep something moving. Force is what changes motion. If an object's velocity is constant, you can conclude the forces on it balance — and that is a fact you can use, not a fact you have to prove.\n\nForces can balance along one axis and not along another. Then only the velocity component along the unbalanced axis changes.",
  "depth": "Formally, translational equilibrium is\n\n$$\\sum_i \\vec{F}_i = 0$$\n\nwhich is **not printed on the equation sheet**; it is the special case $\\vec{a} = 0$ of the second-law equation that is. Because force is a vector, one vector equation is two scalar equations in a plane:\n\n$$\\sum F_x = 0 \\qquad \\sum F_y = 0$$\n\nA projectile shows the axis-by-axis point cleanly: horizontally the forces balance (there are none), so $v_x$ is constant; vertically they do not, so $v_y$ changes. One object, equilibrium in one direction only.\n\nThe first law also defines what counts as a valid viewpoint. An **inertial reference frame** is one in which an observer would find the first law to hold. Stand in a braking bus and your bag slides forward with no object pushing it — from inside the bus the first law appears to fail, so the bus is not an inertial frame. From the road, the bag simply continued at constant velocity while the bus slowed. The exam's stated convention is that any frame is assumed inertial unless the problem says otherwise.\n\nIn practice the first law is a licence to read forces off a description of the motion. 'Moves at constant speed', 'slides at steady velocity', 'hangs at rest' all mean the same thing: set the sum to zero and solve.",
  "formula": "$$\\sum_i \\vec{F}_i = 0 \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow \\quad \\vec{v} = \\text{constant}$$\nIn components: $\\sum F_x = 0$ and $\\sum F_y = 0$.\nNot on the equation sheet as such; it is the $\\vec{a} = 0$ case of\n$\\vec{a}_{\\mathrm{sys}} = \\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{net}}/m_{\\mathrm{sys}}$, which is.",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A 20 kg shop sign hangs at rest from two cables attached to a single point at the top of the sign. Each cable runs to a wall, and each makes an angle of 30° above the horizontal. By symmetry the two cables carry the same tension. Find that tension.",
    "given": "$m = 20$ kg, both cables at 30° above the horizontal, sign at rest, $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$. From the sheet's table, $\\sin 30° = 1/2$.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "State the physics before the algebra: the sign is at rest, so it is in translational equilibrium and $\\sum \\vec{F} = 0$.",
        "why": "'At rest' is the only dynamical information in the problem. Naming the first law converts it into an equation. Without that step there is nothing to solve."
      },
      {
        "do": "Free-body diagram: three arrows from the dot — gravity $mg$ down, and two tensions, each pointing along its cable, up and outwards at 30° above horizontal.",
        "why": "Strings pull along themselves and away from the object, which fixes both tension directions without further thought."
      },
      {
        "do": "Horizontal equation: $T\\cos 30° - T\\cos 30° = 0$, satisfied identically.",
        "why": "Worth writing down once to confirm the symmetry assumption is consistent, rather than assuming it. It also shows the horizontal axis carries no information here, so the vertical one must carry all of it."
      },
      {
        "do": "Vertical equation: $2T\\sin 30° - mg = 0$.",
        "why": "Each cable contributes its vertical component only. The sine goes with the vertical component because the angle is measured from the horizontal — reading the angle's reference line off the diagram is where this step is won or lost."
      },
      {
        "do": "$T = \\dfrac{mg}{2\\sin 30°} = \\dfrac{(20)(9.8)}{2(0.5)} = \\dfrac{196}{1.0} = 196$ N.",
        "why": "At exactly 30°, $2\\sin\\theta = 1$, so the tension in each cable equals the whole weight of the sign — a memorable coincidence and a good hint that shallow cables carry large tensions."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$T = 196$ N in each cable.",
    "check": "Push the geometry to a limit. As the cables approach horizontal, $\\sin\\theta \\to 0$ and $T \\to \\infty$: a cable can never be pulled perfectly straight while supporting a weight, which matches everyday experience with washing lines. At the other limit, both cables vertical, $\\sin 90° = 1$ and $T = mg/2 = 98$ N, which is the two cables sharing the weight equally. The formula reproduces both, and $196$ N sits above the $98$ N floor as it must."
  },
  "trap": "Reading 'equilibrium' as 'not moving'. An object sliding at constant velocity, or a lift moving up at a steady speed, is in equilibrium and its forces sum to zero. The related trap is assuming that a stated velocity implies a net force in that direction — a lift moving upwards at constant speed has $F_N = mg$ exactly, the same as if it were parked."
},
{
  "id": "dyn-second-law",
  "group": "u2",
  "prereqs": [
    "dyn-first-law",
    "dyn-free-body",
    "kin-acceleration"
  ],
  "source": "CED 2.5",
  "title": "Newton's second law in component form",
  "analogy": "Push a supermarket trolley and it speeds up. Push twice as hard and it speeds up twice as fast. Load it with bricks and the same push produces less. Two knobs, and they turn the result in opposite directions.",
  "plain": "When the forces on a system do not balance, the system accelerates. The acceleration points in the same direction as the net force, its magnitude is proportional to the size of the net force, and it is inversely proportional to the system's mass.\n\n$$\\vec{a}_{\\mathrm{sys}} = \\frac{\\sum \\vec{F}}{m_{\\mathrm{sys}}} = \\frac{\\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{net}}}{m_{\\mathrm{sys}}}$$\n\nRead the equation in the direction the arrow of causation runs. Forces are the input; acceleration is the output. Students who read it the other way start looking for a force that acceleration produces, and there is not one.\n\nNote also what the acceleration belongs to: the system's **centre of mass**. Only a non-zero net *external* force can change how the centre of mass moves.",
  "depth": "The equation is a vector equation, and that is not a formality. In two dimensions it is two independent scalar equations:\n\n$$\\sum F_x = ma_x \\qquad \\sum F_y = ma_y$$\n\nForces on different axes do not add as numbers. A 30 N horizontal push and a 40 N vertical lift give a net force of 50 N, not 70 N, and it points on neither axis.\n\nThe version on the equation sheet is\n$\\vec{a}_{\\mathrm{sys}} = \\sum \\vec{F} / m_{\\mathrm{sys}} = \\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{net}} / m_{\\mathrm{sys}}$, and the sheet also carries the momentum form $\\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{net}} = \\Delta\\vec{p}/\\Delta t = m\\,\\Delta\\vec{v}/\\Delta t = m\\vec{a}$. The component versions are not printed; you write them yourself from the free-body diagram.\n\nThe standard method, and it is worth doing in this order every time:\n\n- Choose the system. See [[dyn-system]].\n- Draw the free-body diagram for it. See [[dyn-free-body]].\n- Choose axes, one parallel to the acceleration.\n- Write $\\sum F = ma$ once per axis, with signs read off the axes.\n- Solve. The perpendicular equation is usually the one that gives you the normal force, and the parallel one gives the acceleration.\n\nNewton's first law is the special case $\\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{net}} = 0$, and it is often the perpendicular equation in a problem where the parallel equation is a genuine second-law statement. The same problem can be equilibrium in one direction and not in the other.",
  "whyThisChoice": "Why insist on the axis parallel to the acceleration? Because the perpendicular equation then reads $\\sum F_{\\perp} = 0$, which is one fewer unknown and is exactly where the normal force comes from. Any other axis choice gives the same answer with both equations coupled.",
  "formula": "$$\\vec{a}_{\\mathrm{sys}} = \\frac{\\sum \\vec{F}}{m_{\\mathrm{sys}}} = \\frac{\\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{net}}}{m_{\\mathrm{sys}}}$$\nOn the equation sheet. Also on the sheet in momentum form:\n$$\\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{net}} = \\frac{\\Delta\\vec{p}}{\\Delta t} = m\\frac{\\Delta\\vec{v}}{\\Delta t} = m\\vec{a}$$\nComponent form ($\\sum F_x = ma_x$, $\\sum F_y = ma_y$) is NOT printed.",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A 25 kg crate rests on a frictionless floor. A worker pulls it with a rope that makes an angle of 37° above the horizontal, with a tension of 100 N. Find the crate's horizontal acceleration and the magnitude of the normal force exerted by the floor.",
    "given": "$m = 25$ kg, $T = 100$ N at 37° above the horizontal, frictionless, $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$. From the sheet's table, $\\sin 37° = 3/5 = 0.6$ and $\\cos 37° = 4/5 = 0.8$.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Free-body diagram: gravity $mg$ down, normal force $F_N$ up, rope tension 100 N at 37° above horizontal. Axes horizontal and vertical.",
        "why": "Here the acceleration is horizontal (the crate stays on the floor), so horizontal and vertical axes already have one axis parallel to the acceleration. There is nothing to gain by tilting them."
      },
      {
        "do": "Horizontal: $T\\cos 37° = ma_x$, so $a_x = \\dfrac{(100)(0.8)}{25} = 3.2$ m/s$^2$.",
        "why": "Only the component of the tension along the direction of motion drives the crate forward. Using the full 100 N would give 4.0 m/s$^2$ and would be claiming the rope pulls entirely horizontally, which contradicts the stated angle."
      },
      {
        "do": "Vertical: the crate does not leave the floor, so $a_y = 0$ and $F_N + T\\sin 37° - mg = 0$.",
        "why": "The vertical equation is a first-law statement inside a second-law problem. Notice the tension has an upward component, so it takes some of the crate's weight off the floor."
      },
      {
        "do": "$F_N = mg - T\\sin 37° = (25)(9.8) - (100)(0.6) = 245 - 60 = 185$ N.",
        "why": "This is the answer that shows $F_N \\ne mg$. The normal force adjusts to whatever value makes the vertical forces balance; it is a response, not a formula."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$a_x = 3.2$ m/s$^2$ horizontally, in the direction of the pull; $F_N = 185$ N.",
    "check": "Independent checks on both numbers. The normal force must be positive and less than $mg = 245$ N whenever the rope pulls upward at all, and $185$ N satisfies both. It also predicts a threshold: the crate would lift off when $T\\sin 37° = 245$ N, that is $T = 408$ N — far above the 100 N applied, so the crate staying down is consistent. For the acceleration, a units check: N/kg $=$ (kg$\\cdot$m/s$^2$)/kg $=$ m/s$^2$."
  },
  "trap": "Adding forces on different axes as though they were numbers, and using the full magnitude of an angled force in a single-axis equation. The tell is an acceleration that comes out larger than $F/m$ using the full force, which is impossible for a single applied force. The second trap in this concept is assuming $F_N = mg$ because the floor is horizontal — the angled rope makes it 185 N here, not 245 N."
},
{
  "id": "dyn-gravitation",
  "group": "u2",
  "prereqs": [
    "dyn-second-law"
  ],
  "source": "CED 2.6",
  "title": "Gravitational force, gravitational field, mass and weight",
  "analogy": "Mass is how much stuff you are. Weight is how hard a particular planet pulls on that stuff. Take yourself to a smaller planet and the first number is unchanged while the second drops.",
  "plain": "Any two objects with mass attract each other. The pull is proportional to each mass and falls off as the square of the distance between their centres of mass:\n\n$$\\left|\\vec{F}_g\\right| = G\\frac{m_1 m_2}{r^2}$$\n\nThe force is always attractive, and it acts along the line joining the two centres of mass. Both objects feel the same size of pull — that is a third-law pair.\n\n**Mass** is a property of an object, measured in kilograms, and it is the same everywhere. **Weight** is the gravitational force an astronomical body exerts on a nearby object, measured in newtons, and it depends on where you are. Near Earth's surface, $F_g = mg$.\n\nWeight is not the same thing as what a bathroom scale reads — the scale reads the normal force. Those two agree when you are not accelerating and differ when you are. See [[dyn-normal-apparent-weight]].",
  "depth": "A **gravitational field** is a way of describing a non-contact force at every point in space without mentioning the test object. The field strength produced by a body of mass $M$ at distance $r$ is\n\n$$\\left|\\vec{g}\\right| = \\frac{\\left|\\vec{F}_g\\right|}{m} = G\\frac{M}{r^2}$$\n\nwhich is the force per kilogram. This derived equation is **not on the equation sheet**; the universal law $\\left|\\vec{F}_g\\right| = Gm_1m_2/r^2$ **is**, and so is the constant $G = 6.67 \\times 10^{-11}$ N$\\cdot$m$^2$/kg$^2$.\n\nThe equation sheet gives $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$ and, separately, $g = 9.8$ N/kg. Those are the same number in two guises, and that is not a coincidence: if gravity is the only force on an object, its acceleration in m/s$^2$ is numerically equal to the field strength in N/kg, because $a = F_g/m = g$. The course framework also records the rounder working figure $g \\approx 10$ N/kg near Earth's surface, useful for estimates.\n\nWhen can $g$ be treated as constant? When the change in $r$ over the problem is small enough that $Gm_1m_2/r^2$ barely moves. Climbing a ladder changes $r$ by a few metres against Earth's radius, so $mg$ with constant $g$ is excellent. For a satellite well above the surface it is not, and you go back to the inverse-square law. The derived relation $\\mathrm{Weight} = F_g = mg$ is not printed on the sheet either, though $\\Delta U_g = mg\\Delta y$ is.\n\nTwo meanings of mass sit inside all this. **Inertial mass** is the resistance to having your motion changed — the $m$ in $F = ma$. **Gravitational mass** is what determines the strength of gravitational attraction — the $m$ in $Gm_1m_2/r^2$. There is no obvious reason these should be the same quantity, and experiment has repeatedly found them to be equivalent. That equivalence is why all objects fall with the same acceleration regardless of mass.",
  "whyThisChoice": "Why bother with the field idea when the force law already works? Because the field lets you say what gravity is doing at a place before you decide what to put there. Once you know $g$ at a location, the force on anything is $mg$, with no further reference to Earth's mass or radius.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "A common shortcut is 'weight is mass times 9.8, always'. It is right near Earth's surface and wrong everywhere else, and it hides the fact that $9.8$ is itself $GM_{\\mathrm{Earth}}/R_{\\mathrm{Earth}}^2$ — a number about Earth, not a universal constant. Students who learn it as a constant of nature cannot answer 'what is your weight at twice Earth's radius'.",
  "formula": "$$\\left|\\vec{F}_g\\right| = G\\frac{m_1 m_2}{r^2}$$  (ON the equation sheet)\n$$\\left|\\vec{g}\\right| = \\frac{\\left|\\vec{F}_g\\right|}{m} = G\\frac{M}{r^2}$$  (derived; NOT on the sheet)\n$$F_g = mg$$  (derived; NOT printed on the sheet as a standalone equation)\n$G = 6.67 \\times 10^{-11}$ N$\\cdot$m$^2$/kg$^2$; $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2 = 9.8$ N/kg.",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "Two uniform lead spheres, of mass 5.0 kg and 8.0 kg, are placed so that their centres are 0.50 m apart. Find the magnitude of the gravitational force each exerts on the other, and compare it with the weight of the 5.0 kg sphere near Earth's surface.",
    "given": "$m_1 = 5.0$ kg, $m_2 = 8.0$ kg, $r = 0.50$ m, $G = 6.67 \\times 10^{-11}$ N$\\cdot$m$^2$/kg$^2$, $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Use $\\left|\\vec{F}_g\\right| = Gm_1m_2/r^2$ with $r$ measured centre to centre.",
        "why": "The law is written for the separation of the centres of mass, and for uniform spheres that is the geometric centre. Using a surface-to-surface gap would be measuring the wrong $r$."
      },
      {
        "do": "$m_1 m_2 = (5.0)(8.0) = 40$ kg$^2$ and $r^2 = (0.50)^2 = 0.25$ m$^2$.",
        "why": "Squaring the separation before dividing avoids the common slip of dividing by $0.50$ instead of $0.25$, which would double the answer."
      },
      {
        "do": "$\\left|\\vec{F}_g\\right| = (6.67 \\times 10^{-11})\\dfrac{40}{0.25} = (6.67 \\times 10^{-11})(160) = 1.07 \\times 10^{-8}$ N.",
        "why": "Each sphere feels this same magnitude, pulling towards the other — the two are a third-law pair, so there is no separate calculation for the 8.0 kg sphere."
      },
      {
        "do": "Weight of the 5.0 kg sphere: $F_g = mg = (5.0)(9.8) = 49$ N. Ratio: $49 / (1.07 \\times 10^{-8}) \\approx 4.6 \\times 10^{9}$.",
        "why": "The comparison is the point of the problem. It shows why gravity between ordinary objects is ignorable and why gravitational forces only matter in practice when one of the masses is astronomical."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$\\left|\\vec{F}_g\\right| \\approx 1.1 \\times 10^{-8}$ N on each sphere, directed towards the other. Earth's pull on the 5.0 kg sphere is 49 N, about $5 \\times 10^{9}$ times larger.",
    "check": "Order of magnitude, done independently of the arithmetic: $G \\sim 10^{-10}$, the mass product $\\sim 10^{1.6}$, and dividing by $0.25$ multiplies by 4. That gives something in the $10^{-8}$ range, which matches. A second check: the answer must be enormously smaller than 49 N, because a pair of lead spheres on a bench visibly do not fly together — and it is."
  },
  "trap": "Confusing mass with weight, in either direction. 'The object weighs 5 kg' is a unit error; kilograms measure mass, newtons measure weight. The second trap is the inverse *square*: triple the separation and the force falls by a factor of nine, not three. The third is measuring $r$ from the surface rather than between centres of mass."
},
{
  "id": "dyn-normal-apparent-weight",
  "group": "u2",
  "prereqs": [
    "dyn-gravitation",
    "dyn-second-law"
  ],
  "source": "CED 2.6",
  "title": "Normal force and apparent weight",
  "analogy": "A lift starting upwards presses you into the floor; one starting downwards makes you feel light. Gravity has not changed by a gram. What changed is how hard the floor has to push to do its part of the job.",
  "plain": "The **normal force** is the perpendicular component of the force a surface exerts on an object in contact with it. It points away from the surface.\n\nThe normal force has no formula of its own. It takes whatever value Newton's second law requires perpendicular to that surface. On a horizontal floor with nothing else going on, that happens to be $mg$ — but the $mg$ came out of the equation, it was not put in.\n\nChange anything about the perpendicular direction and the value changes: tilt the surface, press down on the object, pull upwards on it, or accelerate the whole floor up or down.\n\nYour **apparent weight** is the magnitude of the normal force on you — it is literally what a bathroom scale under your feet reads. When you accelerate, your apparent weight differs from the gravitational force on you.",
  "depth": "For an object on a horizontal floor that is itself accelerating vertically, take upwards as positive:\n\n$$F_N - mg = ma_y \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad F_N = m(g + a_y)$$\n\nThis is **not on the equation sheet**; you derive it from the free-body diagram every time, which is safer than memorising it with a sign you might flip. Read it case by case:\n\n- $a_y = 0$: $F_N = mg$. Standing still, or moving at constant velocity.\n- $a_y > 0$ (accelerating upwards): $F_N > mg$, and you feel heavy.\n- $a_y < 0$ (accelerating downwards): $F_N < mg$, and you feel light.\n- $a_y = -g$ (free fall): $F_N = 0$, apparent weightlessness.\n\nThe last case is what astronauts in orbit are in. Gravity is very much still acting on them; there is simply no normal force, because everything around them is falling at the same rate. A system appears weightless when gravity is the only force on it — or when no forces act on it at all.\n\nOn an incline the same principle gives $F_N = mg\\cos\\theta$, from the perpendicular equation in [[dyn-free-body]]. With an extra downward push $P$ on a horizontal floor it is $F_N = mg + P$. With a rope pulling upwards at angle $\\theta$, $F_N = mg - T\\sin\\theta$. There is no memorising list here; there is one method, applied perpendicular to the surface.\n\nThe **equivalence principle** takes this further: an observer in a non-inertial frame cannot distinguish their apparent weight from a genuine gravitational field. Sealed in an accelerating lift, no experiment you can do inside will tell you whether you are accelerating or sitting in stronger gravity.",
  "whyThisChoice": "Why define apparent weight as the normal force rather than as 'how heavy you feel'? Because the normal force is a measurable quantity with a number attached — the scale reading. It turns a sensation into physics, and it is what the course means by the term.",
  "formula": "Horizontal floor, upwards positive:\n$$F_N = m(g + a_y)$$\nIncline at angle $\\theta$, no perpendicular acceleration:\n$$F_N = mg\\cos\\theta$$\nNeither is on the equation sheet. Both come from $\\sum F_{\\perp} = ma_{\\perp}$.",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A 60 kg student stands on a bathroom scale in a lift. Find the scale reading when (a) the lift is at rest, (b) the lift accelerates upwards at 2.0 m/s$^2$, (c) the lift accelerates downwards at 2.0 m/s$^2$, and (d) the cable snaps and the lift is in free fall. In which case, if any, has the gravitational force on the student changed?",
    "given": "$m = 60$ kg, $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$. Upwards is positive throughout.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Free-body diagram for the student: gravity $mg = 588$ N down, normal force $F_N$ up from the scale. Write $F_N - mg = ma_y$.",
        "why": "Only two forces act on the student. The lift, the cable and the motor are outside this system boundary, so they never appear — their whole effect reaches the student through $a_y$."
      },
      {
        "do": "(a) $a_y = 0$: $F_N = mg = 588$ N.",
        "why": "At rest is equilibrium, so the forces balance. This is the baseline the other three cases are read against."
      },
      {
        "do": "(b) $a_y = +2.0$: $F_N = 60(9.8 + 2.0) = 708$ N.",
        "why": "To accelerate the student upwards, the upward force must exceed the downward one, so the scale must push harder than the student's weight. The sign convention does this automatically."
      },
      {
        "do": "(c) $a_y = -2.0$: $F_N = 60(9.8 - 2.0) = 468$ N.",
        "why": "Same equation, negative acceleration. Note that the lift could be moving upwards while decelerating — direction of motion is irrelevant, only the acceleration enters."
      },
      {
        "do": "(d) $a_y = -9.8$: $F_N = 60(9.8 - 9.8) = 0$ N.",
        "why": "In free fall the only force on the student is gravity, so no normal force is needed or available. This is what 'weightless' means physically."
      },
      {
        "do": "In every case the gravitational force is $mg = 588$ N.",
        "why": "$m$ has not changed and $g$ has not changed, so $F_g$ cannot have. What varied is the normal force, which is what the scale reports."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "(a) 588 N; (b) 708 N; (c) 468 N; (d) 0 N. The gravitational force on the student is 588 N in all four cases.",
    "check": "Check the structure rather than the arithmetic. The four readings must bracket the rest value symmetrically for equal and opposite accelerations: $708 - 588 = 120$ and $588 - 468 = 120$, both equal to $ma = (60)(2.0)$. And case (d) must give exactly zero, since $a_y = -g$ makes $g + a_y = 0$ identically — if it had not, the sign convention was inconsistent."
  },
  "trap": "Writing $F_N = mg$ automatically. It is true only when there is no acceleration perpendicular to a horizontal surface and no other force with a perpendicular component. Every one of the standard hard problems — incline, lift, angled pull, extra push, banked curve — breaks it, and this is where friction problems go wrong too, because friction is $\\mu F_N$ and inherits whatever error $F_N$ carries. The second trap is saying gravity is weaker in a descending lift or absent in orbit. It is not; the normal force changed."
},
{
  "id": "dyn-kinetic-friction",
  "group": "u2",
  "prereqs": [
    "dyn-normal-apparent-weight",
    "dyn-second-law"
  ],
  "source": "CED 2.7",
  "title": "Kinetic friction",
  "analogy": "Dragging a heavy box across carpet, pressing down on it makes the drag worse and lifting up on it makes it easier. Spreading the box out flat versus standing it on end makes no difference at all.",
  "plain": "**Kinetic friction** acts when two surfaces in contact are sliding relative to each other. Its direction is opposite to the motion of each surface relative to the other.\n\nIts magnitude is\n\n$$\\left|\\vec{F}_{f,k}\\right| = \\mu_k \\left|\\vec{F}_N\\right|$$\n\nwhere $\\mu_k$ is the **coefficient of kinetic friction**, a dimensionless number that depends only on what the two materials are.\n\nTwo features surprise people. Friction does not depend on how much surface area is in contact — a brick dragged on its face and on its edge feels the same friction. And it does not depend on speed: doubling the sliding speed does not change the kinetic friction force.\n\nWhat it does depend on is the normal force, and that is the link that makes most friction problems really normal-force problems. See [[dyn-normal-apparent-weight]].",
  "depth": "The equation sheet does something worth noticing here. It prints a single friction line,\n\n$$\\left|\\vec{F}_f\\right| \\le \\left|\\mu \\vec{F}_N\\right|$$\n\nwith $\\mu$ defined as 'coefficient of friction'. It does not print separate kinetic and static equations, and it does not tell you that for kinetic friction the relation is an equality rather than an inequality. You have to carry that distinction yourself: kinetic friction *is* $\\mu_k F_N$; static friction is at most $\\mu_s F_N$. See [[dyn-static-friction]].\n\n'Opposite to the relative motion of the surfaces' is more careful than 'opposite to the motion', and the difference shows up in real problems. A box sitting on a conveyor belt that is speeding up under it slides backwards relative to the belt, so friction on the box acts **forwards** — the same direction the box is moving. Friction is not by nature a retarding force; it is a force that resists sliding between surfaces.\n\nOn an incline, kinetic friction has magnitude $\\mu_k mg\\cos\\theta$, not $\\mu_k mg$, because the normal force is $mg\\cos\\theta$. A crate on a horizontal floor pushed downwards at an angle has friction $\\mu_k(mg + P\\sin\\theta)$. In every case: find $F_N$ from the perpendicular equation first, then multiply.\n\nBecause $\\mu_k$ is a ratio of two forces it has no units. If your $\\mu$ comes out with newtons attached, you have divided by the wrong thing.",
  "whyThisChoice": "Why should friction depend on the normal force rather than on contact area? Because real surfaces touch only at microscopic high points. Press harder and those points deform, so more true contact area forms — pressing down increases the real contact even though the apparent area is unchanged. Laying the block flat spreads the same load over more apparent area at lower pressure, and the two effects cancel.",
  "formula": "$$\\left|\\vec{F}_{f,k}\\right| = \\mu_k \\left|\\vec{F}_N\\right|$$\nThe equation sheet prints only the combined form\n$$\\left|\\vec{F}_f\\right| \\le \\left|\\mu \\vec{F}_N\\right|$$\nso the kinetic equality is yours to remember. $\\mu_k$ is dimensionless.",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A 12 kg crate is pushed across a level floor with $\\mu_k = 0.35$. (a) What horizontal force keeps it moving at constant velocity? (b) The worker instead pushes along a handle directed 37° below the horizontal, still at constant velocity. What force magnitude is now needed?",
    "given": "$m = 12$ kg, $\\mu_k = 0.35$, $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$; $\\sin 37° = 0.6$, $\\cos 37° = 0.8$ from the sheet's table. 'Constant velocity' means $\\vec{a} = 0$.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "(a) Constant velocity means equilibrium, so $\\sum F = 0$ on both axes. Vertically $F_N = mg = (12)(9.8) = 117.6$ N.",
        "why": "Nothing else has a vertical component in part (a), so the normal force comes out as $mg$ here — a result, not an assumption. Part (b) will break it."
      },
      {
        "do": "Horizontally $F = \\mu_k F_N = (0.35)(117.6) = 41.2$ N.",
        "why": "At constant velocity the push exactly matches kinetic friction. Kinetic friction is an equality, so this is a determinate calculation, unlike the static case."
      },
      {
        "do": "(b) Now the push has a downward component. Vertical equation: $F_N - mg - F\\sin 37° = 0$, so $F_N = mg + 0.6F$.",
        "why": "Pushing downward at an angle adds to what the floor must support, so $F_N$ rises above $mg$ — and since friction is proportional to $F_N$, the friction rises too. The unknown $F$ now appears in both equations, which is why they must be solved together."
      },
      {
        "do": "Horizontal equation: $F\\cos 37° = \\mu_k F_N$, so $0.8F = 0.35(117.6 + 0.6F)$.",
        "why": "Substituting the expression for $F_N$ rather than a number is the move; treating $F_N$ as still $117.6$ N is the standard mistake here."
      },
      {
        "do": "$0.8F = 41.16 + 0.21F \\Rightarrow 0.59F = 41.16 \\Rightarrow F = 69.8$ N.",
        "why": "Collecting the $F$ terms on one side. The coefficient $0.59 = \\cos 37° - \\mu_k \\sin 37°$ shows the structure: the angle both reduces the useful horizontal component and increases the friction to be overcome."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "(a) about 41 N horizontally. (b) about 70 N along the handle.",
    "check": "The two answers must satisfy an inequality that follows from the physics without redoing the algebra: pushing downwards can only make the job harder, so (b) must exceed (a). It does, by about 70%. As a limiting case, if the handle angle went to zero the formula gives $F = \\mu_k mg / 1 = 41$ N, recovering part (a) exactly. And if $\\cos\\theta - \\mu_k\\sin\\theta$ ever reached zero, no force along the handle could move the crate, which is a real phenomenon."
  },
  "trap": "Treating $\\mu$ as a force and writing $F_f = \\mu$, or writing $F_f = \\mu_k mg$ when the surface is tilted or something else has a perpendicular component. $\\mu_k$ is a dimensionless ratio; the force is $\\mu_k F_N$, and $F_N$ must be found from the perpendicular equation before it is used. A third error is assuming friction always opposes the object's motion — it opposes the sliding of the surfaces past each other, which is why friction can drive a box forward on an accelerating conveyor."
},
{
  "id": "dyn-static-friction",
  "group": "u2",
  "prereqs": [
    "dyn-kinetic-friction"
  ],
  "source": "CED 2.7",
  "title": "Static friction is an inequality",
  "analogy": "Lean gently on a parked car and it does not roll; lean harder and it still does not. The ground is not pushing back with some fixed amount — it is matching you, up to the point where it runs out and the car moves.",
  "plain": "**Static friction** acts between surfaces that are touching and *not* sliding relative to each other. Unlike kinetic friction, it does not have a fixed value. It takes exactly the value and direction needed to stop the object slipping.\n\nThat means the equation is an inequality:\n\n$$\\left|\\vec{F}_{f,s}\\right| \\le \\mu_s \\left|\\vec{F}_N\\right|$$\n\nThere is a ceiling. Once the force trying to cause sliding exceeds $\\mu_s F_N$, static friction has run out, the object slips, and kinetic friction takes over.\n\nSo there are two questions, in this order. First: *does it move?* Compare the applied force with the maximum $\\mu_s F_N$. Second: *what is the friction?* If it does not move, use Newton's second law with $a = 0$ to find the friction — do not use the formula. If it does move, use $\\mu_k F_N$.",
  "depth": "The maximum is a derived equation,\n\n$$F_{f,s,\\max} = \\mu_s F_N$$\n\nand it is a threshold, not the value of the force. The value of static friction below that threshold is determined by the rest of the free-body diagram. A crate pushed with 10 N and not moving has 10 N of static friction on it, whatever $\\mu_s$ is.\n\nThe provided sheet's friction line, $\\left|\\vec{F}_f\\right| \\le \\left|\\mu \\vec{F}_N\\right|$, is written as an inequality precisely because of this case, but it uses a single unlabelled $\\mu$. You supply the subscript.\n\nFor a given pair of surfaces $\\mu_s$ is typically larger than $\\mu_k$. That is why a heavy box is hardest to start moving and then eases, and why the friction force drops discontinuously at the moment of slipping.\n\nThe direction of static friction is whatever prevents slipping, which is not always the direction beginners expect. On a block resting on an incline that does not slide, static friction points **up the slope**. On the driving wheels of a car accelerating forwards, static friction from the road points **forwards** — the tyre pushes backwards on the road, and the road pushes the car forwards. Rolling without slipping is a static-friction situation even though the car is moving fast.\n\nA tidy special case: put a block on a ramp and slowly increase the angle. It begins to slip when $mg\\sin\\theta = \\mu_s mg\\cos\\theta$, that is when $\\tan\\theta = \\mu_s$. Mass cancels, so the angle at which any block of a given material starts to slide is the same.",
  "whyThisChoice": "Why is static friction defined by an inequality when every other force in this unit has a value? Because it is a constraint force. It exists to enforce a condition — 'no relative sliding' — and a constraint force is always whatever size the constraint needs, up to the point where the constraint fails. The normal force works the same way, which is why neither has a formula of its own.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "The tempting simplification is to write $f_s = \\mu_s F_N$ every time, matching the kinetic case. It gives the right answer only in the single instant when the object is on the verge of slipping. In every other stationary situation it overstates the friction, and it produces absurdities — such as a book on a table with nobody touching it apparently experiencing a large sideways force.",
  "formula": "$$\\left|\\vec{F}_{f,s}\\right| \\le \\left|\\mu_s \\vec{F}_N\\right| \\qquad F_{f,s,\\max} = \\mu_s F_N$$\nThe sheet prints $\\left|\\vec{F}_f\\right| \\le \\left|\\mu \\vec{F}_N\\right|$ only.\nTypically $\\mu_s > \\mu_k$. Block on a ramp begins to slip at $\\tan\\theta = \\mu_s$.",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "An 8.0 kg box sits on a level floor with $\\mu_s = 0.50$ and $\\mu_k = 0.30$. (a) A horizontal force of 30 N is applied. Does the box move, and what is the friction force on it? (b) The force is increased to 45 N. Now what is the friction force and the box's acceleration?",
    "given": "$m = 8.0$ kg, $\\mu_s = 0.50$, $\\mu_k = 0.30$, $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Find the normal force first: level floor, no vertical acceleration, no other vertical forces, so $F_N = mg = (8.0)(9.8) = 78.4$ N.",
        "why": "Both friction expressions depend on $F_N$, so it is computed once and reused. Here it happens to equal $mg$; that is a consequence of the geometry, not a general rule."
      },
      {
        "do": "Compute the threshold: $F_{f,s,\\max} = \\mu_s F_N = (0.50)(78.4) = 39.2$ N.",
        "why": "This is the test value, not the answer. It tells you which regime you are in, and it must be computed before anything else can be decided."
      },
      {
        "do": "(a) $30 < 39.2$, so static friction can hold. The box stays at rest, therefore $a = 0$, therefore $\\sum F_x = 0$, therefore $f_s = 30$ N.",
        "why": "The friction is read off Newton's second law, not off $\\mu_s F_N$. Using the formula would give 39.2 N, which would leave a net force of $-9.2$ N and push a stationary box backwards — visibly wrong."
      },
      {
        "do": "(b) $45 > 39.2$, so the box breaks free and slides. Friction is now kinetic: $f_k = \\mu_k F_N = (0.30)(78.4) = 23.5$ N.",
        "why": "Once sliding starts the regime changes and the coefficient changes with it. This is the step where the two coefficients must not be interchanged."
      },
      {
        "do": "$a = \\dfrac{45 - 23.5}{8.0} = \\dfrac{21.5}{8.0} = 2.7$ m/s$^2$.",
        "why": "Newton's second law along the direction of motion, with the kinetic friction opposing it."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "(a) The box does not move; the friction force on it is 30 N, opposing the applied force. (b) The box slides; friction is 23.5 N and the acceleration is about 2.7 m/s$^2$.",
    "check": "Look at what happens right at the transition. Just below 39.2 N the box is still, held by friction of nearly 39.2 N. The instant it exceeds that, friction drops to 23.5 N — a sudden loss of about 16 N of resistance, which is exactly why a heavy box lurches forward when it finally gives. If your two coefficients had been swapped, friction would *increase* on slipping and the box would decelerate at the moment it started to move, which does not happen."
  },
  "trap": "Assuming static friction is always at its maximum. It is at its maximum only in the instant before slipping. Any problem that says 'does not move' or 'remains at rest' is telling you to get the friction from $\\sum F = 0$, and the $\\mu_s F_N$ value is only there to check whether that is possible. The mirror error is using $\\mu_s$ for an object that is already sliding."
},
{
  "id": "dyn-spring-force",
  "group": "u2",
  "prereqs": [
    "dyn-second-law",
    "dyn-first-law"
  ],
  "source": "CED 2.8",
  "title": "Spring forces and Hooke's law",
  "analogy": "A spring is a fussy negotiator. Pull it out and it pulls back; push it in and it pushes back. However far you take it from where it likes to be, it argues in proportion.",
  "plain": "An **ideal spring** has negligible mass and exerts a force proportional to how far its length has been changed from its relaxed (natural) length:\n\n$$\\vec{F}_s = -k\\,\\Delta\\vec{x}$$\n\n$k$ is the **spring constant**, measured in N/m. A large $k$ means a stiff spring. $\\Delta x$ is the change in length from relaxed, not the total length and not the distance from the floor.\n\nThe minus sign says the force opposes the displacement. Stretch the spring and it pulls back inwards; compress it and it pushes back outwards. Either way, the force on the attached object points **towards the equilibrium position** of the object-spring system.\n\nThat is why springs are called restoring: they always act to undo the displacement.",
  "depth": "$\\vec{F}_s = -k\\Delta\\vec{x}$ **is on the provided equation sheet**, printed exactly in that form. The elastic potential energy $U_s = \\frac{1}{2}k(\\Delta x)^2$ is there too, which is Unit 3's business.\n\nThe minus sign belongs to the vector statement. When you are working with magnitudes in a single-axis problem, write $F_s = k|\\Delta x|$ and put the direction in by hand from the diagram. Carrying a stray minus sign through a magnitude calculation is a common source of a negative spring constant, which cannot happen.\n\nA spring hung vertically with a mass on it gives the standard measurement. At rest the mass is in equilibrium, so the spring force balances gravity:\n\n$$k\\,\\Delta x = mg \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad k = \\frac{mg}{\\Delta x}$$\n\nThat is the basis of the usual experiment: hang known masses, measure the extension for each, plot force against extension, and take $k$ as the **gradient** of the straight line. Plotting $F$ against $x$ and taking a single point's ratio works only if the line passes through the origin, and a gradient is robust to a systematic zero error in the ruler where a single ratio is not.\n\n'Ideal' also means the proportionality holds over the range used. A real spring stretched far enough stops being linear and eventually does not return to its relaxed length. The exam convention is that springs are ideal unless stated otherwise.",
  "whyThisChoice": "Why measure $\\Delta x$ from the relaxed length rather than from the position where the hanging mass sits still? Both are used, and they answer different questions. From the relaxed length, $k\\Delta x$ gives the spring force itself. From the hanging equilibrium position, the combined effect of spring and gravity is again proportional to displacement — which is what makes a vertical mass-spring system oscillate the same way a horizontal one does. Say which origin you are using.",
  "formula": "$$\\vec{F}_s = -k\\,\\Delta\\vec{x}$$  (ON the equation sheet)\nMagnitude form: $F_s = k\\left|\\Delta x\\right|$, directed towards equilibrium.\nVertical hanging mass at rest: $k\\Delta x = mg$.\n$k$ is the gradient of a force-versus-extension graph, in N/m.",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A spring hangs vertically from a stand. With a 0.25 kg mass attached and at rest, the spring is 0.080 m longer than its relaxed length. (a) Find the spring constant. (b) Predict the extension when a 0.60 kg mass hangs from the same spring at rest.",
    "given": "$m_1 = 0.25$ kg, $\\Delta x_1 = 0.080$ m, $m_2 = 0.60$ kg, $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$, ideal spring.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Note that the mass is at rest, so Newton's first law applies: the upward spring force equals the downward gravitational force.",
        "why": "Without 'at rest' there is no relation between the spring force and the weight at all. In a lift, or while the mass is bouncing, $k\\Delta x \\ne mg$. This is the assumption the whole calculation rests on, so it is stated first."
      },
      {
        "do": "$k\\,\\Delta x_1 = m_1 g \\Rightarrow k = \\dfrac{(0.25)(9.8)}{0.080} = \\dfrac{2.45}{0.080} = 30.6$ N/m.",
        "why": "Working with magnitudes and putting the directions in from the diagram, so the minus sign in $\\vec{F}_s = -k\\Delta\\vec{x}$ does not produce a negative $k$."
      },
      {
        "do": "(b) Same spring, so the same $k$: $\\Delta x_2 = \\dfrac{m_2 g}{k} = \\dfrac{(0.60)(9.8)}{30.6} = \\dfrac{5.88}{30.6} = 0.192$ m.",
        "why": "$k$ is a property of the spring, not of the load, so it carries over unchanged. That is the whole point of measuring it in part (a)."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "(a) $k \\approx 31$ N/m. (b) The extension is about 0.19 m.",
    "check": "Use proportionality instead of the arithmetic. For an ideal spring, extension is proportional to the hanging weight, so multiplying the mass by $0.60/0.25 = 2.4$ must multiply the extension by 2.4: $(0.080)(2.4) = 0.192$ m. That reproduces part (b) without using $k$ at all, so an arithmetic slip in part (a) could not hide. Units also check: N/m $\\times$ m $=$ N."
  },
  "trap": "Using the spring's total length instead of the change from relaxed length. If the relaxed spring is 0.30 m and it now measures 0.38 m, $\\Delta x$ is 0.08 m, not 0.38 m. The second trap is carrying the minus sign into a magnitude calculation and reporting a negative $k$; the sign encodes direction, and $k$ itself is always positive. The third is assuming $k\\Delta x = mg$ for a mass that is accelerating — that relation is an equilibrium statement."
},
{
  "id": "dyn-circular-motion",
  "group": "u2",
  "prereqs": [
    "dyn-second-law",
    "dyn-free-body",
    "dyn-static-friction",
    "dyn-gravitation"
  ],
  "source": "CED 2.9",
  "title": "Uniform circular motion and centripetal acceleration",
  "analogy": "Swing a conker on a string and let go. It does not fly outwards from the centre; it flies off along the tangent, straight. The string was not pushing it out — it was continually pulling it in, away from the straight line it would otherwise take.",
  "plain": "An object going round a circle at constant speed is still accelerating, because its **direction** keeps changing and velocity is a vector. The acceleration points towards the centre of the circle and is called **centripetal acceleration**:\n\n$$a_c = \\frac{v^2}{r}$$\n\nBy Newton's second law, an acceleration towards the centre requires a net force towards the centre. That net force has a name — centripetal — but the name describes a **role**, not a new kind of force.\n\nSome real force always plays that role. For a conker on a string it is tension. For a car on a flat bend it is static friction from the road. For the Moon it is gravity. For a ball on the inside of a loop it is the normal force plus part of gravity.\n\nSo on a free-body diagram you draw tension, or friction, or gravity — never an arrow labelled 'centripetal force' in addition to them. That would be counting the same force twice.",
  "depth": "The working equation is $\\sum F_{\\text{towards centre}} = ma_c = mv^2/r$. It is the ordinary second law with the radial direction chosen as the axis, which is exactly the 'axis parallel to the acceleration' rule from [[dyn-free-body]]. $a_c = v^2/r$ **is on the provided equation sheet**; $mv^2/r$ as a force is not printed, because it is not a force — it is what the net force must equal.\n\nCentripetal acceleration is the component of acceleration towards the centre. There can also be a **tangential acceleration**, which is the rate at which the speed changes and points along the direction of travel. The total acceleration is the vector sum of the two. In *uniform* circular motion the speed is constant, so the tangential component is zero and the acceleration is purely centripetal.\n\nFor uniform circular motion, the **period** $T$ is the time for one revolution and the **frequency** $f$ is revolutions per second. $T = 1/f$ **is on the equation sheet**. The relation $T = 2\\pi r / v$ is derived — one circumference at constant speed — and is not printed.\n\nThree standard configurations the course names:\n\n- **Vertical loop, at the top.** Gravity points towards the centre there. The minimum speed for maintaining contact is the speed at which gravity alone supplies the whole centripetal requirement: $mg = mv^2/r$, giving the derived result $v = \\sqrt{gr}$. Below that, the object leaves the track.\n- **Banked curve.** Components of the normal force, and of static friction if present, contribute to the net inward force. The course expects quantitative analysis only of the frictionless case where the bank alone is enough; banked curves that need friction are qualitative only.\n- **Conical pendulum.** A component of the tension supplies the inward force while the vertical component balances gravity.\n\nFor a satellite in a circular orbit, gravity is the only inward force, and combining $Gm_1m_2/r^2$ with $mv^2/r$ and $T = 2\\pi r/v$ gives the derived relation $T^2 = \\frac{4\\pi^2}{GM}R^3$ — Kepler's third law. It is **not** on the equation sheet, and the course does not expect Kepler's first or second laws at all.",
  "whyThisChoice": "Why call it a role rather than a force? Because writing 'centripetal force' as a separate entry lets a student produce a free-body diagram whose forces do not sum correctly and never notice. If instead you list the real forces and then ask 'which of these, or which components, point towards the centre?', the diagram stays honest and the equation writes itself.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "The everyday explanation is a 'centrifugal force' flinging you outwards on a roundabout. In the rotating frame of the roundabout that bookkeeping works, but that frame is not inertial, and the exam's stated convention is that frames are inertial unless said otherwise. From the ground there is no outward force: the door presses you inwards, and your body's tendency to continue in a straight line is what makes it feel like being thrown out.",
  "formula": "$$a_c = \\frac{v^2}{r}$$  (ON the equation sheet)\n$$\\sum F_{\\text{towards centre}} = \\frac{mv^2}{r}$$  (the second law, radial axis)\n$$T = \\frac{1}{f}$$  (ON the sheet)   $$T = \\frac{2\\pi r}{v}$$  (derived, not on the sheet)\nTop of a vertical loop, minimum speed: $v = \\sqrt{gr}$  (derived)\nCircular orbit: $T^2 = \\frac{4\\pi^2}{GM}R^3$  (derived, not on the sheet)",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A car of mass 1200 kg drives round a flat, unbanked bend of radius 50 m. The coefficient of static friction between tyres and road is 0.60. Find the maximum speed at which the car can take the bend without sliding, and state whether a heavier car could go faster.",
    "given": "$m = 1200$ kg, $r = 50$ m, $\\mu_s = 0.60$, level road, $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Free-body diagram: gravity down, normal force up, static friction horizontal and pointing towards the centre of the bend. No arrow labelled 'centripetal'.",
        "why": "Friction is the only horizontal force available on a flat road, so it must be the force playing the centripetal role. It is static, not kinetic, because the tyres roll without sliding sideways."
      },
      {
        "do": "Vertical: no vertical acceleration, so $F_N = mg$.",
        "why": "The road is level and nothing else has a vertical component, so this reduces to $mg$. On a banked curve it would not, which is why the perpendicular equation is always written rather than assumed."
      },
      {
        "do": "Radial: $f_s = \\dfrac{mv^2}{r}$, and the fastest safe speed is where $f_s$ reaches its ceiling, $\\mu_s F_N$.",
        "why": "Static friction adopts whatever value the circular motion demands. Faster cornering demands more. The limit is not a new physical law, it is the point at which the demand exceeds what static friction can supply."
      },
      {
        "do": "$\\mu_s mg = \\dfrac{mv^2}{r} \\Rightarrow v = \\sqrt{\\mu_s g r} = \\sqrt{(0.60)(9.8)(50)} = \\sqrt{294} = 17.1$ m/s.",
        "why": "The mass cancels on both sides. That cancellation is the answer to the second part of the question and is worth writing out rather than substituting 1200 kg and losing it."
      },
      {
        "do": "State the conclusion: the maximum speed does not depend on mass, so a heavier car of the same tyres and road could not corner faster.",
        "why": "A heavier car needs more centripetal force, and gets proportionally more friction because $F_N$ rose with it. The two effects cancel exactly."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$v_{\\max} \\approx 17$ m/s (about 62 km/h). A heavier car has the same maximum speed, because mass cancels.",
    "check": "Dimensions first: $\\mu_s$ is dimensionless, so $\\sqrt{gr}$ has units $\\sqrt{(\\mathrm{m/s^2})(\\mathrm{m})} = $ m/s. Correct. Then a functional check: the formula says $v \\propto \\sqrt{r}$, so a bend of four times the radius allows twice the speed — consistent with wide motorway curves being taken far faster than tight slip roads. Finally, 17 m/s on a 50 m bend is a plausible everyday figure, not 2 m/s or 200 m/s."
  },
  "trap": "Drawing 'centripetal force' as an extra arrow on the free-body diagram alongside the tension or friction that is actually supplying it. That double-counts. The twin error is drawing an outward 'centrifugal' arrow, which does not exist in an inertial frame. A third trap is saying that constant speed means zero acceleration — in circular motion the direction is changing, so the acceleration is non-zero even when the speedometer reading never moves."
},
{
  "id": "enr-energy-and-systems",
  "group": "u3",
  "prereqs": [
    "dyn-system"
  ],
  "source": "CED 3.4",
  "title": "Energy, and the system you draw it around",
  "analogy": "Money only makes sense once you say whose wallet you are counting. Move a five-dollar note from your left pocket to your right and your total is unchanged; hand it to a friend and your total drops by five while theirs rises by five. Nothing was created or destroyed either way — but whether the books balance depends entirely on where you drew the line around 'you'.",
  "plain": "Energy is a number you can compute for a system from how it is moving and how its parts are arranged. It has one property that makes it worth the trouble: it is never created and never destroyed. If the number changes, something crossed the boundary you drew.\n\nThat last sentence is the whole unit. Before you write a single equation you have to say what is inside the system and what is outside. Inside, energy can slosh between forms — motion becomes stretch, stretch becomes motion. Outside, energy can only come in or go out, and it does that through work.\n\nA system containing only one object is the simplest case: a lone object can only have kinetic energy, because potential energy needs at least two things to be arranged relative to each other. Add the Earth, or a spring, and the system can now also store potential energy in the arrangement.",
  "depth": "The CED states two things that look separate and are not. First, energy is conserved in all interactions — always, with no exceptions offered. Second, you are free to draw the boundary so that nothing crosses it, which leaves that system's total energy fixed. The second is a *choice you make*; the first is a fact about the universe. You exploit the fact by making the choice well.\n\nIf the total energy of a system does change, that change is exactly equal to what was transferred in or out. So there are only two situations you will ever be in:\n\n- No net work is done on the system and there are no non-conservative interactions inside it. Then the total mechanical energy of the system is constant, and you write $E_i = E_f$.\n- Work is done on the system by something outside it. Then energy has been transferred across the boundary, and you write $E_f = E_i + W_{\\text{external}}$.\n\nNeither line is on the equation sheet. What you are handed is $\\Delta K = \\sum W$ and the individual energy formulas; the bookkeeping structure is yours to carry.\n\nWhere students go wrong is treating 'the system' as if it were fixed by the problem. It is not. A ball falling to Earth can be analysed as *ball alone* (gravity is an external force doing positive work) or as *ball + Earth* (gravity is internal, and gravitational potential energy converts to kinetic). Both give the same answer. Mixing them does not — see [[enr-system-bookkeeping]].",
  "formula": "Total energy of an isolated system: constant.\n$$E_f = E_i + W_{\\text{external}}$$\n(not on the equation sheet — the structure you supply yourself)",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A 2.0 kg book slides across a level table, starting at 3.0 m/s, and comes to rest after 1.5 m. A constant friction force of 6.0 N acts on it. Account for the energy twice: once with the book alone as the system, and once with the book and the table together.",
    "given": "$m = 2.0$ kg, $v_i = 3.0$ m/s, $v_f = 0$, $d = 1.5$ m, friction force $= 6.0$ N.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Compute the book's initial kinetic energy: $K_i = \\frac{1}{2}(2.0)(3.0)^2 = 9.0$ J.",
        "why": "Whatever bookkeeping you choose, this 9.0 J is the thing that has to be accounted for. Fixing the number first stops you arguing about signs in the abstract."
      },
      {
        "do": "System = the book alone. Friction is exerted by the table, which is now *outside* the system, so it is an external force. It points backwards while the book moves forwards, so it does negative work: $W = -(6.0)(1.5) = -9.0$ J.",
        "why": "With a one-object system there is no potential energy available (a lone object can only have kinetic energy), so the only way the 9.0 J can leave is across the boundary. Work is the only door."
      },
      {
        "do": "Check it closes: $K_f = K_i + W = 9.0 - 9.0 = 0$. The book stops. Energy left the system.",
        "why": "This is the honest statement for this choice of system: the book's energy is *not* conserved, and that is fine. Conservation was never promised for an arbitrary system."
      },
      {
        "do": "System = book + table. Now friction is an interaction between two things inside the boundary, so no external work is done.",
        "why": "Changing the boundary changes which forces are external. Nothing about the physical situation changed — only the accounting."
      },
      {
        "do": "The 9.0 J does not leave; it converts. It becomes thermal energy in the rubbing surfaces: $\\Delta E_{\\text{thermal}} = 9.0$ J.",
        "why": "The total energy of this system is constant, so the kinetic energy that disappeared must have gone into another form inside the system. AP Physics 1 expects you to know that non-conservative forces dissipate mechanical energy as thermal energy or sound, and to stop there — it does not ask you to compute temperatures."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "Book alone: 9.0 J transferred out by the external friction force ($W = -9.0$ J). Book + table: 9.0 J of mechanical energy converted to thermal energy inside the system. Same 9.0 J, two descriptions.",
    "check": "Reach 9.0 J by a route that never mentions energy. Friction gives $a = -F/m = -6.0/2.0 = -3.0$ m/s². Then $v^2 = v_0^2 + 2a\\Delta x = 9.0 - 2(3.0)(1.5) = 0$, so the book really does stop in exactly 1.5 m. The kinematics and the energy account agree, which they would not if the 6.0 N or the 1.5 m had been mis-entered."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "You are allowed to choose the system because the laws do not care. Physics fixes the outcome — the book stops after 1.5 m — not the paperwork. The convention exists so that you can pick the boundary that makes the algebra shortest, which is nearly always the one that turns an awkward external force into an internal potential energy.",
  "trap": "Writing 'energy is conserved' as if it were a statement about the object in front of you. Energy is conserved *in all interactions*, but the mechanical energy of a system you chose badly is conserved only when no external work is done and nothing inside is dissipating. State the system out loud before you write $E_i = E_f$, or you will apply it to a sliding block and lose the mark.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "A tempting shortcut is to always pick 'everything' as the system, since then energy is guaranteed constant. It is true and it is useless: with everything inside, the dissipated thermal energy is now a term you have to track and cannot compute. The skill is choosing a boundary that is *big enough to make the interesting force internal and small enough to leave nothing you cannot evaluate*."
},
{
  "id": "enr-kinetic-energy",
  "group": "u3",
  "prereqs": [
    "enr-energy-and-systems",
    "kin-average-velocity"
  ],
  "source": "CED 3.1",
  "title": "Translational kinetic energy",
  "analogy": "Two identical cars, one at 15 m/s and one at 30 m/s. The fast one is not twice as hard to stop, it is four times as hard. Every driver has an intuition that speed is dangerous out of proportion to itself; the square in $\\frac{1}{2}mv^2$ is that intuition written down.",
  "plain": "Kinetic energy is the energy a thing has because it is moving. It depends on mass and on speed, and the speed dependence is *squared*: double the speed and you get four times the kinetic energy, triple it and you get nine times.\n\nKinetic energy is a scalar. It has a size and no direction, and it is never negative, because $v^2$ is never negative. A car travelling east at 20 m/s and an identical car travelling west at 20 m/s have exactly the same kinetic energy. This is the first big difference from momentum, which you meet in Unit 4 and which does point somewhere.\n\nOne subtlety worth meeting early: kinetic energy depends on who is watching. To you, a passenger sitting still in a moving train has zero kinetic energy relative to the train and a great deal relative to the platform. Neither observer is wrong. Different observers measure different values because they measure different speeds.",
  "depth": "The equation is $K = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$, and it **is on the equation sheet**. The $v$ in it is a speed — the magnitude of the velocity — which is why direction drops out and why $K$ is a scalar.\n\nThe quadratic dependence is the part worth internalising, and when a question asks how $K$ changes rather than what it equals, a ratio is the fastest way to answer. If the mass is unchanged and the speed goes from $v$ to $nv$, then\n\n$$\\frac{K_2}{K_1} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{2}m(nv)^2}{\\frac{1}{2}mv^2} = n^2$$\n\nand everything except $n$ cancels. You never need the mass, and you never need $\\frac{1}{2}$. A question that gives you no numbers at all is telling you it wants this move.\n\nThe frame-of-reference point is not decoration. The CED states directly that different observers may measure different values of an object's translational kinetic energy, because they are in different frames. The exam's conventions say frames are inertial unless stated otherwise, so you will not be asked about accelerating observers — but you may well be asked why two people disagree about a value, and 'they are not measuring the same $v$' is the answer.\n\nThe word *translational* is doing work in the name. It counts the energy of the centre of mass moving through space. An object can also be spinning, and that rotational kinetic energy is a separate term you meet in Unit 6.",
  "formula": "$$K = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$$\non the equation sheet · $K$ in joules, $m$ in kg, $v$ in m/s · scalar, never negative",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A 1200 kg car is travelling at 15 m/s. The driver speeds up to 30 m/s. By what factor does the kinetic energy change, and by how many joules? If the same constant braking force stops the car in both cases, by what factor does the stopping distance change?",
    "given": "$m = 1200$ kg, $v_1 = 15$ m/s, $v_2 = 30$ m/s.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Take the ratio before touching a calculator: the speed doubles, so $K_2/K_1 = 2^2 = 4$.",
        "why": "The question asks for a factor, and factors come from ratios. Mass cancels, so computing the two energies first would be doing arithmetic you are about to throw away."
      },
      {
        "do": "Now the joules, since they were asked for: $K_1 = \\frac{1}{2}(1200)(15)^2 = 135000$ J and $K_2 = \\frac{1}{2}(1200)(30)^2 = 540000$ J.",
        "why": "$135000 \\times 4 = 540000$, so the ratio from step 1 survives contact with the numbers. That agreement is free error-checking."
      },
      {
        "do": "For the stopping distance, use the work-energy theorem: the braking force does work $-Fd$, and that must remove all the kinetic energy, so $Fd = K$.",
        "why": "The braking force $F$ is the same in both cases and cancels in the ratio. This is why energy, not kinematics, is the natural tool here — the question never mentions time."
      },
      {
        "do": "Since $d = K/F$ and $F$ is fixed, $d_2/d_1 = K_2/K_1 = 4$.",
        "why": "Distance inherits the square directly. Doubling your speed does not double the distance you need to stop; it quadruples it."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "Kinetic energy rises by a factor of 4, from $1.35 \\times 10^5$ J to $5.40 \\times 10^5$ J. The stopping distance also rises by a factor of 4.",
    "check": "Get the distance ratio again from kinematics, which never mentions energy. Constant force means constant acceleration, so $v^2 = v_0^2 + 2a\\Delta x$ with $v = 0$ gives $\\Delta x = v_0^2/(2|a|)$. That is proportional to $v_0^2$, so doubling $v_0$ quadruples $\\Delta x$. Two independent routes, same factor of 4."
  },
  "trap": "Answering 'twice' when the speed doubles. The other version of the same slip is treating kinetic energy as if it had a direction — writing $K = -\\frac{1}{2}mv^2$ for something moving in the negative direction. Kinetic energy is a scalar and is never negative; the sign lives on *work*, not on $K$."
},
{
  "id": "enr-work-definition",
  "group": "u3",
  "prereqs": [
    "enr-energy-and-systems",
    "dyn-second-law"
  ],
  "source": "CED 3.2",
  "title": "Work as a transfer of energy",
  "analogy": "Work is not effort. Hold a heavy box motionless at arm's length until you are shaking and you will have done zero work on the box, because none of your energy went into it — it went into your own muscle fibres cycling. The box's energy is unchanged, and the box is what the physics is about.",
  "plain": "Work is the amount of energy a force moves into or out of a system by pushing on it over a distance. It is not a kind of energy that things *have*; it is energy in transit, the same way a bank transfer is not money in an account.\n\nBecause it is a transfer, it takes two things to compute: a force, and a displacement of the point where that force is applied. No displacement, no work — however large the force. No force, no work — however far the thing travels.\n\nWork is a scalar, like energy, and its unit is the joule. But unlike kinetic energy it can be positive, negative, or zero. Positive work adds energy to the system, negative work takes it out. That sign is not a formality; it is the direction of the arrow.",
  "depth": "For a constant force the equation is\n\n$$W = Fd\\cos\\theta$$\n\nwhere $\\theta$ is the angle between the force and the displacement. It **is on the equation sheet**, printed there in a second equivalent form as the parallel component of the force times the distance, $W = F_{\\text{parallel}}d$. The two forms are the same statement: $F\\cos\\theta$ *is* the parallel component.\n\nThe CED is careful about *whose* displacement. It is the displacement of the point at which the force is exerted — the point of application. For a block sliding on a table that is the same as the displacement of the centre of mass, and the CED says that when those two are equal you may model the system as a single object and only its kinetic energy can change. For a person crouching and jumping they are not equal, which is why a jumper's centre of mass gains energy while the floor, which exerts the normal force, never moves through any distance and does no work at all.\n\nOnly the component of the force parallel to the displacement changes the system's energy. That is the physical content of the cosine, and it is developed in [[enr-work-angle-and-sign]].\n\nFor a force that is not constant, $Fd\\cos\\theta$ fails, and you fall back on area under a graph — see [[enr-work-from-graph]].",
  "formula": "$$W = Fd\\cos\\theta$$\non the equation sheet (also printed as $W = F_{\\text{parallel}}d$)\n$\\theta$ = angle between the force and the displacement · unit: joule, J = N$\\cdot$m",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A 12 kg crate sits on a frictionless floor. You push it with a constant horizontal force of 50 N through a displacement of 4.0 m. Find the work done on the crate by each of the three forces acting on it, and hence its kinetic energy at the end. It starts at rest.",
    "given": "$m = 12$ kg, applied force $F = 50$ N horizontal, $d = 4.0$ m, floor frictionless, starts from rest.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "List the forces on the crate: your 50 N push (horizontal), gravity $mg$ (down), the normal force (up). There is no friction.",
        "why": "Work is computed force by force, so the first move is always the same one as in Unit 2 — identify every force separately. A free-body diagram is not wasted effort here."
      },
      {
        "do": "Your push: it is parallel to the displacement, so $\\theta = 0$ and $\\cos 0 = 1$. $W = (50)(4.0)(1) = 200$ J.",
        "why": "Parallel and in the same direction is the maximum work a force of that size can do. Nothing is lost to a cosine."
      },
      {
        "do": "Gravity: it points down, the displacement is horizontal, so $\\theta = 90°$ and $\\cos 90° = 0$. $W = 0$.",
        "why": "The crate does not move down at all, so gravity has no distance to act through. This is not a special rule about gravity — it is the cosine doing its job."
      },
      {
        "do": "The normal force: also perpendicular to the displacement, so $W = 0$ as well.",
        "why": "Same reasoning. Two of the three forces on the crate transfer no energy whatsoever, which is typical rather than unusual on flat ground."
      },
      {
        "do": "Net work $= 200 + 0 + 0 = 200$ J. By the work-energy theorem the crate's kinetic energy changes by 200 J, and since it started at rest, $K_f = 200$ J.",
        "why": "The theorem lets you skip acceleration entirely. You were never told the acceleration, never asked for time, and did not need either."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "Push: $+200$ J. Gravity: 0. Normal force: 0. Final kinetic energy 200 J.",
    "check": "Redo it through Unit 2 and Unit 1, which share no steps with the above. $a = F/m = 50/12 = 4.17$ m/s². Then $v^2 = 0 + 2(4.17)(4.0) = 33.3$ m²/s², and $K = \\frac{1}{2}(12)(33.3) = 200$ J. The two routes agree, so neither the 50 N nor the 4.0 m was mis-substituted."
  },
  "trap": "Confusing work with force, or with effort. Holding a suitcase still does no work on the suitcase; carrying it horizontally across a room at steady speed also does no work on it, because your upward force is perpendicular to the horizontal displacement. If you find yourself computing work without having identified a displacement of the point of application, you are not computing work.",
  "whyThisChoice": "Work is defined with distance rather than time on purpose: it is the quantity that makes the energy books balance. Define it any other way and $\\Delta K = \\sum W$ stops being true, and the whole conservation apparatus that makes this unit powerful falls apart."
},
{
  "id": "enr-work-angle-and-sign",
  "group": "u3",
  "prereqs": [
    "enr-work-definition"
  ],
  "source": "CED 3.2",
  "title": "The cosine: perpendicular, negative, and zero work",
  "analogy": "Push a shopping trolley by its handle, which slopes downward. Some of your push drives it forwards and some of it presses it into the floor. Only the forwards part gets you anywhere; the downwards part is spent entirely on the floor. The cosine is the fraction of your push that is actually going somewhere.",
  "plain": "The $\\cos\\theta$ in $W = Fd\\cos\\theta$ selects the part of the force that lies along the motion. Everything else about the force is irrelevant to energy.\n\nThree cases matter and you should be able to spot each in a second:\n\n- The force points *along* the motion. $\\theta = 0$, $\\cos\\theta = 1$, work is positive and maximal. Energy goes in.\n- The force is *perpendicular* to the motion. $\\theta = 90°$, $\\cos 90° = 0$, work is exactly zero. A perpendicular force can bend the path without changing the speed at all.\n- The force points *against* the motion. $\\theta = 180°$, $\\cos 180° = -1$, work is negative. Energy comes out.\n\nNegative work is not a mistake or a sign of a wrong setup. It is how a brake, a catch, or a friction force is described.",
  "depth": "The perpendicular case is worth dwelling on because it explains circular motion. A force perpendicular to the velocity does no work, so it cannot change the kinetic energy, so it cannot change the speed. The CED puts it exactly this way: the perpendicular component can change the direction of the system's motion without changing its kinetic energy. That is why a satellite in a circular orbit keeps a constant speed forever while a force acts on it the whole time, and why the tension in a string does no work on a ball whirled in a circle at constant speed.\n\nFor the sign, a useful reformulation: work is positive when the force has any component in the direction of motion, and negative when it has any component opposite. You do not need to evaluate the cosine to get the sign — you need only ask whether the force is helping or opposing.\n\nThe exam's trigonometry table gives you the values it expects at the angles it uses. At 37°, $\\cos\\theta = 4/5$ and $\\sin\\theta = 3/5$; at 53° they swap. Those exact fractions are printed on the equation sheet, so a 37° problem has clean numbers available to it, and a messy answer is worth going back over.\n\nOne more sign fact you will reuse in [[enr-conservative-forces]]: when a force does negative work on a system through some displacement, and the system then reverses and comes back, a *conservative* force gives back exactly what it took. A non-conservative one does not.",
  "formula": "$$W = Fd\\cos\\theta$$\n$\\theta = 0°$: $W = +Fd$ (maximum) · $\\theta = 90°$: $W = 0$ · $\\theta = 180°$: $W = -Fd$\nfrom the sheet's trig table: $\\cos 37° = 4/5$, $\\cos 53° = 3/5$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A crate is dragged 4.0 m across a level floor by a rope pulling with a constant 50 N at 37° above the horizontal. A constant friction force of 20 N opposes the motion. Find the work done by the rope, by friction, by gravity, and by the normal force, and the net work.",
    "given": "$F_{\\text{rope}} = 50$ N at 37° above horizontal, $d = 4.0$ m horizontal, friction $= 20$ N, $\\cos 37° = 4/5$.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Rope: the angle between the rope force and the displacement is 37°, so $W = (50)(4.0)\\cos 37° = (50)(4.0)(0.80) = 160$ J.",
        "why": "Only the horizontal component of the rope's pull moves the crate anywhere. That component is $50 \\times 0.80 = 40$ N, and $40 \\times 4.0$ is the same 160 J — the two ways of arranging the arithmetic are the same equation."
      },
      {
        "do": "Note what the vertical component does: $50\\sin 37° = 30$ N upward, and the crate's vertical displacement is zero, so that 30 N does exactly zero work.",
        "why": "It is not doing nothing physically — it reduces the normal force and therefore the friction — but it transfers no energy, because it has no displacement along its own direction."
      },
      {
        "do": "Friction: it opposes the motion, so $\\theta = 180°$ and $W = (20)(4.0)(-1) = -80$ J.",
        "why": "The sign comes from the geometry, not from a rule you memorised about friction. Write $\\cos 180° = -1$ rather than putting a minus sign in by hand, and the sign is never in doubt."
      },
      {
        "do": "Gravity and the normal force are both vertical while the displacement is horizontal: $\\theta = 90°$ for each, so both do zero work.",
        "why": "On level ground these two always vanish. Recognising that instantly is worth real time in an 85-minute section."
      },
      {
        "do": "Net work $= 160 - 80 + 0 + 0 = 80$ J.",
        "why": "Work is a scalar, so you add the numbers with their signs. No vector components, no triangles — that is the payoff for the cosine having already done the geometry."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "Rope $+160$ J, friction $-80$ J, gravity 0, normal force 0. Net work $= +80$ J, so the crate's kinetic energy rises by 80 J.",
    "check": "Test the extremes. If the rope were horizontal ($\\theta = 0$) its work would be the full $(50)(4.0) = 200$ J; if vertical ($\\theta = 90°$), zero. Our 160 J sits between them and nearer the horizontal value, which is right for a shallow 37° tilt. And the net work is positive, so the crate should be speeding up — consistent with a 40 N forward pull beating 20 N of friction."
  },
  "trap": "Using the angle the force makes with the *ground*, or with some axis, instead of with the *displacement*. On a ramp these are different, and it is the single commonest way to lose a work calculation. Always ask: what is the angle between this force arrow and the arrow showing where the thing actually went? The other half of the trap is inserting a minus sign for friction *and* using $\\cos 180°$, which cancels the sign back to positive.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "It is tempting to say 'the normal force holds the crate up, so it must be doing work — it is supporting the weight'. Supporting is not transferring. The test is displacement along the force, and there is none. A table supports a book indefinitely at zero energy cost, which is why tables do not need batteries."
},
{
  "id": "enr-work-from-graph",
  "group": "u3",
  "prereqs": [
    "enr-work-definition"
  ],
  "source": "CED 3.2",
  "title": "Work as the area under a force–displacement graph",
  "analogy": "A taxi fare that changes rate partway through the journey cannot be found by multiplying one rate by the whole distance. You break the trip into stretches, cost each stretch, and add. Area under a graph is that same procedure done continuously.",
  "plain": "$W = Fd\\cos\\theta$ assumes the force stays the same the whole way. Plenty of forces do not — a spring gets harder to stretch the further you pull it, a bow gets heavier to draw.\n\nFor those, work is the area under the graph of the parallel component of the force against displacement. Not the area under force against time, and not the slope of anything: the area, under $F$ versus $x$.\n\nArea below the horizontal axis counts as negative, which is how a graph shows a force that has turned around and started opposing the motion.",
  "depth": "The CED states it plainly: work is equal to the area under the curve of a graph of the parallel force component as a function of displacement. There is no equation for this on the equation sheet, because it is not an equation — it is a geometric reading. What the sheet does give you is the geometry you need to evaluate it: $A = bh$ for a rectangle and $A = \\frac{1}{2}bh$ for a triangle. Any graph whose curve is made of straight segments reduces to those two shapes, so 'break the region into rectangles and triangles' is the whole method.\n\nThe constant-force formula is the special case where the graph is a flat line at height $F$, and the area of that rectangle is $Fd$ — so nothing new has been introduced, only generalised.\n\nThis is where the spring's $\\frac{1}{2}$ comes from. Hooke's law makes the force rise linearly from 0 to $k\\Delta x$ as you stretch a spring by $\\Delta x$, so the region under the graph is a triangle of base $\\Delta x$ and height $k\\Delta x$, with area $\\frac{1}{2}k(\\Delta x)^2$. That is exactly the elastic potential energy in [[enr-elastic-pe]], and knowing where the one-half comes from is better than memorising it.\n\nRead the axes before you read the shape. A force-versus-*time* graph has area with units of N$\\cdot$s, which is impulse, not work, and it turns up in Unit 4. The units of the area are your protection: N $\\times$ m gives joules, N $\\times$ s does not.",
  "formula": "$$W = \\text{area under the } F_{\\text{parallel}} \\text{ vs } x \\text{ graph}$$\nnot on the equation sheet · rectangle $A = bh$, triangle $A = \\frac{1}{2}bh$ (both are on the sheet)\narea below the axis counts negative",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A variable force acts along the direction of motion of a cart. On a graph of force against position, the force rises in a straight line from 0 N at $x = 0$ to 12 N at $x = 3.0$ m, then stays constant at 12 N from $x = 3.0$ m to $x = 5.0$ m. How much work does this force do over the full 5.0 m?",
    "given": "Linear ramp from $(0, 0)$ to $(3.0$ m, $12$ N$)$; horizontal segment from $(3.0$ m, $12$ N$)$ to $(5.0$ m, $12$ N$)$.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Reject $W = Fd$ immediately: the force is not constant over the interval, so there is no single $F$ to use.",
        "why": "Naming why the easy formula fails is what stops you using it anyway. The formula is not wrong, it is inapplicable — it has no defined input here."
      },
      {
        "do": "Split the region at $x = 3.0$ m, where the graph changes character. Left piece: a triangle. Right piece: a rectangle.",
        "why": "Split at every kink. Each piece is then a shape whose area is on the equation sheet, and no calculus is needed."
      },
      {
        "do": "Triangle: $A = \\frac{1}{2}bh = \\frac{1}{2}(3.0)(12) = 18$ J.",
        "why": "Base is a distance in metres, height is a force in newtons, so the product is N$\\cdot$m = J. The units confirm you read the axes the right way round."
      },
      {
        "do": "Rectangle: $A = bh = (2.0)(12) = 24$ J.",
        "why": "The base is 2.0 m, the width of the segment, not 5.0 m. Using the endpoint instead of the width is the standard slip here."
      },
      {
        "do": "Total $W = 18 + 24 = 42$ J.",
        "why": "Both pieces are above the axis, so both are positive and you simply add. If a piece dipped below, you would subtract its area."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$W = 42$ J.",
    "check": "Bracket it. If the force had been 12 N over the whole 5.0 m, the work would be 60 J; if it had been 0 N until 3.0 m, it would be 24 J. The true answer must sit between 24 J and 60 J, and 42 J does. A second check: the average force over the 5.0 m is $42/5.0 = 8.4$ N, which is sensibly between 0 and 12 N and closer to 12 because most of the graph is up there."
  },
  "trap": "Taking the slope instead of the area. Slope of an $F$-versus-$x$ graph has units of N/m, which is a spring constant, not an energy. If you cannot remember which one work is, multiply the axis units together: N $\\times$ m is a joule, so it is the area.",
  "whyThisChoice": "Area is the right operation because work is a sum of little contributions $F\\Delta x$, one for each small step of the journey, each computed with the force as it was during *that* step. Adding up thin rectangles is precisely what an area is. That is also why the method survives any shape of curve, not just the straight lines the exam draws."
},
{
  "id": "enr-work-energy-theorem",
  "group": "u3",
  "prereqs": [
    "enr-kinetic-energy",
    "enr-work-angle-and-sign"
  ],
  "source": "CED 3.2",
  "title": "The work-energy theorem",
  "analogy": "Your bank balance changed by exactly the sum of every deposit and withdrawal. Not the biggest one, not the most recent one — the sum, signs included. The work-energy theorem is that sentence with kinetic energy as the balance.",
  "plain": "The change in an object's kinetic energy equals the total work done on it by all the forces acting on it. Not the work done by the force you find most interesting: the sum, with negative work counted as negative.\n\nWritten out, $\\Delta K = \\sum W$, or $K_f - K_i = W_{\\text{net}}$.\n\nThis is the bridge between Unit 2 and this unit. Forces cause acceleration, and acceleration over a distance changes speed; the theorem packages that whole chain into one line that never mentions acceleration or time.",
  "depth": "The equation sheet prints it as $\\Delta K = \\sum_i W_i = \\sum_i F_{\\text{parallel},i}d_i$, so you are handed both the statement and the way to evaluate each term. It is worth noticing that the second form has a $d_i$ per force: each force is paired with the displacement of *its own* point of application.\n\nThere are two ways to use it and you should know which you are doing.\n\n- **Forwards**: you know the forces and the distance, and you want the final speed. Add the works, add the result to $K_i$, solve for $v$.\n- **Backwards**: you know the speeds and the distance, and you want an unknown force. This is how a stopping-distance or a crumple-zone question is built, and it is far quicker than finding the acceleration first.\n\nThe theorem's real advantage over kinematics is that it does not care whether the force was constant. $v^2 = v_0^2 + 2a\\Delta x$ is only valid for constant acceleration; $\\Delta K = \\sum W$ is valid always, because you can always get the work from an area even when the force varies. When a problem shows you a curved $F$-versus-$x$ graph, or a spring, kinematics is simply not available and energy is.\n\nTwo limits on the theorem, both from the CED. It is a statement about kinetic energy, so it applies cleanly when the system can be modelled as a single object — specifically when the place the force is applied travels exactly as far as the centre of mass does, in which case only kinetic energy can change. When they do not move together, the system's configuration changes too, and you need potential energy as well ([[enr-mechanical-energy-conservation]]).",
  "formula": "$$\\Delta K = \\sum W$$\non the equation sheet, printed as $\\Delta K = \\sum_i W_i = \\sum_i F_{\\text{parallel},i}d_i$\nequivalently $\\frac{1}{2}mv_f^2 - \\frac{1}{2}mv_i^2 = W_{\\text{net}}$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A 1500 kg car travelling at 20 m/s brakes to a stop in 40 m. Assuming the braking force is constant and horizontal, find its magnitude.",
    "given": "$m = 1500$ kg, $v_i = 20$ m/s, $v_f = 0$, $d = 40$ m.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Notice the question gives two speeds and a distance and never mentions time. That is the signature of an energy problem.",
        "why": "Choosing the method deliberately is worth more than any algebra step. Time is absent from $\\Delta K = \\sum W$, so nothing is missing; time is present in every other kinematics equation except $v^2 = v_0^2 + 2a\\Delta x$."
      },
      {
        "do": "Compute $\\Delta K = 0 - \\frac{1}{2}(1500)(20)^2 = -300000$ J.",
        "why": "The change is negative because the car slows. Keeping that sign is what will make the braking force come out opposing the motion rather than driving it."
      },
      {
        "do": "Only the braking force does work: gravity and the normal force are vertical while the displacement is horizontal, so they contribute zero.",
        "why": "The theorem needs *all* the works, so you must check the other forces rather than assume them away. Here they genuinely vanish by the cosine."
      },
      {
        "do": "The braking force opposes the motion, so $W = -Fd = -F(40)$. Set that equal to $\\Delta K$: $-40F = -300000$.",
        "why": "Writing the minus sign explicitly, from the geometry, keeps $F$ as a positive magnitude. Then a negative answer would signal an error rather than being absorbed silently."
      },
      {
        "do": "$F = 300000/40 = 7500$ N.",
        "why": "The arithmetic is a single division because the theorem collapsed the whole problem into one equation with one unknown."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$F = 7500$ N, directed opposite the car's motion.",
    "check": "Do it through Unit 2 instead, which shares no equations with the above. Constant force gives constant acceleration, so $a = (v^2 - v_0^2)/(2\\Delta x) = (0 - 400)/80 = -5.0$ m/s². Then $F = ma = (1500)(5.0) = 7500$ N. The agreement rules out an arithmetic slip. As a second sanity check on the size, compare the force to the car itself: 7500 N is about half the car's weight of $1500 \\times 9.8 = 14700$ N, so the deceleration it produces is about half $g$. A hard stop, which is exactly what pulling up from 20 m/s inside 40 m has to be. Every number in that comparison came from the question."
  },
  "trap": "Using only the work done by the force the question is about, and forgetting the others. If a rope hauls a crate up a rough ramp, the theorem needs the rope's work *and* gravity's *and* friction's. The second version of the trap is more subtle: if you have already accounted for gravity as a potential energy, you must not also include its work as a term — see [[enr-system-bookkeeping]]."
},
{
  "id": "enr-conservative-forces",
  "group": "u3",
  "prereqs": [
    "enr-work-definition"
  ],
  "source": "CED 3.2",
  "title": "Conservative and non-conservative forces",
  "analogy": "A refundable deposit versus a booking fee. Take a detour and come back to where you started: the deposit is returned in full, so the round trip cost you nothing. The booking fee is gone whatever you do, and a longer route means more of them. Gravity is the deposit; friction is the fee.",
  "plain": "Some forces give back everything they take. Lift a book and gravity takes energy from you; lower it and gravity gives the same amount back. Take the book on a wandering route to the same shelf and the total is unchanged — gravity's work depends only on where you started and where you ended, never on how you got there. Forces like that are called **conservative**.\n\nOther forces do not. Drag the book across the table by a long winding path and friction eats far more energy than it does on a short straight one. That energy does not come back when you return the book to its starting place. Those are **non-conservative** forces; friction and air resistance are the two the course names.\n\nThe reason this classification matters more than it looks: potential energy exists only for conservative forces. You can define a gravitational potential energy or a spring potential energy, but there is no such thing as 'friction potential energy', because there is no consistent number to store.",
  "depth": "The CED gives three equivalent tests, and any one of them settles the question.\n\n- **Path independence.** The work done by a conservative force depends only on the initial and final configurations of the system, not the route.\n- **The closed loop.** If the system returns to its initial configuration, the work done by a conservative force — equivalently, the change in the system's potential energy — is zero.\n- **Potential energy exists.** Potential energies are associated only with conservative forces.\n\nBy contrast the work done by a non-conservative force is path-dependent, and the CED's named examples are friction and air resistance.\n\nThe closed-loop test is the sharpest instrument. Take a ball on a round trip up and back down: gravity does $-mgh$ going up and $+mgh$ coming down, total zero, conservative. Slide a block out and back along a table: friction opposes the motion in *both* directions, so it does negative work on both legs and the total is $-2F_fd$, not zero, non-conservative. Notice why friction fails the test — it reverses direction when the motion reverses, so its negative work never gets a chance to become positive.\n\nThis is also why you should stop calling dissipated energy 'lost'. Energy is conserved in all interactions; what friction destroys is not energy but *mechanical* energy, and the difference shows up as thermal energy and sound. The course expects you to know that, and to go no further than knowing it.",
  "formula": "conservative: $W_{\\text{closed loop}} = 0$, and a potential energy $U$ can be defined\nnon-conservative: $W$ depends on the path; no $U$ exists\nexamples given by the course: gravity and ideal springs are conservative; friction and air resistance are not",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "You move a 2.0 kg book from the floor onto a shelf 1.0 m above it. Path A: lift it straight up 1.0 m. Path B: lift it 2.0 m to head height, then lower it 1.0 m onto the shelf. Find the work done by gravity along each path. Then find the work done by gravity if you take the book from the shelf back to the floor along either path.",
    "given": "$m = 2.0$ kg, $g = 9.8$ m/s², shelf height 1.0 m, intermediate height 2.0 m.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Path A: gravity points down, the displacement is up, so $\\theta = 180°$ and $W = -mgh = -(2.0)(9.8)(1.0) = -19.6$ J.",
        "why": "Negative because gravity opposes the motion the whole way. The energy came out of you and went into the book-Earth arrangement."
      },
      {
        "do": "Path B, first leg: rising 2.0 m gives $W = -(2.0)(9.8)(2.0) = -39.2$ J.",
        "why": "Same geometry, more distance. Taken alone this looks like the path matters — but the trip is not over."
      },
      {
        "do": "Path B, second leg: descending 1.0 m, gravity now points *along* the displacement, so $\\theta = 0$ and $W = +(2.0)(9.8)(1.0) = +19.6$ J.",
        "why": "Gravity hands back exactly what it took on the way up through that metre. This is the refund, and it is why the total is about to collapse."
      },
      {
        "do": "Path B total: $-39.2 + 19.6 = -19.6$ J — identical to Path A.",
        "why": "Two different routes, same endpoints, same work. That is path independence demonstrated rather than asserted, and it is why we may later replace all of this with a single $\\Delta U_g = mg\\Delta y$."
      },
      {
        "do": "Round trip: shelf back to floor is $+19.6$ J, and added to the $-19.6$ J of getting there the total is 0.",
        "why": "The closed-loop test, passed. If any part of that round trip had involved friction, the total would have been negative no matter which way you went."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "Gravity does $-19.6$ J along both Path A and Path B. Over the closed round trip it does 0 J.",
    "check": "Test the rule on a force known to fail it. Suppose the book were dragged along the floor with 5.0 N of friction: 1.0 m out costs $-5.0$ J, 1.0 m back costs another $-5.0$ J, total $-10$ J for a round trip that ends where it began. Non-zero, so friction is non-conservative — and the contrast confirms the gravity result was not an artefact of the arithmetic."
  },
  "trap": "Assuming a longer path always means more work done by gravity. It does not — only the *height change* counts, so a gentle 50 m ramp and a vertical 5 m drop to the same final height involve identical gravitational work. That is exactly the fact that makes ramp problems solvable by energy when the geometry is too messy for kinematics.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "A plausible-sounding definition is 'a conservative force is one that conserves energy'. Every force conserves energy — energy is conserved in all interactions. What distinguishes a conservative force is that it conserves *mechanical* energy, by storing rather than dissipating, which is a much narrower claim."
},
{
  "id": "enr-potential-energy-system",
  "group": "u3",
  "prereqs": [
    "enr-conservative-forces",
    "enr-energy-and-systems"
  ],
  "source": "CED 3.3",
  "title": "Potential energy belongs to a system, and its zero is a choice",
  "analogy": "A stretched rubber band between your two hands has energy stored in it. Ask which hand the energy is in and the question has no answer — it is in the arrangement. Potential energy is like that: it lives in the relationship between the parts, not in any one of them.",
  "plain": "Potential energy is energy stored in *how the parts of a system are arranged*. Move the parts and the stored amount changes; put them back and it comes back.\n\nTwo things follow, and both are examined.\n\nFirst, it takes at least two objects. A system containing only one object can have kinetic energy and nothing else. A ball on its own has no gravitational potential energy; a ball *and the Earth* do. Saying 'the ball has 50 J of potential energy' is shorthand that almost everyone uses and that will cost you a mark if a question asks you to be precise.\n\nSecond, only the *change* in potential energy has physical meaning, so where you call it zero is up to you. Put the zero at the floor, at the tabletop, at the ceiling — the height differences, and therefore every prediction, come out the same. You choose the zero to make your own arithmetic easy, and then you state it and stick to it.",
  "depth": "The CED's conditions are precise. A system of two or more objects has potential energy if the objects within it interact only through conservative forces — which is why [[enr-conservative-forces]] has to come first. Potential energy is a scalar associated with the *position* of the objects within the system, and it is defined only up to that additive constant.\n\nOn the zero point the CED is unusually direct: where $U = 0$ sits is something you choose, not something the situation hands you, and you choose it to make the analysis easier. It is a modelling choice, not a fact to be discovered. This is why $\\Delta U_g = mg\\Delta y$ is written on the equation sheet as a *change* rather than as $U = mgy$ — the change is what is real.\n\nFor systems of more than two objects, the total potential energy is the sum over each *pair*. Three objects give three pairs, and you would count each pair once.\n\nWhere does the stored energy actually come from? From work done by an external agent against the conservative force. You lift the book, gravity does negative work, and the system's gravitational potential energy rises by exactly the amount gravity's work was negative. Formally, $\\Delta U = -W_{\\text{conservative}}$ — not on the equation sheet, and the source of a great many sign errors, which is why it is safer to reason about heights going up and down than to memorise it.",
  "formula": "$U$ is a property of a system, never of a single object\nonly $\\Delta U$ is physical; the zero is chosen\n$$\\Delta U = -W_{\\text{conservative}}$$\n(not on the equation sheet)",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A 3.0 kg ball sits on a table 1.2 m above the floor and is then knocked off. Compute the gravitational potential energy of the ball-Earth system before and after with the zero taken (a) at the floor and (b) at the tabletop. Show that the two choices make the same prediction for the ball's speed on landing.",
    "given": "$m = 3.0$ kg, drop height 1.2 m, $g = 9.8$ m/s², starts at rest.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Choice (a), zero at the floor. On the table: $U_i = mgy = (3.0)(9.8)(1.2) = 35.28$ J. On the floor: $U_f = 0$. So $\\Delta U = -35.28$ J.",
        "why": "With the zero at the floor both values are positive or zero, which is why most people pick the lowest point in the problem. That is a convenience, not a requirement."
      },
      {
        "do": "Choice (b), zero at the tabletop. On the table: $U_i = 0$. On the floor: $U_f = (3.0)(9.8)(-1.2) = -35.28$ J, because the floor is *below* the zero. So $\\Delta U = -35.28$ J.",
        "why": "A negative potential energy is not a problem or a sign of a wrong setup. It means 'below the level I called zero', nothing more."
      },
      {
        "do": "The two $U$ values differ, but $\\Delta U$ is the same $-35.28$ J in both.",
        "why": "This is the point of the whole exercise. The two choices differ by a constant of 35.28 J added to every value, and a constant cancels in every difference."
      },
      {
        "do": "Predict the landing speed. The lost potential energy becomes kinetic: $\\frac{1}{2}mv^2 = 35.28$ J, so $v^2 = 2(35.28)/3.0 = 23.52$ and $v = 4.85$ m/s.",
        "why": "Only $\\Delta U$ ever enters the energy equation, so the prediction cannot depend on the choice. Had it depended on the choice, potential energy would not be a usable idea."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "(a) 35.28 J then 0 J. (b) 0 J then $-35.28$ J. Both give $\\Delta U = -35.28$ J and a landing speed of 4.85 m/s.",
    "check": "Get the speed from kinematics, which knows nothing about potential energy or its zero. Free fall from rest through 1.2 m: $v^2 = 2g\\Delta y = 2(9.8)(1.2) = 23.52$, so $v = 4.85$ m/s. Identical — and notice the mass cancelled there but not in the energy route, so the two calculations really are independent."
  },
  "trap": "Saying 'the object has potential energy'. Under exam conditions this usually passes, but a question that asks you to identify the energies of a *system*, or to explain why a lone object cannot have potential energy, is testing exactly this. The safe habit is to name the pair every time: the ball-Earth system, the block-spring system.",
  "whyThisChoice": "The zero is left free because nothing measurable depends on it. Physics fixes differences of potential energy, not values, and inventing an absolute zero would be adding a fact to the theory that no experiment could ever check. Leaving it as a choice is the honest option, and it happens also to be the convenient one."
},
{
  "id": "enr-gravitational-pe",
  "group": "u3",
  "prereqs": [
    "enr-potential-energy-system"
  ],
  "source": "CED 3.3",
  "title": "Gravitational potential energy",
  "analogy": "A hiker who has climbed 300 m has banked something real, and it does not matter whether the trail was a direct scramble or a lazy zigzag. Only the altitude gained is on the books.",
  "plain": "Lift something and you store energy in the object-Earth system. Near the surface of the Earth the amount is mass times $g$ times the *height change*:\n\n$$\\Delta U_g = mg\\Delta y$$\n\nThree details do the work. $m$ is the object's mass in kilograms. $g$ is 9.8 m/s² at Earth's surface, a number the equation sheet gives you. $\\Delta y$ is the vertical height change only — horizontal movement contributes nothing, whatever the path length.\n\nGoing up makes $\\Delta y$ positive and the stored energy rises; coming down makes it negative and the energy is released, usually into kinetic energy.",
  "depth": "The near-surface form is an approximation, and the CED says why it is allowed: stay close to a planet's surface and its gravitational field is near enough to uniform. If $g$ does not change over the heights in your problem, the force is constant, and constant force times distance gives an energy that is linear in height. Go far enough away and $g$ falls off, and you need the general form\n\n$$U_G = -\\frac{Gm_1m_2}{r}$$\n\nfor two roughly spherical masses. Both equations **are on the equation sheet**, with $G = 6.67 \\times 10^{-11}$ N$\\cdot$m²/kg² in the constants. Almost every Unit 3 problem uses the near-surface form; the general form belongs to orbit and satellite questions.\n\nThe minus sign in the general form is not a different convention wearing a disguise — it is the same convention. It comes from putting the zero of potential energy at infinite separation, so every finite separation is *below* zero. It is a choice, exactly as [[enr-potential-energy-system]] says, and this is simply the choice that makes the algebra clean when there is no floor to stand on.\n\nNote the sheet's own labelling: the near-surface change is written with a lowercase subscript, $\\Delta U_g = mg\\Delta y$, and the general form with a capital, $U_G$. Do not let the two blur into each other.\n\nThe path independence is the practical payoff. A ball rolling down a curved ramp, a pendulum swinging through an arc, a skier on a slope — all have gravitational potential energy changes given by the vertical drop alone. That is what makes energy methods solve problems that kinematics cannot touch, because kinematics needs the acceleration along the path and energy does not.",
  "formula": "near Earth's surface:\n$$\\Delta U_g = mg\\Delta y$$\ngeneral, two spherical masses:\n$$U_G = -\\frac{Gm_1m_2}{r}$$\nboth on the equation sheet · $g = 9.8$ m/s², $G = 6.67 \\times 10^{-11}$ N$\\cdot$m²/kg²",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A 0.20 kg ball rolls without friction down a curved ramp, dropping a vertical height of 0.80 m. The ramp's surface is 1.5 m long and its shape is complicated. Find the change in gravitational potential energy of the ball-Earth system, and the ball's speed at the bottom if it started at rest.",
    "given": "$m = 0.20$ kg, vertical drop $\\Delta y = -0.80$ m, ramp length 1.5 m, $g = 9.8$ m/s², frictionless, starts at rest.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Discard the 1.5 m. Only the vertical drop enters $\\Delta U_g = mg\\Delta y$.",
        "why": "The 1.5 m is there to see whether you know gravity is conservative. Path length is irrelevant to gravitational work; it would matter only if friction were present, since friction is path-dependent."
      },
      {
        "do": "$\\Delta U_g = (0.20)(9.8)(-0.80) = -1.568$ J.",
        "why": "Negative because the ball descends. The system's stored energy has gone down by 1.568 J, and since the ramp is frictionless that energy has nowhere to go but into motion."
      },
      {
        "do": "The system loses 1.568 J of potential energy and gains the same in kinetic energy: $K_f = 1.568$ J.",
        "why": "This step needs the ramp to be frictionless and needs no external work — the normal force from the ramp is perpendicular to the motion at every instant, so it does none. Check that before assuming it."
      },
      {
        "do": "Solve $\\frac{1}{2}(0.20)v^2 = 1.568$ for $v$: $v^2 = 15.68$, $v = 3.96$ m/s.",
        "why": "Only now is the mass used, and notice it cancels if you do it symbolically: $v = \\sqrt{2g|\\Delta y|}$, independent of mass."
      },
      {
        "do": "Note the shape of the answer: $v = \\sqrt{2g|\\Delta y|}$, which is the free-fall result.",
        "why": "A frictionless ramp changes the *direction* of the trip, not the speed at the bottom. That is a genuine physical prediction and a good thing to recognise on sight."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$\\Delta U_g = -1.57$ J; final speed $v = 3.96$ m/s.",
    "check": "Compare with straight free fall through the same 0.80 m, using kinematics only: $v^2 = v_0^2 + 2g\\Delta y = 0 + 2(9.8)(0.80) = 15.68$, so $v = 3.96$ m/s. The match is not a coincidence — it is the physical content of gravity being conservative — and it would break immediately if the 0.80 m or the 9.8 had been entered wrongly."
  },
  "trap": "Using the distance travelled along a slope instead of the vertical height. On a ramp of length $L$ inclined at $\\theta$, the height is $L\\sin\\theta$, not $L$. The mirror-image error is using the height when the question is about friction, where the *path length* is the relevant distance — see [[enr-dissipation-friction]]. One problem can need both numbers, for different terms."
},
{
  "id": "enr-elastic-pe",
  "group": "u3",
  "prereqs": [
    "enr-potential-energy-system",
    "dyn-spring-force"
  ],
  "source": "CED 3.3",
  "title": "Elastic potential energy in a spring",
  "analogy": "Drawing a bow gets harder the further back you pull. The last centimetre costs far more effort than the first, which is why the stored energy grows faster than the draw does — it goes as the square.",
  "plain": "Stretch or compress an ideal spring and the block-spring system stores energy:\n\n$$U_s = \\frac{1}{2}k(\\Delta x)^2$$\n\n$k$ is the spring constant in N/m, a measure of stiffness, and $\\Delta x$ is how far the spring sits from its natural, unstretched length — stretched or compressed, it makes no difference which.\n\nThe square means that compressing twice as far stores four times the energy, exactly as doubling a speed quadruples kinetic energy. And because the displacement is squared, stretching and compressing by the same amount store the same energy — a spring does not care which way you deform it.",
  "depth": "The equation **is on the equation sheet**, alongside Hooke's law $\\vec{F}_s = -k\\Delta\\vec{x}$. The pairing is not accidental, and you can derive one from the other with the graph method in [[enr-work-from-graph]]: the spring's force rises linearly from 0 to $k\\Delta x$, so the area under the force-versus-displacement graph is a triangle of area $\\frac{1}{2}(\\Delta x)(k\\Delta x) = \\frac{1}{2}k(\\Delta x)^2$. The one-half is the triangle, and knowing that means you can never confuse this with a rectangle-shaped $k(\\Delta x)^2$.\n\nThe reference point is where students slip. $\\Delta x$ is measured from the spring's equilibrium length — not from the floor, not from the position of the block, not from wherever the spring happens to be at the start of the problem. If a spring is already compressed 0.05 m and you compress it a further 0.07 m, the stored energy at the end uses $\\Delta x = 0.12$ m, not 0.07 m.\n\nBecause $U_s$ goes as the square, a two-position spring problem needs *both* squares: the energy released is $\\frac{1}{2}k(\\Delta x_i)^2 - \\frac{1}{2}k(\\Delta x_f)^2$, which is not $\\frac{1}{2}k(\\Delta x_i - \\Delta x_f)^2$. Subtracting first and squaring after is a genuinely common way to lose the mark.\n\n'Ideal' is doing real work in the phrase 'ideal spring'. The exam's conventions state that springs are assumed to be ideal unless otherwise stated — massless, obeying Hooke's law exactly, and losing nothing to internal friction — so an ideal spring is conservative and its energy is fully recoverable.",
  "formula": "$$U_s = \\frac{1}{2}k(\\Delta x)^2$$\non the equation sheet, with $\\vec{F}_s = -k\\Delta\\vec{x}$\n$\\Delta x$ measured from the spring's natural length · $k$ in N/m · stretch and compression store the same",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A spring of constant 250 N/m is compressed 0.12 m and used to launch a 0.30 kg cart horizontally along a frictionless track. Find the energy stored and the launch speed. Then state what the speed would be if the spring were compressed 0.24 m instead.",
    "given": "$k = 250$ N/m, $\\Delta x = 0.12$ m, $m = 0.30$ kg, frictionless and horizontal.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "$U_s = \\frac{1}{2}(250)(0.12)^2 = \\frac{1}{2}(250)(0.0144) = 1.8$ J.",
        "why": "Square the displacement before multiplying. Squaring 0.12 to 0.0144 is where a factor of ten most often goes missing, so it is worth writing the intermediate value down."
      },
      {
        "do": "The track is horizontal, so there is no change in gravitational potential energy, and it is frictionless, so nothing is dissipated. All 1.8 J goes to kinetic energy.",
        "why": "Say out loud which energy terms are *absent* and why. That is the step that stops you carrying a $mg\\Delta y$ term you do not need or omitting one you do."
      },
      {
        "do": "$\\frac{1}{2}(0.30)v^2 = 1.8$, so $v^2 = 12$ and $v = 3.5$ m/s.",
        "why": "The whole calculation is one energy equation. Doing it with forces would mean handling an acceleration that changes continuously as the spring extends, which the constant-acceleration kinematics equations cannot do at all."
      },
      {
        "do": "For 0.24 m: the compression doubles, so the stored energy is $2^2 = 4$ times larger, 7.2 J.",
        "why": "Ratios again, and no need to redo the arithmetic from scratch."
      },
      {
        "do": "Four times the kinetic energy means $\\sqrt{4} = 2$ times the speed. Double the unrounded $\\sqrt{12} = 3.46$ m/s rather than the rounded 3.5, and $v = 6.9$ m/s.",
        "why": "Energy scales as the square of speed, so a factor of four in energy is a factor of two in speed. Composing the two squares is the reasoning the exam is really after."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "Stored energy 1.8 J, launch speed 3.5 m/s. At double the compression, 7.2 J and 6.9 m/s.",
    "check": "Units first: N/m $\\times$ m² = N$\\cdot$m = J, so the 1.8 is genuinely joules. Then a limiting case: as $k \\to 0$ the spring becomes limp, $U_s \\to 0$ and the cart does not move, which is right. And direct arithmetic on the doubled case confirms the ratio shortcut: $\\frac{1}{2}(250)(0.24)^2 = 7.2$ J, and $v = \\sqrt{2(7.2)/0.30} = \\sqrt{48} = 6.9$ m/s, matching to rounding."
  },
  "trap": "Measuring $\\Delta x$ from the wrong place, or subtracting displacements before squaring. Both leave a plausible-looking number, which is what makes them dangerous. Write down the spring's natural length explicitly at the start of any two-position spring problem and the first error becomes impossible."
},
{
  "id": "enr-mechanical-energy-conservation",
  "group": "u3",
  "prereqs": [
    "enr-kinetic-energy",
    "enr-gravitational-pe",
    "enr-elastic-pe"
  ],
  "source": "CED 3.4",
  "title": "Conservation of mechanical energy",
  "analogy": "A pendulum is a machine for converting height into speed and back, over and over, with the total never changing. At the top it is all height and no speed; at the bottom all speed and no height. Nothing is added and nothing leaks — the sum just moves between two accounts.",
  "plain": "Mechanical energy is what you get by adding up a system's kinetic and potential energies: $E = K + U$.\n\nIf no work is done on the system from outside and nothing inside it dissipates energy, that sum stays the same. Then whatever kinetic energy appears must have come from potential energy, and vice versa, and you can write\n\n$$K_i + U_i = K_f + U_f$$\n\nThat single line solves an enormous number of problems, and it solves them without ever mentioning acceleration, time, or the shape of the path.\n\nWhich is exactly when to reach for it. If a question gives you positions and speeds but no time, and especially if the path is curved or the force varies, energy is the tool and kinematics is not.",
  "depth": "The CED sets the conditions carefully, and you should quote them to yourself before using the equation. If nothing outside the system does any work on it **and** nothing inside it dissipates energy, then the system's mechanical energy stays at whatever value it started with. Both halves are required. If work *is* done on the system, energy has been transferred across the boundary and the total changes by exactly that amount.\n\nNone of $E = K + U$, $K_i + U_i = K_f + U_f$, or its dissipative extension is printed on the equation sheet. You are handed $K = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$, $\\Delta U_g = mg\\Delta y$, $U_s = \\frac{1}{2}k(\\Delta x)^2$ and $\\Delta K = \\sum W$, and you assemble the conservation statement yourself. That is worth knowing before exam day.\n\nThe procedure that does not go wrong:\n\n- Define the system, and say whether the Earth and the spring are inside it.\n- Choose the zero of gravitational potential energy and mark it on your diagram.\n- Check the two conditions: no external work, no dissipation inside.\n- Write every energy term at the initial state and every term at the final state.\n- Set the sums equal and solve.\n\nThe reason energy beats kinematics on a curved track is worth stating explicitly. On a curve the acceleration changes direction and magnitude continuously, so $v^2 = v_0^2 + 2a\\Delta x$ — which assumes constant acceleration — is unusable. Energy asks only where you started and where you finished, because gravity is conservative and the normal force from the track is always perpendicular to the motion and therefore does no work.",
  "formula": "$$E_{\\text{mech}} = K + U$$\nwhen no external work is done and nothing dissipates:\n$$K_i + U_i = K_f + U_f$$\nneither is on the equation sheet · the individual $K$ and $U$ formulas are",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A skateboarder of mass 50 kg starts from rest at the top of a smooth curved ramp 2.5 m high and rolls to the bottom. Friction and air resistance are negligible. Find the speed at the bottom. Explain why the shape of the ramp does not appear in the answer.",
    "given": "$m = 50$ kg, $h = 2.5$ m, starts from rest, frictionless, $g = 9.8$ m/s².",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Choose the system: skateboarder + Earth. Set the zero of gravitational potential energy at the bottom of the ramp.",
        "why": "Putting the Earth inside makes gravity an internal force, so it appears as a potential energy and not as work. Putting the zero at the lowest point makes $U_f = 0$ and kills a term."
      },
      {
        "do": "Check the conditions. The only other force is the ramp's normal force, which is perpendicular to the motion everywhere, so it does no work; the ramp is frictionless, so nothing dissipates. Mechanical energy is therefore constant.",
        "why": "This is the step people skip, and it is the step that makes the equation legal. If the ramp had friction, this whole approach would need the extra term from [[enr-dissipation-friction]]."
      },
      {
        "do": "Initial state: $K_i = 0$ (at rest) and $U_i = mgh = (50)(9.8)(2.5) = 1225$ J. Final state: $U_f = 0$ (at the zero) and $K_f$ unknown.",
        "why": "Writing all four terms, including the zeros, makes it visible that nothing has been forgotten. Zeros written down are safer than zeros assumed."
      },
      {
        "do": "Set them equal: $0 + 1225 = K_f + 0$, so $K_f = 1225$ J.",
        "why": "Conservation is doing the entire job. The ramp's shape never entered because gravitational potential energy depends only on height and the normal force never transferred any energy."
      },
      {
        "do": "$\\frac{1}{2}(50)v^2 = 1225$, so $v^2 = 49$ and $v = 7.0$ m/s.",
        "why": "Note that solving symbolically gives $v = \\sqrt{2gh}$ and the mass cancels — so a 20 kg child on the same ramp arrives at exactly the same 7.0 m/s."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$v = 7.0$ m/s, independent of both the skateboarder's mass and the ramp's shape.",
    "check": "Compare with an object simply dropped 2.5 m, a case kinematics can handle on its own: $v = \\sqrt{2g\\Delta y} = \\sqrt{2(9.8)(2.5)} = \\sqrt{49} = 7.0$ m/s. The frictionless ramp gives the same final speed as a free drop from the same height, as it must if the normal force does no work. As a magnitude check, 7 m/s is about 25 km/h, which is a fast but believable skateboard speed off a 2.5 m ramp."
  },
  "trap": "Applying $K_i + U_i = K_f + U_f$ when the conditions do not hold. The two ways this happens: there is friction or air resistance somewhere in the system, or something outside the system is pushing on it. Check both before writing the line, every time. A related slip is measuring heights from two different zeros within one problem — pick a zero, draw it, and never move it."
},
{
  "id": "enr-system-bookkeeping",
  "group": "u3",
  "prereqs": [
    "enr-mechanical-energy-conservation",
    "enr-work-energy-theorem"
  ],
  "source": "CED 3.4",
  "title": "Counting the same energy twice",
  "analogy": "You would not record a fifty-dollar payment both as an expense and as a reduction in assets and then add the two together — that would show a hundred dollars leaving when only fifty did. Treating gravity as a force doing work *and* as a potential energy in the same equation is that error exactly.",
  "plain": "Gravity can be handled in one of two ways, and only one of them at a time.\n\n**Way 1: the object alone is the system.** The Earth is outside it, so gravity is an external force. It does work on the object, and that work goes into $\\Delta K = \\sum W$. There is no gravitational potential energy in this description, because a single-object system cannot have potential energy at all.\n\n**Way 2: the object and the Earth together are the system.** Now gravity is an internal, conservative interaction. It does not appear as work; it appears as gravitational potential energy, and you use $K_i + U_i = K_f + U_f$.\n\nBoth are correct and both give the same answer. Writing $\\Delta K = W_{\\text{gravity}} - \\Delta U_g$ is not a third way; it is way 1 and way 2 glued together, and it counts the same energy twice.",
  "depth": "The CED's own structure predicts the error. It says that if your system holds one object and nothing else, kinetic energy is the only kind it can carry, and separately that a system whose parts pull on each other conservatively may hold potential energy as well. Which sentence applies is decided by the boundary you drew. Draw one, and the sentence follows; draw none, and you can drift between the two mid-problem without noticing.\n\nThe same choice appears with springs. If the spring is inside the system, use $U_s = \\frac{1}{2}k(\\Delta x)^2$ and do not compute the spring's work. If it is outside, compute the work it does — as the area under its force-displacement graph — and do not write a $U_s$ term. Choosing 'inside' is almost always easier, because $U_s$ is a formula and the work of a variable force is a geometry problem.\n\nThe double-count is detectable, and here is the tell: the wrong route gives an energy exactly twice the right one, so a speed larger by a factor of $\\sqrt{2} \\approx 1.41$. If your answer comes out about 41% too fast on a falling-object problem, this is the reason.\n\nA discipline that makes the error nearly impossible: write the system on the page before writing any equation, then list the external forces. If gravity is on that list, it does work and there is no $U_g$. If it is not, there is a $U_g$ and gravity does no work. There is no third arrangement.",
  "formula": "object alone: $\\Delta K = W_{\\text{gravity}} + W_{\\text{other}}$, and no $U_g$ exists\nobject + Earth: $K_i + U_i = K_f + U_f$, and gravity does no work\nnever both in one equation",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A 50 kg skateboarder descends a smooth ramp of height 2.5 m from rest. Solve for the speed at the bottom twice, once with each system choice, and then show what the double-counted version would predict.",
    "given": "$m = 50$ kg, $h = 2.5$ m, from rest, frictionless, $g = 9.8$ m/s².",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "System = skateboarder alone. Gravity is external and does work $W = +mgh = (50)(9.8)(2.5) = +1225$ J; the normal force is perpendicular and does none.",
        "why": "Positive because the displacement has a downward component and gravity points down. With this system there is no potential energy term available — a single-object system can only have kinetic energy — so all of the accounting must go through work."
      },
      {
        "do": "Apply the work-energy theorem: $\\Delta K = 1225$ J, so $K_f = 1225$ J and $v = \\sqrt{2(1225)/50} = 7.0$ m/s.",
        "why": "One equation, one unknown. The ramp shape still never appears, because gravity's work depends only on the vertical drop."
      },
      {
        "do": "System = skateboarder + Earth. Gravity is now internal, so it does no work; instead $\\Delta U_g = mg\\Delta y = -1225$ J.",
        "why": "The same 1225 J, wearing different clothes. It is the *same number* appearing once in each description, which is the clue to why adding both descriptions is wrong."
      },
      {
        "do": "Apply conservation: $0 + 1225 = K_f + 0$, so $K_f = 1225$ J and $v = 7.0$ m/s. Identical to the first route.",
        "why": "The agreement is the proof that the choice of system is a bookkeeping convention and not a physical assumption."
      },
      {
        "do": "Now the error: writing $K_f = W_{\\text{gravity}} + (U_i - U_f) = 1225 + 1225 = 2450$ J gives $v = \\sqrt{2(2450)/50} = 9.9$ m/s.",
        "why": "Nothing in the algebra objects. The only thing wrong is that gravity was allowed to contribute twice, once as work and once as released potential energy, which invents 1225 J out of nothing."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "Both correct routes give $v = 7.0$ m/s. The double-counted version gives 9.9 m/s, too fast by a factor of $\\sqrt{2}$.",
    "check": "Test the wrong answer against a case you can do independently. Drop the skateboarder straight down 2.5 m in free fall: $v = \\sqrt{2(9.8)(2.5)} = 7.0$ m/s. A frictionless ramp cannot possibly deliver more speed than a free drop from the same height, since it has the same energy available and no way to add more, so 9.9 m/s is impossible on physical grounds alone — no arithmetic needed to reject it."
  },
  "trap": "This is the trap. Gravity is either a force doing work or a potential energy, never both in one equation, and the same applies to a spring. The symptom is an answer high by $\\sqrt{2}$ on a fall from rest, or an energy total that is exactly double what it should be. Write the system boundary down before the equations and the error cannot occur.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "Some students try to keep both terms and 'fix it' with a minus sign: $\\Delta K = W_{\\text{gravity}} - \\Delta U_g$. On a simple fall this gives exactly twice the right number, because $W_{\\text{gravity}} = -\\Delta U_g$ makes the two terms identical and subtracting a negative adds it back, so you are really just computing $2W_{\\text{gravity}}$ minus... nothing consistent. It falls apart the moment another force is present, and it hides the question the exam is actually asking — which system did you choose?"
},
{
  "id": "enr-dissipation-friction",
  "group": "u3",
  "prereqs": [
    "enr-mechanical-energy-conservation",
    "enr-conservative-forces",
    "dyn-kinetic-friction"
  ],
  "source": "CED 3.2",
  "title": "When friction is in the system: dissipated energy",
  "analogy": "Brake pads on a long descent get hot enough to smell. That heat is not a side effect of the energy going somewhere — it *is* where the energy went. Nothing was lost; it just stopped being useful for going fast.",
  "plain": "Once friction or air resistance is involved, mechanical energy is no longer constant. Some of it becomes thermal energy in the rubbing surfaces, and some becomes sound. The total energy is still conserved; the *mechanical* part is not.\n\nThe bookkeeping stays simple. The energy dissipated is the friction force times the length of the path over which it acted:\n\n$$\\Delta E_{\\text{mech}} = F_f d\\cos\\theta$$\n\nand since friction always opposes the motion, $\\cos\\theta = -1$ and the mechanical energy always goes down.\n\nThe distance in that expression is the actual path length travelled, not the straight-line displacement and not the height dropped. A longer route means more dissipation, which is precisely what makes friction non-conservative.",
  "depth": "The working model is this: multiply the friction force by the whole length of path the surfaces rubbed over, and that product is the mechanical energy lost. Two features of that sentence are load-bearing. It is a *model* rather than an exact law, so treat it as the standard estimate and not as a guarantee. And it is the *path*, not the straight-line displacement, which is why a curved or repeated route dissipates more than a direct one.\n\nThis equation is **not on the equation sheet**. Neither is the working form you will actually write:\n\n$$K_i + U_i = K_f + U_f + E_{\\text{dissipated}}$$\n\nwhich just says the mechanical energy you started with either survives as mechanical energy or was dissipated. Getting the dissipated term on the correct side is where signs go wrong; putting it on the *final* side, as a positive amount, means you never have to think about its sign at all.\n\nAP Physics 1 draws a firm boundary here. Knowing that a non-conservative force can drain mechanical energy away as thermal energy or as sound, and being able to say how much left, is inside the course. Working out the resulting temperature rise, or following that heat as it moves, is not — that belongs to AP Physics 2.\n\nOne subtlety on a ramp: the same problem needs two different distances. The height drop $\\Delta y$ goes into $\\Delta U_g$, and the distance along the slope $L$ goes into $F_fL$. Confusing them is the standard way an inclined-plane energy problem fails.",
  "formula": "$$\\Delta E_{\\text{mech}} = F_f d\\cos\\theta$$\n$$K_i + U_i = K_f + U_f + E_{\\text{dissipated}}$$\nneither is on the equation sheet · $d$ = path length, not displacement · $\\theta = 180°$ for friction, so mechanical energy always falls",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A 4.0 kg box slides along a level floor at 8.0 m/s. A constant friction force of 12 N acts on it. Find its speed after it has slid 6.0 m, and the energy dissipated.",
    "given": "$m = 4.0$ kg, $v_i = 8.0$ m/s, $F_f = 12$ N, $d = 6.0$ m, level floor.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Take the box as the system and note that friction is external to it, so $K_i + U_i = K_f + U_f$ does not apply. The floor is level, so no gravitational potential energy changes either way.",
        "why": "State which conservation statement is *unavailable* and why. Skipping this is how an unjustified $K_i = K_f$ gets written."
      },
      {
        "do": "Initial kinetic energy: $K_i = \\frac{1}{2}(4.0)(8.0)^2 = 128$ J.",
        "why": "Fixing the starting number first means the dissipation is then a straight subtraction rather than an equation to rearrange."
      },
      {
        "do": "Energy dissipated: $E_{\\text{dissipated}} = F_fd = (12)(6.0) = 72$ J.",
        "why": "The 6.0 m is the path length along which friction acted, which on a straight level slide happens to equal the displacement. On a curved or reversing path it would not, and the path length is what counts."
      },
      {
        "do": "$K_f = 128 - 72 = 56$ J.",
        "why": "Positive, so the box is still moving. If it had come out negative, the physical reading would be that the box stopped before 6.0 m and the model needs redoing with the true stopping distance."
      },
      {
        "do": "$\\frac{1}{2}(4.0)v^2 = 56$, so $v^2 = 28$ and $v = 5.3$ m/s.",
        "why": "Note the speed fell only from 8.0 to 5.3 while the kinetic energy fell by more than half — the square again."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "72 J dissipated as thermal energy and sound; final speed 5.3 m/s.",
    "check": "Do it with forces and kinematics, sharing no step with the above. $a = -F_f/m = -12/4.0 = -3.0$ m/s². Then $v^2 = v_0^2 + 2a\\Delta x = 64 - 2(3.0)(6.0) = 64 - 36 = 28$, so $v = 5.3$ m/s. Two independent routes agree. As a bonus check, the box would stop after $64/6.0 = 10.7$ m, comfortably beyond 6.0 m, so it was indeed still moving."
  },
  "trap": "Using the vertical height, or the straight-line displacement, in $F_fd$ instead of the distance actually travelled along the surface. On an incline this is the single most common error in the unit: $\\Delta U_g$ takes the height, $E_{\\text{dissipated}}$ takes the slope length, and a problem that gives you both numbers is testing whether you know which goes where. The second trap is calling the dissipated energy 'lost' — it left the mechanical account, not the universe."
},
{
  "id": "enr-power",
  "group": "u3",
  "prereqs": [
    "enr-work-definition",
    "enr-energy-and-systems"
  ],
  "source": "CED 3.5",
  "title": "Power: average and instantaneous",
  "analogy": "Two removal firms carry the same piano up the same stairs. They do identical work on it. One takes four minutes and one takes twelve, and only one of them will still be in business. Power is the difference between them, and work cannot see it.",
  "plain": "Power is the rate at which energy is transferred or converted — energy per unit time. Its unit is the watt, and one watt is one joule per second.\n\nAverage power is just the total energy moved divided by the time it took:\n\n$$P_{\\text{avg}} = \\frac{W}{\\Delta t} = \\frac{\\Delta E}{\\Delta t}$$\n\nInstantaneous power is the rate right now, and for a constant force it works out as force times speed:\n\n$$P_{\\text{inst}} = Fv\\cos\\theta$$\n\nDoing the same job faster does not change the work done. It changes the power required, in exact proportion.",
  "depth": "Both equations **are on the equation sheet**, the average form printed with two equal expressions — $W/\\Delta t$ and $\\Delta E/\\Delta t$ — because power is the rate of *any* energy change, not only the kind done by a force. Energy transferred across a boundary, or converted from one form to another inside the system, both count.\n\nThe instantaneous form is derived rather than fundamental, and seeing the derivation makes it stick: over a short time $\\Delta t$ a constant force does work $F\\cos\\theta \\Delta x$, so dividing by $\\Delta t$ gives $F\\cos\\theta(\\Delta x/\\Delta t) = Fv\\cos\\theta$. The cosine is the same cosine as in [[enr-work-angle-and-sign]], and it is between the force and the *velocity*.\n\nThat formula explains a fact everyone has noticed without explaining: a car's top speed is set by its engine power, not its force. At top speed the car is not accelerating, so the driving force exactly balances the drag; with $P$ fixed, $v = P/F$, and if you want to go faster you must either raise $P$ or lower the drag. The same equation says why a car accelerates hardest at low speed — with $P$ capped, a small $v$ permits a large $F$.\n\nWhen are the two forms the same number? When the rate is constant. A motor lifting a load at constant speed delivers constant power, so the average over the trip equals the instantaneous value at every moment. When the rate varies — a sprinter accelerating, a spring extending — they differ, and you must read which one the question wants.\n\nSign carries over intact. Power is negative when energy is being taken *out*, as a brake takes it out of a car.",
  "formula": "$$P_{\\text{avg}} = \\frac{W}{\\Delta t} = \\frac{\\Delta E}{\\Delta t}$$\n$$P_{\\text{inst}} = Fv\\cos\\theta$$\nboth on the equation sheet · unit: watt, W = J/s",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "An elevator motor raises an 800 kg elevator car at a constant 1.5 m/s. Find the power the motor delivers. Then find the average power over a 20 m rise, and explain why the two agree.",
    "given": "$m = 800$ kg, $v = 1.5$ m/s constant, rise $= 20$ m, $g = 9.8$ m/s².",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Constant speed means zero acceleration, so the net force is zero and the cable tension equals the weight: $F = mg = (800)(9.8) = 7840$ N.",
        "why": "Power needs a force, and this is a Unit 2 step. Assuming $F = mg$ without checking that the acceleration is zero is exactly the habit that fails on an accelerating elevator."
      },
      {
        "do": "The force is upward and the velocity is upward, so $\\theta = 0$ and $\\cos\\theta = 1$. $P_{\\text{inst}} = Fv = (7840)(1.5) = 11760$ W, about 11.8 kW.",
        "why": "Using $P = Fv$ avoids computing any work or any time at all — it reads the rate straight off the force and the speed."
      },
      {
        "do": "Now the average. Work done over the rise: $W = Fd = (7840)(20) = 156800$ J.",
        "why": "Constant force through a straight vertical displacement, so $Fd$ applies directly with no cosine to worry about."
      },
      {
        "do": "Time taken: $\\Delta t = d/v = 20/1.5 = 40/3 = 13.33$ s.",
        "why": "Constant speed, so distance over speed gives the time exactly. If the elevator accelerated, this step would need kinematics instead."
      },
      {
        "do": "$P_{\\text{avg}} = 156800/(40/3) = 156800 \\times 3/40 = 11760$ W. The same value.",
        "why": "They agree because the rate never varied: constant force at constant speed means constant power, so the average over any interval equals the instantaneous value throughout."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$P = 1.18 \\times 10^4$ W (11.8 kW) by both routes.",
    "check": "Check the units and then the magnitude. W = J/s = (N$\\cdot$m)/s = N$\\cdot$(m/s), so 'newtons times metres per second' really is watts, which is $P = Fv$ verified dimensionally. For magnitude, one outside comparison — a rough real-world figure, not a syllabus number: 11.8 kW is around what a small domestic electrical supply delivers, which is believable for a single elevator motor and would be absurd if the answer had come out at 11.8 W or 11.8 MW."
  },
  "trap": "Treating power as if it were energy. 'The motor supplies 11.8 kW of energy' is wrong on units alone — a watt is a joule per second, so a power figure only becomes an energy once you multiply by a time. The other trap is using $P = Fv$ with a speed that is changing: that formula gives the *instantaneous* power at the speed you put in, so plugging in an average speed for an accelerating object gives neither the true average power nor the instantaneous power at any particular moment.",
  "whyThisChoice": "Power exists as a separate quantity because engineering constraints are almost always rate constraints. A rope will hold any load if you lift it slowly enough; a motor will do any job given time. What limits real machines is how fast they can move energy, and that is a quantity work cannot express."
},
{
  "id": "mom-momentum",
  "group": "u4",
  "prereqs": [],
  "source": "CED 4.1",
  "title": "Linear momentum",
  "analogy": "Two shopping trolleys roll towards you at walking pace. One is empty, one is loaded with bags of flour. Both have the same velocity, and neither is hard to nudge sideways, but the loaded one is much harder to stop. Momentum is the quantity that captures that difference: it is what you have to take away from a moving object to bring it to rest.",
  "plain": "Momentum is mass times velocity. An object with a lot of mass, or a lot of speed, or both, has a lot of momentum. A parked lorry has none, because its velocity is zero, however heavy it is.\n\nThe part students underuse is that momentum is a **vector**. It points the same way the velocity points. A 2 kg ball moving east and an identical ball moving west do not have the same momentum — they have momenta of the same size and opposite direction, and if you put both in one system the total is zero.\n\nIn one dimension you handle the direction with a sign: pick a positive direction, and anything moving the other way gets a minus. In two dimensions you keep two separate numbers, an east-west component and a north-south one, and you never mix them. Almost every avoidable mistake in this unit comes from treating momentum as though it were a plain number.",
  "depth": "The defining equation is $\\vec{p} = m\\vec{v}$, and it is **on the provided equation sheet**. Mass is a positive scalar, so $\\vec{p}$ is $\\vec{v}$ scaled up by $m$: same direction, magnitude $mv$. The unit is the kilogram metre per second, kg·m/s, which follows from the definition rather than being a named unit of its own.\n\nBecause $\\vec{p}$ is a vector, in two dimensions it decomposes exactly the way velocity does:\n\n$$p_x = m v_x$$\n\n$$p_y = m v_y$$\n\nThese two components are independent. That independence is not a convenience — it is why [[mom-conservation]] gives you two equations from one collision, one for each direction, and why a collision that scatters objects sideways still has to balance in the sideways direction on its own.\n\nTotalling momentum over a system means adding vectors, not magnitudes. Add the $x$ components to get the total $x$ component, add the $y$ components to get the total $y$ component, and only then find the magnitude with $\\sqrt{p_x^2 + p_y^2}$ and the direction with the tangent. Since velocity is measured relative to a frame of reference, so is momentum; the CED's stated convention is that the frame of any problem is inertial unless you are told otherwise.",
  "formula": "$$\\vec{p} = m\\vec{v}$$\n\n$$p_x = m v_x \\quad p_y = m v_y$$\n\n$$\\left|\\vec{p}\\right| = \\sqrt{p_x^2 + p_y^2}$$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "Two carts slide on a frictionless horizontal surface without touching each other. Cart A has mass 3.0 kg and moves due east at 4.0 m/s. Cart B has mass 4.0 kg and moves due north at 3.0 m/s. Treating the two carts as one system, find the magnitude and direction of the system's total momentum.",
    "given": "$m_A = 3.0$ kg east at $v_A = 4.0$ m/s; $m_B = 4.0$ kg north at $v_B = 3.0$ m/s. Take east as $+x$ and north as $+y$.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Set up axes and write each momentum in components: cart A is $(12, 0)$ kg·m/s, cart B is $(0, 12)$ kg·m/s.",
        "why": "Momentum inherits its direction from velocity, so a purely eastward velocity gives a purely $x$ momentum. Writing components first is what stops you from adding 12 and 12 to get 24."
      },
      {
        "do": "Add componentwise: $p_x = 12 + 0 = 12$ kg·m/s and $p_y = 0 + 12 = 12$ kg·m/s.",
        "why": "The total momentum of a system is the vector sum of the parts' momenta. The $x$ and $y$ directions do not talk to each other, so each sum is done on its own."
      },
      {
        "do": "Magnitude: $\\left|\\vec{p}\\right| = \\sqrt{12^2 + 12^2} = 12\\sqrt{2} \\approx 17$ kg·m/s.",
        "why": "Only now, with both components known, does Pythagoras apply. Doing this before adding would give the length of one cart's momentum, not the system's."
      },
      {
        "do": "Direction: $\\tan\\theta = p_y / p_x = 1$, so $\\theta = 45$ degrees north of east.",
        "why": "The tangent of the angle from the $+x$ axis is the ratio of the components. The equation sheet's trig table gives $\\tan 45^\\circ = 1$, which confirms the angle without a calculator."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "About 17 kg·m/s, directed 45 degrees north of east.",
    "check": "Two independent checks. First, symmetry: the two momentum components came out exactly equal, and a vector with equal $x$ and $y$ components must lie at 45 degrees — no arithmetic needed. Second, bounding: the magnitude of a vector sum can never exceed the sum of the magnitudes, $12 + 12 = 24$, and can never be less than the difference, $0$. Our 17 sits between them, so an answer of 24 (adding magnitudes) or 12 (dropping a cart) would have been caught here."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Momentum is defined as $m\\vec{v}$ rather than, say, $mv^2$, because it is the combination that Newton's third law makes bookkeepable. Third-law force pairs are equal and opposite and act for the same time, so their contributions to $m\\vec{v}$ cancel exactly within a system. Nothing similar happens for $mv^2$, which is why kinetic energy has no comparable conservation rule in collisions.",
  "trap": "Adding momentum magnitudes instead of vectors. If a 2 kg object moving at 3 m/s east meets a 2 kg object moving at 3 m/s west, the total momentum of the pair is zero, not 12 kg·m/s. In one dimension the same error shows up as a dropped minus sign, and a dropped minus sign in this unit typically halves or doubles the final answer rather than changing it slightly, so it is not a small error."
},
{
  "id": "mom-impulse",
  "group": "u4",
  "prereqs": [
    "mom-momentum"
  ],
  "source": "CED 4.2",
  "title": "Impulse and the force–time graph",
  "analogy": "Pushing a stalled car is not about how hard you shove; it is about how hard, for how long. A brief violent shove and a gentle sustained push can deliver the same result. Impulse is the quantity that says how much total 'push' was delivered, and it counts both the force and the time.",
  "plain": "Impulse is the average force multiplied by the length of time that force acts. It is a vector, and it points the same way as the net force that produced it.\n\nReal forces during a collision are not constant. A tennis ball squashes against the strings: the force starts at zero, climbs to a peak while the ball is most deformed, then falls back to zero as the ball leaves. If you graph that force against time you get a hump, not a flat line. The impulse is the **area under that hump**.\n\nThe average force is not a separate measurement. It is defined as whatever constant force would produce the same area over the same time — the height of the rectangle with the same area as the hump. That is why $\\vec{J} = \\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{avg}}\\Delta t$ and 'area under the curve' are the same statement, not two competing methods.",
  "depth": "$\\vec{J} = \\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{avg}}\\,\\Delta t$ appears **on the equation sheet**, in the fuller form $\\vec{J} = \\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{avg}}\\,\\Delta t = \\Delta\\vec{p}$; the second equality is [[mom-impulse-momentum-theorem]]. The unit is the newton second, N·s, which is dimensionally identical to kg·m/s — a useful sanity check, since impulse and momentum must share a unit if they are to be set equal.\n\nThe graphical statement is that the impulse delivered by the net external force equals the area under a graph of that net force against time. Area, here, is signed: a force acting in the negative direction plots below the axis and contributes negative impulse. A ball that is pushed one way and then the other can receive a large area of each sign and end with a small net impulse.\n\nFor AP purposes the areas you are asked to read are made of rectangles and triangles, and the equation sheet's geometry table gives you $A = bh$ and $A = \\frac{1}{2}bh$ for exactly this. Once you have the area you have the impulse; dividing by the total time gives the average force, and the peak of the graph gives the maximum force, which is a different number.",
  "formula": "$$\\vec{J} = \\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{avg}}\\,\\Delta t$$\n\n$$J = \\text{signed area under the } F\\text{-vs-}t \\text{ graph}$$\n\n$$F_{\\mathrm{avg}} = \\frac{J}{\\Delta t}$$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A force sensor records the net horizontal force on a ball during a single kick. The force rises in a straight line from 0 to 600 N over the first 0.020 s of contact, then falls in a straight line back to 0 over the next 0.030 s. Find the impulse delivered to the ball and the average force during the kick.",
    "given": "Peak force 600 N; rise time 0.020 s; fall time 0.030 s; total contact time $\\Delta t = 0.050$ s.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Recognise the graph as one triangle: base 0.050 s, height 600 N.",
        "why": "The rise and the fall are both straight lines starting and ending on the axis, so together they enclose a single triangle. Splitting it into two triangles and adding gives the same answer; treating it as one is fewer chances to slip."
      },
      {
        "do": "Impulse = area = $\\frac{1}{2}(0.050)(600) = 15$ N·s.",
        "why": "The impulse from a varying force is the area under the force-time curve. The triangle formula $A = \\frac{1}{2}bh$ is on the provided equation sheet, so this step needs nothing memorised."
      },
      {
        "do": "Average force: $F_{\\mathrm{avg}} = J / \\Delta t = 15 / 0.050 = 300$ N.",
        "why": "The average force is defined as the constant force delivering the same impulse over the same interval. Rearranging $J = F_{\\mathrm{avg}}\\Delta t$ is the definition, not an approximation."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "Impulse 15 N·s, directed along the kick; average force 300 N.",
    "check": "Two checks that do not reuse the same arithmetic. First, the average force must lie strictly between 0 and the peak of 600 N, and for a triangle that starts and ends at zero it must be exactly half the peak — 300 N is exactly half, so the area was computed with the right factor. Second, units: N·s is kg·m/s² times s, which is kg·m/s, the unit of momentum. If the impulse had come out in anything else, it could not be set equal to a change in momentum."
  },
  "trap": "Reading the **peak** force off the graph and calling it the average. In the worked example that would give an impulse of $600 \\times 0.050 = 30$ N·s, double the truth. The second version of this trap is forgetting that area below the time axis is negative: a graph showing $+8$ N·s of area above and $-3$ N·s below delivers 5 N·s, not 11 N·s."
},
{
  "id": "mom-impulse-momentum-theorem",
  "group": "u4",
  "prereqs": [
    "mom-impulse",
    "dyn-second-law"
  ],
  "source": "CED 4.2",
  "title": "The impulse–momentum theorem",
  "analogy": "A bank account tells the story. Your balance is momentum; a deposit or withdrawal is impulse. The balance only changes when something is paid in or taken out, and the change in the balance is exactly the size of the transaction. Nothing else can move it.",
  "plain": "The impulse delivered to a system equals that system's change in momentum. In symbols, $\\vec{J} = \\Delta\\vec{p}$. That is the whole theorem, and almost every problem in this unit is an application of it.\n\nChange in momentum means final minus initial, as vectors: $\\Delta\\vec{p} = \\vec{p} - \\vec{p}_0$. If a ball comes in at 30 m/s and leaves at 40 m/s the other way, the change is not 10 units — it is 70, because the two velocities have opposite signs.\n\nNewton's second law is not a separate law sitting alongside this. It is this theorem, divided by the time, for a system whose mass does not change. Net force is the rate at which momentum changes; if $m$ is constant, the rate at which $m\\vec{v}$ changes is $m$ times the rate at which $\\vec{v}$ changes, which is $m\\vec{a}$. Same statement, rearranged.",
  "depth": "The equation sheet carries both forms:\n\n$$\\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{net}} = \\frac{\\Delta \\vec{p}}{\\Delta t} = m\\frac{\\Delta \\vec{v}}{\\Delta t} = m\\vec{a}$$\n\n$$\\vec{J} = \\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{avg}}\\,\\Delta t = \\Delta\\vec{p}$$\n\nThe chain in the first line is the argument, printed on the sheet: net force is the rate of change of momentum always, and equals $m\\vec{a}$ only when $m$ is constant. The CED's boundary statement is explicit that AP Physics 1 does not require quantitative analysis of systems whose mass changes with time, so on this exam the two forms coincide — but knowing which one is the more fundamental is what lets you answer questions about why the theorem is stated in terms of momentum at all.\n\nThere are two graph readings that follow directly. On a graph of net external force against time, the **area** is the impulse ([[mom-impulse]]). On a graph of the system's momentum against time, the **slope** is the net external force. They are inverse readings of the same relationship, and a question that gives you one graph and asks about the other quantity is testing exactly this.\n\nBeing a vector equation, $\\vec{J} = \\Delta\\vec{p}$ holds separately in each direction: $J_x = \\Delta p_x$ and $J_y = \\Delta p_y$. A horizontal impulse cannot change vertical momentum.",
  "formula": "$$\\vec{J} = \\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{avg}}\\,\\Delta t = \\Delta\\vec{p} = \\vec{p} - \\vec{p}_0$$\n\n$$\\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{net}} = \\frac{\\Delta\\vec{p}}{\\Delta t} = m\\vec{a} \\quad (m \\text{ constant})$$\n\n$$\\text{slope of a } p\\text{-vs-}t \\text{ graph} = F_{\\mathrm{net}}$$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A 0.15 kg ball is thrown horizontally at 30 m/s and is struck by a bat, leaving along the same line at 40 m/s in the opposite direction. The bat and ball are in contact for 1.0 ms. Find the impulse delivered to the ball and the average force the bat exerts on it.",
    "given": "$m = 0.15$ kg; $v_0 = 30$ m/s towards the bat; $v = 40$ m/s away; $\\Delta t = 1.0$ ms $= 0.0010$ s; $g = 9.8$ m/s².",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Choose $+x$ as the direction the ball leaves. Then $v_0 = -30$ m/s and $v = +40$ m/s.",
        "why": "One axis, fixed before any arithmetic. The incoming ball travels against the chosen positive direction, so its velocity carries a minus sign. Choosing the axis after computing is how sign errors get in."
      },
      {
        "do": "$\\Delta p = m(v - v_0) = 0.15 \\left(40 - (-30)\\right) = 0.15 \\times 70 = 10.5$ kg·m/s in the $+x$ direction.",
        "why": "Change means final minus initial as a vector. The double negative is the physics: the bat first had to stop 30 m/s of incoming motion and then create 40 m/s of outgoing motion, and both jobs cost impulse in the same direction."
      },
      {
        "do": "By the theorem, $J = \\Delta p = 10.5$ N·s, in the $+x$ direction.",
        "why": "The impulse-momentum theorem is what converts a kinematic fact — the velocities before and after — into a statement about force, without knowing anything about how the force varied during contact."
      },
      {
        "do": "$F_{\\mathrm{avg}} = J/\\Delta t = 10.5 / 0.0010 = 1.05 \\times 10^{4}$ N.",
        "why": "Only the average is obtainable here; the peak force was larger. Nothing in the given information constrains the shape of the force-time curve, so nothing more than the average can honestly be extracted."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "Impulse $1.05 \\times 10^{1}$ N·s, i.e. 10.5 N·s away from the bat; average force about $1.1 \\times 10^{4}$ N in that same direction.",
    "check": "An independent check on the magnitude: the ball's weight is $mg = 0.15 \\times 9.8 = 1.5$ N, so the bat's average force is roughly 7000 times the ball's weight. That is the right order of magnitude for a bat impact, and it also justifies ignoring gravity's impulse during the 1.0 ms of contact — see [[mom-collisions-explosions]]. A second check: had the ball merely stopped rather than reversed, $\\Delta p$ would have been $0.15 \\times 30 = 4.5$ kg·m/s. Reversing must cost more than stopping, and 10.5 is indeed larger. An answer of $0.15 \\times 10 = 1.5$ kg·m/s would have failed this test at once."
  },
  "rejectedAlternative": "A tempting move is to find the acceleration first with a kinematics equation and then use $F = ma$. It is not wrong, but it needs an extra assumption the problem does not give you: that the acceleration was constant during contact. It was not — the force rose and fell. The impulse-momentum route needs only the before and after states, which is exactly what a collision hands you.",
  "trap": "Computing $\\Delta p$ as $m(40 - 30) = 1.5$ kg·m/s by subtracting speeds instead of velocities. Whenever an object reverses direction, the two velocities have opposite signs and the change is the **sum** of the speeds times the mass. This single error is the most common wrong answer in the whole unit."
},
{
  "id": "mom-collision-time",
  "group": "u4",
  "prereqs": [
    "mom-impulse-momentum-theorem"
  ],
  "source": "CED 4.2",
  "title": "Why longer contact time means smaller force",
  "analogy": "Catching a cricket ball, you pull your hands back as it arrives. The ball still has to be brought to rest — you cannot negotiate that away. What you can choose is whether to do it in five milliseconds or in fifty. Everything about airbags, crumple zones, crash mats and bending your knees on landing is that choice.",
  "plain": "Rearrange the theorem: $F_{\\mathrm{avg}} = \\Delta p / \\Delta t$. The change in momentum sits on top; the duration sits underneath. If you are stuck with the same change in momentum, stretching the time makes the average force smaller in exact proportion.\n\nThis is the practical payoff of the entire unit. An airbag does not reduce how much momentum the driver must lose — the driver arrives at 15 m/s and ends at rest either way, so $\\Delta p$ is fixed. What the airbag does is take longer over it. Five times the stopping time is one fifth the average force.\n\nCrumple zones do the same by collapsing rather than resisting: a car that deforms takes longer to stop than one that does not. So does a running track with give in it, a helmet's foam liner, and bending your knees when you land. All of them buy time.",
  "depth": "With the before and after velocities fixed, $\\Delta \\vec{p}$ is fixed, so $\\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{avg}} \\propto 1/\\Delta t$. On a force-time graph the area is the same in both cases and only the shape changes: without protection the pulse is tall and narrow, with protection it is short and wide. Equal areas, very different heights. That picture is the clearest way to hold the idea, and it is exactly the graphical statement from [[mom-impulse]].\n\nTwo refinements matter. First, $F_{\\mathrm{avg}}$ is an average, and injury tracks the **peak**. A protective device that spreads the force smoothly over the interval keeps the peak near the average; one that bottoms out and then slams delivers a spike even if the average is modest. Second, stretching the stopping time usually means stretching the stopping distance too, which is why crumple zones must be long enough to crumple.\n\nNone of this is a separate equation. There is nothing extra to memorise beyond $\\vec{J} = \\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{avg}}\\Delta t = \\Delta\\vec{p}$, which is on the provided equation sheet.",
  "formula": "$$F_{\\mathrm{avg}} = \\frac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t} \\quad \\text{with } \\Delta p \\text{ fixed}$$\n\n$$F_{\\mathrm{avg}} \\propto \\frac{1}{\\Delta t}$$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A 70 kg driver travelling at 15 m/s is brought to rest in a crash. Against a rigid steering column the stop takes 0.10 s. With an airbag it takes 0.50 s. Find the change in momentum and the average force in each case, and state what the airbag has and has not changed.",
    "given": "$m = 70$ kg; $v_0 = 15$ m/s; $v = 0$; $\\Delta t_1 = 0.10$ s; $\\Delta t_2 = 0.50$ s; $g = 9.8$ m/s².",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "$\\Delta p = m(0 - 15) = -1050$ kg·m/s, the same in both cases.",
        "why": "The before and after velocities are what set $\\Delta p$, and the airbag changes neither. Establishing this first is what makes the rest of the problem a one-line comparison instead of two separate problems."
      },
      {
        "do": "Rigid: $F_{\\mathrm{avg}} = 1050 / 0.10 = 1.05 \\times 10^{4}$ N.",
        "why": "With the impulse fixed, the only remaining input is the duration. The sign simply says the force opposes the motion; magnitudes are what get compared."
      },
      {
        "do": "Airbag: $F_{\\mathrm{avg}} = 1050 / 0.50 = 2.1 \\times 10^{3}$ N.",
        "why": "Same numerator, five times the denominator. The factor of five is visible before any arithmetic, which is the point the question is really testing."
      },
      {
        "do": "State the conclusion: the impulse is unchanged at 1050 N·s; the average force falls by a factor of 5.",
        "why": "Questions on this topic almost always ask what the device changed. Naming the quantity that stayed fixed is usually worth as much as the number that moved."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$\\Delta p = 1050$ kg·m/s in magnitude in both cases. Average force $1.05 \\times 10^{4}$ N without the airbag, $2.1 \\times 10^{3}$ N with it — a fivefold reduction. The airbag changes the force, not the momentum change.",
    "check": "Compare with body weight, which is an independent yardstick: $mg = 70 \\times 9.8 = 686$ N. The rigid stop is about 15 times body weight, the airbag stop about 3 times. So the rigid stop is the dangerous one at roughly fifteen times body weight and the airbag stop the survivable one at roughly three \u2014 which is the entire point of lengthening the collision. The ratio of the two forces, $10500/2100 = 5$, matches the ratio of the times, $0.50/0.10 = 5$, exactly as the inverse proportionality demands. Had the arithmetic slipped, that ratio would not have come out at 5."
  },
  "trap": "Saying the airbag 'reduces the impulse' or 'reduces the change in momentum'. It does not, given the same initial and final velocities: those are fixed by how fast the driver was going and the fact that they end at rest. Only the time — and therefore the force — changes. Write $\\Delta p$ down first and note that it is common to both cases; that single line prevents the error."
},
{
  "id": "mom-collisions-explosions",
  "group": "u4",
  "prereqs": [
    "mom-impulse-momentum-theorem"
  ],
  "source": "CED 4.1",
  "title": "Collisions and explosions as models",
  "analogy": "A photograph taken just before two cars meet and another taken just after tell you almost everything you need. What happened in between — the crumpling, the noise, the glass — is a mess you do not have to model, provided the mess was violent enough and brief enough that the outside world had no time to interfere.",
  "plain": "A collision is a **model**, not just an event. You are allowed to call an interaction a collision when the forces the objects exert on each other are much larger than any net external force acting on them during the interaction. That condition is what licenses everything else.\n\nBecause the internal forces dominate for a very short time, only the states immediately before and immediately after matter. You do not track the deformation, so you may treat each object as a simple object with a mass and a velocity — no shape, no internal structure.\n\nAn explosion is the same model run the other way: forces internal to the system push its parts apart. A compressed spring released between two carts, a firework, a person jumping off a stationary raft. The bookkeeping is identical, because in both cases the forces doing the work are internal to the system you chose.",
  "depth": "The criterion deserves to be taken quantitatively, because it is what turns [[mom-conservation]] from an assumption into a justified approximation. During a collision, gravity and friction have not stopped acting. They simply have very little time in which to deliver impulse, and impulse is force times time ([[mom-impulse]]). If the interaction force is a hundred times larger and acts for the same brief interval, the external impulse is about one percent of the internal one, and treating the total momentum as constant across the collision introduces an error of that size.\n\nThis also explains a common piece of exam wording. 'Immediately before' and 'immediately after' are not decoration: they restrict the interval to the one in which the external impulse is negligible. A cart that collides and then rolls for three seconds against friction has certainly lost momentum in those three seconds — but not during the collision.\n\nFor an explosion the same logic applies, and the usual starting condition makes it especially clean: if the system was at rest before, its total momentum was zero, so the pieces must fly apart with momenta that sum to zero. Two pieces means equal and opposite momenta — equal in magnitude, not equal in speed, unless the masses happen to match.",
  "formula": "$$\\left|\\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{internal}}\\right| \\gg \\left|\\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{net, external}}\\right| \\quad \\text{during the interaction}$$\n\n$$\\text{explosion from rest:} \\quad \\sum \\vec{p}_{\\mathrm{after}} = 0$$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "Two carts collide on a level track. Sensors show that the 0.50 kg cart's velocity changes by 2.4 m/s during a contact time of 0.0050 s. The track exerts a total friction force of 0.30 N on the pair of carts. Show quantitatively whether this interaction may be modelled as a collision.",
    "given": "$m = 0.50$ kg; $\\left|\\Delta v\\right| = 2.4$ m/s; $\\Delta t = 0.0050$ s; external friction on the system $= 0.30$ N.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Internal impulse on the 0.50 kg cart: $J_{\\mathrm{int}} = m\\left|\\Delta v\\right| = 0.50 \\times 2.4 = 1.2$ N·s.",
        "why": "The impulse the other cart delivered is exactly this cart's change in momentum, by the impulse-momentum theorem. This is measurable from the velocities alone, without knowing the contact force."
      },
      {
        "do": "Average internal force: $F_{\\mathrm{int}} = 1.2 / 0.0050 = 240$ N.",
        "why": "Converting to a force lets the comparison be made against the external force directly, in the same units, which is how the CED states the criterion."
      },
      {
        "do": "External impulse over the same interval: $J_{\\mathrm{ext}} = 0.30 \\times 0.0050 = 1.5 \\times 10^{-3}$ N·s.",
        "why": "The right comparison is over the collision interval, not over the whole experiment. Friction acting for a long time afterwards is a separate question and does not bear on whether the collision itself conserved momentum."
      },
      {
        "do": "Take the ratio: $1.5 \\times 10^{-3} / 1.2 \\approx 1.3 \\times 10^{-3}$, about 0.13 per cent.",
        "why": "A ratio, not a difference, is what the criterion asks for. It also tells you the size of the error you accept by assuming momentum is conserved — here, far below any realistic measurement precision."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "The internal force is 240 N against an external 0.30 N, and the external impulse is about 0.13 per cent of the internal one. The interaction is well modelled as a collision, and total momentum may be treated as constant across it.",
    "check": "An independent route to the same conclusion: compare forces rather than impulses. $240 / 0.30 = 800$, and since both forces act over the identical 0.0050 s, the impulse ratio must also be 800 — matching $1/(1.3 \\times 10^{-3}) \\approx 770$ to rounding. The two routes agreeing means the contact time did not get used twice or dropped. Note also that even if the contact time were a hundred times longer, the ratio would be unchanged, because both impulses scale with the same $\\Delta t$ — the criterion is about the forces."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "The definition is written as a comparison of forces rather than an absolute threshold because there is no universal cut-off. A 0.30 N friction force is negligible next to a cart collision and enormous next to two dust grains drifting together. What makes something a collision is the ratio, and the ratio is a property of the situation, not of the numbers alone.",
  "trap": "Reading 'no net external force' as 'no external forces exist'. Gravity acts on every colliding object on Earth and does not switch off. The claim is only that its **impulse over the collision interval** is negligible compared with the internal one. State it that way in a justification question; a bare 'there are no external forces' is false and is usually marked as such."
},
{
  "id": "mom-conservation",
  "group": "u4",
  "prereqs": [
    "mom-collisions-explosions",
    "dyn-third-law"
  ],
  "source": "CED 4.3",
  "title": "Conservation of linear momentum",
  "analogy": "Imagine a sealed room where people can pass a heavy medicine ball to each other on frictionless floor. Every throw pushes the thrower backwards exactly as much as it pushes the ball forwards. However long they play, the total momentum of everyone and everything in the room is what it was when the doors closed. Only something reaching in from outside could change it.",
  "plain": "If the net external force on a system is zero, the system's total momentum does not change. Total momentum before equals total momentum after — as vectors, summed over every part of the system.\n\nThe reason is one line long. Change in momentum equals impulse ([[mom-impulse-momentum-theorem]]). No net external force means no external impulse. No external impulse means no change. Internal forces cannot do it, because they come in Newton's-third-law pairs: equal, opposite, and acting for exactly the same length of time, so the two impulses cancel exactly.\n\nNote carefully what is **not** required. Nothing here mentions kinetic energy, or whether the objects bounce or stick, or how messy the interaction was. Momentum is conserved in a collision that turns half the kinetic energy into heat and noise, because heat and noise are not momentum and cannot carry it away. That is why momentum, not energy, is the tool for collisions.\n\nIn two dimensions the statement holds in each direction separately. The east-west momenta must balance among themselves and the north-south momenta among themselves.",
  "depth": "The conservation statement itself is **not printed on the equation sheet**. What the sheet gives you is $\\vec{J} = \\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{avg}}\\Delta t = \\Delta\\vec{p}$, from which conservation follows in one step, and the centre-of-mass velocity\n\n$$\\vec{v}_{\\mathrm{cm}} = \\frac{\\sum \\vec{p}_i}{\\sum m_i} = \\frac{\\sum m_i \\vec{v}_i}{\\sum m_i}$$\n\nwhich is the same fact in another dress: a collection of objects can be described as one system with one centre-of-mass velocity, and that velocity is constant when there is no net external force. If you are asked what a collision does to the centre of mass of the system, the answer is nothing.\n\nWorking form, per direction:\n\n$$\\sum m_i v_{ix,\\mathrm{before}} = \\sum m_i v_{ix,\\mathrm{after}}$$\n\nThe CED's boundary statement sets the level of the two-dimensional work precisely: one dimension is treated both qualitatively and quantitatively, two dimensions **semiquantitatively**. Exam questions will not require you to solve simultaneous equations, but they may ask you to set the equations up correctly and to reason about how changing a mass, a speed or an angle would change the other quantities. So practise writing the two component equations and reading dependencies off them, rather than drilling algebra you will not be asked for.\n\nIf the total momentum of a system does change, that change is the impulse the surroundings delivered — the two statements are not alternatives, they are the same equation with the right-hand side zero or not.",
  "formula": "$$\\sum \\vec{p}_{\\mathrm{before}} = \\sum \\vec{p}_{\\mathrm{after}} \\quad \\text{when } \\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{net, external}} = 0$$\n\n$$\\sum m_i v_{ix} \\text{ conserved, and } \\sum m_i v_{iy} \\text{ conserved, separately}$$\n\n$$\\vec{v}_{\\mathrm{cm}} = \\frac{\\sum m_i \\vec{v}_i}{\\sum m_i} = \\text{constant}$$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "Two pucks slide on frictionless ice. Puck A has mass 2.0 kg and moves due east at 3.0 m/s. Puck B has mass 3.0 kg and moves due north at 2.0 m/s. They collide and stick together. Find the velocity of the combined pucks immediately after the collision, and state whether kinetic energy was conserved.",
    "given": "$m_A = 2.0$ kg east at 3.0 m/s; $m_B = 3.0$ kg north at 2.0 m/s; they stick. East is $+x$, north is $+y$.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Choose the system as both pucks, and justify: the ice is frictionless and the vertical forces balance, so there is no net external horizontal force.",
        "why": "Conservation is a statement about a system, and the system has to be named before the equation means anything. Naming it also forces you to check the condition rather than assume it."
      },
      {
        "do": "Total momentum before, by component: $p_x = (2.0)(3.0) = 6.0$ kg·m/s and $p_y = (3.0)(2.0) = 6.0$ kg·m/s.",
        "why": "Puck A contributes nothing to $y$ and puck B nothing to $x$, because momentum points along velocity. Splitting into components here is what makes the two-dimensional problem into two one-dimensional problems."
      },
      {
        "do": "After sticking, one object of mass 5.0 kg. Apply conservation in each direction: $5.0 v_x = 6.0$ so $v_x = 1.2$ m/s; $5.0 v_y = 6.0$ so $v_y = 1.2$ m/s.",
        "why": "The $x$ and $y$ equations are independent, and each has a single unknown — which is why this two-dimensional problem needs no simultaneous equations, in line with the CED's semiquantitative treatment."
      },
      {
        "do": "Combine: speed $= \\sqrt{1.2^2 + 1.2^2} = 1.2\\sqrt{2} \\approx 1.7$ m/s, at $\\tan^{-1}(1.2/1.2) = 45$ degrees north of east.",
        "why": "The components were the physics; assembling them into a magnitude and direction is presentation. Doing it last means a Pythagoras slip cannot corrupt the conservation step."
      },
      {
        "do": "Kinetic energy: before, $\\frac{1}{2}(2.0)(3.0)^2 + \\frac{1}{2}(3.0)(2.0)^2 = 9.0 + 6.0 = 15$ J. After, $\\frac{1}{2}(5.0)(1.2\\sqrt{2})^2 = \\frac{1}{2}(5.0)(2.88) = 7.2$ J.",
        "why": "Kinetic energy has to be computed separately, never assumed. It is a scalar, so no components are involved — and its value was not needed to get the velocity, which is the point about which law does which job."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "About 1.7 m/s at 45 degrees north of east. Kinetic energy fell from 15 J to 7.2 J, so it was not conserved: this is an inelastic collision, and in fact a perfectly inelastic one ([[mom-perfectly-inelastic]]).",
    "check": "The 45-degree result is confirmed by symmetry without arithmetic: the two momentum components were equal before the collision, so they are equal after, so the direction must be exactly halfway between east and north. Second, the kinetic-energy result is a check on the whole solution rather than a restatement of it — a perfectly inelastic collision must **lose** kinetic energy, never gain it. If the final kinetic energy had come out above 15 J, the velocity would have been wrong. Third, the final speed of 1.7 m/s lies below both initial speeds, as it must when momenta that are not parallel are combined."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Momentum conservation is stated with the condition 'no net external force on the system' rather than 'no external forces' because the condition that actually matters is the sum. A puck on ice has gravity pulling down and the normal force pushing up; both are external, and both are large, but they cancel, so horizontal momentum is conserved regardless.",
  "trap": "Applying conservation to a system with a genuine net external force, most often by forgetting that a wall, the floor, or a hand is outside the system you chose. If a ball bounces off a wall, the ball alone does not conserve momentum — see [[mom-system-choice]]. The second version of the trap is adding momentum magnitudes across directions: in the worked example, writing $6.0 + 6.0 = 12$ kg·m/s for the total gives a final speed of 2.4 m/s, which is wrong by a factor of $\\sqrt{2}$."
},
{
  "id": "mom-system-choice",
  "group": "u4",
  "prereqs": [
    "mom-conservation"
  ],
  "source": "CED 4.3",
  "title": "Choosing the system",
  "analogy": "Draw a chalk circle on the floor. Anything inside is the system; anything outside is the surroundings. Forces that cross the line can change what is inside; forces entirely within the circle cannot. Where you draw the circle is your decision, and drawing it well is most of the skill.",
  "plain": "Momentum is conserved for a system with no net external force. You are the one who decides what the system is, and different choices give different — but equally correct — answers to 'is momentum conserved here?'\n\nTake a ball bouncing off a wall. If the system is the ball alone, its momentum clearly changes: it was going one way and now goes the other. The wall pushed it, and the wall is outside the system, so that push is an external force delivering an external impulse.\n\nNow enlarge the system to include the wall and whatever the wall is fixed to. The same push is now internal — it is one half of a third-law pair that lives entirely inside the chalk circle — and the total momentum of the enlarged system is constant. The ball's momentum change is exactly balanced by the wall's.\n\nThe trick, whenever you are asked to use conservation, is to draw the circle so that the force you would rather not deal with ends up inside it.",
  "depth": "Three statements from the CED do the work here, and they are worth separating because they sound similar. Momentum is conserved in all interactions. If the net external force on the system you selected is zero, that system's total momentum is constant. If that net force is not zero, momentum crosses the boundary you drew — flowing in from the surroundings, or leaking out into them. So conservation is universal at the level of the interaction; what varies with your choice of system is whether momentum stays put or flows across the boundary.\n\nA subsystem gaining momentum is never a violation. It is a transfer, and the transfer has a size: the impulse, $\\vec{J} = \\Delta\\vec{p}$.\n\nThe practical rule: include everything that exerts a force you cannot otherwise account for. A person jumping off a raft — include both. A rifle and its bullet — include both, and the recoil is not mysterious, it is the other half of zero total momentum. A cart pushed by a hand — the hand is external and momentum is not conserved for the cart, so use forces or energy instead.\n\nThere is a cost to enlarging the system, and it is worth knowing: the extra object is usually so massive that its velocity change is unmeasurable. That does not make the momentum it received small. Momentum is $m\\vec{v}$, and a huge $m$ with a tiny $\\vec{v}$ can be a perfectly ordinary amount of momentum — which is exactly how the books balance.",
  "formula": "$$\\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{net, external}} = 0 \\;\\to\\; \\sum \\vec{p} \\text{ constant}$$\n\n$$\\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{net, external}} \\ne 0 \\;\\to\\; \\Delta \\sum \\vec{p} = \\vec{J}_{\\mathrm{external}}$$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A 0.20 kg ball is thrown horizontally at 8.0 m/s at a wall and rebounds along the same line at 6.0 m/s. The wall is bolted to a $1.0 \\times 10^{4}$ kg concrete block that rests on frictionless rollers and is initially at rest. (a) Find the ball's change in momentum. (b) Taking ball and block as one system, find the block's velocity after the bounce.",
    "given": "$m_{\\mathrm{ball}} = 0.20$ kg; $v_0 = 8.0$ m/s towards the wall; $v = 6.0$ m/s away; $M_{\\mathrm{block}} = 1.0 \\times 10^{4}$ kg, initially at rest.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Take $+x$ as the ball's initial direction. Ball: $v_0 = +8.0$ m/s, $v = -6.0$ m/s.",
        "why": "One axis for the whole problem, including the block. Mixing axes between parts (a) and (b) is the standard way this problem goes wrong."
      },
      {
        "do": "(a) $\\Delta p_{\\mathrm{ball}} = 0.20(-6.0) - 0.20(+8.0) = -1.2 - 1.6 = -2.8$ kg·m/s.",
        "why": "Final minus initial, as vectors. With the ball alone as the system this is nonzero, and that is the correct answer, not a contradiction: the wall is external to that system."
      },
      {
        "do": "(b) Enlarge the system to ball plus block. The wall's push on the ball and the ball's push on the wall are now both internal, so total momentum is constant.",
        "why": "This is the whole move. Redrawing the boundary converts an external force into an internal third-law pair, and the pair's impulses cancel."
      },
      {
        "do": "Conservation: $p_{\\mathrm{total}} = 0.20(8.0) + 0 = 1.6$ kg·m/s before. After: $-1.2 + (1.0 \\times 10^{4}) v_{\\mathrm{block}} = 1.6$, so $v_{\\mathrm{block}} = 2.8 / (1.0 \\times 10^{4}) = 2.8 \\times 10^{-4}$ m/s.",
        "why": "The block's momentum change must be $+2.8$ kg·m/s — the exact opposite of the ball's, which part (a) already gave us. Reusing that result is faster and less error-prone than starting again."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "(a) $2.8$ kg·m/s in magnitude, directed away from the wall. (b) The block moves at $2.8 \\times 10^{-4}$ m/s away from the ball's original position — 0.28 mm per second, far too slow to notice.",
    "check": "Add the final momenta independently and see whether they reproduce the initial total: ball $-1.2$ plus block $+2.8$ gives $+1.6$ kg·m/s, which is exactly the initial total. That check uses the final numbers rather than the equation they came from, so an arithmetic slip in the last step would break it. A second, qualitative check: the block must move **away** from the ball's approach direction, since the ball pushed it that way, and the sign came out positive as required."
  },
  "rejectedAlternative": "A student who concludes 'momentum is not conserved when a ball bounces off a wall' has said something true of one system and false as a general claim. The honest version always names the system: 'momentum is not conserved for the ball alone, because the wall exerts an external force on it; it is conserved for the ball together with the wall and what the wall is attached to.' On a justification question the naming is the mark.",
  "trap": "Assuming that because a massive object's velocity change is negligible, its momentum change is negligible too. In the worked example the block picks up the full 2.8 kg·m/s that the ball lost, at a speed of 0.28 mm/s. Treating that momentum as zero would mean momentum had simply vanished. Very large mass makes the **velocity** small, not the momentum."
},
{
  "id": "mom-elastic-inelastic",
  "group": "u4",
  "prereqs": [
    "mom-conservation",
    "enr-kinetic-energy"
  ],
  "source": "CED 4.4",
  "title": "Elastic and inelastic collisions",
  "analogy": "Two superballs meeting and springing apart give back everything they took: for a moment the motion is stored in their squashed rubber and then handed back. Two lumps of putty meeting do not; the squashing stays squashed, and the motion that went into it comes out as warmth and a dull noise. Momentum, meanwhile, is unaffected by which pair you used.",
  "plain": "Every collision with no net external force conserves momentum. Only some conserve kinetic energy. The ones that do are called **elastic**; the ones that do not are **inelastic**.\n\nIn an elastic collision the system's total kinetic energy after equals its total kinetic energy before. That does not mean each object keeps its own kinetic energy — a moving ball can hand nearly all of its kinetic energy to a stationary one. It is the **total** for the system that is unchanged.\n\nIn an inelastic collision the total kinetic energy goes down. The missing kinetic energy has not been destroyed. Non-conservative forces during the collision turned it into other forms: heat in the deformed metal, sound, permanent bending. Total energy is conserved; kinetic energy was not.\n\nThat distinction is the single most confused point in the unit, so state it in the reverse direction too: 'energy is conserved, therefore the collision is elastic' is invalid reasoning. Total energy is conserved in every collision, elastic or not. Elastic is a claim about *kinetic* energy specifically.",
  "depth": "Kinetic energy is $K = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$, **on the provided equation sheet**. For a system, sum it over the parts. The two tests you run on a collision are therefore different in kind:\n\n$$\\sum m_i \\vec{v}_i \\text{ before} = \\sum m_i \\vec{v}_i \\text{ after} \\quad \\text{(momentum: vector, always, if no net external force)}$$\n\n$$\\sum \\frac{1}{2} m_i v_i^2 \\text{ before} = \\sum \\frac{1}{2} m_i v_i^2 \\text{ after} \\quad \\text{(kinetic energy: scalar, only if elastic)}$$\n\nMomentum is a vector and directions matter; kinetic energy is a scalar built from $v^2$ and is never negative, so a reversal of direction costs momentum everything and kinetic energy nothing. That asymmetry is the structural reason the two behave differently.\n\nWhy can momentum survive what kinetic energy does not? Because the internal forces are third-law pairs acting for equal times, so their impulses cancel exactly whatever they do to the material. Work, by contrast, is force times **displacement**, and the two members of a third-law pair generally move through different displacements, so their works do not cancel. The difference is where the lost kinetic energy goes.\n\nA useful derived shortcut in one dimension: for a perfectly elastic collision, the relative speed of approach equals the relative speed of separation. This follows from the two conservation equations together; it is **not** on the equation sheet and is not stated in the CED, so use it to check answers rather than as a quotable law.\n\nNote also that 'inelastic' is a broad category. Any loss of kinetic energy makes a collision inelastic. Sticking together is the extreme case — see [[mom-perfectly-inelastic]] — not the definition.",
  "formula": "$$K = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$$\n\n$$\\text{elastic:} \\quad \\sum K_{\\mathrm{before}} = \\sum K_{\\mathrm{after}}$$\n\n$$\\text{inelastic:} \\quad \\sum K_{\\mathrm{after}} < \\sum K_{\\mathrm{before}}$$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "On a frictionless track, a 1.0 kg glider moving at 4.0 m/s strikes a stationary 3.0 kg glider. After the collision the 1.0 kg glider moves at 2.0 m/s back the way it came, and the 3.0 kg glider moves at 2.0 m/s forwards. Verify that momentum is conserved and classify the collision.",
    "given": "$m_1 = 1.0$ kg, $v_{1,0} = +4.0$ m/s; $m_2 = 3.0$ kg, $v_{2,0} = 0$; afterwards $v_1 = -2.0$ m/s, $v_2 = +2.0$ m/s.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Momentum before: $p = (1.0)(4.0) + (3.0)(0) = 4.0$ kg·m/s.",
        "why": "The stationary glider contributes nothing, but writing its zero explicitly keeps the system definition honest: it is in the system, it just has no momentum yet."
      },
      {
        "do": "Momentum after: $p = (1.0)(-2.0) + (3.0)(2.0) = -2.0 + 6.0 = 4.0$ kg·m/s. Conserved.",
        "why": "The minus sign is doing real work: the first glider now travels backwards, so its momentum subtracts. Using speeds here would give $2.0 + 6.0 = 8.0$ and a false verdict."
      },
      {
        "do": "Kinetic energy before: $\\frac{1}{2}(1.0)(4.0)^2 = 8.0$ J.",
        "why": "Kinetic energy is a scalar, so the direction of travel is irrelevant and no signs appear. Computing it separately, rather than inferring it from the momentum result, is the whole point of the classification."
      },
      {
        "do": "Kinetic energy after: $\\frac{1}{2}(1.0)(2.0)^2 + \\frac{1}{2}(3.0)(2.0)^2 = 2.0 + 6.0 = 8.0$ J. Equal, so the collision is elastic.",
        "why": "Equality of total kinetic energy is the definition of elastic. Note that the individual energies changed — the first glider went from 8.0 J to 2.0 J — which the definition permits."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "Momentum is conserved at 4.0 kg·m/s and total kinetic energy is unchanged at 8.0 J, so the collision is elastic.",
    "check": "Use the derived relative-speed relation as an independent test. Before the collision the gliders approach at $4.0 - 0 = 4.0$ m/s. Afterwards they separate at $2.0 - (-2.0) = 4.0$ m/s. For a perfectly elastic one-dimensional collision those two must be equal, and they are — a result reached without recomputing either energy, so it would catch a squaring error. A second check: the lighter glider bouncing backwards is what you expect when it strikes a heavier stationary object, so the signs are physically sensible."
  },
  "rejectedAlternative": "The seductive wrong move is to classify a collision by whether the objects bounced. Bouncing does not make a collision elastic; two cars that bounce apart after crumpling have lost most of their kinetic energy. The only test is arithmetic: add up $\\frac{1}{2}mv^2$ before and after and compare.",
  "trap": "'Total energy is always conserved, so every collision is elastic.' This inverts the reasoning. Conservation of total energy is a universal law and therefore tells you nothing about which collision you are looking at. Elastic means the **kinetic** energy of the system is the same before and after, which is a special and fairly rare condition. The second half of the same trap runs the other way: 'kinetic energy was lost, so momentum was lost too.' Momentum conservation depends only on the absence of a net external force, and has nothing to do with where the energy went."
},
{
  "id": "mom-perfectly-inelastic",
  "group": "u4",
  "prereqs": [
    "mom-elastic-inelastic"
  ],
  "source": "CED 4.4",
  "title": "Perfectly inelastic collisions",
  "analogy": "A dart thudding into a hanging bag of sand. There is one object afterwards, not two, and it moves off at whatever speed the momentum bookkeeping allows. All the argument about who goes how fast disappears, because they go the same speed by definition.",
  "plain": "In a perfectly inelastic collision the objects stick together and move off with a single common velocity. Coupling railway wagons, a dart in a block, a lump of clay hitting a cart and staying there.\n\nThis is the easiest case to compute, because 'they move together' removes an unknown. In one dimension you have one conservation equation and one unknown velocity, so it always solves directly:\n\n- add the momenta before,\n- add the masses,\n- divide.\n\nIt is also the case that loses the most kinetic energy of any collision with the same masses and initial velocities. That last result is **derived, not quoted**: the CED says only that the objects stick and share a velocity, so treat it as a check on your reasoning rather than as a law you can cite. Once the objects share a velocity there is no relative motion left between them, and the kinetic energy that lived in that relative motion is gone into heat and deformation. What survives is the kinetic energy of the system as a whole moving along.",
  "depth": "The setup, in one dimension, is\n\n$$m_1 v_1 + m_2 v_2 = (m_1 + m_2) v_f$$\n\n$$v_f = \\frac{m_1 v_1 + m_2 v_2}{m_1 + m_2}$$\n\nNeither line is on the equation sheet as such, but the right-hand side of the second is exactly the sheet's $\\vec{v}_{\\mathrm{cm}} = \\frac{\\sum m_i \\vec{v}_i}{\\sum m_i}$. That is not a coincidence and it is worth holding on to: the common final velocity of a perfectly inelastic collision **is** the centre-of-mass velocity of the system, which was constant throughout ([[mom-conservation]]). If you can find $\\vec{v}_{\\mathrm{cm}}$ before the collision, you already have the answer.\n\nFor the special case of one object initially at rest, $m_2 v_2 = 0$ and the algebra collapses to $v_f = v_1 \\, m_1 / (m_1 + m_2)$. The fraction of kinetic energy that survives is then $m_1/(m_1+m_2)$ — the same fraction — which is a fast way to see that a light projectile hitting a heavy target loses almost all of it.\n\nIn two dimensions nothing changes conceptually: apply the same equation in each direction, as in the worked example under [[mom-conservation]]. Because the objects share one final velocity there is still no need for simultaneous equations, which is why sticking collisions are the two-dimensional case AP Physics 1 can ask about quantitatively.",
  "formula": "$$m_1 \\vec{v}_1 + m_2 \\vec{v}_2 = (m_1 + m_2)\\vec{v}_f$$\n\n$$\\vec{v}_f = \\frac{m_1 \\vec{v}_1 + m_2 \\vec{v}_2}{m_1 + m_2} = \\vec{v}_{\\mathrm{cm}}$$\n\n$$\\text{target at rest:} \\quad \\frac{K_f}{K_0} = \\frac{m_1}{m_1 + m_2}$$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A 0.040 kg dart is fired horizontally at 25 m/s into a 0.960 kg cart at rest on a frictionless horizontal track. The dart embeds itself in the cart. Find the velocity of the cart-and-dart immediately afterwards, and the fraction of the original kinetic energy that remains.",
    "given": "$m_d = 0.040$ kg at $v_0 = 25$ m/s; $m_c = 0.960$ kg at rest; dart embeds.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "System: dart plus cart. The track is frictionless and horizontal, so there is no net external horizontal force during the impact.",
        "why": "The condition has to be checked before conservation is used. Here it is given by 'frictionless', and the vertical forces cancel, so the horizontal component of total momentum is constant."
      },
      {
        "do": "Momentum before: $p = (0.040)(25) + (0.960)(0) = 1.0$ kg·m/s.",
        "why": "Immediately before the impact only the dart is moving. The dart's flight before that, and anything after, are outside the interval the collision model covers."
      },
      {
        "do": "After, one object of mass $0.040 + 0.960 = 1.000$ kg moving at $v_f$: $1.000 v_f = 1.0$, so $v_f = 1.0$ m/s.",
        "why": "'Embeds itself' is the statement that the two share a velocity, which is what reduces two unknowns to one. Without it, one equation could not determine two final velocities."
      },
      {
        "do": "Kinetic energy before: $\\frac{1}{2}(0.040)(25)^2 = 12.5$ J. After: $\\frac{1}{2}(1.000)(1.0)^2 = 0.50$ J. Fraction remaining $= 0.50/12.5 = 0.040$, i.e. 4.0 per cent.",
        "why": "Kinetic energy must be computed from the velocities, never assumed conserved. The 96 per cent that vanished went into deforming the dart and cart and into heat — it is not missing, it is simply not kinetic."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "The cart and dart move off together at 1.0 m/s in the dart's original direction. Only 4.0 per cent of the original kinetic energy remains — 0.50 J of the original 12.5 J.",
    "check": "Two independent checks. First, bounding: the final speed must be greater than zero and less than 25 m/s, since the dart is slowed and the cart is speeded up until they match, and 1.0 m/s sits in that range. Roughly, the mass increased by a factor of 25, so the speed should fall by about a factor of 25, and $25/25 = 1$ — consistent. Second, the surviving-energy fraction should equal $m_d/(m_d + m_c) = 0.040/1.000 = 0.040$, which matches the 4.0 per cent computed from the energies directly. Those two routes share no arithmetic, so agreement means neither the velocity nor the squaring went wrong."
  },
  "trap": "Using conservation of kinetic energy to find the final velocity. In a perfectly inelastic collision kinetic energy is precisely what is **not** conserved, so that route gives a wrong answer that looks respectable. Momentum first, always; energy afterwards, and only to compute how much was lost. The related slip is dividing by only one of the masses at the end — the moving object after the collision has the **combined** mass."
},
{
  "id": "rot-angular-quantities",
  "group": "u5",
  "prereqs": [
    "kin-constant-a-equations"
  ],
  "source": "CED 5.1",
  "title": "Angular position, velocity, and acceleration",
  "analogy": "A clock's minute hand and its hour hand are on the same axle but different lengths. The tip of the minute hand travels much further in an hour than the tip of the hour hand does. If you insist on describing them with distance and speed, they look like two unrelated motions. If you describe them by the *angle* each has swept, the hour hand has turned through a twelfth of what the minute hand turned, and that single number describes the whole hand — tip, middle, and base.",
  "plain": "When something spins, every part of it moves, but the parts move different distances. A point near the rim of a wheel travels far; a point near the axle barely moves. That makes ordinary distance and speed awkward: there is no single distance the wheel travelled.\n\nThe angle is better. Every point on a rigid wheel sweeps through the **same angle** in the same time. So we describe rotation with three angular quantities. **Angular position** $\\theta$ says which way round the system is turned. **Angular displacement** $\\Delta\\theta$ is how much it turned. **Angular velocity** $\\omega$ is how fast the angle is changing. **Angular acceleration** $\\alpha$ is how fast the angular velocity is changing.\n\nAngles here are measured in **radians**, not degrees. One full turn is $2\\pi$ radians. Radians are not an arbitrary choice — they are what makes the link between angle and arc length come out as a clean multiplication, which is the subject of [[rot-linear-rotational-link]].\n\nRotation only has two directions to choose between, clockwise and counterclockwise, so a plus sign and a minus sign are enough to carry direction. You pick one direction to call positive at the start of the problem and stay with it.",
  "depth": "The CED defines angular displacement as the angle, in radians, that a point on a **rigid system** sweeps out as the system turns about one **named axis**. Both qualifiers matter. A rigid system holds its shape while different points on it move in different directions, which is exactly why it cannot be treated as a single point-like object. And the axis has to be named, because the same motion described about a different axis is a different rotation.\n\nThe definitions are rates, built the same way the translational ones are:\n\n$$\\Delta\\theta = \\theta - \\theta_0$$\n\n$$\\omega_{\\mathrm{avg}} = \\frac{\\Delta\\theta}{\\Delta t}$$\n\n$$\\alpha_{\\mathrm{avg}} = \\frac{\\Delta\\omega}{\\Delta t}$$\n\n**These three definitions are not printed on the provided equation sheet.** The sheet's right-hand column starts at $\\omega = \\omega_0 + \\alpha t$. You are expected to carry the definitions of average angular velocity and average angular acceleration in your head, exactly as you carry $v_{\\mathrm{avg}} = \\Delta x/\\Delta t$. What the sheet does give you is $\\alpha$ = angular acceleration, $\\theta$ = angle or angular position, $\\omega$ = angular speed in its variable key, so the symbols are at least confirmed for you.\n\nOn sign: the CED says one direction of rotation about an axis — clockwise or counterclockwise — is *typically* taken as mathematically positive and the other becomes negative. There is no universal rule that counterclockwise is positive. It is a choice you declare. What is not a choice is consistency: once $\\omega$ counterclockwise is positive, an $\\alpha$ that speeds up counterclockwise rotation is also positive, and one that slows it is negative.\n\nA negative $\\alpha$ therefore does **not** mean slowing down. It means the angular velocity is becoming more negative. A wheel spinning clockwise ($\\omega < 0$) with $\\alpha < 0$ is speeding up. The rule is the same one you learned for straight-line motion: speeding up when $\\omega$ and $\\alpha$ have the same sign, slowing when they have opposite signs.",
  "formula": "$$\\Delta\\theta = \\theta - \\theta_0$$\n\n$$\\omega_{\\mathrm{avg}} = \\frac{\\Delta\\theta}{\\Delta t}$$\n\n$$\\alpha_{\\mathrm{avg}} = \\frac{\\Delta\\omega}{\\Delta t}$$\n\n1 revolution $= 2\\pi$ radians",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A potter's wheel turns steadily through 5.0 complete revolutions in 4.0 s. The potter then presses a hand against the rim and the wheel comes uniformly to rest in a further 2.5 s. Taking the wheel's original direction as positive, find (a) the angular displacement of the first stage in radians, (b) the average angular velocity of the first stage, and (c) the average angular acceleration during the braking.",
    "given": "5.0 rev in 4.0 s, then to rest in 2.5 s. 1 rev $= 2\\pi$ rad.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Convert 5.0 revolutions to radians: $\\Delta\\theta = 5.0 \\times 2\\pi = 31.4$ rad.",
        "why": "Every angular formula in this course assumes radians. Revolutions and degrees are fine for stating a problem but will give wrong answers the moment you use $v = r\\omega$ or a kinematic equation, so convert first and never later."
      },
      {
        "do": "Average angular velocity of stage one: $\\omega_{\\mathrm{avg}} = \\Delta\\theta/\\Delta t = 31.4/4.0 = 7.9$ rad/s.",
        "why": "Use the definition, not a kinematic equation. The problem says the turning is steady, so there is no acceleration to account for and the definition alone is enough."
      },
      {
        "do": "Note that at the start of the braking the wheel is turning at $\\omega_0 = 7.9$ rad/s and at the end $\\omega = 0$.",
        "why": "The stages have to be joined at their shared instant. The final angular velocity of stage one is the initial angular velocity of stage two — that is the only piece of information carrying across."
      },
      {
        "do": "Average angular acceleration: $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{avg}} = \\Delta\\omega/\\Delta t = (0 - 7.85)/2.5 = -3.1$ rad/s$^2$.",
        "why": "The sign comes out of the subtraction, not from a decision about whether braking is negative. Because we declared the original spin positive, $\\Delta\\omega$ is negative and so is $\\alpha$."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "(a) $\\Delta\\theta = 31$ rad  (b) $\\omega_{\\mathrm{avg}} = 7.9$ rad/s  (c) $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{avg}} = -3.1$ rad/s$^2$",
    "check": "Check the sign against the physics rather than the algebra: $\\omega$ is positive and $\\alpha$ is negative, opposite signs, which must mean slowing — and the wheel is being braked. Check the size by scaling: 7.9 rad/s is a little over one revolution per second, and stopping that in 2.5 s should take roughly 3 rad/s of change per second. It does."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Radians rather than degrees, because a radian is defined as the angle for which arc length equals radius. That definition is what makes $s = r\\theta$ true with no conversion factor bolted on, and it is why $v = r\\omega$ and $a_T = r\\alpha$ are equally clean. In degrees, every one of those equations would need a factor of $\\pi/180$.",
  "trap": "Three ways students lose the mark here. **One:** leaving an angle in revolutions or degrees and feeding it into a formula. **Two:** reading a negative $\\alpha$ as 'slowing down'. It means 'angular velocity becoming more negative', which is speeding up if the spin is already negative. **Three:** treating $\\omega$ as belonging to a point rather than to the system. Every point on a rigid body shares one $\\omega$; only the linear speed differs from point to point."
},
{
  "id": "rot-kinematics-equations",
  "group": "u5",
  "prereqs": [
    "rot-angular-quantities",
    "kin-constant-a-equations"
  ],
  "source": "CED 5.1",
  "title": "The rotational kinematic equations are the translational ones",
  "analogy": "Two languages can have the same grammar and different words. If you already speak one, learning the second is a matter of swapping vocabulary, not of relearning how sentences are built. Rotational kinematics is translational kinematics with five words swapped.",
  "plain": "You already know three equations for motion in a straight line under constant acceleration. There are three matching equations for rotation under constant angular acceleration, and they are the same equations. Every $x$ became a $\\theta$, every $v$ became an $\\omega$, every $a$ became an $\\alpha$. Nothing else changed — not the structure, not the powers, not the halves.\n\nThis is not a memory trick someone invented to make the list shorter. It is a fact about the mathematics. The straight-line equations follow from the definitions $v = \\Delta x/\\Delta t$ and $a = \\Delta v/\\Delta t$ plus the assumption that $a$ is constant. The angular definitions have exactly that shape, so the same derivation runs again and produces the same three results with different letters on them.\n\nThe practical consequence is that you already know how to solve rotational kinematics problems. Same method: list what you have, list what you want, find the equation missing the quantity you neither have nor want.",
  "depth": "Written side by side, the two columns of the provided equation sheet make the correspondence visible. On the left:\n\n$$v_x = v_{x0} + a_x t$$\n\n$$x = x_0 + v_{x0} t + \\frac{1}{2} a_x t^2$$\n\n$$v_x^2 = v_{x0}^2 + 2 a_x \\left( x - x_0 \\right)$$\n\nOn the right:\n\n$$\\omega = \\omega_0 + \\alpha t$$\n\n$$\\theta = \\theta_0 + \\omega_0 t + \\frac{1}{2} \\alpha t^2$$\n\n$$\\omega^2 = \\omega_0^2 + 2 \\alpha \\left( \\theta - \\theta_0 \\right)$$\n\n**All six are on the provided equation sheet**, so you will not have to recall them. What you do have to recall is the condition under which they are legal, because the sheet does not print conditions. The CED states them for *constant* angular acceleration. If $\\alpha$ changes during the interval — a fan whose braking gets stronger as it slows, a motor ramping up — these equations are simply wrong, and no amount of careful arithmetic recovers the answer.\n\nWhen $\\alpha$ is not constant you are not stranded. The definitions still hold, so $\\omega_{\\mathrm{avg}} = \\Delta\\theta/\\Delta t$ is still true, and the area under an $\\omega$-versus-$t$ graph is still the angular displacement. That is the route [[rot-motion-graphs]] takes.\n\nOne further relation is worth carrying: for constant $\\alpha$ only, $\\omega_{\\mathrm{avg}} = (\\omega_0 + \\omega)/2$. It is not on the sheet. It follows from the first two sheet equations, and it is often the fastest way to a displacement, as well as being a genuinely independent check on an answer you got another way.",
  "formula": "$$\\omega = \\omega_0 + \\alpha t$$\n\n$$\\theta = \\theta_0 + \\omega_0 t + \\frac{1}{2} \\alpha t^2$$\n\n$$\\omega^2 = \\omega_0^2 + 2 \\alpha \\left( \\theta - \\theta_0 \\right)$$\n\nvalid only while $\\alpha$ is constant",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A ceiling fan is turning at 18 rad/s when it is switched off. It slows uniformly at 1.5 rad/s$^2$ until it stops. How long does it take to stop, and through how many revolutions does it turn while stopping?",
    "given": "$\\omega_0 = 18$ rad/s, $\\omega = 0$, $\\alpha = -1.5$ rad/s$^2$ (taking the spin direction positive).",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Assign the sign: the fan spins in the positive direction, it is slowing, so $\\alpha = -1.5$ rad/s$^2$.",
        "why": "Signs have to be fixed before any equation is used, because the equations do not know which way the fan is turning. Writing $\\alpha = +1.5$ here would give a negative time, which is the algebra telling you the sign was wrong."
      },
      {
        "do": "Use $\\omega = \\omega_0 + \\alpha t$: $0 = 18 + (-1.5)t$, so $t = 12$ s.",
        "why": "Choose this equation because it is the one that does not contain $\\theta$ — and $\\theta$ is a quantity we neither know nor were asked for in this part. Picking the equation by what it leaves out is faster than picking by what it contains."
      },
      {
        "do": "Use $\\omega^2 = \\omega_0^2 + 2\\alpha(\\theta - \\theta_0)$: $0 = 18^2 + 2(-1.5)\\Delta\\theta$, so $\\Delta\\theta = 324/3.0 = 108$ rad.",
        "why": "This route avoids reusing $t$. If the time had been computed wrongly, an equation that uses $t$ would inherit the error silently; this one would not."
      },
      {
        "do": "Convert: $108/(2\\pi) = 17$ revolutions.",
        "why": "The question asked for revolutions. Radians are the working unit; revolutions are the reporting unit, and the conversion is the last step, never an intermediate one."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "It takes 12 s and turns through 108 rad, which is about 17 revolutions.",
    "check": "Independent route to the same displacement: with constant $\\alpha$, $\\omega_{\\mathrm{avg}} = (18 + 0)/2 = 9.0$ rad/s, and $\\Delta\\theta = \\omega_{\\mathrm{avg}} t = 9.0 \\times 12 = 108$ rad. Two different equations, same number, and this one would have caught an arithmetic slip in the squaring."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "The analogy is worth learning as a structure rather than as a table, because it keeps paying out. Later it extends: force becomes torque, mass becomes rotational inertia, $F_{\\mathrm{net}} = ma$ becomes $\\tau_{\\mathrm{net}} = I\\alpha$, and in Unit 6 kinetic energy and momentum follow the same pattern. A student who has internalised the mapping gets most of two units for the price of one.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "It is tempting to convert every rotation problem into a linear one by multiplying through by $r$ and using the translational equations on the rim. That works for a single point on a single circle, and it fails as soon as the problem involves the whole body — because different points have different $r$, so there is no one linear displacement to solve for. The angular description exists precisely because it is the one description the whole rigid body shares.",
  "trap": "Using these three equations when $\\alpha$ is not constant. The exam will sometimes hand you a graph of $\\omega$ against $t$ that curves, or a description like 'the braking torque increases as it slows'. Both mean $\\alpha$ varies, and both make these equations invalid. Also watch the units on $\\Delta\\theta$: the equations return radians, and a question asking for revolutions is testing whether you noticed."
},
{
  "id": "rot-motion-graphs",
  "group": "u5",
  "prereqs": [
    "rot-angular-quantities",
    "rot-kinematics-equations"
  ],
  "source": "CED 5.1",
  "title": "Reading graphs of rotational motion",
  "plain": "A graph of angular velocity against time carries the whole story of a rotation, and you can get at it without any equation. The **slope** of the graph is the angular acceleration, because slope is change in $\\omega$ over change in $t$, which is the definition of $\\alpha$. The **area** between the graph and the time axis is the angular displacement, because area is $\\omega$ multiplied by $t$, which is the definition of $\\Delta\\theta$ rearranged.\n\nThe same reading applies one level up and one level down. On a graph of angular position against time, the slope is the angular velocity. On a graph of angular acceleration against time, the area is the change in angular velocity.\n\nThe reason to work this way, rather than always reaching for an equation, is that slopes and areas do not need the acceleration to be constant. A curved $\\omega$-versus-$t$ graph breaks the three kinematic equations but not the graph reading.",
  "depth": "The CED puts all three rotational quantities — angle, angular velocity, angular acceleration — on the time axis, and treats the resulting graphs as a way of reading off how the three relate to one another. It does not print slope-and-area rules as equations, and it does not need to: they follow directly from the definitions in [[rot-angular-quantities]]. Slope of $\\theta$-$t$ is $\\Delta\\theta/\\Delta t$, which *is* $\\omega_{\\mathrm{avg}}$. Area under $\\omega$-$t$ over an interval is $\\omega_{\\mathrm{avg}}\\Delta t$, which *is* $\\Delta\\theta$.\n\nAreas below the axis count as negative. A wheel that spins one way, stops, and spins back the other way traces an $\\omega$-$t$ graph that crosses zero; the negative area cancels part of the positive area, and the total is the net angular displacement — not the total angle turned through. If a question asks how far round it went in total, you add the magnitudes of the areas instead. This is the identical distinction to displacement versus distance in straight-line motion.\n\nBecause the graph gives $\\Delta\\theta$, not $\\theta$, you cannot read absolute angular position off an $\\omega$-$t$ graph. You need $\\theta_0$ from the problem statement.\n\nGraph work is disproportionately valuable on this exam: Practice 1.C is creating qualitative sketches of graphs, and the Translation Between Representations free-response question is 12 points built on moving between a described scenario, a derivation, and a graph.",
  "formula": "slope of $\\theta$ vs $t$ $= \\omega$\n\nslope of $\\omega$ vs $t$ $= \\alpha$\n\narea under $\\omega$ vs $t$ $= \\Delta\\theta$\n\narea under $\\alpha$ vs $t$ $= \\Delta\\omega$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A turntable starts from rest. Its angular velocity rises in a straight line from 0 to 8.0 rad/s over the first 4.0 s, then stays constant at 8.0 rad/s for the next 3.0 s. Using the graph rather than the kinematic equations, find the angular acceleration in each phase and the total angular displacement over the 7.0 s.",
    "given": "Phase 1: $\\omega$ from 0 to 8.0 rad/s in 4.0 s. Phase 2: $\\omega = 8.0$ rad/s for 3.0 s.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Phase 1 slope: $\\alpha = (8.0 - 0)/4.0 = 2.0$ rad/s$^2$.",
        "why": "Slope of an angular-velocity graph is angular acceleration by definition. The graph is a straight line, so the slope is the same everywhere in the phase and the average equals the instantaneous value."
      },
      {
        "do": "Phase 2 slope: the line is horizontal, so $\\alpha = 0$.",
        "why": "Constant angular velocity means no angular acceleration, which by the rotational form of Newton's first law also means the net torque is zero — see [[rot-equilibrium]]. The graph is telling you something about the forces, not only about the motion."
      },
      {
        "do": "Phase 1 area: a triangle, $\\frac{1}{2}(4.0)(8.0) = 16$ rad.",
        "why": "Area under the curve is angular displacement. Splitting the shape into a triangle and a rectangle is not a trick, it is just how you compute the area of this particular graph."
      },
      {
        "do": "Phase 2 area: a rectangle, $(3.0)(8.0) = 24$ rad. Total $\\Delta\\theta = 16 + 24 = 40$ rad.",
        "why": "The two phases are consecutive, so the displacements simply add. Nothing cancels because $\\omega$ never goes negative — the graph never drops below the axis."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$\\alpha = 2.0$ rad/s$^2$ in phase 1, $\\alpha = 0$ in phase 2, total $\\Delta\\theta = 40$ rad (about 6.4 revolutions).",
    "check": "Independent route on phase 1 only, using an equation instead of the graph: $\\theta = \\frac{1}{2}\\alpha t^2 = \\frac{1}{2}(2.0)(4.0)^2 = 16$ rad, matching the triangle. Also a sense check on phase 2: at a steady 8.0 rad/s for 3.0 s the turntable must turn 24 rad, and 24 rad is under 4 revolutions in 3 seconds, which is a believable record-player speed."
  },
  "trap": "Reading the *value* of the $\\omega$-$t$ graph when the question wants the *slope*, or the slope when it wants the area. A common version: 'at $t = 4$ s the angular velocity is largest, so the angular acceleration is largest there' — false, and often exactly backwards, since a curve frequently peaks where its slope is zero. Second trap: forgetting that area below the axis is negative, and reporting total angle turned when the question asked for net angular displacement, or the reverse."
},
{
  "id": "rot-linear-rotational-link",
  "group": "u5",
  "prereqs": [
    "rot-angular-quantities"
  ],
  "source": "CED 5.2",
  "title": "Connecting linear and rotational motion",
  "analogy": "On a marching band turning a corner, everyone pivots through the same angle at the same time, but the player on the outside of the turn has to run while the player on the inside almost marks time. One angular speed, many linear speeds, and the difference between them is just how far out you are standing.",
  "plain": "A rigid rotating body has one angular velocity, shared by every point on it. It does not have one linear velocity. A point twice as far from the axis sweeps a circle twice as big in the same time, so it moves twice as fast.\n\nThree equations connect the two descriptions, and all three have the same shape: the linear quantity equals the radius times the angular quantity. Arc length is radius times angle, $s = r\\theta$. Linear speed is radius times angular speed, $v = r\\omega$. The **tangential** acceleration — the part that changes speed — is radius times angular acceleration, $a_T = r\\alpha$.\n\nThe direction of $v$ here is tangential: along the circle, perpendicular to the line joining the point to the axis. A point on a spinning wheel is never moving toward or away from the axis, only around it.",
  "depth": "The CED states the arc relation as $\\Delta s = r\\Delta\\theta$ for a point at distance $r$ from a fixed axis, then gives the derived relations:\n\n$$s = r\\theta$$\n\n$$v = r\\omega$$\n\n$$a_T = r\\alpha$$\n\n**$v = r\\omega$ and $a_T = r\\alpha$ are both printed on the provided equation sheet**, in the right-hand column. $s = r\\theta$ is on the sheet too, but in an unexpected place: it appears in the geometry table on the constants page, listed under Circle alongside $A = \\pi r^2$ and $C = 2\\pi r$. Knowing that saves you rederiving it.\n\nThe CED also states, flatly, that for a rigid system all points have the same angular velocity and the same angular acceleration. That single sentence is what makes rotational description worth having. It also tells you which quantity to carry between parts of a problem: if two points on the same rigid body appear, transfer $\\omega$ between them, never $v$.\n\nAll three relations require radians. That is not a convention, it is the definition of the radian doing its work — see [[rot-angular-quantities]]. Feed degrees into $v = r\\omega$ and the answer is wrong by a factor of about 57.\n\nNote what $a_T = r\\alpha$ is and is not. It is only the tangential component of the acceleration of that point. A point on a rotating body also has a centripetal component, and that component is present even when $\\alpha = 0$. Those two components are the whole subject of [[rot-tangential-vs-centripetal]].",
  "formula": "$$s = r\\theta$$\n\n$$v = r\\omega$$\n\n$$a_T = r\\alpha$$\n\n$\\theta$ in radians; $r$ measured from the axis, perpendicular to it",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A grinding wheel of radius 0.15 m spins at 40 rad/s. (a) How fast is a point on the rim moving? (b) How fast is a point 0.050 m from the axis moving, and what is its angular velocity? (c) The motor then gives the wheel an angular acceleration of 12 rad/s$^2$. What is the tangential acceleration of a rim point?",
    "given": "$r_{\\mathrm{rim}} = 0.15$ m, $\\omega = 40$ rad/s, $r_{\\mathrm{inner}} = 0.050$ m, $\\alpha = 12$ rad/s$^2$.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Rim speed: $v = r\\omega = (0.15)(40) = 6.0$ m/s.",
        "why": "Use $v = r\\omega$ rather than working out a circumference and a period, because the angular speed is already given and this is one multiplication instead of three steps."
      },
      {
        "do": "Inner point: $v = (0.050)(40) = 2.0$ m/s, and $\\omega = 40$ rad/s, unchanged.",
        "why": "The wheel is rigid, so the angular velocity is a property of the whole wheel and is the same number for both points. Only $r$ changed, so only $v$ changed. This is the step where students most often wrongly carry $v$ across instead of $\\omega$."
      },
      {
        "do": "Tangential acceleration at the rim: $a_T = r\\alpha = (0.15)(12) = 1.8$ m/s$^2$.",
        "why": "$a_T$, not the total acceleration. The rim point is also accelerating toward the axis; this equation gives only the part along the direction of motion, the part that is changing its speed."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "(a) 6.0 m/s  (b) 2.0 m/s, with the same 40 rad/s  (c) $a_T = 1.8$ m/s$^2$",
    "check": "Independent route to the rim speed, avoiding $v = r\\omega$ entirely: at 40 rad/s the period of one revolution is $T = 2\\pi/40 = 0.157$ s, and the rim's circumference is $2\\pi(0.15) = 0.942$ m, so the rim speed is $0.942/0.157 = 6.0$ m/s. Same answer by a route that only uses geometry. And the ratio check: the inner point is at one third the radius and moves at one third the speed, as it must if they complete a turn together."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "The radius in these equations is measured perpendicular from the **axis of rotation**, not from any convenient corner of the object and not along the object's surface. That is because the circle a point traces has that perpendicular distance as its radius, and nothing else about the geometry matters to the motion.",
  "trap": "Carrying the wrong quantity between two points on the same rigid body. Angular velocity and angular acceleration are shared; linear speed and tangential acceleration are not. A question that gives you the speed of a belt on a small pulley and asks about a large pulley on the same shaft is testing exactly this — transfer $\\omega$ through the shaft, and transfer $v$ through the belt."
},
{
  "id": "rot-tangential-vs-centripetal",
  "group": "u5",
  "prereqs": [
    "rot-linear-rotational-link",
    "dyn-circular-motion"
  ],
  "source": "CED 5.2",
  "title": "Tangential and centripetal acceleration are different things",
  "analogy": "Driving round a roundabout, two separate things can happen to your motion. The steering wheel changes which way you are pointing. The pedals change how fast you are going. They are independent: you can steer without accelerating, accelerate without steering, or do both. Centripetal acceleration is the steering; tangential acceleration is the pedals.",
  "plain": "A point on a rotating body has an acceleration with two parts, and they point in perpendicular directions.\n\nThe **centripetal** part points inward, toward the axis. Its job is to bend the motion into a circle. It is present whenever the point is going round at all, even at perfectly constant speed. Its size is $a_c = v^2/r$.\n\nThe **tangential** part points along the direction of motion. Its job is to change the speed. It is present only when the rotation is speeding up or slowing down. Its size is $a_T = r\\alpha$.\n\nSo a wheel turning at a steady rate has centripetal acceleration everywhere and zero tangential acceleration. A wheel just starting from rest, at the very first instant, has tangential acceleration and zero centripetal acceleration, because its speed is still zero. Most of the time it has both.",
  "depth": "The two components are perpendicular, so when a problem wants the total acceleration of a point you combine them with Pythagoras:\n\n$$a = \\sqrt{a_T^2 + a_c^2}$$\n\nThat combination is not printed on the equation sheet, though $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$ is, in the geometry table. What is printed is $a_c = v^2/r$ in the left-hand column and $a_T = r\\alpha$ in the right-hand column. The fact that they sit in different columns of the sheet is a fair summary of how separately students should treat them.\n\nA useful rewriting: substituting $v = r\\omega$ into $a_c = v^2/r$ gives\n\n$$a_c = \\omega^2 r$$\n\nThis form is **derived, not printed**. It is often the more convenient one, because rotational problems usually hand you $\\omega$ rather than $v$. Note the different dependence on radius: $a_c$ grows with $r$ at fixed $\\omega$, so the rim of a wheel is under far more centripetal acceleration than the hub.\n\nThe two components also answer different questions about the forces, once you connect them through Newton's second law. The centripetal component demands a net inward force. The tangential component demands a net force along the direction of motion, which in a rotating rigid body traces back to a net torque. This is why the CED notes that describing a rotating rigid system fully may require doing the linear and rotational analyses separately.",
  "formula": "$$a_c = \\frac{v^2}{r} = \\omega^2 r$$\n\n$$a_T = r\\alpha$$\n\n$$a = \\sqrt{a_T^2 + a_c^2}$$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A disk of radius 0.15 m is speeding up with a constant angular acceleration of 12 rad/s$^2$. At the instant its angular velocity is 3.0 rad/s, find the tangential acceleration, the centripetal acceleration, and the total acceleration of a point on the rim.",
    "given": "$r = 0.15$ m, $\\alpha = 12$ rad/s$^2$, $\\omega = 3.0$ rad/s at the instant in question.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Tangential: $a_T = r\\alpha = (0.15)(12) = 1.8$ m/s$^2$.",
        "why": "This part depends on $\\alpha$ and not at all on how fast the disk is currently spinning. It would be 1.8 m/s$^2$ at every instant while $\\alpha$ stays 12 rad/s$^2$."
      },
      {
        "do": "Centripetal: $a_c = \\omega^2 r = (3.0)^2 (0.15) = 1.35$ m/s$^2$.",
        "why": "Use the $\\omega^2 r$ form rather than $v^2/r$ because $\\omega$ is what we were given; going via $v = r\\omega$ first would add a step and a chance to slip. This part depends on $\\omega$ and not on $\\alpha$, which is why the answer is only valid at this one instant."
      },
      {
        "do": "Combine perpendicular components: $a = \\sqrt{(1.8)^2 + (1.35)^2} = \\sqrt{3.24 + 1.8225} = 2.25$ m/s$^2$.",
        "why": "Pythagoras rather than addition, because the two components are at right angles — one along the motion, one across it. Adding them arithmetically would be the same error as adding perpendicular force components."
      },
      {
        "do": "Direction: $\\tan\\phi = a_c/a_T = 1.35/1.8 = 0.75$, so the total acceleration is 37 degrees from the tangential direction, tilted inward.",
        "why": "The exam's trig table gives a tangent of 3/4 at 37 degrees, so this angle is recognisable without a calculator. It also makes the point that the total acceleration of a point on a spinning-up wheel is neither along the motion nor toward the centre."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$a_T = 1.8$ m/s$^2$, $a_c = 1.35$ m/s$^2$, total $a = 2.25$ m/s$^2$ at 37 degrees inward from the tangent.",
    "check": "Limiting case: rewind to the instant the disk was released from rest, $\\omega = 0$. Then $a_c = 0$ and the total acceleration should equal $a_T = 1.8$ m/s$^2$, pointing purely along the motion — which is what the formula gives. Fast-forward instead to a high $\\omega$ and $a_c$ should swamp $a_T$, so the total should turn to point almost straight at the axis. Both limits behave, and the answer 2.25 m/s$^2$ correctly sits above the larger component and below their sum."
  },
  "trap": "Two specific errors. **One:** believing that constant angular velocity means zero acceleration. It means zero *tangential* acceleration; the centripetal acceleration is still there and is often large. **Two:** adding $a_T$ and $a_c$ arithmetically. They are perpendicular, so the total is the hypotenuse, never the sum. A third, subtler one: writing $a_c = \\alpha^2 r$. It is $\\omega^2 r$ — angular *velocity* squared, not angular acceleration."
},
{
  "id": "rot-torque",
  "group": "u5",
  "prereqs": [
    "rot-angular-quantities",
    "dyn-free-body"
  ],
  "source": "CED 5.3",
  "title": "Torque",
  "analogy": "A door is the whole of this concept. Push it near the handle and it swings easily. Push with the same force near the hinge and almost nothing happens. Push with the same force straight along the door, toward the hinge, and nothing happens at all. The force never changed; where and how you applied it did.",
  "plain": "Torque is how effectively a force turns something. It depends on three things: how big the force is, how far from the axis you apply it, and at what angle.\n\nOnly the part of the force that is perpendicular to the line from the axis to the application point does any turning. The part pointing straight along that line is wasted — it pulls the object against its axle instead of spinning it. That is the whole content of the $\\sin\\theta$ in the formula.\n\n$$\\tau = rF\\sin\\theta$$\n\nHere $r$ is the distance from the axis to where the force acts, $F$ is the size of the force, and $\\theta$ is the angle between the force and that line from the axis. Torque is measured in newton-metres.\n\nTwo consequences fall out immediately. A force applied *at* the axis produces no torque, because $r = 0$. A force pointing directly toward or away from the axis produces no torque, because $\\sin\\theta = 0$ at both 0 and 180 degrees. Neither of those forces is zero. Their torques are.",
  "depth": "The CED is precise about which part of a force turns things: only the component at right angles to the line running from the axis out to the application point contributes anything. Everything else about the force is irrelevant to the torque. There are two printed forms of the same equation, and the extract is explicit that they are both as printed and should not be silently merged. The course framework prints\n\n$$\\tau = rF_{\\perp} = rF\\sin\\theta$$\n\nand the provided equation sheet prints\n\n$$\\tau = r_{\\perp}F = rF\\sin\\theta$$\n\nThey agree numerically, because $rF\\sin\\theta$ can be grouped either as (full distance) $\\times$ (perpendicular part of the force) or as (perpendicular distance) $\\times$ (full force). The second grouping is the lever arm reading, which [[rot-lever-arm]] takes up. **The equation is on the sheet**, in the sheet's $r_{\\perp}F$ form, so you will be handed it — but the sheet will not tell you which angle $\\theta$ is, and that is where the marks are lost.\n\n$\\theta$ sits between two specific lines: the force arrow itself, and the line running from the axis out to the spot where that force is applied. It is not the angle to the horizontal, not the angle to the ground, and not the angle to some other convenient line in the figure unless those happen to coincide. When a problem hands you an angle, your first job is to work out which two lines it lies between.\n\nOn direction, the CED draws a firm boundary. You are expected to handle torque *magnitudes* with vector conventions, and you are not expected to treat the direction of a torque as a vector at all — that is placed outside the course. Rotational direction is described only as clockwise or counterclockwise about a stated axis. So you will assign each torque a plus or a minus according to which way it would turn the system, and you will never be asked for a torque vector pointing along the axle.\n\nThe CED introduces **force diagrams** for this work. They resemble free-body diagrams, but where a free-body diagram collapses the object to a point, a force diagram must show *where on the body* each force acts relative to the axis — because that location is half of what determines the torque.",
  "formula": "$$\\tau = r F_{\\perp} = r F \\sin\\theta$$   (course framework form)\n\n$$\\tau = r_{\\perp} F = r F \\sin\\theta$$   (equation sheet form)\n\n$\\theta$ = angle between the force and the line from axis to application point. Units: N$\\cdot$m",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A door is 0.80 m wide, hinged along one edge. A student pushes with a force of 25 N applied at the outer edge, directed at 53 degrees to the plane of the door. (a) Find the torque about the hinge. (b) Find the torque if the same force is applied perpendicular to the door at the same place. (c) Find the torque if the same force is applied at the outer edge but directed along the door, toward the hinge.",
    "given": "$r = 0.80$ m, $F = 25$ N, $\\theta = 53$ degrees. From the exam's trig table, $\\sin\\theta = 4/5$ at 53 degrees.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Identify the two lines the angle sits between: the line from the hinge to the point of application runs along the door, and the force is at 53 degrees to the door. So $\\theta = 53$ degrees is already the angle the formula wants.",
        "why": "This identification is the step that decides whether the answer is right. If the problem had given the angle to the door *frame* or to the floor, the number in $\\sin\\theta$ would be different, and no amount of correct arithmetic afterwards would save it."
      },
      {
        "do": "(a) $\\tau = rF\\sin\\theta = (0.80)(25)(0.8) = 16$ N$\\cdot$m.",
        "why": "Using the exam's own trig table value $\\sin\\theta = 4/5$ at 53 degrees rather than a calculator keeps the arithmetic exact and is faster."
      },
      {
        "do": "(b) Perpendicular means $\\theta = 90$ degrees and $\\sin\\theta = 1$, so $\\tau = (0.80)(25)(1) = 20$ N$\\cdot$m.",
        "why": "This is the maximum torque this force can produce at this distance. Computing it gives you a ceiling to test every other answer against."
      },
      {
        "do": "(c) Along the door means $\\theta = 0$ and $\\sin\\theta = 0$, so $\\tau = 0$.",
        "why": "Not because the force is small — it is the same 25 N — but because its line of action passes through the hinge. A force whose line passes through the axis has no turning effect on that axis at all."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "(a) 16 N$\\cdot$m  (b) 20 N$\\cdot$m  (c) 0",
    "check": "The perpendicular case must be the largest possible torque for a 25 N force at 0.80 m, since $\\sin\\theta$ can never exceed 1. Our angled answer, 16 N$\\cdot$m, is below that ceiling, and the ratio $16/20 = 0.8$ is exactly the sine of 53 degrees, as it has to be. Units check: N $\\times$ m gives N$\\cdot$m, which is torque and not energy, even though a joule is also a newton-metre — the difference is that in torque the force and the distance are perpendicular, and in work they are parallel."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Why the perpendicular component and not the whole force? Because the parallel component points straight at the axis, and the axle simply pushes back on it. It contributes nothing to turning, and it would be wrong to count it. The $\\sin\\theta$ is not a correction factor added for accuracy — it is the piece of the force that is actually doing the job, with the useless piece removed.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "A plausible-sounding rule is 'bigger force means bigger torque'. It is false as stated, and the exam tests it. A 200 N force applied 2 cm from the axis at a poor angle can produce less torque than a 20 N force applied 50 cm out and perpendicular. Torque is a product of three things, and a large value of one of them says nothing on its own.",
  "trap": "Forgetting the $\\sin\\theta$ entirely and writing $\\tau = rF$, which is only correct at 90 degrees. Almost as common: using the angle the diagram happens to label rather than the angle between the force and the position vector. And believing that a force acting at the axis, or pointing at the axis, must do *something* rotationally — it does not, and problems are built to reward the students who notice that a whole force can be discarded."
},
{
  "id": "rot-lever-arm",
  "group": "u5",
  "prereqs": [
    "rot-torque"
  ],
  "source": "CED 5.3",
  "title": "The lever arm — reading torque geometrically",
  "analogy": "Extend the line of the force in both directions, right through the object and out the other side, like a laser beam. Now measure the shortest distance from the axis to that beam. That distance — not the distance to your hand — is what decides the torque.",
  "plain": "There is a second way to read $\\tau = rF\\sin\\theta$, and for many problems it is the faster one.\n\nInstead of splitting the *force* into a useful part and a wasted part, split the *distance*. Draw the **line of action**: the infinite straight line along which the force acts. The **lever arm** is the perpendicular distance from the axis to that line. Then the torque is simply the lever arm times the full force:\n\n$$\\tau = r_{\\perp}F$$\n\nThe two readings give the same number, because $r\\sin\\theta$ is exactly the perpendicular distance from the axis to the line of action. Which one you use is a matter of what the diagram makes easy to see.\n\nThe lever-arm picture makes one thing obvious that the algebra hides: a force whose line of action passes through the axis has a lever arm of zero, so it has no torque, whatever its size. You can see that on a sketch without computing anything.",
  "depth": "The CED defines the lever arm geometrically: extend the force to an infinite line, then measure from the axis to that line along the shortest, perpendicular path. That length is the lever arm. The equation sheet prints torque in exactly this grouping, $\\tau = r_{\\perp}F$, while the course framework prints the other grouping, $\\tau = rF_{\\perp}$. Both appear in the extract as printed, and neither is more correct.\n\nGeometrically, the lever arm is one leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is the position vector of length $r$ and whose angle at the axis is $\\theta$. So $r_{\\perp} = r\\sin\\theta$, which is why the two forms agree. On a drawn figure you can often measure the lever arm directly and skip trigonometry entirely.\n\nThe lever-arm reading is what makes the design of tools intelligible. A long spanner is not stronger than a short one; it lets the same hand force act along a line further from the bolt. Slipping a pipe over the handle to lengthen it is a lever-arm intervention. A door handle is placed at the edge furthest from the hinges for the same reason.\n\nIt also gives you a quick qualitative sort. Faced with several forces on a diagram and asked which produces the largest torque, extend each line of action, eyeball which passes furthest from the axis, and weight that by the force size. You will usually get the ranking before anyone else has finished writing down $\\sin\\theta$.",
  "formula": "$$r_{\\perp} = r\\sin\\theta$$\n\n$$\\tau = r_{\\perp}F$$\n\nlever arm = perpendicular distance from the axis to the line of action",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A mechanic pulls with a force of 120 N on a spanner, at a point 0.25 m from the centre of the bolt. The force is directed at 30 degrees to the handle. (a) What is the lever arm? (b) What torque is applied to the bolt? (c) If the mechanic wants the same torque while pulling perpendicular to the handle at the same point, what force is needed?",
    "given": "$r = 0.25$ m, $F = 120$ N, angle to handle = 30 degrees. From the exam's trig table, $\\sin\\theta = 1/2$ at 30 degrees.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "(a) Lever arm: $r_{\\perp} = r\\sin\\theta = (0.25)(0.5) = 0.125$ m.",
        "why": "Compute the geometry before touching the force. The lever arm is a purely geometric quantity — it would be 0.125 m whether the mechanic pulled with 120 N or 12 N — so separating it out makes part (c) almost free."
      },
      {
        "do": "(b) $\\tau = r_{\\perp}F = (0.125)(120) = 15$ N$\\cdot$m.",
        "why": "The lever arm is already the perpendicular distance, so it multiplies the *whole* force. Multiplying it by a component as well would be applying $\\sin\\theta$ twice, which is the standard way this calculation goes wrong."
      },
      {
        "do": "(c) Perpendicular pulling gives a lever arm of the full 0.25 m, so $F = \\tau/r_{\\perp} = 15/0.25 = 60$ N.",
        "why": "Pulling at a right angle doubles the lever arm relative to the 30-degree pull, so it takes half the force for the same torque. This is why you are taught to pull square to a spanner rather than at an angle."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "(a) $r_{\\perp} = 0.125$ m  (b) $\\tau = 15$ N$\\cdot$m  (c) 60 N",
    "check": "Limiting-case check rather than a substitution: the largest torque a 120 N force could ever give at 0.25 m is $(0.25)(120) = 30$ N$\\cdot$m, achieved at 90 degrees. Our 30-degree answer is 15 N$\\cdot$m, exactly half — and the sine of 30 degrees is exactly one half, so the halving is required, not coincidental. Part (c) is consistent with this: half the lever arm needs twice the force, and 60 N is half of 120 N with the lever arm doubled."
  },
  "trap": "Measuring the lever arm along the handle to where the hand grips, rather than perpendicular to the line of action. That gives $r$, not $r_{\\perp}$, and it silently drops the $\\sin\\theta$. The reverse error is applying $\\sin\\theta$ to both the distance and the force, which squares it. Pick one grouping — $r_{\\perp}F$ or $rF_{\\perp}$ — and use it once."
},
{
  "id": "rot-inertia",
  "group": "u5",
  "prereqs": [
    "rot-angular-quantities",
    "dyn-second-law"
  ],
  "source": "CED 5.4",
  "title": "Rotational inertia depends on where the mass is",
  "analogy": "Hold a broom vertically with your hand at the bottom and wave the head back and forth: hard work. Now turn it over, hold it near the head, and wave the handle: much easier. Identical broom, identical mass, and your arm can feel the difference. What changed is how far the mass sits from the point you are turning it about.",
  "plain": "Rotational inertia, written $I$, is to rotation what mass is to straight-line motion: it measures how hard the system is to get spinning, or to stop spinning.\n\nBut it is not just mass. It depends on **how the mass is distributed relative to the axis**. Mass far from the axis counts for far more than mass close to it — and not proportionally, but as the square of the distance. Move a lump of mass twice as far out and it contributes four times as much rotational inertia.\n\nFor a single object a perpendicular distance $r$ from the axis, $I = mr^2$. For several objects, you add their contributions:\n\n$$I = \\sum m_i r_i^2$$\n\nThe most important consequence is this: **an object does not have a rotational inertia. An object has a rotational inertia about a specified axis.** Ask for the rotational inertia of a metre stick and the only honest answer is a question — about which axis? Through the centre, it is one number. Through the end, it is four times as much.",
  "depth": "The CED frames rotational inertia as the quantity saying how strongly a rigid system resists having its rotation changed, and makes both ingredients explicit: the amount of mass, and where that mass sits relative to the axis. **$I = \\sum m_i r_i^2$ is on the provided equation sheet.** Units are kg$\\cdot$m$^2$, which the sheet does not print but which falls straight out of the formula.\n\nTwo boundary statements narrow what you will be asked to do. First, calculations are capped at systems of at most five objects laid out in two dimensions — in practice, sums over point masses rather than integrals. Second, you do not need to memorise the rotational inertia of extended rigid bodies such as rods, disks, hoops or spheres; the CED says those will be **provided within the exam**. That is worth knowing before you spend a week memorising a table you will be handed.\n\nWhat you are expected to have is a *qualitative* grasp of what drives $I$ up or down, and the CED supplies the standard comparison to test it: match a hoop against a solid disk of equal mass and equal radius, and the hoop wins on rotational inertia. The reason is the $r^2$. A hoop holds every gram out at the full radius; a disk spreads its material from the centre outward, and the material near the centre contributes almost nothing.\n\nThe $r^2$ is what makes rotational inertia counterintuitive. Intuition wants heavy things to be hard to spin. The formula says a light rim beats a heavy hub. Two systems of identical mass can differ in $I$ by a large factor purely through arrangement — and a figure skater exploits precisely this, though the consequences of that belong to angular momentum, in Unit 6.\n\nDistances in the sum are measured **perpendicular to the axis**. Mass sitting on the axis itself contributes exactly zero, because $r = 0$, no matter how much of it there is.",
  "formula": "$$I = m r^2$$   (one object at perpendicular distance $r$)\n\n$$I_{\\mathrm{tot}} = \\sum I_i = \\sum m_i r_i^2$$\n\nUnits: kg$\\cdot$m$^2$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "Two 2.0 kg balls are attached to the ends of a rod of length 1.2 m whose own mass is negligible. Find the rotational inertia of the system (a) about an axis through the centre of the rod, perpendicular to it, and (b) about an axis through one end, perpendicular to the rod. Comment on what changed.",
    "given": "$m = 2.0$ kg each, rod length $L = 1.2$ m, rod mass negligible.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "(a) With the axis at the centre, each ball is 0.60 m from it. $I = (2.0)(0.60)^2 + (2.0)(0.60)^2 = 0.72 + 0.72 = 1.44$ kg$\\cdot$m$^2$.",
        "why": "Treat each ball separately and sum, because the formula is a sum over objects. Note the rod contributes nothing — it is stated massless, so every $m_i$ for the rod is zero."
      },
      {
        "do": "(b) With the axis at one end, one ball is at $r = 0$ and the other at $r = 1.2$ m. $I = (2.0)(0)^2 + (2.0)(1.2)^2 = 0 + 2.88 = 2.88$ kg$\\cdot$m$^2$.",
        "why": "The ball sitting on the axis drops out entirely. This is not an approximation — mass at zero distance from the axis genuinely contributes nothing to rotational inertia, because it does not have to move when the system turns."
      },
      {
        "do": "Compare: the mass is identical in both cases, 4.0 kg, but $I$ doubled.",
        "why": "This is the whole point of the concept. Nothing about the object changed between (a) and (b) — only the axis we chose to spin it about. So $I$ cannot be a property of the object alone."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "(a) $I = 1.44$ kg$\\cdot$m$^2$  (b) $I = 2.88$ kg$\\cdot$m$^2$ — exactly twice as much, for the same 4.0 kg of matter.",
    "check": "Independent route to (b) using [[rot-parallel-axis]]: the centre of mass is at the rod's midpoint, so shifting the axis a distance $d = 0.60$ m out gives $I' = I_{\\mathrm{cm}} + Md^2 = 1.44 + (4.0)(0.60)^2 = 1.44 + 1.44 = 2.88$ kg$\\cdot$m$^2$. Same answer, completely different calculation. As a bound check, no axis in this system can give more than $(4.0)(1.2)^2 = 5.76$ kg$\\cdot$m$^2$, which would require all the mass at 1.2 m; 2.88 sits sensibly below that."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Why $r^2$ and not $r$? Because rotational inertia is about how much motion a given rotation actually demands. A point at distance $r$ has to move at speed $v = r\\omega$, and the effort of getting mass moving scales with the square of its speed. One factor of $r$ comes from the speed the point must reach; the other comes from the leverage needed to reach it. Both together give $r^2$.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "The natural guess is that rotational inertia is just mass, or mass times some measure of size. That guess predicts that two objects with the same mass are equally hard to spin, and it is wrong in a way you can feel with a broom in your hands. It also predicts that a hoop and a disk of equal mass and radius behave identically, which they do not — the hoop is markedly harder to spin, because all of its mass sits at the maximum radius.",
  "trap": "Quoting a rotational inertia without naming an axis, and — the harder version — computing $I$ about one axis and then using it in $\\tau_{\\mathrm{net}} = I\\alpha$ with the torques taken about a *different* axis. The torques and the rotational inertia must be about the same axis or the equation means nothing. Also: forgetting that mass on the axis contributes zero, and forgetting that $r$ is measured perpendicular to the axis, not along the object."
},
{
  "id": "rot-parallel-axis",
  "group": "u5",
  "prereqs": [
    "rot-inertia"
  ],
  "source": "CED 5.4",
  "title": "The parallel-axis theorem",
  "plain": "Since rotational inertia depends on the axis, you might expect to redo the whole calculation every time the axis moves. For a shift to a **parallel** axis, you do not have to.\n\nThere is a shortcut. If you know the rotational inertia about an axis through the centre of mass, then the rotational inertia about any axis parallel to it, a distance $d$ away, is\n\n$$I' = I_{\\mathrm{cm}} + Md^2$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the total mass. The extra term is what you would get if all the mass were a single lump at distance $d$.\n\nOne thing follows straight away. $Md^2$ can never be negative, so $I'$ is always at least $I_{\\mathrm{cm}}$. **The centre-of-mass axis gives the smallest rotational inertia of any parallel axis.** An object is easiest to spin about its own centre.",
  "depth": "The CED states both halves. Fix a plane and slide the rotational axis around inside it: the inertia you measure bottoms out when that axis runs through the centre of mass. The parallel-axis theorem is then what carries you from that minimum value out to any axis parallel to it. **$I' = I_{\\mathrm{cm}} + Md^2$ is on the provided equation sheet**, in the right-hand column, immediately after $I = \\sum m_i r_i^2$.\n\nThree conditions on its use, none of which the sheet states:\n\n- The two axes must be **parallel**. Tilting the axis is a different calculation, and not one this course asks for.\n- $I_{\\mathrm{cm}}$ must be about an axis through the **centre of mass**, not merely about some other convenient axis. You cannot chain the theorem from an arbitrary starting axis and get the right answer.\n- $d$ is the distance **between the two axes**, measured perpendicular to them — not the distance from the axis to the far end of the object.\n\nWhere this earns its keep on the exam is with extended bodies. The CED says the rotational inertias of extended rigid systems will be provided to you, and what gets provided is typically the centre-of-mass value. If the problem then pivots the rod at its end, or the disk at its rim, the parallel-axis theorem is the bridge between what you were handed and what you need.\n\nThe minimum property is also a fast qualitative tool. If a question asks which of several parallel axes gives the largest angular acceleration for a given torque, the answer is the one nearest the centre of mass, because that is where $I$ is smallest and $\\alpha = \\tau/I$ is therefore largest.",
  "formula": "$$I' = I_{\\mathrm{cm}} + M d^2$$\n\n$M$ = total mass; $d$ = perpendicular distance between the two parallel axes; $I_{\\mathrm{cm}}$ must be about a centre-of-mass axis",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A uniform rod has mass 3.0 kg and length 2.0 m. Its rotational inertia about an axis through its centre, perpendicular to the rod, is given as 1.0 kg$\\cdot$m$^2$. Find its rotational inertia about a parallel axis through one end, and about a parallel axis 0.50 m from the centre.",
    "given": "$M = 3.0$ kg, $L = 2.0$ m, $I_{\\mathrm{cm}} = 1.0$ kg$\\cdot$m$^2$ (provided, as extended-body values are on the exam).",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Confirm the given value is a centre-of-mass value. It is: the problem says the axis passes through the centre of the uniform rod.",
        "why": "The theorem is only valid starting from the centre-of-mass axis. If you were instead handed the inertia about the end and asked for the centre, you would have to subtract rather than add — and students who apply the formula mechanically add in both directions."
      },
      {
        "do": "For the end axis, $d$ = distance from centre to end = $L/2 = 1.0$ m.",
        "why": "$d$ is axis-to-axis, not axis-to-far-end. Using 2.0 m here — the rod's full length — is the single most common error in this calculation and would give 13 kg$\\cdot$m$^2$ instead of 4.0."
      },
      {
        "do": "$I' = I_{\\mathrm{cm}} + Md^2 = 1.0 + (3.0)(1.0)^2 = 4.0$ kg$\\cdot$m$^2$.",
        "why": "The added term uses the *total* mass, because in shifting the axis every part of the body moves further out on average by the same amount, and the theorem bundles that into one term."
      },
      {
        "do": "For the axis 0.50 m from the centre, $I' = 1.0 + (3.0)(0.50)^2 = 1.0 + 0.75 = 1.75$ kg$\\cdot$m$^2$.",
        "why": "Same theorem, smaller $d$. Comparing the two answers shows the $d^2$ dependence directly: doubling the offset from 0.50 m to 1.0 m quadrupled the added term, from 0.75 to 3.0."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "About the end, $I' = 4.0$ kg$\\cdot$m$^2$. About the axis 0.50 m from centre, $I' = 1.75$ kg$\\cdot$m$^2$.",
    "check": "Bound check, independent of the theorem: every part of the rod lies between 0 and 2.0 m from the end axis, so $I$ about the end must be less than $ML^2 = (3.0)(2.0)^2 = 12$ kg$\\cdot$m$^2$, the value it would have if all 3.0 kg sat at the far tip. It must also exceed $I_{\\mathrm{cm}} = 1.0$. Our 4.0 kg$\\cdot$m$^2$ sits inside those bounds and toward the low end, which is right — half the rod's mass is within 1.0 m of the end. Monotonicity also checks: 1.75 for the nearer axis is less than 4.0 for the further one, as the minimum property demands."
  },
  "trap": "Using the wrong $d$ — the object's length, or the distance to its far edge, instead of the separation of the two axes. Second, applying the theorem from a non-centre-of-mass starting axis. Third, going the wrong way: moving *toward* the centre of mass means subtracting $Md^2$, not adding it, and the give-away that you have erred is an $I'$ smaller than $I_{\\mathrm{cm}}$, which is impossible."
},
{
  "id": "rot-equilibrium",
  "group": "u5",
  "prereqs": [
    "rot-torque",
    "rot-lever-arm",
    "dyn-free-body"
  ],
  "source": "CED 5.5",
  "title": "Rotational equilibrium and Newton's first law for rotation",
  "analogy": "A tug-of-war rope with two evenly matched teams goes nowhere — the forces balance. Now imagine two people pushing opposite ends of a revolving door with equal force: the net force on the door is zero, and yet it spins. Balanced forces and balanced turning effects are two separate conditions, and satisfying one says nothing about the other.",
  "plain": "Newton's first law says an object keeps a constant velocity unless a net force acts. The rotational version says a system keeps a **constant angular velocity** unless a net **torque** acts.\n\n**Rotational equilibrium** means the net torque is zero: $\\sum \\tau = 0$. It does not mean stationary. A wheel spinning steadily forever is in rotational equilibrium just as much as a wheel sitting still.\n\nThe important structural point is that this condition is *independent* of the translational one. A system can be in rotational equilibrium and not translational equilibrium, or the other way round. The revolving door above has balanced forces and unbalanced torques. A box sliding across a floor while not rotating has the reverse.\n\nSo a body that is genuinely static — not moving and not turning — must satisfy **both** conditions at once:\n\n$$\\sum F = 0$$\n\n$$\\sum \\tau = 0$$\n\nThat is two equations, and that is what lets you solve for two unknown forces.",
  "depth": "The CED assembles this from three pieces. Rotational equilibrium is the case where the torques on a system cancel, $\\sum \\tau_i = 0$. Newton's first law in rotational dress then says a constant angular velocity requires exactly that cancellation. And running the same statement backwards gives the corollary to the second law: if the torques do not cancel, the angular velocity has to be changing. The CED is also explicit that a system may be in rotational equilibrium without being in translational equilibrium, and the other way round. **Neither $\\sum F = 0$ nor $\\sum \\tau = 0$ is printed on the equation sheet** as an equilibrium condition; they are the zero cases of $\\vec{a}_{\\mathrm{sys}} = \\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{net}}/m_{\\mathrm{sys}}$ and $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{sys}} = \\tau_{\\mathrm{net}}/I_{\\mathrm{sys}}$, both of which are on the sheet.\n\nThe practical skill here is **choosing the axis**. In a static problem you are free to write the torque equation about any point you like, because the body is not rotating about anything. Choose the point where an unknown force acts, and that force's torque is zero — $r = 0$, so it drops out of the equation entirely, and you are left with fewer unknowns than you started with. This is not a shortcut you take when convenient; on a two-support problem it is the difference between one equation in one unknown and one equation in two.\n\nThe reason you are allowed this freedom: shifting the axis changes each force's torque by (the shift) times (that force's perpendicular component). Summing over all the forces, the total change is (the shift) times (the net perpendicular force), and in translational equilibrium the net force is zero. So the net torque is the same about every axis, and if it vanishes about one it vanishes about all.\n\nTwo more habits. Weight acts at the **centre of mass**, and for a uniform object that is its geometric centre — so a uniform plank's weight is drawn as a single downward arrow at its midpoint, and the lever arm is measured to there. And forces are only balanced as *vectors*: horizontal and vertical components each sum to zero separately.\n\nThe CED's boundary statement rules out analysing rotation in several planes at once, so every problem you meet will be a single-plane, clockwise-versus-counterclockwise balance.",
  "formula": "$$\\sum \\tau_i = 0$$   (rotational equilibrium: constant $\\omega$)\n\n$$\\sum F = 0$$   (translational equilibrium: constant $\\vec{v}$)\n\nStatic equilibrium requires both. The torque equation may be written about any axis.",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A uniform plank of mass 20 kg and length 4.0 m rests horizontally on two supports. Support A is at the left end; support B is 3.0 m from the left end. A person of mass 50 kg stands 2.5 m from the left end. Find the upward force each support exerts on the plank. Use $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$.",
    "given": "Plank $m_p = 20$ kg, uniform, $L = 4.0$ m. Person $m_q = 50$ kg at 2.5 m from the left end. Support A at 0 m, support B at 3.0 m. $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Draw the force diagram and locate every force. $F_A$ up at 0 m; $F_B$ up at 3.0 m; plank weight $(20)(9.8) = 196$ N down at 2.0 m; person's weight $(50)(9.8) = 490$ N down at 2.5 m.",
        "why": "A force diagram, not a free-body diagram: the positions matter as much as the magnitudes here, and collapsing the plank to a point would throw away exactly the information the torque equation needs. The plank is uniform, so its whole weight is placed at its geometric centre, 2.0 m."
      },
      {
        "do": "Choose the axis at support A, and take counterclockwise as positive.",
        "why": "This is the deliberate move. $F_A$ is an unknown, and putting the axis where $F_A$ acts makes its lever arm zero, so it vanishes from the torque equation. That converts a problem with two unknowns into an equation with one. Any axis is legal here because the plank is static; this axis is the one that does work for us."
      },
      {
        "do": "Write $\\sum \\tau = 0$ about A: $F_B(3.0) - (196)(2.0) - (490)(2.5) = 0$.",
        "why": "$F_B$ would rotate the plank one way about A; both weights would rotate it the other way, so they carry the opposite sign. Every lever arm here is a horizontal distance because every force is vertical, so each $\\sin\\theta$ is 1 and no trigonometry is needed."
      },
      {
        "do": "Solve: $F_B(3.0) = 392 + 1225 = 1617$, so $F_B = 539$ N.",
        "why": "One equation, one unknown — the payoff from the axis choice."
      },
      {
        "do": "Now use $\\sum F = 0$ vertically: $F_A + F_B = 196 + 490 = 686$ N, so $F_A = 686 - 539 = 147$ N.",
        "why": "The second equilibrium condition supplies the second equation. Torque alone could never have given both forces; you need the pair. Note the order — solving for $F_B$ first made this a subtraction rather than another torque equation."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$F_A = 147$ N and $F_B = 539$ N.",
    "check": "Redo the torque equation about support B instead, a genuinely different calculation. About B, support A is 3.0 m to the left, the plank's centre is 1.0 m to the left, and the person is 0.5 m to the left. Balancing: $F_A(3.0) = (196)(1.0) + (490)(0.5) = 196 + 245 = 441$, so $F_A = 147$ N — matching. A physical sense check too: the person stands closer to B than to A, so B should carry more of the load, and it does, by a wide margin."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Choosing the axis at an unknown force is worth making into a reflex. On a free-response question it can be the difference between a clean two-line solution and a simultaneous-equation slog with two chances to slip. And because any axis is valid for a static body, recomputing about a second axis is the best available independent check on your answer — it uses different numbers, different lever arms, and a different equation, so an arithmetic error will not survive both.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "Students often assume the axis must be a real physical pivot — a hinge, an axle, the point the object would actually turn about. For a body in static equilibrium that is not required, and insisting on it throws away the whole technique. The axis in a static torque equation is a bookkeeping choice, not a physical claim about the object.",
  "trap": "Believing rotational equilibrium means at rest. It means constant angular velocity, which includes spinning steadily. Second, treating balanced forces as implying balanced torques — the CED explicitly says a system can be in one without the other. Third, forgetting that weight acts at the centre of mass, and either omitting the plank's own weight or placing it at the wrong point. Fourth, writing torques about one axis and forces at another point, then mixing them; the torque equation must be internally consistent about a single stated axis."
},
{
  "id": "rot-newton-second-rotational",
  "group": "u5",
  "prereqs": [
    "rot-inertia",
    "rot-torque",
    "dyn-second-law"
  ],
  "source": "CED 5.6",
  "title": "Newton's second law in rotational form",
  "analogy": "Push a shopping trolley and it accelerates; push a loaded one the same way and it accelerates less. Twist a bicycle wheel by its spokes and it speeds up; twist a heavy flywheel with the same effort and it barely stirs. The relationship is identical — effort divided by reluctance gives response — with turning effort in place of push and rotational inertia in place of mass.",
  "plain": "If the torques on a system do not balance, its angular velocity must change. How fast it changes is given by the rotational version of Newton's second law:\n\n$$\\alpha = \\frac{\\tau_{\\mathrm{net}}}{I}$$\n\nMore net torque gives more angular acceleration. More rotational inertia gives less. It is $F_{\\mathrm{net}} = ma$ with each symbol replaced by its rotational partner: force by torque, mass by rotational inertia, acceleration by angular acceleration.\n\nThis completes the mapping. Position, velocity, acceleration became angle, angular velocity, angular acceleration. Force became torque. Mass became rotational inertia. The physics did not change; the vocabulary did.\n\nOne warning that the analogy hides: because $I$ depends on the axis, the same net torque can produce very different angular accelerations for the same object, depending on where you pivot it.",
  "depth": "The CED sets out three claims: a non-zero net torque is what makes angular velocity change; doubling the net torque doubles the rate of that change, in the same rotational sense; and doubling the rotational inertia halves it. The equation is\n\n$$\\alpha_{\\mathrm{sys}} = \\frac{\\sum \\tau}{I_{\\mathrm{sys}}} = \\frac{\\tau_{\\mathrm{net}}}{I_{\\mathrm{sys}}}$$\n\n**and it is on the provided equation sheet**, written exactly that way, directly mirroring the sheet's translational $\\vec{a}_{\\mathrm{sys}} = \\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{net}}/m_{\\mathrm{sys}}$ in the other column. The sheet is quietly teaching the analogy by its layout.\n\nBoth $\\sum\\tau$ and $I$ must be taken about the **same axis**. This is the constraint with no translational counterpart, and it is where most errors live. Mass does not care which axis you chose; rotational inertia is defined by it.\n\nThe CED adds a point worth taking seriously: a full description of a rotating rigid system can require you to carry out the linear analysis and the rotational analysis as **two separate calculations**. A pulley with a hanging block is the standard case. The block obeys $F_{\\mathrm{net}} = ma$ along a line. The pulley obeys $\\tau_{\\mathrm{net}} = I\\alpha$ about its axle. Neither equation alone closes the problem. They are joined by a constraint — the string does not slip or stretch, so the block's linear acceleration equals the rim's tangential acceleration, $a = R\\alpha$ — and by Newton's third law, since the tension pulling back on the block is the same tension pulling the rim forward.\n\nThat coupling produces a result students find surprising. When a pulley has rotational inertia, the tension in the string is **not** equal to the hanging block's weight, and it is not the same on both sides of a pulley with two loads. It cannot be: if the tension equalled the weight the block would not accelerate, and if the tensions on either side were equal there would be no net torque to spin the pulley up. The massless, frictionless pulley of earlier problems is the special case $I = 0$, where those two statements happen to become true.",
  "formula": "$$\\alpha_{\\mathrm{sys}} = \\frac{\\sum \\tau}{I_{\\mathrm{sys}}} = \\frac{\\tau_{\\mathrm{net}}}{I_{\\mathrm{sys}}}$$\n\ntorques and $I$ about the SAME axis\n\nstring-over-pulley constraint (not on the sheet): $a = R\\alpha$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A pulley of radius 0.10 m and rotational inertia 0.020 kg$\\cdot$m$^2$ turns on a frictionless axle. A light string is wrapped around its rim and a 2.0 kg block hangs from the free end. The string does not slip. Find the linear acceleration of the block, the angular acceleration of the pulley, and the tension in the string. Use $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$.",
    "given": "$R = 0.10$ m, $I = 0.020$ kg$\\cdot$m$^2$, $m = 2.0$ kg, $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$, string does not slip, axle frictionless.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Treat block and pulley as two separate systems. For the block, taking downward as positive: $mg - T = ma$.",
        "why": "They obey different laws — the block translates, the pulley rotates — so one equation cannot cover both. Separating them is what the CED means by performing the linear and rotational analyses independently. Note $T$ is written as an unknown, not as $mg$: assuming $T = mg$ would force $a = 0$, contradicting the fact that the block falls."
      },
      {
        "do": "For the pulley, torques about the axle: only the tension has a lever arm, and it acts perpendicular at the rim, so $\\tau_{\\mathrm{net}} = TR$ and $TR = I\\alpha$.",
        "why": "The pulley's weight and the axle's force both act at or through the axle, so their lever arms are zero and they contribute no torque. Identifying that they drop out is half the work; students who include them get stuck with unknowns they cannot find."
      },
      {
        "do": "Link the two with the no-slip constraint: $a = R\\alpha$, so $\\alpha = a/R$.",
        "why": "Without this the two equations are about different unknowns and cannot be combined. The constraint is physical: the string is inextensible and does not slip, so the block's speed equals the rim's tangential speed at every instant, and therefore their rates of change match too."
      },
      {
        "do": "Substitute: $T = I\\alpha/R = Ia/R^2$. Then $mg - Ia/R^2 = ma$, giving $a = mg/(m + I/R^2)$.",
        "why": "Deriving symbolically before substituting numbers exposes the structure: the quantity $I/R^2$ acts like an extra mass added to the system. That is a result worth seeing, and it makes the limiting checks below possible."
      },
      {
        "do": "Numbers: $I/R^2 = 0.020/0.010 = 2.0$ kg, so $a = (2.0)(9.8)/(2.0 + 2.0) = 4.9$ m/s$^2$. Then $\\alpha = a/R = 49$ rad/s$^2$ and $T = m(g - a) = (2.0)(4.9) = 9.8$ N.",
        "why": "Substitute only at the end, so a single arithmetic slip does not propagate through three equations. $T$ is recovered from the block equation because it is the simpler of the two."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$a = 4.9$ m/s$^2$, $\\alpha = 49$ rad/s$^2$, $T = 9.8$ N.",
    "check": "Limiting case: if the pulley had no rotational inertia, $I/R^2 = 0$ and the expression gives $a = g$ with $T = 0$ — free fall on a weightless pulley, which is right. Here $I/R^2$ came out as 2.0 kg, exactly equal to the block's mass, so the effective mass doubled and the acceleration should be exactly half of $g$. It is: 4.9 is half of 9.8. Separately, the tension must be less than the block's weight of 19.6 N, since the block accelerates downward; 9.8 N is, and it is not 19.6 N, which is the answer a student gets by assuming $T = mg$."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Rotational inertia rather than mass in the denominator, because mass alone does not determine how hard a system is to spin up — the arrangement does, as [[rot-inertia]] shows. Writing $\\tau = m\\alpha$ would predict that a hoop and a disk of the same mass respond identically to the same torque, and they demonstrably do not.",
  "trap": "Assuming the tension equals the hanging weight. It only does when the pulley has no rotational inertia, and if the problem bothered to give you $I$, it does not. Second: computing torques about one axis and $I$ about another. Third: mixing $a$ and $\\alpha$ — they differ by a factor of $R$ and their units are different, so a units check will catch it. Fourth: dropping the constraint $a = R\\alpha$ and then wondering why there are three unknowns and two equations."
},
{
  "id": "rem-rotational-ke",
  "group": "u6",
  "prereqs": [
    "enr-kinetic-energy",
    "rot-inertia"
  ],
  "source": "CED 6.1",
  "title": "Rotational kinetic energy",
  "analogy": "Hold a bicycle wheel off the ground and spin it hard. The wheel is going nowhere — its centre never moves — and yet if you touch the rim you will know about it. The energy is not in the wheel travelling. It is in every little piece of rim travelling, in a circle, past you.",
  "plain": "A spinning object has kinetic energy even when it is not going anywhere. That is the whole idea, and it is worth pausing on, because the version of kinetic energy you already know, $K = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$, gives zero for an object whose centre of mass sits still.\n\nThe way out is to stop looking at the object as one lump. Chop it into tiny pieces. Every piece is moving in a circle at some speed, so every piece has $\\frac{1}{2}mv^2$ of its own. Add them all up and you get the object's rotational kinetic energy. Pieces near the axis move slowly and contribute little; pieces out at the rim move fast and contribute a lot.\n\nDoing that sum every time would be miserable, so it is done once and packed into the rotational inertia $I$. After that the energy is $K = \\frac{1}{2}I\\omega^2$ — the same shape as $\\frac{1}{2}mv^2$, with $I$ where the mass was and $\\omega$ where the speed was. Rotational kinetic energy is a plain number of joules. It has no direction, and it is never negative, because $\\omega$ is squared.",
  "depth": "$$K = \\frac{1}{2}I\\omega^2$$\n\n**This equation is on the provided equation sheet** (right column, CED p. 212), printed with the same symbol $K$ as the translational form in the left column. Which one you mean is decided by whether you wrote $m$ and $v$ or $I$ and $\\omega$, not by the letter.\n\nWhere it comes from: the sheet also gives $I = \\sum m_i r_i^2$. A piece of the body at distance $r_i$ from the axis moves at $v_i = r_i\\omega$, so its kinetic energy is $\\frac{1}{2}m_i r_i^2\\omega^2$. Every piece shares the same $\\omega$ — that is what makes a body rigid — so $\\omega^2$ factors out of the sum and what is left inside is exactly $\\sum m_i r_i^2$. The derivation is also the reason $I$ has to be quoted *about a stated axis*: the $r_i$ are distances from that axis and nothing else.\n\nTwo consequences the CED states outright. First, a rigid system can have rotational kinetic energy while its centre of mass is at rest, because the individual points still have speed. Second, rotational kinetic energy is a scalar. You never resolve it into components and you never give it a sign to indicate which way the thing is spinning — a wheel turning clockwise at $10$ rad/s and one turning anticlockwise at $10$ rad/s have identical kinetic energy.\n\nThe equation sheet carries only $I = \\sum m_i r_i^2$ and the parallel-axis result $I' = I_{\\mathrm{cm}} + Md^2$. There is no table of rotational inertias for discs, hoops or spheres on it, so any such value has to arrive in the problem statement or be built from those two expressions.",
  "formula": "K = ½ I ω²          rotational kinetic energy      [ON the equation sheet]\n\n  I : rotational inertia about the axis of rotation, kg·m²\n  ω : angular speed, RADIANS per second — not rev/s, not deg/s\n  K : joules; a scalar, never negative, no direction\n\nbuilt from   I = Σ mᵢ rᵢ²   and   vᵢ = rᵢ ω     [both on the sheet]",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A flywheel used to store energy has a rotational inertia of $0.45$ kg·m² about its axle and spins at $120$ rad/s. How much kinetic energy does it hold? If it is allowed to slow to $40$ rad/s while driving a machine, how much energy has it delivered?",
    "given": "I = 0.45 kg·m² about the axle; ω0 = 120 rad/s; ω = 40 rad/s.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Write $K = \\frac{1}{2}I\\omega^2$ and nothing else.",
        "why": "The rotational inertia already contains everything about how the mass is spread out. We do not need the flywheel's radius, its mass, or the speed of its rim — asking for those would be re-doing a sum that $I$ has already done."
      },
      {
        "do": "$K_0 = \\frac{1}{2}(0.45)(120)^2 = 3240$ J.",
        "why": "The angular speed is already in rad/s, which is the only unit $\\frac{1}{2}I\\omega^2$ is written for. Had the problem said \"1150 rpm\" the conversion would come first, before anything is squared."
      },
      {
        "do": "$K = \\frac{1}{2}(0.45)(40)^2 = 360$ J.",
        "why": "Kinetic energy is a property of a state, not of a process. So evaluate it at the two states and subtract, rather than trying to follow the torque the machine applied and for how long."
      },
      {
        "do": "Energy delivered $= 3240 - 360 = 2880$ J.",
        "why": "Energy that left the flywheel went into the machine. The flywheel's own accounting is just the drop in its store; we do not have to know what the machine did with it."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "It holds $3.2 \\times 10^3$ J and delivers $2.9 \\times 10^3$ J, leaving $3.6 \\times 10^2$ J still spinning.",
    "check": "Scaling, not substitution: $K \\propto \\omega^2$, so cutting the angular speed to one third must leave one ninth of the energy. $3240/9 = 360$ ✓, and the fraction released is $8/9 = 0.889$, giving $0.889 \\times 3240 = 2880$ ✓. Units also work out: kg·m² × s⁻² = kg·m²/s² = J."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Why does the rotational formula copy the translational one so exactly? Because it *is* the translational one, summed. Nothing new was assumed. That is a useful thing to notice, because it tells you the two are not rival formulas to choose between — they are two slices of one accounting, which is what [[rem-total-kinetic-energy]] is about.",
  "trap": "Two ways to lose the mark. (1) Using an angular speed that is not in radians per second. Revolutions per second and degrees per second both look like rates and both give the wrong answer, badly, because the error is squared. (2) Reaching for $\\frac{1}{2}mv^2$ with the mass of the whole object and the speed of the rim. That treats every gram as if it were at the rim; the real answer is smaller, and $I$ is exactly the correction."
},
{
  "id": "rem-total-kinetic-energy",
  "group": "u6",
  "prereqs": [
    "rem-rotational-ke"
  ],
  "source": "CED 6.1",
  "title": "Total kinetic energy: translation plus rotation",
  "analogy": "A frisbee crossing a garden is doing two separate things at once: going across, and spinning. Stop the spin and it still has to be caught. Stop the flight and the spin is still there in your hand. Two independent stores, and the total is the sum.",
  "plain": "An object that is both moving and spinning carries kinetic energy of two kinds. The first is the ordinary $\\frac{1}{2}mv^2$ of the whole mass, computed with the speed of the **centre of mass**. The second is the $\\frac{1}{2}I\\omega^2$ of the spin about the centre of mass. You add them.\n\nThe split is not arbitrary. It works because the motion of every particle can be written as \"the way the centre of mass is going\" plus \"the way this particle circles the centre of mass\", and when you total up the kinetic energy of all the particles the cross-terms cancel. So the two stores really are independent — which is what lets you fill one without touching the other.\n\nNotice what this means for anything rolling downhill. The gravitational potential energy released has two places to go, not one. Whatever ends up in the spin is not available to make the object travel faster.",
  "depth": "$$K_{\\mathrm{tot}} = K_{\\mathrm{trans}} + K_{\\mathrm{rot}} = \\frac{1}{2}mv_{\\mathrm{cm}}^2 + \\frac{1}{2}I_{\\mathrm{cm}}\\omega^2$$\n\nThe CED prints $K_{\\mathrm{tot}} = K_{\\mathrm{trans}} + K_{\\mathrm{rot}}$ as a relevant equation under CED 6.5, but **that combined line is not on the equation sheet**. The two halves are: $K = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$ sits in the left column of CED p. 212 and $K = \\frac{1}{2}I\\omega^2$ in the right. You are expected to know that they add.\n\nTwo conditions make the split legitimate, and both matter. The $v$ must be the speed of the centre of mass, not of any convenient point on the object. The $I$ must be about an axis **through the centre of mass**, not about the contact point or the pivot. Use $I$ about some other axis and you will double-count, because an $I$ about a far axis already contains the translational motion via the parallel-axis term $Md^2$.\n\nThere is a legitimate alternative that people sometimes meet later: treat a rolling body as pure rotation about the contact point, using $I_{\\mathrm{contact}} = I_{\\mathrm{cm}} + mr^2$, and write $K = \\frac{1}{2}I_{\\mathrm{contact}}\\omega^2$ with no translational term at all. It gives the same number. What you may not do is mix them — pick one description and stay in it.\n\nCED 6.1 also flags the opposite extreme: for an object rotating about a **fixed** axis, the rotational kinetic energy about that axis *is* the total kinetic energy. A grindstone on its spindle has no translational term to add, because its centre of mass is not going anywhere.",
  "formula": "K_tot = K_trans + K_rot = ½ m v_cm²  +  ½ I_cm ω²\n\n  the combined line is NOT on the equation sheet; both halves are\n  v_cm : speed of the CENTRE OF MASS\n  I_cm : rotational inertia about an axis THROUGH the centre of mass\n\nfixed axis, centre of mass at rest   →   K_tot = ½ I ω²  only\nsliding without spinning             →   K_tot = ½ m v²  only",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A $6.0$ kg bowling ball of radius $R$ rolls without slipping across a level lane at $4.0$ m/s. For this ball $I_{\\mathrm{cm}} = \\frac{2}{5}mR^2$. Find its total kinetic energy, and the fraction of that total which is rotational.",
    "given": "m = 6.0 kg; v_cm = 4.0 m/s; I_cm = (2/5)mR²; rolling without slipping, so ω = v/R.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "$K_{\\mathrm{trans}} = \\frac{1}{2}(6.0)(4.0)^2 = 48$ J.",
        "why": "This half only ever needs the mass and the centre-of-mass speed. The ball being round, hollow or spinning has no effect on it."
      },
      {
        "do": "Replace $\\omega$ with $v/R$ before doing anything else.",
        "why": "We were given a linear speed and no angular speed. Rolling without slipping is precisely the licence to convert one into the other, and doing the substitution early is what makes $R$ disappear."
      },
      {
        "do": "$K_{\\mathrm{rot}} = \\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{2}{5}mR^2\\right)\\left(\\frac{v}{R}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{5}mv^2 = 0.2(6.0)(4.0)^2 = 19.2$ J.",
        "why": "The $R^2$ cancels against the $1/R^2$. This is not luck: it happens for any shape whose $I$ is written as a multiple of $mR^2$, which is why the radius of a rolling object almost never matters."
      },
      {
        "do": "$K_{\\mathrm{tot}} = 48 + 19.2 = 67.2$ J; rotational fraction $= 19.2/67.2 = 0.286$.",
        "why": "Add rather than choose. The two stores are independent, so the total is a sum, not a maximum."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$K_{\\mathrm{tot}} = 67$ J, of which $28.6\\%$ — that is $2/7$ — is rotational.",
    "check": "An independent route to the fraction. Writing $I = \\beta mR^2$ gives $K_{\\mathrm{rot}}/K_{\\mathrm{trans}} = \\beta$ exactly, for any rolling body. Here $\\beta = 2/5$, so rotational should be $2/5$ of translational: $0.4 \\times 48 = 19.2$ ✓, and the rotational share of the total is $\\frac{2/5}{1 + 2/5} = 2/7 = 0.286$ ✓. Neither $m$, $v$ nor $R$ appears — a sign the algebra is right, since the fraction should be a property of the shape alone."
  },
  "trap": "Adding $\\frac{1}{2}mv^2$ to $\\frac{1}{2}I\\omega^2$ where $I$ was taken about the contact point. That $I$ is $I_{\\mathrm{cm}} + mr^2$, and the $mr^2$ part *is* the translational energy in disguise, so you count it twice and get an answer that is too big by exactly $\\frac{1}{2}mv^2$."
},
{
  "id": "rem-work-by-torque",
  "group": "u6",
  "prereqs": [
    "rem-rotational-ke",
    "enr-work-definition",
    "rot-torque"
  ],
  "source": "CED 6.2",
  "title": "Work done by a torque",
  "analogy": "Leaning on a stuck bolt with a spanner and getting nowhere is tiring, but no energy has gone into the bolt. The moment it starts to turn, it does. Torque alone is not energy; torque *through an angle* is.",
  "plain": "A torque transfers energy into or out of a rotating system, but only if the system actually turns while the torque is applied. The amount transferred is the torque multiplied by the angle turned through:\n\n$$W = \\tau\\,\\Delta\\theta$$\n\nCompare this with the translational version, $W = Fd$. Force times distance becomes torque times angle. The parallel is exact, and it is not a coincidence: a force pushing along a circular arc of radius $r$ through an angle $\\Delta\\theta$ covers an arc length $r\\Delta\\theta$, so $W = F(r\\Delta\\theta) = (Fr)\\Delta\\theta = \\tau\\Delta\\theta$.\n\nIf the torque is not constant — a spring-driven mechanism, say, where the torque falls as it unwinds — you cannot multiply. Instead you take the area under a graph of torque against angular position. That is the same relationship, just handled the way you would handle a varying force with a force-against-distance graph.",
  "depth": "$$W = \\tau\\,\\Delta\\theta$$\n\n**This equation is on the provided equation sheet** (right column, CED p. 212). Its cousin $W = Fd\\cos\\theta$ is in the left column; do not let the two $\\theta$ symbols confuse you. In $W = Fd\\cos\\theta$ the angle is between the force and the displacement. In $W = \\tau\\Delta\\theta$ the angle is how far the body turned, in radians. The equation sheet's own variable key lists $\\theta$ as \"angle or angular position\", which is exactly this ambiguity made official.\n\nThe radian is doing quiet work here. $W = \\tau\\Delta\\theta$ only comes out right because arc length is $s = r\\theta$ with $\\theta$ in radians — that identity is on CED p. 211 under geometry. Feed it revolutions and the answer is out by a factor of $2\\pi$ per revolution.\n\nOnce you have $W$, the work–energy theorem takes over unchanged. The net work done on a rigid body about a fixed axis equals its change in rotational kinetic energy, so $\\tau_{\\mathrm{net}}\\Delta\\theta = \\frac{1}{2}I\\omega^2 - \\frac{1}{2}I\\omega_0^2$. This is the rotational route that avoids time entirely, which is what makes it the right tool when a problem gives you an angle and asks for a speed.\n\nThe CED explicitly wants you to read work off the **area under a torque-against-angular-position graph**. Keep that separate in your head from the area under a torque-against-**time** graph, which is angular impulse and gives you a change in angular momentum instead — see [[rem-angular-impulse]]. Same-looking graph, different horizontal axis, completely different quantity.",
  "formula": "W = τ Δθ            work done by a torque         [ON the equation sheet]\n\n  Δθ : angular displacement in RADIANS  (1 rev = 2π rad)\n  W  : joules — positive if the torque and the rotation agree in sense\n\nvarying torque      W = area under a τ-vs-θ graph\nnet work            τ_net Δθ = ΔK = ½Iω² − ½Iω₀²\n\nDO NOT CONFUSE:  area under τ-vs-θ  = work        (joules)\n                 area under τ-vs-t  = angular impulse = ΔL  (kg·m²/s)",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A motor applies a constant torque of $12$ N·m to a grindstone with rotational inertia $3.0$ kg·m² about its spindle. The grindstone starts at rest and the motor turns it through $5.0$ complete revolutions. How much work does the motor do, and how fast is the grindstone then turning? Friction is negligible.",
    "given": "τ = 12 N·m constant; I = 3.0 kg·m²; Δθ = 5.0 rev; ω0 = 0.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Convert: $\\Delta\\theta = 5.0 \\times 2\\pi = 31.4$ rad.",
        "why": "$W = \\tau\\Delta\\theta$ inherits the radian from $s = r\\theta$. Revolutions are a perfectly good way to state an angle and a useless way to compute with one."
      },
      {
        "do": "$W = \\tau\\Delta\\theta = 12 \\times 31.4 = 377$ J.",
        "why": "The torque is constant, so the area under the torque-against-angle graph is a rectangle and multiplication is enough. If the motor's torque faded with speed we would need the actual area instead."
      },
      {
        "do": "Set $W = \\Delta K = \\frac{1}{2}I\\omega^2 - 0$.",
        "why": "Energy rather than dynamics, because the problem handed us an **angle** and asked for a **speed** — no time is mentioned anywhere. Going via $\\alpha$ would force us to invent a time and then eliminate it again."
      },
      {
        "do": "$\\omega = \\sqrt{2W/I} = \\sqrt{2(377)/3.0} = \\sqrt{251} = 15.9$ rad/s.",
        "why": "Starting from rest means the initial kinetic energy is zero, so all $377$ J is in the final spin — no subtraction to get wrong."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$W = 3.8 \\times 10^2$ J; the grindstone ends at $16$ rad/s.",
    "check": "Redo it with no energy at all, using Unit 5's rotational kinematics. $\\alpha = \\tau/I = 12/3.0 = 4.0$ rad/s², and the equation sheet gives $\\omega^2 = \\omega_0^2 + 2\\alpha(\\theta - \\theta_0) = 0 + 2(4.0)(31.4) = 251$, so $\\omega = 15.9$ rad/s ✓. Two independent routes agreeing is much stronger evidence than re-substituting into $W = \\tau\\Delta\\theta$."
  },
  "trap": "Leaving $\\Delta\\theta$ in revolutions. Five revolutions is $31.4$ radians, and using $5.0$ makes the work — and therefore the energy — too small by a factor of $2\\pi$. The second trap is subtler: reporting the work as though it were the *net* work when friction or a load torque is also acting. $W = \\tau\\Delta\\theta$ gives the work of **that one torque**; only the net torque's work equals $\\Delta K$."
},
{
  "id": "rem-angular-momentum",
  "group": "u6",
  "prereqs": [
    "rem-rotational-ke",
    "mom-momentum",
    "rot-inertia"
  ],
  "source": "CED 6.3",
  "title": "Angular momentum, for a body and for a particle",
  "analogy": "Stand at the edge of a roundabout and watch a friend walk past in a straight line across the playground. From where you stand they sweep round you — slowly when they are far off, quickly as they pass closest. They are not going in a circle, but relative to you they are going *round*. That sweeping is what angular momentum measures.",
  "plain": "Angular momentum is the rotational counterpart of ordinary momentum. There are two expressions for it and they are not two different quantities — they are the same quantity, computed in the two situations you meet.\n\nFor a rigid body spinning about an axis, $L = I\\omega$. Rotational inertia in place of mass, angular speed in place of speed. That one is easy to believe because it matches $p = mv$ symbol for symbol.\n\nFor a single object treated as a point — a ball, a puck, a satellite — the angular momentum **about a chosen point** is $L = rmv\\sin\\theta$, where $r$ is the distance from the chosen point to the object and $\\theta$ is the angle between that line and the velocity. The surprise here is that an object travelling in a perfectly straight line has non-zero angular momentum about any point that is not on its line of travel. It is not rotating. It still has $L$.\n\nBecause you had to *choose* the point, angular momentum is only ever defined relative to a stated axis. \"The angular momentum of the ball\" is an incomplete sentence. \"The angular momentum of the ball about the pivot\" is a physical quantity.",
  "depth": "$$L = I\\omega$$\n\n$$L = rmv\\sin\\theta$$\n\n**Both are on the provided equation sheet** (right column, CED p. 212), printed one under the other, which is the sheet's way of telling you they describe the same thing.\n\nThe quantity $r\\sin\\theta$ is the **perpendicular distance from the chosen point to the line along which the object is moving** — sometimes called the lever arm or impact parameter. Writing $L = mv(r\\sin\\theta)$ rather than $L = (rm)(v\\sin\\theta)$ is usually the faster reading: find the perpendicular miss-distance, multiply by the linear momentum, done. Note the structural echo of torque, $\\tau = rF\\sin\\theta$, which is on the same sheet — angular momentum is to momentum as torque is to force.\n\nFor a free particle moving in a straight line at constant velocity, $r$ grows and $\\sin\\theta$ shrinks in exactly compensating amounts, so $r\\sin\\theta$ — and therefore $L$ — is constant. That is not a coincidence either: nothing is exerting a torque on it, and [[rem-angular-impulse]] says no torque means no change in $L$.\n\nThe CED sets an explicit boundary here. You are expected to handle the **magnitude** of angular momentum with one-dimensional sign conventions — pick one sense of rotation as positive and be consistent — but the direction of angular momentum as a vector in space is beyond the scope of AP Physics 1. So no right-hand rules and no three-dimensional vector arguments are required, or expected.",
  "formula": "L = I ω               rigid body about its axis     [ON the equation sheet]\nL = r m v sinθ        any object about a point      [ON the equation sheet]\n\n  r sinθ = perpendicular distance from the point to the line of motion\n  units: kg·m²/s\n  choose ONE sense of rotation as positive and keep it all problem\n\nboundary: AP Physics 1 handles magnitudes with 1-D signs only —\nthe direction of L as a vector is outside the course.",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A $0.50$ kg ball slides across frictionless ice in a straight line at a steady $8.0$ m/s. A point $O$ is marked on the ice. At one instant the ball is $3.0$ m from $O$ and its velocity makes an angle of 30° with the line joining $O$ to the ball. Find the ball's angular momentum about $O$ at that instant, and state what happens to it a second later.",
    "given": "m = 0.50 kg; v = 8.0 m/s constant; r = 3.0 m; θ = 30°, and sin 30° = 1/2 from the trig table on CED p. 211.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Use $L = rmv\\sin\\theta$, not $L = I\\omega$.",
        "why": "The ball is a single object moving along a line, not a rigid body turning about an axle. There is no $\\omega$ to use and no $I$ to quote."
      },
      {
        "do": "$L = (3.0)(0.50)(8.0)\\sin\\theta$ with $\\sin\\theta = 0.5$, so $L = 6.0$ kg·m²/s.",
        "why": "The $\\sin\\theta$ is what discards the part of the motion aimed straight at or away from $O$. Motion along the line to $O$ does no sweeping and contributes no angular momentum."
      },
      {
        "do": "Identify $r\\sin\\theta = 3.0 \\times 0.5 = 1.5$ m as the perpendicular distance from $O$ to the ball's path.",
        "why": "This is the geometric meaning of the formula and it is what makes the next step obvious rather than surprising."
      },
      {
        "do": "A second later $L$ is still $6.0$ kg·m²/s.",
        "why": "The path is a fixed straight line, so its perpendicular distance from $O$ never changes, and neither do $m$ or $v$. Equivalently: no force acts, so no torque about $O$ acts, so $L$ about $O$ cannot change."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$L = 6.0$ kg·m²/s about $O$, and it stays at $6.0$ kg·m²/s.",
    "check": "Compute it a different way at a different instant. Take the moment of closest approach, where the ball is $1.5$ m from $O$ and moving at 90° to the line, where $\\sin\\theta = 1$: $L = (1.5)(0.50)(8.0)(1) = 6.0$ kg·m²/s ✓. Two different $r$ values and two different angles giving one number is a real test of the reasoning, not a re-substitution."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Why bother with angular momentum for something moving in a straight line? Because the moment it hits something off-centre — a door, a rod pinned at one end, a merry-go-round — that straight-line motion becomes rotation, and the conserved bookkeeping across the collision is angular momentum about the pivot. If you had assigned the incoming object $L = 0$ because it \"was not rotating\", you would have manufactured angular momentum out of nothing.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "A tempting shortcut is to define angular momentum as $L = mvr$ and drop the $\\sin\\theta$, on the grounds that it is usually a right angle anyway. It is a right angle for circular motion, which is why the shortcut survives so long. It fails for every straight-line and elliptical case — exactly the cases where angular momentum earns its keep, such as [[rem-orbital-motion]].",
  "trap": "Answering \"zero, it is not rotating\". Angular momentum is defined about a point, and any object whose path misses that point has angular momentum about it. The mirror-image trap is quoting an $L$ without saying about what: change the reference point and the number changes, so an unqualified value is not an answer."
},
{
  "id": "rem-angular-impulse",
  "group": "u6",
  "prereqs": [
    "rem-angular-momentum",
    "mom-impulse",
    "rot-newton-second-rotational"
  ],
  "source": "CED 6.3",
  "title": "Angular impulse and the rotational impulse–momentum theorem",
  "analogy": "To get a heavy door swinging you can shove hard for an instant or lean gently for several seconds. The door ends up moving the same way either time, provided the torque multiplied by the time is the same. That product is the thing that matters.",
  "plain": "Angular impulse is torque multiplied by the time for which it acts. Its whole point is that it equals the change in angular momentum:\n\n$$\\Delta L = \\tau\\,\\Delta t$$\n\nThis is the rotational twin of $\\vec{J} = \\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{avg}}\\Delta t = \\Delta\\vec{p}$. Read left to right it is a prediction — apply this torque for that long and you know exactly how much angular momentum you have added. Read right to left it is a measurement — if something's angular momentum changed, a torque acted, and you can work out how much.\n\nWhen the torque is not steady, the product becomes an area. The area under a graph of torque against **time** is the angular impulse. And going the other way, the slope of a graph of angular momentum against time is the net torque at that moment.",
  "depth": "$$\\Delta L = \\tau\\,\\Delta t$$\n\n**This equation is on the provided equation sheet** (right column, CED p. 212). The word \"angular impulse\" does not appear on the sheet — only this equation — so you need to recognise that the product $\\tau\\Delta t$ *is* the angular impulse.\n\nThe CED derives it from the rotational form of Newton's second law: $$\\tau_{\\mathrm{net}} = \\frac{\\Delta L}{\\Delta t} = I\\frac{\\Delta\\omega}{\\Delta t} = I\\alpha$$ The middle-to-right step assumes the rotational inertia is constant. That assumption is worth holding on to, because the whole of [[rem-conservation-angular-momentum]] turns on cases where $I$ is **not** constant — and there $\\tau_{\\mathrm{net}} = \\Delta L/\\Delta t$ still holds while $\\tau_{\\mathrm{net}} = I\\alpha$ does not.\n\nTwo graph facts the CED asks for by name. The net torque is the **slope** of an angular-momentum-against-time graph. The angular impulse is the **area** under a net-torque-against-time graph. Slope and area are inverse readings of the same pair of axes, which is why they come as a pair.\n\nAngular impulse points the same way as the torque that delivered it. Within the one-dimensional conventions this course uses, that reduces to a sign: a torque in the direction you called positive increases $L$, one in the other direction decreases it.",
  "formula": "angular impulse = τ Δt = ΔL = L − L₀        [ΔL = τΔt is ON the sheet]\n\n  units: N·m·s = kg·m²/s  (identical to the units of L, as they must be)\n\nτ_net = ΔL/Δt = I Δω/Δt = I α     — last equality needs CONSTANT I\n\ngraphs:   slope of L-vs-t  =  net torque\n          area under τ-vs-t =  angular impulse = ΔL",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A wheel with rotational inertia $0.20$ kg·m² is at rest on a frictionless axle. A motor applies a torque that grows steadily from $0$ at $t = 0$ to $3.0$ N·m at $t = 4.0$ s, then switches off. How fast is the wheel turning at $t = 4.0$ s?",
    "given": "I = 0.20 kg·m²; ω0 = 0; torque rises linearly from 0 to 3.0 N·m over 4.0 s.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Refuse to use $\\tau = I\\alpha$ with $\\tau = 3.0$ N·m.",
        "why": "The torque is not constant, so there is no single $\\alpha$ to put into the kinematics equations. Using the final torque would assume it acted the whole time."
      },
      {
        "do": "Take the area under the torque-against-time graph: a triangle of base $4.0$ s and height $3.0$ N·m, so area $= \\frac{1}{2}(4.0)(3.0) = 6.0$ N·m·s.",
        "why": "Area under torque-against-time is angular impulse whatever shape the graph is. A varying torque costs you nothing here — this is exactly the situation the impulse form was built for."
      },
      {
        "do": "$\\Delta L = 6.0$ kg·m²/s, and since $L_0 = 0$, $L = 6.0$ kg·m²/s.",
        "why": "N·m·s and kg·m²/s are the same units written two ways, so the area *is* an angular momentum with no conversion needed."
      },
      {
        "do": "$L = I\\omega$, so $\\omega = 6.0/0.20 = 30$ rad/s.",
        "why": "The wheel is rigid and its $I$ is fixed, so converting between $L$ and $\\omega$ is a single division. If the wheel had changed shape, this last step is the one that would break."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$\\omega = 30$ rad/s.",
    "check": "Independent route via an average. A torque rising linearly from $0$ to $3.0$ N·m has a mean value of $1.5$ N·m, and a constant $1.5$ N·m for $4.0$ s gives $\\tau\\Delta t = 6.0$ kg·m²/s — the same angular impulse, reached without any triangle. As a sanity bound, a constant $3.0$ N·m for the full $4.0$ s would give $60$ rad/s, so the true answer must be under that ✓."
  },
  "trap": "Confusing the two graph areas. Area under torque-against-**angle** is work, in joules; area under torque-against-**time** is angular impulse, in kg·m²/s. Both graphs have torque up the side and both are read as areas, and a student who does not check the horizontal axis will answer one question with the other's number. The units are the giveaway."
},
{
  "id": "rem-conservation-angular-momentum",
  "group": "u6",
  "prereqs": [
    "rem-angular-impulse",
    "mom-conservation"
  ],
  "source": "CED 6.4",
  "title": "Conservation of angular momentum",
  "analogy": "A swivel chair spun by someone else keeps turning until something outside stops it. Anything you do with your own arms and legs while sitting in it can change how fast you turn, but it cannot start you turning and it cannot stop you. Inside jobs redistribute; only outside jobs change the total.",
  "plain": "If the net external torque on a system is zero, that system's total angular momentum does not change. That is the whole statement, and everything hard about it lives in the two words \"system\" and \"external\".\n\nThe total angular momentum of a system is the sum of the angular momenta of its parts, all measured about the same axis. Parts can trade angular momentum with each other freely — by Newton's third law, whatever angular impulse one part gives another comes straight back, equal and opposite, so the internal trades cancel in the total. Only an interaction with *something outside the system* can move the total.\n\nThis is why the choice of system is a decision and not a formality. Draw the boundary around the skater alone and her angular momentum is constant. Draw it around one of her arms and it is not, because the rest of her is now outside and is pulling on it.\n\nThe most useful consequence: a system that changes shape can change its angular speed without any torque at all. Move mass closer to the axis, and $I$ falls; since $L = I\\omega$ has to stay put, $\\omega$ must rise.",
  "depth": "$$\\text{if } \\tau_{\\mathrm{net,ext}} = 0 \\text{ then } L_{\\mathrm{total}} \\text{ is constant, so } I_0\\omega_0 = I\\omega$$\n\n**No conservation statement appears on the equation sheet.** What the sheet gives you is $\\Delta L = \\tau\\Delta t$, and conservation is what that becomes when $\\tau = 0$. Knowing it as a *consequence* rather than a separate rule is what stops you applying it when a torque is present.\n\nThe CED phrases it in two complementary ways, and both are worth carrying. Angular momentum is conserved in all interactions — that is the underlying law. And: whether the angular momentum *of a chosen system* is constant depends on whether the net external torque on that system is zero. If it is not zero, angular momentum is transferred between system and environment, and the amount transferred is exactly the angular impulse.\n\nHow to check for zero external torque in practice. A force applied at the axis has $r = 0$ and so contributes no torque about that axis — this is why the axle of a merry-go-round can push as hard as it likes without disturbing the angular momentum about the vertical axis. A force parallel to the position vector has $\\sin\\theta = 0$ and likewise contributes nothing; that is the case for gravity on an orbiting satellite. Gravity and the normal force on a horizontal turntable are both vertical and produce no torque about the vertical axis.\n\nA caution about scope. \"Angular momentum is conserved\" and \"kinetic energy is conserved\" are independent claims, and the second is usually false when a system changes shape or things stick together. That is important enough to have its own concept: [[rem-l-conserved-k-not]].",
  "formula": "τ_net,ext = 0     ⟹     L_total constant     ⟹     I₀ω₀ = I ω\n\n  not on the equation sheet — it is ΔL = τΔt with τ = 0\n\ntotal for a system:   L_total = L₁ + L₂ + …   (all about the SAME axis)\n\nzero external torque, common reasons:\n  force acts AT the axis            → r = 0     → τ = 0\n  force acts ALONG the radius       → sinθ = 0  → τ = 0\n  force is parallel to the axis     → no turning about that axis",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A playground roundabout is a uniform platform with rotational inertia $120$ kg·m² about its vertical axle, turning freely at $1.5$ rad/s. A $30$ kg child, standing still on the ground, steps straight onto the rim at radius $2.0$ m and stays there. Find the new angular speed.",
    "given": "I_platform = 120 kg·m²; ω0 = 1.5 rad/s; m_child = 30 kg at r = 2.0 m; the child arrives with no motion of her own.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Choose the system to be platform **and** child together.",
        "why": "The two exert large, messy friction forces on each other while she settles. Putting both inside the boundary makes all of that internal, so it cancels in the total and we never need to know any of it."
      },
      {
        "do": "Argue that the external torque about the axle is zero: gravity and the normal forces are vertical, and the axle's force acts at $r = 0$.",
        "why": "Conservation is not a free assumption; it is a claim that has to be justified from the forces. Skipping this step is where marks go."
      },
      {
        "do": "Before: $L_0 = I\\omega_0 = 120 \\times 1.5 = 180$ kg·m²/s. The child contributes nothing, because she is not moving.",
        "why": "Her angular momentum about the axle is $rmv\\sin\\theta$ with $v = 0$."
      },
      {
        "do": "After: treat the child as a point mass on the rim, $I_{\\mathrm{child}} = mr^2 = 30(2.0)^2 = 120$ kg·m², so $I_{\\mathrm{tot}} = 120 + 120 = 240$ kg·m².",
        "why": "$I = \\sum m_i r_i^2$ from the equation sheet, with the child as a single term. Rotational inertias about a common axis add."
      },
      {
        "do": "$\\omega = L_0/I_{\\mathrm{tot}} = 180/240 = 0.75$ rad/s.",
        "why": "$L$ is the quantity that carried across the messy interval, so we divide it by the new $I$ rather than trying to track the slowing down."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$\\omega = 0.75$ rad/s.",
    "check": "The rotational inertia exactly doubled, so the angular speed must exactly halve: $1.5/2 = 0.75$ ✓. Now an independent audit that also catches a conceptual error: kinetic energy went from $\\frac{1}{2}(120)(1.5)^2 = 135$ J to $\\frac{1}{2}(240)(0.75)^2 = 67.5$ J. Half the energy is gone, into friction under her shoes. That is what should happen when something lands and sticks, and if the energy had come out *unchanged* it would be a sign the working was wrong."
  },
  "trap": "Applying conservation without checking for external torques, or checking about the wrong axis. A torque can be zero about one point and enormous about another, so \"there is no external torque\" is only meaningful with an axis named. The other frequent loss is drawing the system boundary too tightly — around the platform only, say — and then wondering why the numbers do not balance. They do not balance because the child is outside, pushing."
},
{
  "id": "rem-l-conserved-k-not",
  "group": "u6",
  "prereqs": [
    "rem-conservation-angular-momentum",
    "rem-rotational-ke"
  ],
  "source": "CED 6.4",
  "title": "Conserved angular momentum does not mean conserved energy",
  "analogy": "Pulling a bucket up a well conserves the amount of water in it and does not conserve the effort you put in. Nothing about the water staying constant promises anything about the energy — they are separate accounts, and you paid into one of them.",
  "plain": "This is the single most reliable trap in the unit. A skater spinning freely pulls her arms in. Her rotational inertia falls, her angular momentum is unchanged, so her angular speed rises. Almost everyone gets that far. Then comes the question of kinetic energy, and the instinct is that it must also stay the same, because two conservation laws feel like they should travel together.\n\nThey do not. If $L$ is fixed and $I$ halves, then $\\omega$ doubles — but kinetic energy goes as $\\omega^2$ and only as $I$ to the first power, so $\\frac{1}{2}I\\omega^2$ **doubles too**. The energy went up.\n\nThat is not a violation of anything. The skater did work: pulling her arms inwards against the outward tendency of the rotation took muscular effort, and that effort is where the extra joules came from. Let her arms back out and she slows down again and gets the energy back — the arms do work on her.\n\nThe reverse case is just as common and just as misread. When something lands on a spinning platform and sticks, or when two rotating parts clutch together, angular momentum is conserved and kinetic energy **falls**, into friction and heat. Both directions, same lesson: $L$ and $K$ are different accounts.",
  "depth": "Substituting $\\omega = L/I$ into $K = \\frac{1}{2}I\\omega^2$ gives the cleanest statement of the whole issue:\n\n$$K = \\frac{L^2}{2I}$$\n\n**This is not on the equation sheet** — it is one line of algebra from two expressions that are, $K = \\frac{1}{2}I\\omega^2$ and $L = I\\omega$. It is worth deriving once and remembering, because it makes the dependence obvious: at fixed $L$, kinetic energy is inversely proportional to rotational inertia. Halve $I$ and you double $K$; double $I$ and you halve $K$. Energy is conserved only in the one case where $I$ does not change, and in that case nothing interesting was happening anyway.\n\nWhere the energy comes from and goes, physically. The parts of a spinning body follow curved paths, which requires an inward force. To move a part further inwards you must pull harder than that, and you pull it through a displacement, so you do positive work on it — arms in, energy up. Letting it move outwards, the force and the displacement oppose, so the work is negative — arms out, energy down. In the sticking case the energy is dissipated by kinetic friction between surfaces that are sliding past each other, exactly as in a perfectly inelastic linear collision.\n\nThe parallel with linear momentum is worth making explicit, because it is the same structure you already accepted in Unit 4. In a perfectly inelastic collision, momentum is conserved and kinetic energy is not, and nobody finds that troubling. Angular momentum behaves the same way. The reason it feels stranger is only that the skater speeds *up*, so it looks like something was got for free.\n\nHow to decide which applies. Ask whether any agent inside the system did work or dissipated energy. Muscles, motors and springs put energy in; friction and sticking take it out; a rigid body that simply keeps spinning does neither. Angular momentum, meanwhile, only cares about external torques and ignores all of that.",
  "formula": "K = L² / (2I)        derived from K = ½Iω² and L = Iω — NOT on the sheet\n\n  at fixed L:   K ∝ 1/I\n\n  I halves   → ω doubles   → K DOUBLES     (something did work: muscles)\n  I doubles  → ω halves    → K HALVES      (something dissipated it: friction)\n  I constant → ω constant  → K constant\n\nL conserved  ⇏  K conserved.   Two independent accounts.",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A skater spins freely on frictionless ice with her arms out, with rotational inertia $4.0$ kg·m² about her vertical axis, at $2.0$ rad/s. She pulls her arms in, reducing her rotational inertia to $1.6$ kg·m². Find her new angular speed, her kinetic energy before and after, and account for the difference.",
    "given": "I0 = 4.0 kg·m²; ω0 = 2.0 rad/s; I = 1.6 kg·m²; no external torque about the vertical axis.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "$L = I_0\\omega_0 = 4.0 \\times 2.0 = 8.0$ kg·m²/s, and this is the quantity that survives the manoeuvre.",
        "why": "The ice is frictionless and gravity acts vertically, so nothing outside her exerts a torque about her spin axis. Her arms are inside the system, so their pulling is internal."
      },
      {
        "do": "$\\omega = L/I = 8.0/1.6 = 5.0$ rad/s.",
        "why": "Use $L$, not $\\tau = I\\alpha$. Her rotational inertia is changing during the manoeuvre, and $\\tau_{\\mathrm{net}} = I\\alpha$ assumes constant $I$ — the form that survives is $\\tau_{\\mathrm{net}} = \\Delta L/\\Delta t$."
      },
      {
        "do": "$K_0 = \\frac{1}{2}(4.0)(2.0)^2 = 8.0$ J; $K = \\frac{1}{2}(1.6)(5.0)^2 = 20$ J.",
        "why": "Evaluate the energy from scratch at each state rather than assuming it carried over. Assuming it carried over is the error this whole concept exists to prevent."
      },
      {
        "do": "$\\Delta K = +12$ J, supplied by her muscles doing work pulling her arms inwards.",
        "why": "Energy has to come from somewhere, and naming the source is the difference between an answer and a full answer. The arms move inwards while she pulls inwards, so the work is positive."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$\\omega = 5.0$ rad/s; $K$ rises from $8.0$ J to $20$ J; the extra $12$ J is work done by the skater's muscles.",
    "check": "Use the derived form, which is an independent route: $K = L^2/(2I)$ gives $K_0 = 8.0^2/(2 \\times 4.0) = 8.0$ J ✓ and $K = 8.0^2/(2 \\times 1.6) = 20$ J ✓. It also predicts the ratio without any arithmetic: $K$ should rise by the factor $I_0/I = 4.0/1.6 = 2.5$, and $20/8.0 = 2.5$ ✓."
  },
  "rejectedAlternative": "The plausible wrong move is to set $\\frac{1}{2}I_0\\omega_0^2 = \\frac{1}{2}I\\omega^2$ and solve — conserving energy instead of angular momentum. It gives $\\omega = \\omega_0\\sqrt{I_0/I} = 2.0\\sqrt{2.5} = 3.2$ rad/s, a plausible-looking number that is simply wrong. The reason it is wrong is not that energy conservation is a bad law but that this system is not isolated *energetically*: the skater is a source. It is isolated *rotationally*, which is a different statement.",
  "trap": "Writing \"angular momentum is conserved, therefore kinetic energy is conserved\". It is the kind of distinction a question can be built on, because the two conservation laws look interchangeable and are not. The safe habit: whenever a question tells you a rotating system changed shape or picked something up, compute $K$ twice and compare — never carry it across."
},
{
  "id": "rem-rolling-without-slipping",
  "group": "u6",
  "prereqs": [
    "rem-total-kinetic-energy",
    "rot-linear-rotational-link"
  ],
  "source": "CED 6.5",
  "title": "Rolling without slipping, and what friction is doing",
  "analogy": "Look at the tyre marks a car leaves when it pulls away normally: none. Now look at the ones it leaves when the wheels spin on ice: black, and hot. In the first case the rubber touching the road is momentarily standing still on it. In the second it is sliding, and sliding is what burns energy.",
  "plain": "Rolling without slipping means the object's surface does not slide across the ground. That single condition ties its travelling to its turning:\n\n$$\\Delta x_{\\mathrm{cm}} = r\\Delta\\theta$$\n\n$$v_{\\mathrm{cm}} = r\\omega$$\n\n$$a_{\\mathrm{cm}} = r\\alpha$$\n\nRoll a wheel through one full turn and it advances by exactly one circumference, because the length of rim laid down equals the ground covered. That is all the first equation says; the other two are the same statement per second and per second squared.\n\nThe strange and useful fact is that the point of the object actually touching the ground is, at that instant, not moving at all. It is moving forward at $v$ because the whole object is, and backwards at $\\omega r = v$ because of the spin, and the two cancel. Meanwhile the top of the wheel is doing both at once and moves at $2v$.\n\nSo the friction at the contact is **static** friction, not kinetic. Static friction acts where there is no sliding, which means its point of application never moves through a displacement, which means it does no work and dissipates no energy. It still exerts a torque, and that torque is what gets the object spinning — it just does not cost anything in energy.",
  "depth": "$$\\Delta x_{\\mathrm{cm}} = r\\Delta\\theta$$\n\n$$v_{\\mathrm{cm}} = r\\omega$$\n\n$$a_{\\mathrm{cm}} = r\\alpha$$\n\n**The equation sheet carries all three, though not in those words.** CED p. 212 prints $\\Delta x_{\\mathrm{cm}} = r\\Delta\\theta$, $v = r\\omega$ and $a_T = r\\alpha$; CED 6.5 writes the last two with a $\\mathrm{cm}$ subscript. Same relationships. What the sheet does not tell you is **when** you may use them, which is only when the object rolls without slipping.\n\nWhy static friction can supply a torque yet dissipate nothing. Energy is dissipated when a force acts through a displacement *at its point of application relative to the surface*. In rolling, the material at the contact is instantaneously at rest with respect to the ground, so the contact point does not slide and the friction force does no work on the system. The CED states this outright: for ideal cases, rolling without slipping implies the frictional force does not dissipate energy. It is the licence that makes energy conservation usable for rolling problems.\n\nThe slipping case is different in kind, not degree. When a wheel slips, the contact material *is* sliding across the surface, so the friction is kinetic, its point of application moves relative to the surface, and it dissipates energy. The CED also warns that when slipping, the centre-of-mass motion and the rotational motion **cannot** be related — $v = r\\omega$ is simply false, and you have no constraint linking them. A spinning wheel on ice can have huge $\\omega$ and $v \\approx 0$.\n\nTwo boundary statements apply here. Rolling friction — the small resistance from a real deforming tyre — is outside the course entirely. And the quantitative relationships during rolling-while-slipping are outside the course too; you are expected to describe qualitatively what happens to the linear and angular quantities (typically, kinetic friction slows the spinning and speeds the sliding until $v = r\\omega$ is reached and the object begins to roll), but not to compute it.",
  "formula": "ROLLING WITHOUT SLIPPING  (and only then):\n\n  Δx_cm = r Δθ        [on the sheet]\n  v_cm  = r ω         [on the sheet as v = rω]\n  a_cm  = r α         [on the sheet as a_T = rα]\n\n  contact point speed = 0      centre = v      top of wheel = 2v\n  friction is STATIC → does no work → no energy dissipated\n\nSLIPPING:  v ≠ rω, no constraint at all; friction is KINETIC and\n           DOES dissipate energy.  (Quantitative treatment is out of scope.)",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A wheel of radius $0.35$ m rolls without slipping in a straight line, covering $20$ m in $5.0$ s at a steady rate. Find the number of revolutions it makes, its angular speed, and the speed of the point at the very top of the wheel.",
    "given": "r = 0.35 m; Δx_cm = 20 m; Δt = 5.0 s; rolling without slipping.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "State the constraint first: because it rolls without slipping, $\\Delta x_{\\mathrm{cm}} = r\\Delta\\theta$ and $v_{\\mathrm{cm}} = r\\omega$.",
        "why": "These are not general facts about wheels. Written down as conditional on the no-slip assumption, they stay honest, and you will notice if a later part of a question removes that assumption."
      },
      {
        "do": "$\\Delta\\theta = 20/0.35 = 57.1$ rad, which is $57.1/2\\pi = 9.09$ revolutions.",
        "why": "The constraint equation delivers radians, because it comes from arc length $s = r\\theta$. Convert to revolutions only at the end, for reporting."
      },
      {
        "do": "$v_{\\mathrm{cm}} = 20/5.0 = 4.0$ m/s, so $\\omega = v_{\\mathrm{cm}}/r = 4.0/0.35 = 11.4$ rad/s.",
        "why": "The speed of the centre is ordinary kinematics; the constraint is only needed to turn it into an angular speed."
      },
      {
        "do": "Top of the wheel: $v_{\\mathrm{top}} = v_{\\mathrm{cm}} + \\omega r = 4.0 + 4.0 = 8.0$ m/s.",
        "why": "Every point carries the centre's motion plus its own circular motion about the centre. At the top those point the same way and add; at the contact they oppose and cancel to zero."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$9.1$ revolutions; $\\omega = 11$ rad/s; the top of the wheel moves at $8.0$ m/s.",
    "check": "Get the revolutions from pure geometry instead, with no constraint equation at all: the circumference is $2\\pi(0.35) = 2.199$ m, and laying that down $20/2.199 = 9.10$ times covers $20$ m ✓. The top-speed result also passes a limiting test — if the wheel were sliding without spinning, every point would move at $4.0$ m/s and the top would not be special; the factor of two appears only because it is rolling."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Why insist on the phrase \"without slipping\" every time, when every real wheel you have seen rolls? Because the constraint is doing enormous work in these problems — it is the only reason you can convert between $v$ and $\\omega$, and the only reason you are allowed to use energy conservation despite friction being present. Both licences vanish the moment it slips.",
  "trap": "Two errors. Using $v = r\\omega$ for a wheel that is skidding or spinning on ice, where there is no relationship at all between $v$ and $\\omega$. And assuming that because friction is present, mechanical energy must be lost — for rolling without slipping the friction is static, does no work, and takes nothing."
},
{
  "id": "rem-rolling-energy",
  "group": "u6",
  "prereqs": [
    "rem-rolling-without-slipping",
    "rem-total-kinetic-energy",
    "enr-mechanical-energy-conservation"
  ],
  "source": "CED 6.5",
  "title": "Rolling down a slope: how the energy splits",
  "analogy": "Two people are given the same wages and told to save a fixed fraction. The one told to save more has less to spend, however much they earn. A rolling object is the same: gravity pays out the same energy per kilogram, and the shape of the object fixes how much has to be locked into the spin.",
  "plain": "Release a hoop and a solid disc together at the top of a ramp. They do not arrive together. The disc wins, every time, and it is not close.\n\nThe reason is the energy split. Gravity releases $mgh$ to both. But that energy has two places to go — into travelling and into spinning — and the rolling constraint means an object cannot go fast without also spinning fast. A hoop has all its mass out at the rim, so a given angular speed costs it more energy than it costs the disc. More of the hoop's budget is locked into the spin, less is left for travelling, and it arrives slower.\n\nHere is the part that catches people. The answer does not depend on the mass, and it does not depend on the radius. A heavy hoop and a light hoop tie. A big disc and a small disc tie. Only the *shape* — how the mass is distributed relative to the radius — decides.\n\nAnd every rolling object loses to a block sliding down a frictionless ramp, which has no spin to pay for and gets the whole $mgh$ as speed.",
  "depth": "Write the rotational inertia of any rolling body as $I = \\beta mr^2$, where $\\beta$ is a pure number set by the shape: $\\beta = 1$ for a thin hoop, $\\frac{1}{2}$ for a uniform disc or solid cylinder, $\\frac{2}{5}$ for a solid sphere. **None of those values is on the equation sheet** — the sheet has only $I = \\sum m_i r_i^2$ and $I' = I_{\\mathrm{cm}} + Md^2$ — so a problem that needs one has to hand it to you.\n\nNow conserve energy from rest at height $h$, using [[rem-rolling-without-slipping]] to write $\\omega = v/r$:\n\n$$mgh = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2 + \\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\beta mr^2\\right)\\left(\\frac{v}{r}\\right)^2$$\n\n$$mgh = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2\\left(1 + \\beta\\right)$$\n\n$$v = \\sqrt{\\frac{2gh}{1 + \\beta}}$$\n\nBoth $m$ and $r$ cancel — $m$ because it multiplies every term, $r$ because $\\beta mr^2$ meets $v^2/r^2$. That cancellation is the algebraic content of \"mass and radius do not matter\", and it is worth doing once rather than memorising the conclusion.\n\nThe formula also tells you the ranking without further work, since $v$ falls as $\\beta$ rises: sphere ($\\frac{2}{5}$) beats disc ($\\frac{1}{2}$) beats hoop ($1$), and a frictionless sliding block ($\\beta = 0$, the limit of no rotation) beats all of them at $v = \\sqrt{2gh}$.\n\nWhy energy conservation is legitimate here despite friction being present: the friction is static, so it does no work. And why energy rather than forces: the force route needs a free-body diagram, a torque equation, the rolling constraint to link $a$ and $\\alpha$, simultaneous equations for the friction force, and then a kinematics step — five moves where energy takes one. Use forces when the question asks for the friction force or the acceleration; use energy when it asks for a speed after a known drop.",
  "formula": "write   I = β m r²        β = 1 (hoop), ½ (disc/cylinder), ⅖ (solid sphere)\n                          NONE of these β values is on the equation sheet, and the CED says a\n                          question supplies any it needs — learn the ORDER, not the numbers\n\nfrom rest, rolling without slipping, dropping a height h:\n\n      m g h = ½ m v² (1 + β)        →       v = √( 2gh / (1 + β) )\n\n  m cancels, r cancels — only the SHAPE matters\n  bigger β → more energy locked in the spin → slower arrival\n\n  sliding block β=0 : v = √(2gh)      fastest, nothing to spin\n  sphere      β=2/5 : v = √(10gh/7)\n  disc        β=1/2 : v = √(4gh/3)\n  hoop        β=1   : v = √(gh)       slowest",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A uniform disc ($I = \\frac{1}{2}mr^2$) and a thin hoop ($I = mr^2$) are released from rest at the same moment from the top of a ramp and roll without slipping down a vertical drop of $1.5$ m. Find the speed of each at the bottom. Does it matter that the hoop is three times as massive as the disc?",
    "given": "h = 1.5 m; g = 9.8 m/s² (CED p. 211); I_disc = ½mr², I_hoop = mr²; both start from rest and roll without slipping.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Choose energy conservation rather than forces.",
        "why": "We are given a height and asked for a speed, with no time and no question about the friction force. Energy connects those two directly. The force route would require solving for the friction force first, which the question never asks about."
      },
      {
        "do": "Justify that mechanical energy is conserved: the objects roll without slipping, so the friction is static and does no work.",
        "why": "Friction is present and visible in the problem. Without this sentence, using energy conservation is an unjustified assumption — and it is a stated mark in this kind of question."
      },
      {
        "do": "Write $mgh = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2 + \\frac{1}{2}I\\omega^2$ and substitute $\\omega = v/r$ and $I = \\beta mr^2$ to get $mgh = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2(1 + \\beta)$.",
        "why": "Substituting the constraint before solving is what collapses two unknowns ($v$ and $\\omega$) into one. Leaving $\\omega$ in place leaves the problem unsolvable."
      },
      {
        "do": "Disc, $\\beta = \\frac{1}{2}$: $v = \\sqrt{2(9.8)(1.5)/1.5} = \\sqrt{19.6} = 4.4$ m/s.",
        "why": "$1 + \\beta = 1.5$ for the disc. Note that neither $m$ nor $r$ was ever needed — they had already cancelled."
      },
      {
        "do": "Hoop, $\\beta = 1$: $v = \\sqrt{2(9.8)(1.5)/2} = \\sqrt{14.7} = 3.8$ m/s.",
        "why": "Same expression, one number changed. The whole difference between the two objects lives in $\\beta$."
      },
      {
        "do": "The hoop's larger mass is irrelevant.",
        "why": "$m$ multiplies every term in the energy equation and divides out. Gravity supplies more energy to a heavier object and that object needs proportionally more to reach any given speed; the two effects cancel exactly."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "Disc $4.4$ m/s, hoop $3.8$ m/s. The disc arrives first, and the mass difference makes no difference at all.",
    "check": "A limiting case the formula must satisfy: set $\\beta = 0$, meaning nothing to spin, and it should reduce to the familiar free-fall-from-height result $v = \\sqrt{2gh} = \\sqrt{29.4} = 5.4$ m/s. It does ✓ — and both rolling answers come out below it, which they must, since some of the energy went into spinning. The ratio is also checkable: $v_{\\mathrm{disc}}/v_{\\mathrm{hoop}} = \\sqrt{2/1.5} = 1.155$, and $4.4/3.8 = 1.15$ ✓."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Why is energy the right tool here and force the wrong one? Because the question spans a whole journey — top of ramp to bottom — and asks only about the endpoints. Energy methods are indifferent to what happened in between: the ramp can be curved, the angle can change, and the answer is the same as long as the drop is $1.5$ m and nothing dissipates. A force analysis would need the angle, would need it to be constant, and would deliver an acceleration you then have to integrate. Reach for energy whenever the question is \"how fast after falling this far\".",
  "trap": "Expecting mass or radius to matter, and hunting for values that were never needed. The related loss is using $v = \\sqrt{2gh}$ for a rolling object: that is the sliding answer, and it is too fast, because it silently gives the object's rotational share to its translation."
},
{
  "id": "rem-orbital-motion",
  "group": "u6",
  "prereqs": [
    "rem-conservation-angular-momentum",
    "dyn-gravitation",
    "dyn-circular-motion"
  ],
  "source": "CED 6.6",
  "title": "Orbiting satellites: gravity, energy and angular momentum",
  "analogy": "A conker on a string whirled round your head needs the string to keep it turning. Cut the string and it leaves in a straight line. For a satellite, gravity is the string — and because it always pulls straight towards the centre, it never speeds the satellite along its path in a circular orbit, only bends it.",
  "plain": "A satellite in orbit is falling, continuously, and missing. The only force on it is the gravitational pull of the central body, and that force points along the line joining them. Nothing else is needed: gravity **is** the centripetal force, not an extra force alongside it.\n\nFor a circular orbit this makes everything simple. The distance never changes, so the gravitational potential energy never changes; the speed never changes, so the kinetic energy never changes; and the total is constant too. Setting the gravitational force equal to the mass times the centripetal acceleration gives the orbital speed, and it depends only on the central mass and the radius — not on the satellite's own mass.\n\nFor an elliptical orbit the distance does change, so the potential and kinetic energies each change, trading against one another. Two things stay constant. The total mechanical energy, because gravity is a conservative force and nothing else acts. And the satellite's angular momentum about the central body, because gravity pulls straight along the line to the centre and therefore exerts no torque about it.\n\nThat second one has a consequence you can see with a telescope: since $L = rmv\\sin\\theta$ must hold constant, a satellite close in has to be moving fast, and far out has to be moving slowly.",
  "depth": "Setting gravity equal to the mass times the centripetal acceleration for a circular orbit of radius $r$ about a central mass $M$:\n\n$$G\\frac{Mm}{r^2} = \\frac{mv^2}{r}$$\n\nwhich rearranges to\n\n$$v = \\sqrt{\\frac{GM}{r}}$$\n\nThe two starting equations, $\\left|\\vec{F}_g\\right| = Gm_1m_2/r^2$ and $a_c = v^2/r$, are **both on the equation sheet**, as is $G = 6.67 \\times 10^{-11}$ N·m²/kg² on CED p. 211. The result $v = \\sqrt{GM/r}$ is not; you derive it, and the derivation takes one line. The satellite's own mass cancels, which is why a heavy satellite and a light one can share an orbit.\n\nGravitational potential energy over these distances is $$U_G = -G\\frac{m_1m_2}{r}$$ which **is on the equation sheet**. The negative sign is not a convention chosen for aesthetics: the CED defines the potential energy to be zero when the satellite is infinitely far away, and since the two bodies attract, bringing them closer releases energy, so any finite separation must sit below zero. Note that $\\Delta U_g = mg\\Delta y$ — also on the sheet — is the near-surface approximation of the same thing and is not valid across orbital distances.\n\nWhat is constant, exactly. A **circular** orbit holds four quantities steady: the system's total mechanical energy, the gravitational potential energy stored in it, and — for the satellite alone — its kinetic energy and its angular momentum. An **elliptical** orbit holds only two: total mechanical energy and angular momentum; kinetic and potential each rise and fall as the radius changes. Angular momentum is on both lists, and that is the point of putting orbits in this unit.\n\nEscape velocity follows from the energy bookkeeping. The CED defines it as the speed at which the total mechanical energy of the satellite–central-body system equals zero, so that the satellite coasts outward and its speed reaches zero only at infinite distance. Setting $\\frac{1}{2}mv^2 - GMm/r = 0$ gives $$v_{\\mathrm{esc}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2GM}{r}}$$ The CED labels this a **derived equation**, and it is **not on the equation sheet** — but the two ingredients are, so it is reconstructable. It is exactly $\\sqrt{2}$ times the circular orbital speed at the same radius, and it too is independent of the satellite's mass. Since escaping corresponds to zero total energy, anything still bound in an orbit has total mechanical energy less than zero.",
  "formula": "circular orbit:   G M m / r²  =  m v² / r     →     v = √( G M / r )\n                  the two inputs ARE on the sheet; the result is not\n                  satellite's own mass cancels\n\nU_G = − G m₁ m₂ / r        [ON the sheet]   zero taken at r = ∞\n                           (Δ U_g = m g Δy is the NEAR-SURFACE version only)\n\nv_esc = √( 2 G M / r )     CED \"derived equation\" — NOT on the sheet\n                           = √2 × circular speed at the same r\n\nCONSTANT in a CIRCULAR orbit :  E_mech, U, K, L   — all four\nCONSTANT in an ELLIPTICAL orbit: E_mech, L        — U and K each change\n\nG = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N·m²/kg²   [CED p. 211]",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A satellite of mass $m$ moves in a circular orbit of radius $7.0 \\times 10^6$ m about a planet of mass $6.0 \\times 10^{24}$ kg. Find its orbital speed, and the speed it would need at that same radius to escape the planet entirely.",
    "given": "M = 6.0 × 10²⁴ kg; r = 7.0 × 10⁶ m; G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N·m²/kg² (CED p. 211). The planet's surface is at 6.4 × 10⁶ m from its centre.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Set the gravitational force equal to $ma_c$: $GMm/r^2 = mv^2/r$.",
        "why": "Gravity is the only force acting, and the orbit is circular, so the net force must be exactly what circular motion at that radius requires. Adding a separate \"centripetal force\" would be counting the same pull twice."
      },
      {
        "do": "Cancel $m$ and one power of $r$: $v = \\sqrt{GM/r}$.",
        "why": "The satellite's mass appears on both sides. That cancellation is physically meaningful — orbital speed is a property of the orbit, not of what is in it — and it is also a hint that the problem never needed to give us $m$."
      },
      {
        "do": "$GM = (6.67 \\times 10^{-11})(6.0 \\times 10^{24}) = 4.00 \\times 10^{14}$.",
        "why": "Computing the product once keeps it available for the escape calculation and for the check, and keeps the powers of ten under control."
      },
      {
        "do": "$v = \\sqrt{4.00 \\times 10^{14} / 7.0 \\times 10^6} = \\sqrt{5.72 \\times 10^7} = 7.6 \\times 10^3$ m/s.",
        "why": "Straight substitution once the symbolic form is settled — the reason to derive symbolically first is that errors are visible in symbols and invisible in a long string of digits."
      },
      {
        "do": "Escape: set total mechanical energy to zero, $\\frac{1}{2}mv^2 - GMm/r = 0$, giving $v_{\\mathrm{esc}} = \\sqrt{2GM/r} = \\sqrt{1.14 \\times 10^8} = 1.07 \\times 10^4$ m/s.",
        "why": "Escape is defined by an energy condition, not a force one. There is no radius at which gravity stops; what ends is the satellite's ability to be pulled back, and that is a question about whether the energy budget reaches zero."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "Orbital speed $7.6 \\times 10^3$ m/s; escape speed at that radius $1.07 \\times 10^4$ m/s.",
    "check": "Independent check on the planet's mass, using the surface radius supplied. The field strength at the surface would be $GM/R^2 = 4.00 \\times 10^{14} / (6.4 \\times 10^6)^2 = 9.8$ N/kg — which matches the value on CED p. 211 for Earth's surface, so a $6.0 \\times 10^{24}$ kg planet of that size is self-consistent, and the arithmetic on $GM$ is right. Structural check on the second answer: $v_{\\mathrm{esc}}/v = \\sqrt{2} = 1.414$, and $1.07 \\times 10^4 / 7.6 \\times 10^3 = 1.41$ ✓."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Why does orbital motion sit in a unit about rotational energy and momentum rather than with gravitation in Unit 2? Because the thing that makes an elliptical orbit tractable is angular momentum conservation. A central force — one always directed at a fixed point — has $\\sin\\theta = 0$ in $\\tau = rF\\sin\\theta$, so it exerts no torque about that point, and $L = rmv\\sin\\theta$ is fixed for the whole orbit. That single fact links the speed at one end of the ellipse to the speed at the other without solving any differential equation.",
  "trap": "Three recurring losses. Treating the centripetal force as an extra force to be added to gravity in a free-body diagram — gravity is the whole net force. Using $U = mgh$ over orbital distances, when $g$ is not constant out there and the correct expression is $U_G = -Gm_1m_2/r$. And assuming a satellite in an elliptical orbit has constant speed, when the constant quantities are the total energy and the angular momentum — which together *require* the speed to change."
},
{
  "id": "osc-restoring-force",
  "group": "u7",
  "prereqs": [
    "dyn-spring-force"
  ],
  "source": "CED 7.1",
  "title": "The restoring force that defines SHM",
  "analogy": "Put a marble in a smooth round-bottomed bowl and nudge it. The further from the bottom it goes, the steeper the surface it sits on, so the harder the bowl pushes it back. Push is always towards the bottom, and the push grows in step with how far out the marble is. That pairing — always back, and proportional — is the whole of simple harmonic motion.",
  "plain": "Simple harmonic motion is one particular kind of repeating motion. Every repeating motion is *periodic*: a bouncing ball, a heart beat, a planet's orbit. Simple harmonic motion is the special case where the force pulling the object back satisfies a strict condition.\n\nThe condition has two halves. First, there is an **equilibrium position** — the one place where the net force on the object is zero. Second, whenever the object is away from that place, the net force points **back towards it**, and the size of that force is **proportional to how far away the object is**. Twice as far out, twice the force. That force is called the restoring force.\n\nA force that always points back towards equilibrium will produce some kind of oscillation no matter how it depends on distance. What the proportionality buys you is a *specific* oscillation: a smooth sine curve in time, with a period that turns out not to depend on how far you pulled it. Nothing else does that.\n\nAn ideal spring supplies exactly this force, which is why springs are the standard example. A pendulum swinging through small angles supplies it too, approximately — see [[osc-small-angle]].",
  "depth": "Write the displacement from equilibrium as $\\Delta x$. The defining statement of SHM is that the net force obeys\n\n$$F_{\\mathrm{net}} = -k\\,\\Delta x$$\n\nand so, by Newton's second law, $ma_x = -k\\,\\Delta x$. The CED prints this second form as a *derived* equation for CED 7.1, which means you are expected to be able to produce it, not to find it printed on the exam. What **is** on the provided equation sheet is the ideal-spring force law $\\vec{F}_s = -k\\,\\Delta\\vec{x}$ and $\\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{net}} = m\\vec{a}$, so you can assemble $ma_x = -k\\Delta x$ from two sheet equations in one line.\n\nThe minus sign is not decoration. It says the force vector and the displacement vector point opposite ways, which is what makes the force *restoring* rather than driving. If the sign were positive, a small nudge would grow without limit and the object would never come back.\n\nRearranging gives $a_x = -(k/m)\\Delta x$: the acceleration is proportional to the displacement and opposite to it. This is the sentence to carry around. It immediately tells you that acceleration is largest where displacement is largest (the turning points) and zero at equilibrium — see [[osc-phase-relationships]] — and it is the reason [[osc-position-time]] comes out as a sinusoid, because a sinusoid is the function whose second derivative is a negative constant times itself.\n\nNote that $k$ here is a stand-in for whatever constant of proportionality the system supplies. For a spring it is genuinely the spring constant in N/m. For a pendulum the restoring *torque* is proportional to the angular displacement instead, which is why a small-angle pendulum also counts as SHM.",
  "formula": "$$F_{\\mathrm{net}} = -k\\,\\Delta x \\qquad ma_x = -k\\,\\Delta x \\qquad a_x = -\\frac{k}{m}\\Delta x$$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A 0.50 kg glider on a horizontal frictionless air track is attached to a spring of spring constant $k = 20$ N/m. The glider is pulled 0.080 m to the right of equilibrium and released. Find the magnitude and direction of its acceleration at the moment of release, and again when it has returned to 0.040 m from equilibrium.",
    "given": "$m = 0.50$ kg, $k = 20$ N/m, first displacement $\\Delta x = +0.080$ m, second displacement $\\Delta x = +0.040$ m. Track is frictionless and horizontal.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Identify the equilibrium position as the point where the spring is neither stretched nor compressed, and measure all displacements from there.",
        "why": "The defining equation only means anything once you have fixed the origin. On a horizontal track the spring's natural length is the one place where the net force is zero, so that is the equilibrium position by definition."
      },
      {
        "do": "Write $F_{\\mathrm{net}} = -k\\Delta x$, so $a_x = -k\\Delta x/m$.",
        "why": "Gravity and the normal force are vertical and cancel; the spring force is the only horizontal force. So the spring force IS the net force, and the sheet equation for a spring becomes the defining equation for SHM without any extra work."
      },
      {
        "do": "At $\\Delta x = +0.080$ m: $a_x = -(20)(0.080)/0.50 = -3.2$ m/s².",
        "why": "Substituting the release displacement. The negative sign is a result, not a choice — it tells you the acceleration points to the left, back towards equilibrium, while the glider sits to the right."
      },
      {
        "do": "At $\\Delta x = +0.040$ m: $a_x = -(20)(0.040)/0.50 = -1.6$ m/s².",
        "why": "Half the displacement, half the acceleration. Doing the second point is the whole reason for the question: it demonstrates the proportionality that defines SHM rather than just asserting it."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "3.2 m/s² directed towards equilibrium at 0.080 m, and 1.6 m/s² towards equilibrium at 0.040 m. The acceleration is exactly halved when the displacement is halved.",
    "check": "Units: (N/m)(m)/kg = N/kg, and 1 N/kg = 1 m/s², so the answer is an acceleration. Limiting case: put $\\Delta x = 0$ into the same expression and you get $a = 0$, which must be true, because equilibrium is *defined* as the place where the net force vanishes. An algebra slip that left a constant term would fail that test."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Why insist on proportionality, rather than accepting any force that points back? Because proportionality is what makes the period independent of amplitude. If the restoring force grew faster than the displacement, a bigger pull would produce a proportionally larger acceleration and the oscillator would finish its trip early; if it grew more slowly, a bigger pull would take longer. Only exact proportionality makes the extra distance and the extra force cancel, so every amplitude takes the same time. That cancellation is what a pendulum clock is built on.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "A ball bouncing back and forth between two walls on a frictionless floor is periodic — it repeats forever with a definite period — and there is certainly a force sending it back. But that force is zero everywhere in the middle and enormous for an instant at each wall, not proportional to displacement. The position-time graph is a zigzag of straight lines, not a sine curve, and the period *does* change if you change the speed. It is periodic motion that is not simple harmonic motion.",
  "trap": "Students write '$F = kx$' and lose the sign, then get an acceleration pointing away from equilibrium and do not notice. Also: the $x$ in $-k\\Delta x$ is displacement **from equilibrium**, not from the origin of whatever coordinate system the diagram happens to use, and not the length of the spring. If the problem measures position from a wall, convert first."
},
{
  "id": "osc-period-frequency",
  "group": "u7",
  "prereqs": [
    "osc-restoring-force"
  ],
  "source": "CED 7.2",
  "title": "Amplitude, period, frequency, angular frequency",
  "analogy": "Think of a swing. How far out it goes is one thing; how often it comes back is a different thing. You can describe 'how often' either as the time for one full return trip, or as the number of return trips per second. They are the same information written two ways.",
  "plain": "Four numbers describe an oscillation, and it pays to keep them straight.\n\n- **Amplitude** $A$ is the largest displacement from equilibrium, measured one way, in metres. It is *not* the total width of the swing — that is twice the amplitude.\n- **Period** $T$ is the time for one complete cycle, in seconds. One complete cycle means back to the same place moving the same way, so out and back, not just out.\n- **Frequency** $f$ is the number of complete cycles per second, in hertz.\n- **Angular frequency** $\\omega$ repackages the frequency in radians per second, which is what the sine and cosine functions actually want.\n\nPeriod and frequency are reciprocals of each other. If something completes 4 cycles each second, one cycle takes a quarter of a second. There is no physics in that relationship; it is arithmetic about counting.\n\nThe physics is in what sets $T$, and that is the subject of [[osc-spring-period]] and [[osc-pendulum-period]]. The important early fact is that amplitude is not on the list of things that set it.",
  "depth": "The reciprocal relation\n\n$$T = \\frac{1}{f}$$\n\n**is on the provided equation sheet**, printed in the right-hand column with $T$ = period and $f$ = frequency in the variable list. The sheet also defines $A$ as 'amplitude or area', so the symbol is official.\n\nAngular frequency needs a careful word. The CED writes the oscillation as $x = A\\cos(2\\pi f t)$, with $2\\pi f$ sitting inside the bracket, and the equation sheet prints it that way too. The quantity $2\\pi f$ is what everyone calls the angular frequency $\\omega$:\n\n$$\\omega = 2\\pi f = \\frac{2\\pi}{T}$$\n\nThat identity follows in one step from $T = 1/f$, but it is **not printed on the equation sheet as an oscillation relation** — the sheet's $\\omega$ entries belong to the rotational-kinematics block. So know the name, and be ready to write $2\\pi f$ out in full if you would rather not rely on a symbol the sheet does not hand you.\n\nWhy $2\\pi$? Because one full cycle corresponds to the cosine's argument advancing by $2\\pi$ radians. Setting $t = T$ in $\\cos(2\\pi f t)$ gives $\\cos(2\\pi f T) = \\cos(2\\pi)$, back to the start. The $2\\pi$ is a units conversion between 'cycles' and 'radians', nothing deeper.\n\nAmplitude stands apart from the other three. Period, frequency and angular frequency are three names for one piece of information. Amplitude is genuinely independent information, and for an ideal oscillator you may set it to anything you like without touching the other three (CED 7.3.A.2). It does, however, control the energy — see [[osc-energy]].",
  "formula": "$$T = \\frac{1}{f} \\qquad \\omega = 2\\pi f = \\frac{2\\pi}{T}$$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A mass on a spring is filmed oscillating. In 15.0 s it completes 24 full cycles, and the video shows it moving between two extreme positions 6.0 cm apart. Find its frequency, its period, its amplitude, and its angular frequency.",
    "given": "24 cycles in 15.0 s; extreme positions 6.0 cm apart.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "$f = 24/15.0 = 1.60$ Hz.",
        "why": "Frequency is defined as cycles per unit time, so it comes straight from a count and a clock. Counting many cycles rather than timing one is deliberate: the timing error is shared out over 24 cycles instead of falling on one."
      },
      {
        "do": "$T = 1/f = 1/1.60 = 0.625$ s.",
        "why": "The sheet relation $T = 1/f$. No physics about springs is needed — this step would be identical for a pendulum or a heartbeat."
      },
      {
        "do": "$A = 6.0/2 = 3.0$ cm $= 0.030$ m.",
        "why": "The 6.0 cm is the full peak-to-peak width, from one extreme to the other. Amplitude is measured from **equilibrium** to one extreme, and equilibrium sits halfway between the extremes, so amplitude is half the width. This is the step most people get wrong."
      },
      {
        "do": "$\\omega = 2\\pi f = 2\\pi(1.60) = 10.1$ rad/s.",
        "why": "Converting cycles per second into radians per second, because the cosine in $x = A\\cos(2\\pi f t)$ takes radians. Note we are not given a new measurement here — this is the same information as $f$, in different clothes."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$f = 1.60$ Hz, $T = 0.625$ s, $A = 0.030$ m, $\\omega = 10.1$ rad/s.",
    "check": "Order of magnitude on the period: 1.60 cycles per second is a bit more than one cycle per second, so one cycle must take a bit less than 1 s. 0.625 s passes. And $\\omega$ should be roughly six times $f$ because $2\\pi \\approx 6.3$; $10.1/1.60 = 6.3$. A dropped factor of 2 in $\\omega$ would show up here immediately."
  },
  "trap": "Two amplitude errors and one period error account for most lost marks. (1) Reading the peak-to-peak distance off a graph and calling it $A$ — it is $2A$. (2) Timing from one extreme to the *other* extreme and calling that the period — that is $T/2$; a full cycle returns to the starting point moving in the starting direction. (3) Writing $\\omega = f$ or $\\omega = 2\\pi/f$; it is $2\\pi f$, equivalently $2\\pi/T$."
},
{
  "id": "osc-spring-period",
  "group": "u7",
  "prereqs": [
    "osc-period-frequency",
    "osc-restoring-force"
  ],
  "source": "CED 7.2",
  "title": "Period of a mass–spring oscillator",
  "analogy": "A heavy door on a stiff spring closer snaps shut fast; a heavy door on a weak spring drifts shut slowly. Stiffness hurries the motion, mass slows it. The formula says exactly that and nothing more.",
  "plain": "For a mass on an ideal spring, the period depends on two things: the mass and the spring constant. More mass means a longer period, because the same force produces less acceleration. A stiffer spring means a shorter period, because the restoring force at any given displacement is bigger.\n\nBoth dependencies come in under a square root, which softens them. To double the period you must **quadruple** the mass, not double it.\n\nTwo things are absent from the formula, and their absence is the point. **Amplitude** is absent: pull the mass out twice as far and it still takes the same time to come back. **The strength of gravity** is absent: the same oscillator would keep the same period on the Moon, on the Space Station, or on its side on a frictionless table.\n\nThat last one confuses people because a hanging spring obviously stretches under gravity. It does — but see the trap below. Gravity moves the equilibrium point; it does not change the timing.",
  "depth": "$$T_s = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{m}{k}}$$\n\n**This is on the provided equation sheet**, printed as $T_s$ in the right-hand column. You do not have to memorise it, but you do have to know what each symbol means and which quantities are missing from it.\n\nWhere it comes from, in words: [[osc-restoring-force]] gives $a_x = -(k/m)\\Delta x$, so the ratio $k/m$ is the only combination of the system's properties that appears anywhere in the motion. That ratio has units of s$^{-2}$, so $\\sqrt{m/k}$ has units of seconds and is the only time you can build from $m$ and $k$. The $2\\pi$ is fixed by the geometry of a full cycle. Dimensional analysis alone gets you everything but the $2\\pi$, and it also explains the absences: $A$ has units of metres and could only enter as a ratio to some other length, and there is no other length in the problem; $g$ never enters $a_x = -(k/m)\\Delta x$ at all.\n\nThe functional dependence is worth stating as a rule you can apply without a calculator: $T \\propto \\sqrt{m}$ and $T \\propto 1/\\sqrt{k}$. So multiplying the mass by 4 doubles $T$; multiplying $k$ by 4 halves it; multiplying both by 4 leaves $T$ alone.\n\nThe vertical hanging spring deserves its own paragraph. Hang mass $m$ on a spring and it settles where the spring force balances the weight — a new equilibrium a distance $mg/k$ below the natural length. Now measure displacement from *that* point. The net force is the extra spring force from the extra stretch, which is again $-k\\Delta x$: the constant weight and the constant part of the spring force cancel each other out identically at every position. Same $k$, same $m$, same equation, therefore the same $T_s = 2\\pi\\sqrt{m/k}$, with no $g$ in it.",
  "formula": "$$T_s = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{m}{k}} \\qquad T_s \\propto \\sqrt{m} \\qquad T_s \\propto \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{k}}$$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A 0.25 kg block on a frictionless horizontal surface is attached to a spring with $k = 40$ N/m and set oscillating with amplitude 0.10 m. (a) Find the period. (b) The experiment is repeated with a 1.00 kg block on the same spring, released from an amplitude of 0.20 m. Find the new period.",
    "given": "$m_1 = 0.25$ kg, $k = 40$ N/m, $A_1 = 0.10$ m; $m_2 = 1.00$ kg, $A_2 = 0.20$ m.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Note that the amplitudes are given but will not be used.",
        "why": "Deciding what to ignore is part of the work. $A$ does not appear in $T_s = 2\\pi\\sqrt{m/k}$, so both amplitude figures are there to see whether you will reach for them. Writing this down protects you from inventing a use for them later."
      },
      {
        "do": "(a) $T_s = 2\\pi\\sqrt{0.25/40} = 2\\pi\\sqrt{6.25\\times10^{-3}} = 2\\pi(0.0791) = 0.497$ s, so 0.50 s.",
        "why": "Straight substitution into the sheet equation. Take the square root before multiplying by $2\\pi$ — putting the $2\\pi$ inside the root is a standard and invisible error."
      },
      {
        "do": "(b) The mass has gone from 0.25 kg to 1.00 kg, a factor of 4, and $k$ is unchanged.",
        "why": "Set the problem up as a ratio rather than recomputing from scratch. Ratios expose the physics — 'four times the mass' — and they cancel the constants, so a calculator slip in $2\\pi$ cannot contaminate the second answer."
      },
      {
        "do": "$T \\propto \\sqrt{m}$, so $T_2 = \\sqrt{4}\\,T_1 = 2(0.497) = 0.99$ s.",
        "why": "Applying the functional dependence. The amplitude doubled as well, and that changes nothing, which is the result the question is really testing."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "(a) $T = 0.50$ s. (b) $T = 0.99$ s — twice as long, because the mass is four times as large; the doubled amplitude is irrelevant.",
    "check": "Units first: $\\sqrt{\\mathrm{kg}/(\\mathrm{N/m})} = \\sqrt{\\mathrm{kg\\cdot m/N}}$, and since 1 N = 1 kg·m/s², that is $\\sqrt{\\mathrm{s^2}}$ = seconds. Good. Independent recomputation of (b) from the formula: $2\\pi\\sqrt{1.00/40} = 2\\pi(0.158) = 0.993$ s, matching the ratio answer to three figures. If the two routes disagreed, one of them has an arithmetic error."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Why is $T$ built from $m$ and $k$ and nothing else? Because those are the only properties the equation of motion knows about. Everything about the trajectory follows from $a_x = -(k/m)\\Delta x$ plus how you started it, and the starting conditions set the amplitude and where in the cycle you begin — not the rate. A quantity the equation of motion never sees cannot appear in the period.",
  "trap": "The vertical-spring trap. A mass hangs from a spring, and students reason 'gravity pulls it down, so gravity must be part of the restoring force, so $g$ must be in the period.' It is not. Gravity shifts the equilibrium position down by $mg/k$ and then cancels out of the restoring force entirely, because it is a *constant* force and the restoring force is what is left over after constants cancel. Same spring, same mass, same period, hanging or horizontal. The second trap is using $T \\propto m$ instead of $T \\propto \\sqrt{m}$ and doubling the period for a doubled mass."
},
{
  "id": "osc-pendulum-period",
  "group": "u7",
  "prereqs": [
    "osc-period-frequency",
    "osc-restoring-force"
  ],
  "source": "CED 7.2",
  "title": "Period of a simple pendulum",
  "analogy": "A tall grandfather clock ticks slowly and a short pocket-watch balance ticks fast. Length is what sets the rate. Hanging a heavier weight on the same string would not make the clock run fast or slow — which is why clock makers adjust the *length* of the pendulum rod, never its weight.",
  "plain": "A simple pendulum is a small heavy bob on a light string, swinging through a small angle. Its period depends on the length of the string and on the local strength of gravity — and on nothing else.\n\nThe mass of the bob is not in the formula. This surprises almost everyone the first time, and it has the same explanation as free fall: a heavier bob feels a proportionally larger restoring force, but it also has proportionally more inertia to move, and the two effects cancel exactly.\n\nThe amplitude is not in the formula either — as long as the angle stays small. That condition is real and it is the subject of [[osc-small-angle]].\n\nNotice the contrast with [[osc-spring-period]]: the spring period contains mass and no $g$; the pendulum period contains $g$ and no mass. They are almost mirror images, and pairing them is a good way to remember both.",
  "depth": "$$T_p = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{\u2113}{g}}$$\n\n**This is on the provided equation sheet**, printed as $T_p$ with $\u2113$ = length. So is $g = 9.8$ m/s², in the constants block. You will be handed both.\n\nWhy a pendulum is SHM at all: CED 7.1 makes the argument in terms of torque. Displace the bob by a small angle $\\theta$ and gravity exerts a restoring **torque** about the pivot that is proportional to $\\theta$. Proportional restoring influence, measured back towards equilibrium — that is the same defining condition as [[osc-restoring-force]], written for an angular coordinate instead of a linear one. The mass appears on both sides of the rotational version of Newton's second law and divides out, which is the formal statement of the cancellation described above.\n\nFunctional dependence, for the 'predict a new value' questions: $T_p \\propto \\sqrt{\u2113}$ and $T_p \\propto 1/\\sqrt{g}$. Quadrupling the length doubles the period. Moving to a planet with a quarter of Earth's $g$ also doubles the period. Changing the bob from lead to cork does nothing at all — provided the string stays light compared with the bob and air resistance stays negligible, which the exam's stated conventions let you assume unless told otherwise.\n\n$\u2113$ is measured from the pivot to the **centre** of the bob, not to the top of it.",
  "formula": "$$T_p = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{\u2113}{g}} \\qquad T_p \\propto \\sqrt{\u2113} \\qquad T_p \\propto \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{g}}$$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A student wants to build a pendulum with a period of exactly 2.0 s at the Earth's surface. (a) What length of string is needed? (b) The student only has string of that length but replaces the 50 g bob with a 200 g bob of the same size. What is the new period? (c) The same pendulum is taken to a location where the gravitational field strength is one quarter of Earth's. What is the period there?",
    "given": "$T_p = 2.0$ s, $g = 9.8$ m/s² (from the equation sheet's constants). Bob mass changes from 50 g to 200 g; then $g' = g/4$.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "(a) Rearrange $T_p = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\u2113/g}$ for $\u2113$: square both sides to get $T_p^2 = 4\\pi^2\u2113/g$, so $\u2113 = gT_p^2/(4\\pi^2)$.",
        "why": "Solve symbolically before substituting. The unknown is under a square root, so squaring is the move that frees it; doing the algebra first means one arithmetic pass instead of several, and it makes the structure $\u2113 \\propto T^2$ visible."
      },
      {
        "do": "$\u2113 = (9.8)(2.0)^2/(4\\pi^2) = 39.2/39.48 = 0.993$ m, so about 0.99 m.",
        "why": "Substituting the sheet value of $g$. Note $4\\pi^2 = 39.48$, which happens to be almost 39.2 — that near-coincidence is why a one-metre pendulum has a period close to two seconds."
      },
      {
        "do": "(b) The period is unchanged at 2.0 s.",
        "why": "Mass does not appear in $T_p = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\u2113/g}$. There is no calculation to do, and doing one would mean you had used a formula that does not exist. Saying *why* — the extra weight is exactly matched by the extra inertia — is what earns the mark."
      },
      {
        "do": "(c) $T \\propto 1/\\sqrt{g}$, so quartering $g$ multiplies $T$ by $\\sqrt{4} = 2$: the new period is 4.0 s.",
        "why": "Ratio reasoning again. $\u2113$ is unchanged, so every constant cancels and only the change in $g$ survives. Recomputing from scratch would work but would risk an arithmetic error for no gain."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "(a) $\u2113 = 0.99$ m. (b) Still 2.0 s — the bob's mass is irrelevant. (c) 4.0 s.",
    "check": "Units on (a): $(\\mathrm{m/s^2})(\\mathrm{s^2})$ = metres. Good. Physical sense on (c): weaker gravity means a gentler restoring pull, so the swing should be *slower*, and 4.0 s is indeed longer than 2.0 s. Had the answer come out as 1.0 s, the square-root dependence had been applied upside down."
  },
  "rejectedAlternative": "A tempting wrong model: 'the bob is heavier, so gravity pulls it down harder, so it swings faster.' The first half is true — the restoring force really is larger for a larger mass. The error is forgetting that force is not motion. The heavier bob needs more force to achieve the same acceleration, by exactly the same factor, so the acceleration at any given angle is identical. It is the same argument that explains why a heavy ball and a light ball dropped together land together.",
  "trap": "Three regulars. (1) Putting the bob's mass into the pendulum period, or $g$ into the spring period — check which formula you are in. (2) Measuring $\u2113$ to the top of the bob rather than to its centre. (3) Assuming the amplitude does not matter *at any angle*; the formula is derived for small angles only, and at large angles the real period is longer than the formula predicts."
},
{
  "id": "osc-small-angle",
  "group": "u7",
  "prereqs": [
    "osc-pendulum-period"
  ],
  "source": "CED 7.1",
  "title": "The small-angle condition, and what breaks without it",
  "analogy": "A road curving away from a straight fence looks parallel to it for the first few metres and obviously not parallel after a hundred. The approximation is not true-or-false; it is good, then poor, and you should know roughly where the changeover is.",
  "plain": "A pendulum is only a simple harmonic oscillator when the swing is small. The reason is that the restoring influence is proportional to the *sine* of the angle, not to the angle itself. For small angles those two are nearly equal, so the restoring influence is nearly proportional to the angle and the defining condition of SHM is nearly met.\n\nFor large angles they part company. The sine grows more slowly than the angle, so the restoring pull at the far end of a big swing is weaker than proportionality would demand. A weaker pull means the pendulum takes longer to get back. So a real pendulum released from a large angle has a period **longer** than $2\\pi\\sqrt{\u2113/g}$, and the period now depends on amplitude after all.\n\nNothing dramatic happens at any particular angle. The approximation just gets steadily worse, and at some point the error exceeds what your experiment can tolerate.\n\nA spring, by contrast, needs no such condition — an ideal spring obeys $F = -k\\Delta x$ exactly, at any stretch. Real springs have their own limit, past which they deform permanently, but that is a materials fact rather than a mathematical approximation.",
  "depth": "The approximation is $\\sin\\theta \\approx \\theta$ with $\\theta$ **in radians**. It is the first term of the sine's expansion, and it is why the CED's claim in CED 7.1 is carefully hedged: a pendulum at *small* angular displacement can be *modeled as* simple harmonic motion, because the restoring torque is then proportional to the angular displacement.\n\nYou can quantify the damage using values on the provided equation sheet's trigonometry table alone. At 30°, $\\theta = \\pi/6 = 0.524$ rad while $\\sin\\theta = 1/2 = 0.500$; the true restoring torque is about 4.6% smaller than the proportional model claims. At 60°, $\\theta = \\pi/3 = 1.047$ rad while $\\sin\\theta = \\sqrt{3}/2 = 0.866$; the shortfall is about 17%. The discrepancy always has the same sign — $\\sin\\theta < \\theta$ for every positive angle — so the correction to the period always goes one way: the real period is always longer, never shorter.\n\n$$\\sin\\theta \\approx \\theta \\quad (\\theta \\text{ in radians, } \\theta \\text{ small})$$\n\nThis approximation is **not** printed on the equation sheet. The sheet gives you a table of trigonometric values for common angles, which is what you would use to test the approximation, but the approximation itself is something you carry in your head.\n\nFor experimental design this matters twice over. If you are measuring $g$ from a pendulum, a large release angle biases your result — a period that is too long makes $g$ come out too small, systematically, no matter how many trials you average. And if you are testing the claim that period is independent of amplitude, you must stay in the small-angle regime, or you will 'disprove' a rule you never satisfied the conditions for.",
  "formula": "$$\\text{SHM requires } \\tau_{\\mathrm{restoring}} \\propto \\theta, \\text{ but gravity gives } \\tau \\propto \\sin\\theta$$\n$$\\sin\\theta \\approx \\theta \\text{ for small } \\theta \\text{ in radians}$$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "Using only the trigonometric values printed on the AP equation sheet, estimate the fractional error made by the small-angle approximation at a release angle of 30° and at 60°. State whether the true period of a pendulum released from 60° is longer or shorter than $2\\pi\\sqrt{\u2113/g}$, and justify the direction.",
    "given": "From the sheet's table: $\\sin 30° = 1/2$, $\\sin 60° = \\sqrt{3}/2$. Conversion: 180° = $\\pi$ rad.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Convert to radians: 30° = $\\pi/6$ = 0.524 rad; 60° = $\\pi/3$ = 1.047 rad.",
        "why": "The approximation $\\sin\\theta \\approx \\theta$ is false in degrees — $\\sin 30° = 0.5$ is nothing like 30. Radians are the only angle measure in which the sine of a small angle is numerically close to the angle, which is the whole reason radians exist."
      },
      {
        "do": "At 30°: compare $\\sin\\theta = 0.500$ with $\\theta = 0.524$. Fractional shortfall $= (0.524-0.500)/0.524 = 0.046$, about 5%.",
        "why": "Expressing the gap as a fraction rather than a raw difference, because the raw difference has no meaning without a scale to compare it to. 5% is the number to remember for 30°."
      },
      {
        "do": "At 60°: compare $\\sin\\theta = 0.866$ with $\\theta = 1.047$. Fractional shortfall $= (1.047-0.866)/1.047 = 0.173$, about 17%.",
        "why": "Doubling the angle more than triples the error. The approximation degrades faster than linearly, which is why 'a bit past small' is riskier than it sounds."
      },
      {
        "do": "Direction: $\\sin\\theta < \\theta$, so the real restoring torque is weaker than the SHM model assumes; a weaker restoring influence means slower return; therefore the true period is **longer** than $2\\pi\\sqrt{\u2113/g}$.",
        "why": "This is a sign argument, not a calculation, and sign arguments are what the exam's 'justify' verb asks for. Chaining weaker force to smaller acceleration to longer time is the logical sequence that earns credit."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "About 5% at 30° and about 17% at 60°. The true period at 60° is longer than $2\\pi\\sqrt{\u2113/g}$, because the restoring torque falls short of proportionality by more and more as the angle grows.",
    "check": "Limiting case: let the angle go to zero. Both $\\theta$ and $\\sin\\theta$ go to zero together and their ratio approaches 1, so the fractional error must approach 0 — and our two numbers, 5% at 30° and 17% at 60°, do shrink as the angle shrinks. A calculation that gave a *larger* error at 30° than at 60° would be wrong on this test alone. Second check: 45° should fall between the two, and $\\sin 45° = \\sqrt{2}/2 = 0.707$ against $\\theta = 0.785$ gives 10%, which it does."
  },
  "trap": "Doing the comparison in degrees. $\\sin\\theta \\approx \\theta$ is only true in radians, and a calculator left in degree mode will silently produce nonsense. The second trap is treating 'small angle' as a rule with a hard cut-off and arguing about whether 15° counts; the honest answer is that the error is continuous and you should quote it."
},
{
  "id": "osc-position-time",
  "group": "u7",
  "prereqs": [
    "osc-period-frequency"
  ],
  "source": "CED 7.3",
  "title": "Position as a function of time",
  "analogy": "Watch a point on the rim of a slowly turning wheel from the side, edge-on. The point appears to slide back and forth in a straight line, fast through the middle, slowing to a stop at each end, and its shadow traces a sine curve against time. That shadow is what SHM looks like.",
  "plain": "Plot the displacement of a simple harmonic oscillator against time and you get a sine curve. Not a zigzag, not a series of arcs — a sinusoid, the same shape whatever the system, as long as the restoring force obeys the defining proportionality.\n\nThe curve is set by three numbers. Its height above the axis is the amplitude $A$. The time from one peak to the next peak is the period $T$. Where you are on the curve at $t = 0$ is fixed by where you released the object.\n\nThere are two standard starting points. Release the object from rest at maximum displacement and the curve starts at a peak, which is a cosine. Start it from equilibrium with a push and the curve starts at zero, which is a sine. Same motion, same period, same amplitude; only the choice of when you started the clock differs.\n\nReading these graphs is a large fraction of what Unit 7 asks of you. The features that carry information are the maxima, the minima, and the crossings — see [[osc-phase-relationships]].",
  "depth": "$$x = A\\cos(2\\pi f t) \\qquad x = A\\sin(2\\pi f t)$$\n\n**Both forms are on the provided equation sheet**, printed side by side in the right-hand column, and both appear in CED 7.3. The sheet does not tell you which to use; that is determined by the initial condition.\n\n- Use the **cosine** when the object starts at $x = +A$ at $t = 0$, that is, released from rest at maximum displacement. At $t = 0$, $\\cos 0 = 1$ and $x = A$.\n- Use the **sine** when the object starts at $x = 0$ at $t = 0$, moving in the positive direction. At $t = 0$, $\\sin 0 = 0$.\n\nThe argument $2\\pi f t$ is a pure number in radians. This is the one place in the unit where calculator mode genuinely matters: evaluating $\\cos(2\\pi f t)$ with the calculator in degrees returns a number that looks plausible and is wrong.\n\nUseful landmarks within one cycle, taking the cosine form:\n\n- $t = 0$: $x = +A$, at a turning point.\n- $t = T/4$: the argument is $\\pi/2$, $x = 0$, passing through equilibrium.\n- $t = T/2$: the argument is $\\pi$, $x = -A$, the other turning point.\n- $t = 3T/4$: $x = 0$ again, passing through the other way.\n- $t = T$: back to $+A$, one full cycle.\n\nChanging the amplitude stretches the curve vertically and leaves the horizontal spacing untouched. That is CED 7.3.A.2 in graphical form: amplitude does not change the period. On a graph, an oscillator with double the amplitude has peaks twice as tall in exactly the same places along the time axis.",
  "formula": "$$x = A\\cos(2\\pi f t) \\quad \\text{(starts at } x = +A\\text{)}$$\n$$x = A\\sin(2\\pi f t) \\quad \\text{(starts at } x = 0\\text{)}$$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A block on a spring is pulled 0.12 m to the right and released from rest at $t = 0$. It oscillates at 2.5 Hz. (a) Write the equation for its displacement. (b) Find its displacement at $t = 0.10$ s. (c) Find the first time after release at which the block is 0.12 m to the *left* of equilibrium.",
    "given": "$A = 0.12$ m, $f = 2.5$ Hz, released from rest at maximum displacement at $t = 0$.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Choose the cosine form: $x = (0.12)\\cos(2\\pi(2.5)t) = 0.12\\cos(5\\pi t)$, with $x$ in metres and $t$ in seconds.",
        "why": "The initial condition decides between the sheet's two forms. 'Released from rest at maximum displacement' means $x = +A$ when $t = 0$, and only the cosine does that. Choosing sine here would describe a block that started at the centre — a different experiment."
      },
      {
        "do": "Find the period first: $T = 1/f = 1/2.5 = 0.40$ s.",
        "why": "Because $t = 0.10$ s is then recognisable as exactly $T/4$, and quarter-cycle landmarks can be read off without trusting a calculator. Converting an awkward time into a fraction of a cycle is almost always the faster route."
      },
      {
        "do": "(b) At $t = T/4$ the cosine's argument is $2\\pi f (T/4) = \\pi/2$, and $\\cos(\\pi/2) = 0$. So $x = 0$.",
        "why": "A quarter of a cycle after starting at a peak, the object is at equilibrium. Note this also tells you it is moving at its fastest right then, which is the link to [[osc-phase-relationships]]."
      },
      {
        "do": "(c) The far side, $x = -A$, is reached half a cycle after starting at $+A$: $t = T/2 = 0.20$ s.",
        "why": "$\\cos(\\pi) = -1$, so the argument must reach $\\pi$, which happens at $t = T/2$. Asking for the *first* such time matters — it recurs every full period after that, at 0.60 s, 1.00 s and so on."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "(a) $x = 0.12\\cos(5\\pi t)$ metres. (b) $x = 0$. (c) $t = 0.20$ s.",
    "check": "Sanity on the form: substitute $t = 0$ into the answer to (a) and you get $x = 0.12$ m, which is where the block was released — the equation reproduces the stated initial condition. Consistency between (b) and (c): equilibrium at 0.10 s and the far turning point at 0.20 s are a quarter-cycle apart, which is exactly the spacing a sinusoid demands between a zero and the following extreme. If (c) had come out at 0.15 s, the two answers would be geometrically incompatible."
  },
  "trap": "Two failure modes. (1) Using the sine when the object was released from rest at maximum displacement — read the initial condition before you pick the form. (2) Leaving the calculator in degree mode when evaluating $\\cos(2\\pi f t)$; the argument is in radians. A third, quieter one: assuming the peak-to-peak vertical extent of the graph is $A$ when it is $2A$."
},
{
  "id": "osc-phase-relationships",
  "group": "u7",
  "prereqs": [
    "osc-position-time",
    "osc-restoring-force"
  ],
  "source": "CED 7.3",
  "title": "Where displacement, velocity and acceleration peak",
  "analogy": "Think of a ball thrown straight up. At the top it is momentarily still, yet gravity is acting on it as strongly as ever. 'Not moving' and 'not accelerating' are different statements. An oscillator's turning points are the same situation: zero speed, maximum acceleration.",
  "plain": "At every moment an oscillator has a displacement, a velocity and an acceleration, and the three do not peak together. They are staggered through the cycle in a fixed pattern, and knowing that pattern answers a large share of Unit 7 questions with no calculation at all.\n\n**At the turning points** — the two extremes, $x = \\pm A$ — the object has momentarily stopped, so the speed is **zero**. But it is as far from equilibrium as it ever gets, so the restoring force and therefore the acceleration are at their **maximum**.\n\n**At equilibrium** — $x = 0$ — the net force is zero by definition, so the acceleration is **zero**. But the object has been accelerated all the way in from the turning point, so this is where it is moving **fastest**.\n\nSo displacement and acceleration hit their extremes together, at opposite signs, and velocity hits its extreme exactly where both of them are passing through the middle. Speed is greatest where displacement is zero; acceleration is greatest where displacement is greatest.",
  "depth": "The acceleration statement is not an observation to memorise; it is [[osc-restoring-force]] restated. Since $a_x = -(k/m)\\Delta x$, acceleration is a fixed negative multiple of displacement at *every* instant. So the $a$-versus-$t$ graph is the $x$-versus-$t$ graph flipped upside down and rescaled — same zeros, same extreme times, opposite sign. Nothing more to know.\n\nVelocity is the slope of the $x$-versus-$t$ graph. A sinusoid's slope is steepest where it crosses the axis and zero at its peaks, which gives the velocity pattern directly. Velocity is a quarter-cycle out of step with displacement.\n\nPutting the three together for the cosine start $x = A\\cos(2\\pi f t)$:\n\n- $t = 0$: $x = +A$, $v = 0$, $a$ at maximum magnitude, negative.\n- $t = T/4$: $x = 0$, $|v|$ at maximum, $a = 0$.\n- $t = T/2$: $x = -A$, $v = 0$, $a$ at maximum magnitude, positive.\n- $t = 3T/4$: $x = 0$, $|v|$ at maximum the other way, $a = 0$.\n\nMagnitudes: the acceleration maximum follows straight from the defining equation, $|a|_{\\max} = kA/m$. The speed maximum does **not** come from the defining equation and is not on the equation sheet; the clean route to it is energy conservation, $\\tfrac{1}{2}kA^2 = \\tfrac{1}{2}mv_{\\max}^2$, giving $v_{\\max} = A\\sqrt{k/m}$ — see [[osc-energy]].\n\nOne consequence worth internalising, because it feels wrong: at half the amplitude, acceleration is exactly half its maximum (it is proportional to $x$), but the speed is still about 87% of its maximum. Speed falls off slowly at first and then collapses near the turning points, because it depends on $\\sqrt{A^2 - x^2}$, not on $x$ linearly.",
  "formula": "$$a_x = -\\frac{k}{m}x \\qquad |a|_{\\max} = \\frac{kA}{m} \\ \\text{at } x = \\pm A \\qquad |v|_{\\max} \\ \\text{at } x = 0$$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A 0.40 kg block oscillates on a spring of spring constant $k = 25$ N/m with amplitude $A = 0.060$ m. (a) Find the maximum magnitude of its acceleration and say where in the motion it occurs. (b) At the instant the block is at $x = A/2$, what fraction of the maximum acceleration does it have, and what fraction of the maximum speed?",
    "given": "$m = 0.40$ kg, $k = 25$ N/m, $A = 0.060$ m.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "(a) The largest acceleration occurs at the largest displacement, so put $x = A$ into $a_x = -(k/m)x$: $|a|_{\\max} = (25)(0.060)/0.40 = 3.75$ m/s².",
        "why": "Because acceleration is proportional to displacement, finding its maximum is the same question as finding the maximum displacement — and that is the amplitude, by definition. No calculus and no time-dependence needed."
      },
      {
        "do": "State where: at the two turning points $x = \\pm A$, which are also the two instants where the block is momentarily at rest.",
        "why": "The question asks 'where', and the counter-intuitive part is that maximum acceleration coincides with zero speed. Saying both together is what shows you understand the phase relationship rather than having memorised a formula."
      },
      {
        "do": "(b) Acceleration: $a \\propto x$, so at $x = A/2$ the acceleration is exactly $1/2$ of maximum, i.e. 1.875 m/s².",
        "why": "Direct proportionality means fractions map across unchanged. This half of the question is easy precisely because the relationship is linear — which is what sets up the contrast with the next step."
      },
      {
        "do": "Speed: energy conservation gives $\\tfrac{1}{2}kA^2 = \\tfrac{1}{2}kx^2 + \\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2$, so $v = v_{\\max}\\sqrt{1 - x^2/A^2}$. At $x = A/2$ this is $\\sqrt{1 - 1/4} = \\sqrt{3}/2 = 0.866$.",
        "why": "Speed is NOT proportional to anything simple in $x$, so the fraction cannot be guessed. Energy is the right tool because it connects position to speed without needing time, and the constant-acceleration kinematics equations are unavailable here — the acceleration is changing at every instant."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "(a) 3.75 m/s², at the two turning points $x = \\pm 0.060$ m, where the speed is zero. (b) Half the maximum acceleration, but $\\sqrt{3}/2 \\approx 87\\%$ of the maximum speed.",
    "check": "Units on (a): (N/m)(m)/kg = N/kg = m/s². Limiting cases on the speed formula: at $x = A$ it gives $v = v_{\\max}\\sqrt{1-1} = 0$, correct for a turning point; at $x = 0$ it gives $v = v_{\\max}$, correct for equilibrium. Both endpoints are right, so the expression is at least the right shape. The value $\\sqrt{3}/2$ is also on the exam's trigonometry table, a useful cross-reference for the arithmetic."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Why reason about phase rather than differentiate? Because AP Physics 1 is algebra-based and the exam rewards the graphical argument: acceleration is a flipped copy of displacement because $a \\propto -x$, and velocity is the slope of displacement. Those two sentences reconstruct the entire pattern from scratch in about ten seconds, which is more reliable under exam pressure than a memorised list of which curve leads which.",
  "trap": "The single most common Unit 7 error: saying acceleration is greatest where the object is moving fastest. It is the exact opposite — maximum speed sits at zero acceleration, and maximum acceleration sits at zero speed. The underlying confusion is treating 'stopped' as 'no force acting'. At a turning point the object is instantaneously at rest and the net force on it is at its largest of the whole cycle, which is precisely why it does not stay there."
},
{
  "id": "osc-energy",
  "group": "u7",
  "prereqs": [
    "osc-phase-relationships",
    "osc-restoring-force",
    "enr-mechanical-energy-conservation"
  ],
  "source": "CED 7.4",
  "title": "Energy in a simple harmonic oscillator",
  "analogy": "A skateboarder in a half-pipe. All height and no speed at the lip, all speed and no height at the bottom, and the total never changes if the ramp is smooth. The two stores trade back and forth twice per round trip, and the sum is the one number that stays put.",
  "plain": "An oscillator carries a fixed total amount of mechanical energy, and it shuffles that energy back and forth between two forms: kinetic energy, from motion, and potential energy, from being displaced from equilibrium.\n\nAt the turning points, everything is potential. The object is momentarily still, so its kinetic energy is zero, and its potential energy is at its maximum for that motion. At equilibrium, everything is kinetic. The object is at maximum speed and the stored potential energy is at its minimum.\n\nBetween those points the two trade off continuously, and their sum is constant. That is why kinetic energy is largest exactly where potential energy is smallest — they are not two independent facts but one fact seen twice.\n\nThe amplitude controls the total. Pull an oscillator out further and you have put more energy in, so it will move faster at the middle. The relationship is not proportional: doubling the amplitude does not double the energy, it **quadruples** it, because the stored energy in a spring goes as the square of the stretch.",
  "depth": "For a system with no dissipation the statement is simply conservation of energy applied to an oscillator:\n\n$$E_{\\mathrm{total}} = K + U = \\text{constant}$$\n\nFor an object–spring system the two pieces are $K = \\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2$ and $U_s = \\tfrac{1}{2}k(\\Delta x)^2$, **both of which are on the provided equation sheet**. Evaluating the total at a turning point, where $v = 0$ and $\\Delta x = A$, gives\n\n$$E_{\\mathrm{total}} = \\tfrac{1}{2}kA^2$$\n\nwhich the CED lists under CED 7.4 as the relevant equation for a spring–object system. It is **not printed on the equation sheet** — you either remember it or, better, re-derive it in one line by writing $U_s = \\tfrac{1}{2}k(\\Delta x)^2$ at $\\Delta x = A$. That re-derivation is worth practising, because it also shows you *why* the total is set by the amplitude.\n\nEquating the total at any two points gives the working relation\n\n$$\\tfrac{1}{2}kA^2 = \\tfrac{1}{2}kx^2 + \\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2$$\n\nfrom which $v = \\sqrt{(k/m)(A^2 - x^2)}$, and at $x = 0$, $v_{\\max} = A\\sqrt{k/m}$.\n\nBecause $E \\propto A^2$, the energy scaling is a favourite for 'predict the new value' questions: triple the amplitude and the total energy is multiplied by 9, while the period is unchanged. Note what this does and does not touch — amplitude changes the *energy* and the *maximum speed*, and leaves the *period* alone ([[osc-spring-period]]).\n\nOne more piece of structure: the energy exchange happens at twice the frequency of the motion. The object passes through equilibrium twice per cycle, so kinetic energy peaks twice per cycle, while displacement peaks positively only once.",
  "formula": "$$E_{\\mathrm{total}} = U + K \\qquad E_{\\mathrm{total}} = \\tfrac{1}{2}kA^2 \\qquad E \\propto A^2$$\n$$\\tfrac{1}{2}kA^2 = \\tfrac{1}{2}kx^2 + \\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2 \\qquad v_{\\max} = A\\sqrt{\\frac{k}{m}}$$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A 0.30 kg block on a frictionless horizontal surface is attached to a spring with $k = 12$ N/m. It is pulled 0.20 m from equilibrium and released. (a) Find the total mechanical energy of the system. (b) Find the block's speed when it is 0.10 m from equilibrium. (c) The experiment is repeated with the block pulled out 0.40 m instead. By what factor does the total energy change, and by what factor does the period change?",
    "given": "$m = 0.30$ kg, $k = 12$ N/m, $A = 0.20$ m; then $A' = 0.40$ m. Frictionless.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Decide on energy rather than kinematics.",
        "why": "This is the key decision and it should be conscious. The constant-acceleration equations, including $v^2 = v_0^2 + 2a\\Delta x$, are on the sheet and are **illegal here**: they are derived on the assumption that $a$ is constant, and in SHM $a = -(k/m)x$ changes continuously as the block moves. Energy conservation carries no such assumption — it relates speed to position directly, which is exactly the question asked."
      },
      {
        "do": "(a) Evaluate the total at the release point, where $v = 0$: $E = \\tfrac{1}{2}kA^2 = \\tfrac{1}{2}(12)(0.20)^2 = 0.24$ J.",
        "why": "Choosing the turning point as the evaluation point is the trick that makes this a one-term calculation instead of two. The energy is constant, so whatever you compute at the most convenient instant is the value at every instant."
      },
      {
        "do": "(b) At $x = 0.10$ m: $U_s = \\tfrac{1}{2}(12)(0.10)^2 = 0.060$ J, so $K = 0.24 - 0.060 = 0.18$ J.",
        "why": "Subtracting rather than solving a quadratic. Because the total is fixed, finding one store gives the other for free — this is what conservation *buys* you."
      },
      {
        "do": "Solve $K = \\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2$ for $v$: $v = \\sqrt{2K/m} = \\sqrt{2(0.18)/0.30} = \\sqrt{1.2} = 1.1$ m/s.",
        "why": "The kinetic-energy definition run backwards. Speed, not velocity — energy is a scalar and cannot tell you which way the block is going, only how fast. At $x = +0.10$ m the block passes twice per cycle, once each way, at this same speed."
      },
      {
        "do": "(c) $E \\propto A^2$, so doubling $A$ multiplies $E$ by 4, to 0.96 J. The period contains no $A$, so it is unchanged.",
        "why": "Two different functional dependences in one breath, which is the comparison the question exists to test. Energy is quadratic in amplitude; period is independent of it."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "(a) $E = 0.24$ J. (b) $v = 1.1$ m/s. (c) Total energy $\\times 4$; period unchanged ($\\times 1$).",
    "check": "Independent route to (b): the maximum speed is $v_{\\max} = \\sqrt{2E/m} = \\sqrt{2(0.24)/0.30} = 1.265$ m/s, and our answer is $1.095/1.265 = 0.866 = \\sqrt{3}/2$ of it. That is precisely the fraction [[osc-phase-relationships]] predicts at $x = A/2$, derived there from a different starting point — two independent routes agreeing. Units: $\\sqrt{\\mathrm{J/kg}} = \\sqrt{\\mathrm{m^2/s^2}}$ = m/s. Bound: 1.1 m/s must be less than $v_{\\max} = 1.27$ m/s, and it is."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Energy rather than kinematics, always, when a question links speed to position in SHM. The three constant-acceleration equations printed at the top of the equation sheet are tempting because they are right there and they contain $v$, $a$ and $\\Delta x$. They are invalid for an oscillator, and using them produces a confident wrong number rather than an obvious error. The tell is in the defining equation itself: $a$ is proportional to $x$, so as $x$ changes, $a$ changes, so $a$ is not constant.",
  "rejectedAlternative": "You could instead write $x = A\\cos(2\\pi f t)$, find the time at which $x = 0.10$ m, and evaluate the velocity at that time. It gives the same answer and it is much more work: you need an inverse cosine, a period, and a velocity function that is not on the equation sheet. Energy skips time entirely, and time was never asked for.",
  "trap": "Three. (1) Using $v^2 = v_0^2 + 2a\\Delta x$ in SHM — the acceleration is not constant, so the equation does not apply, no matter which value of $a$ you pick. (2) Thinking doubling the amplitude doubles the energy; it quadruples it, because $U_s = \\tfrac{1}{2}k(\\Delta x)^2$ is quadratic. (3) Sliding from 'amplitude changes the energy' to 'amplitude changes the period'. It changes the energy and the maximum speed; the period is untouched (CED 7.3.A.2)."
},
{
  "id": "flu-fluid-structure",
  "group": "u8",
  "prereqs": [],
  "source": "CED 8.1",
  "title": "What makes something a fluid",
  "analogy": "A brick wall and a crowd of people are both made of many small units. Push the wall on one side and the whole wall moves as one shape. Push the crowd and it deforms around your hand and closes in behind you. The crowd has no shape of its own; it takes the shape of the room. That is the whole difference.",
  "plain": "Solids, liquids and gases are all made of atoms and molecules. What separates them is not what the particles are but how strongly they hold on to each other. In a solid the particles are locked into fixed neighbours, so the material keeps its own shape. In a liquid they touch but slide past each other. In a gas they barely interact at all and fly apart to fill whatever they are in.\n\nA **fluid** is anything with no fixed shape. Liquids and gases are both fluids. That single sentence is the definition the course uses, and it is worth noticing what it does *not* say: it says nothing about being wet, nothing about pouring, and nothing about density. Air is a fluid.\n\nUnit 8 will look unfamiliar and mostly is not. Almost everything in it is Newton's laws and conservation of energy applied to an object that has no shape of its own. Buoyancy is a net force. Bernoulli's equation is energy conservation. If you already have the mechanics, you have most of this unit.",
  "depth": "The course models fluids as **ideal**: incompressible, and with no viscosity. Take those one at a time.\n\n*Incompressible* means the volume, and therefore the density, of a given amount of the fluid does not change no matter how hard you squeeze it. Squeeze a sealed syringe of water and the model says the water inside occupies exactly the volume it did before. This is the assumption that makes [[flu-continuity]] work: what goes in must come out at the same volume rate, because the fluid cannot bunch up in the middle.\n\n*No viscosity* means no internal friction. Real fluid layers drag on each other and on the pipe wall, which costs energy. An ideal fluid does not, which is why [[flu-bernoulli]] can be written as plain energy conservation with no dissipation term.\n\nBoth are modelling choices, not facts about water. The exam's stated conventions say fluids are assumed ideal, and pipes assumed completely filled by fluid, unless a question says otherwise — so you may use the assumption without arguing for it, but you should know it is what your equations rest on. A question that tells you the flow is turbulent, or the fluid is honey, is telling you the assumption has been withdrawn.",
  "formula": "Ideal fluid:\n  incompressible  -> density is constant\n  no viscosity    -> no internal friction, no energy lost to drag",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A sealed syringe holds $2.0 \\times 10^{-5}$ m³ of water. You push the plunger hard enough to raise the pressure inside by $1.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa (one atmosphere). Treating the water as an ideal fluid, what are the new volume and the new density of the water inside?",
    "given": "$V_0 = 2.0 \\times 10^{-5}$ m³; pressure increase $\\Delta P = 1.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa; the water is ideal.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Read the word *ideal* as a physical statement, not decoration: an ideal fluid is incompressible.",
        "why": "The whole question turns on one property. Naming which property applies is the move that decides the answer, before any arithmetic."
      },
      {
        "do": "Incompressible means the volume of a given amount of fluid does not change with the pressure exerted on it. So $V = 2.0 \\times 10^{-5}$ m³, unchanged.",
        "why": "The pressure number is deliberately large. It is there to test whether you apply the model or reach for an intuition about squeezing."
      },
      {
        "do": "The mass of water in the sealed syringe has not changed either — nothing left. With $\\rho = m/V$ and both $m$ and $V$ fixed, $\\rho$ is unchanged.",
        "why": "Density is a ratio. It can only move if one of the two quantities moves, so checking both is quicker than reasoning about density directly."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "Volume unchanged at $2.0 \\times 10^{-5}$ m³; density unchanged.",
    "check": "Test the model against a case where it is *not* used. If the syringe held air, a gas, doubling the pressure would roughly halve the volume, and since the mass is fixed the density would roughly double. The answers differ, which confirms the result came from the incompressibility assumption and not from arithmetic that would have given the same thing either way."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Why define a fluid by shape rather than by phase? Because the physics that follows — pressure rising with depth, buoyancy, flow through a pipe — depends only on the material being unable to hold a shape. Splitting liquids from gases at this level would mean writing every rule twice for no gain.",
  "trap": "Reading \"fluid\" as \"liquid\". Gases are fluids, and fluid questions about air are fair game. Also, do not carry incompressibility into a situation where the question has told you the fluid is a gas being compressed — the assumption is the default, not a law."
},
{
  "id": "flu-density",
  "group": "u8",
  "prereqs": [
    "flu-fluid-structure"
  ],
  "source": "CED 8.1",
  "title": "Density",
  "plain": "Density is how much mass is packed into each cubic metre: $\\rho = m/V$. It is the property that characterises a fluid, and it is the number that decides almost everything else in this unit — how fast pressure builds with depth, how big the buoyant force is, whether something floats.\n\nThe key thing about density is that it is an *intensive* property: it does not depend on how much you have. Half a bucket of water has half the mass and half the volume, so the same density. A lead pellet and a lead statue have the same density. This is why density, not mass, is the right thing to compare when you ask whether an object floats.\n\nIts unit is kg/m³. Watch the cubes: converting cm to m divides a volume by $10^{6}$, not by $10^{2}$.",
  "depth": "$\\rho = m/V$ **is on the equation sheet**, in the right-hand column. What is *not* on the sheet is the density of any actual substance. The sheet carries $g$, the gravitational constant, and one atmosphere of pressure, and that is the whole list. So any fluid density a problem needs, the problem must give you. If you find yourself trying to recall a density, re-read the stem.\n\nRearranged, $m = \\rho V$, and this is the form you will use most. It is how [[flu-buoyancy]] turns a displaced volume into a weight, and how [[flu-hydrostatic]] turns a depth into a pressure. Whenever a fluid problem asks for a force, the chain is almost always volume $\\to$ mass $\\to$ weight, and $\\rho$ is the first link.\n\nFor a compound object made of two materials, the overall density is the *total* mass over the *total* volume, not the average of the two densities. A boat is mostly air, and that is exactly why its overall density can be far below the steel it is built from.",
  "formula": "$$\\rho = \\frac{m}{V}$$\nrearranged: $m = \\rho V$,  $V = \\frac{m}{\\rho}$\nunits: kg/m³   (1 g/cm³ = 1000 kg/m³)",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A rectangular block of wood measures 0.20 m by 0.10 m by 0.050 m and has a mass of 0.72 kg. Find its density. The water it will be dropped into has density 1000 kg/m³ — will the block float?",
    "given": "$m = 0.72$ kg; block $0.20 \\times 0.10 \\times 0.050$ m; $\\rho_{\\text{water}} = 1000$ kg/m³.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Volume of a rectangular solid: $V = \u2113 w h = 0.20 \\times 0.10 \\times 0.050 = 1.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ m³.",
        "why": "The geometry formulas for rectangular solids, cylinders and spheres are on the equation sheet's geometry table, so this step never needs memory — but it does need you to notice which solid you have."
      },
      {
        "do": "$\\rho = m/V = 0.72 / (1.0 \\times 10^{-3}) = 720$ kg/m³.",
        "why": "Density is defined this way; no other relation is needed, and reaching for one would be a sign of having misread the question."
      },
      {
        "do": "Compare with the fluid: $720 < 1000$, so the block is less dense than water and floats.",
        "why": "Floating is decided by a comparison of densities, not masses — see [[flu-floating]]. A 0.72 kg block is lighter than a bathtub of water and heavier than a thimble of it, so mass alone answers nothing."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$\\rho = 720$ kg/m³, which is less than 1000 kg/m³, so the block floats.",
    "check": "Order of magnitude, from a different direction. 720 kg/m³ is 0.72 g/cm³, and a cubic centimetre of this wood would have a mass of 0.72 g — lighter than the 1 g of a cubic centimetre of water, which is consistent with wood floating in everyday experience. An arithmetic slip of $10^{3}$ would have produced 0.72 kg/cm³ or 0.00072 g/cm³, both absurd for a solid."
  },
  "rejectedAlternative": "**Specific gravity** — the ratio of a substance's density to the density of water — is a term you will meet in older textbooks and in engineering. It is dimensionless, so a specific gravity of 0.72 means the same thing as 720 kg/m³ in water. It is worth recognising if you see it, but note that it does not appear in the CED extract and is not on the equation sheet, so an exam question would have to define it for you. Work in kg/m³ and compare densities directly.",
  "trap": "Unit conversion on volumes. A block measured in centimetres gives a volume in cm³, and 1 m³ = $10^{6}$ cm³, not $10^{2}$. The tell is an answer that is off by a factor of a thousand or a million — a density of 0.72 kg/m³ would be thinner than air."
},
{
  "id": "flu-pressure",
  "group": "u8",
  "prereqs": [
    "flu-fluid-structure"
  ],
  "source": "CED 8.2",
  "title": "Pressure, and why it has no direction",
  "analogy": "Stand on snow in boots and you sink. Stand on the same snow in snowshoes and you do not. Your weight has not changed by a newton. What changed is the area it was spread over.",
  "plain": "Pressure is the perpendicular force on a surface divided by the area of that surface: $P = F_{\\perp}/A$. Its unit is the pascal, and 1 Pa is 1 N/m². Only the component of the force perpendicular to the surface counts; a force sliding along a surface contributes nothing to the pressure on it.\n\nThe part that trips people up: **pressure is a scalar**. It has a size and no direction. That sounds wrong, because force has a direction and pressure is built out of force. But the pressure at a point in a fluid is a single number that describes how hard the fluid pushes on *any* surface you put there, whatever way that surface faces. Turn the surface around and the number does not change; what changes is the direction of the force it produces.\n\nSo pressure does not push in a direction. A surface converts pressure into a force, and the direction of that force is set by the surface — always perpendicular to it, always pushing in. That is why a diver at a given depth feels the same squeeze on the top of the head, the soles of the feet, and both ears at once.",
  "depth": "$P = F_{\\perp}/A$ **is on the equation sheet**. It works in both directions, and the reverse direction is the one exams lean on: given a pressure and an area, the force on that area is $F = PA$. Every buoyancy derivation, every hydraulic-lift problem and every \"what force does the water exert on the window\" question runs through $F = PA$.\n\nWhere does a fluid's pressure come from? From the collective interactions between the fluid's particles and whatever surface they meet. Any single particle collision delivers a tiny impulse in one particular direction. Sum over an enormous number of them arriving from all directions and the directional information averages out; what survives is a rate of momentum transfer per unit area, which is a scalar. That is the microscopic reason pressure has no direction of its own — it is already an average over directions.\n\nNotice also that pressure is what makes a fluid move. A fluid element with higher pressure on one side than the other has a net force on it, and by Newton's second law it accelerates from the high-pressure side toward the low-pressure side. That is the sentence connecting this unit to Unit 2, and it is the reason [[flu-continuity]] and [[flu-bernoulli]] have anything to talk about: without a pressure difference, nothing flows.",
  "formula": "$$P = \\frac{F_{\\perp}}{A}$$\nrearranged: $F_{\\perp} = PA$\n1 Pa = 1 N/m²;  1 atm = $1.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa\nP is a SCALAR. F is a vector, perpendicular to the surface.",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A person of mass 60 kg stands on one foot. The sole in contact with the floor has area $1.4 \\times 10^{-2}$ m². Find the pressure the person exerts on the floor, and compare it with atmospheric pressure.",
    "given": "$m = 60$ kg; $A = 1.4 \\times 10^{-2}$ m²; $g = 9.8$ m/s²; 1 atm = $1.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "The perpendicular force on the floor is the person's weight: $F = mg = 60 \\times 9.8 = 588$ N.",
        "why": "Standing still means zero acceleration, so by Newton's second law the floor's normal force on the person equals the person's weight, and by Newton's third law the person pushes down on the floor with the same 588 N. The floor is horizontal, so all of it is perpendicular."
      },
      {
        "do": "$P = F_{\\perp}/A = 588 / (1.4 \\times 10^{-2}) = 4.2 \\times 10^{4}$ Pa.",
        "why": "Definition of pressure. Nothing about fluids yet — pressure is defined for any contact force on any area."
      },
      {
        "do": "Compare: $4.2 \\times 10^{4} / 1.0 \\times 10^{5} = 0.42$, so about 0.42 atm.",
        "why": "The comparison is the interesting part. Atmospheric pressure is larger than the pressure of a person standing on one foot."
      },
      {
        "do": "Ask why the atmosphere is not crushing the foot: the air pushes on the top of the foot with $1.0 \\times 10^{5} \\times 1.4 \\times 10^{-2} = 1400$ N as well, and on every other face.",
        "why": "This is the scalar point made physical. Pressure acts on every surface at once, so a uniform pressure produces no net force on a body — only pressure *differences* do."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$P \\approx 4.2 \\times 10^{4}$ Pa, roughly 0.4 atm.",
    "check": "Dimensional check from the other end. Pa = N/m², so N/m² $\\times$ m² should return newtons: $4.2 \\times 10^{4} \\times 1.4 \\times 10^{-2} = 588$ N, the weight we started from. Separately, a sanity bound: an adult's weight is a few hundred newtons and a foot is a few hundred square centimetres, giving tens of kilopascals — the answer sits where it should, well under an atmosphere."
  },
  "trap": "Treating pressure as a vector and writing it into a free-body diagram with an arrow. Pressure is a scalar; the arrows on a diagram are *forces*, obtained from pressure by $F = PA$ with the direction supplied by the surface. Related: forgetting the perpendicular. If a force acts at an angle to a surface, only $F\\cos\\theta$ into the surface contributes to the pressure on it."
},
{
  "id": "flu-hydrostatic",
  "group": "u8",
  "prereqs": [
    "flu-pressure",
    "flu-density"
  ],
  "source": "CED 8.2",
  "title": "Pressure with depth",
  "plain": "Go deeper into a fluid and the pressure rises, because there is more fluid above you and it all has weight that has to be held up. The relation is\n\n$P = P_0 + \\rho g h$,\n\nwhere $h$ is the depth below the reference level, $\\rho$ is the fluid's density, and $P_0$ is the pressure at that reference level — usually the pressure of the air pressing on the open surface.\n\nRead it as two pieces added together. The $P_0$ is what was already there before you went under. The $\\rho g h$ is the extra, and it is exactly the weight of the column of fluid standing above one square metre, spread over that square metre.\n\nThree things it depends on, and only three: the fluid's density, $g$, and the depth. Not the shape of the container, not how much fluid there is in total, not how wide the surface is. That is surprising enough that it gets its own page: [[flu-hydrostatic-paradox]].",
  "depth": "$P = P_0 + \\rho g h$ **is on the equation sheet**, as is the gauge-pressure piece $P_{\\mathrm{gauge}} = \\rho g h$ on its own line.\n\nYou can build the $\\rho g h$ term rather than accept it. Take an imaginary column of fluid of cross-sectional area $A$ reaching from the surface down to depth $h$. Its volume is $Ah$, so its mass is $\\rho A h$ and its weight is $\\rho A h g$. That column is not accelerating, so by Newton's first law the forces on it balance: the fluid below pushes up on its base with $PA$, the air pushes down on its top with $P_0 A$, and gravity pulls down with $\\rho A h g$. Setting up-forces equal to down-forces gives $PA = P_0 A + \\rho A h g$, and dividing by $A$ gives the equation. The $A$ cancelling is the whole reason the container's width never appears.\n\nTwo consequences worth carrying. First, $h$ is measured **downward from the reference level**, so two points at the same depth in the same connected body of fluid are at the same pressure, however far apart they are horizontally. That fact powers [[flu-pascal-hydraulic]]. Second, the equation is for a fluid at rest. In moving fluid the pressure also depends on the speed, which is [[flu-bernoulli]] — and Bernoulli's equation reduces to this one when both speeds are zero, which is a good way to remember that they are not rival formulas.",
  "formula": "$$P = P_0 + \\rho g h$$\n$$P_{\\mathrm{gauge}} = \\rho g h$$\nh = depth BELOW the reference level (positive downward)\nSame depth, same connected fluid  ->  same pressure.",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A diver is 12 m below the surface of a fresh-water lake, which is open to the air. Find the gauge pressure and the absolute pressure at the diver's depth.",
    "given": "$h = 12$ m; $\\rho = 1000$ kg/m³; $g = 9.8$ m/s²; $P_0 = P_{\\mathrm{atm}} = 1.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Identify the reference level as the lake surface, where the pressure is atmospheric, so $P_0 = 1.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa.",
        "why": "The equation needs a reference, and choosing the open surface is what makes $P_0$ a number you have rather than an unknown. The lake being open to the air is the phrase in the stem that licenses this."
      },
      {
        "do": "$P_{\\mathrm{gauge}} = \\rho g h = 1000 \\times 9.8 \\times 12 = 1.176 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa.",
        "why": "This term alone is the extra pressure due to the water, which is what a pressure gauge on the diver's equipment would read."
      },
      {
        "do": "$P = P_0 + \\rho g h = 1.0 \\times 10^{5} + 1.176 \\times 10^{5} = 2.18 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa.",
        "why": "Absolute pressure is the total, and it is the quantity Bernoulli's equation and the ideal-gas relations expect. Which one a question wants is decided by the word gauge or absolute, so it must be read off the stem, not guessed."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$P_{\\mathrm{gauge}} = 1.18 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa; $P_{\\mathrm{absolute}} = 2.18 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa.",
    "check": "An independent rule of thumb: about every 10 m of water adds one atmosphere, since $1000 \\times 9.8 \\times 10 = 9.8 \\times 10^{4}$ Pa, near enough to $1.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa. At 12 m the gauge pressure should therefore be a little over 1 atm and the absolute pressure a little over 2 atm. Both answers match, which would have caught a factor-of-ten slip or a forgotten $P_0$."
  },
  "trap": "Using the depth below the *surface* when the reference pressure you substituted was measured somewhere else, and mixing up $h$ with height above the ground. In $P = P_0 + \\rho g h$ the $h$ is how far you are **below** the point where the pressure is $P_0$. If a problem gives you heights measured upward, convert first; a sign error here flips a pressure increase into a decrease."
},
{
  "id": "flu-gauge-absolute",
  "group": "u8",
  "prereqs": [
    "flu-hydrostatic"
  ],
  "source": "CED 8.2",
  "title": "Gauge pressure versus absolute pressure",
  "plain": "Absolute pressure is the real, total pressure at a point. Gauge pressure is how much that exceeds a reference pressure, and the reference is nearly always the atmosphere. They differ by one atmosphere:\n\nabsolute = reference + gauge,  or  $P = P_0 + P_{\\mathrm{gauge}}$.\n\nThe name comes from real instruments. A tyre gauge, a blood-pressure cuff and a diver's depth gauge all have air on one side of their sensing element, so what they report is the *difference*. A flat tyre reads zero on a gauge and yet still has air in it at one atmosphere.\n\nWhich one a problem wants is decided by one word in the stem. Read it. If it says gauge, the atmospheric term never enters. If it says absolute, or if you are putting the pressure into Bernoulli's equation, keep the $P_0$.",
  "depth": "Why does the distinction matter physically rather than just bookkeeping-wise? Because a *uniform* pressure applied everywhere on a body produces no net force on it — the pushes on opposite faces cancel. Net forces come from pressure *differences*. When the atmosphere is pressing on both sides of a wall, tyre casing or eardrum, the atmospheric contribution cancels out and the gauge pressure is exactly the part that produces the net force. That is why engineers quote gauge pressure: it is the number that decides whether something bursts.\n\nThe equation-sheet lines $P = P_0 + \\rho g h$ and $P_{\\mathrm{gauge}} = \\rho g h$ together tell you that for a column of fluid, the gauge pressure *is* the $\\rho g h$ term. They are both on the sheet, printed as separate lines, and the pairing is the definition in disguise: whatever you add on top of the reference is the gauge part.\n\nOne case where you must use absolute: [[flu-bernoulli]]. Bernoulli's equation balances $P$ against energy densities, and only the absolute pressure represents the true energy per unit volume associated with the fluid's pressure. Using gauge pressures on both sides of Bernoulli happens to work when both points are exposed to the same reference, because the $P_0$ then cancels — but that is a coincidence of the setup, not a rule, and it fails as soon as one point is inside a sealed vessel.",
  "formula": "$$P_{\\mathrm{absolute}} = P_0 + P_{\\mathrm{gauge}}$$\nFor a fluid column:  $P_{\\mathrm{gauge}} = \\rho g h$\n$P_0 = P_{\\mathrm{atm}} = 1.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa (on the sheet)\nNet force on a wall with atmosphere behind it:  $F = P_{\\mathrm{gauge}} A$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A bicycle tyre gauge reads $2.2 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa. (a) What is the absolute pressure of the air inside? (b) Consider a $1.0 \\times 10^{-2}$ m² patch of the tyre wall, with tyre air inside and the atmosphere outside. What is the net outward force on that patch from the two air pressures?",
    "given": "$P_{\\mathrm{gauge}} = 2.2 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa; $A = 1.0 \\times 10^{-2}$ m²; $P_{\\mathrm{atm}} = 1.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "(a) $P = P_0 + P_{\\mathrm{gauge}} = 1.0 \\times 10^{5} + 2.2 \\times 10^{5} = 3.2 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa.",
        "why": "A gauge reads the excess over the atmosphere it sits in, so recovering the absolute value means adding the atmosphere back. Nothing about tyres is used — this is the definition."
      },
      {
        "do": "(b) Inside air pushes out on the patch with $F_{\\text{out}} = P_{\\mathrm{abs}} A = 3.2 \\times 10^{5} \\times 1.0 \\times 10^{-2} = 3200$ N.",
        "why": "Converting a pressure to a force needs the absolute pressure on that side, because the inside air does not know about the outside air."
      },
      {
        "do": "Outside air pushes in with $F_{\\text{in}} = P_{\\mathrm{atm}} A = 1.0 \\times 10^{5} \\times 1.0 \\times 10^{-2} = 1000$ N.",
        "why": "The atmosphere is a real pressure acting on the other face, so it belongs on the free-body diagram of the patch. Leaving it off is what produces a 3200 N answer."
      },
      {
        "do": "Net outward $= 3200 - 1000 = 2200$ N.",
        "why": "Forces are vectors and these two are opposite, so they subtract. This is the quantity that decides whether the tyre wall holds."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "(a) $3.2 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa absolute. (b) 2200 N net outward.",
    "check": "A limiting case. Let the tyre go flat so the gauge reads zero. Then the absolute pressure is $1.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa, inside and outside push equally, and the net force is zero — which matches the fact that a flat tyre is limp rather than being crushed by the atmosphere. The general pattern the two cases share is that the net force came out as $P_{\\mathrm{gauge}} A$ both times, which is the reason gauge pressure is the useful engineering number."
  },
  "trap": "Adding the atmosphere twice, or not at all. If a stem says \"the gauge reads\", the atmosphere is already excluded and you add it once to get absolute. If a stem gives you a pressure with no adjective and the container is open to the air, it is almost always absolute. And when computing a net force across a wall with air on the far side, subtract the atmosphere — otherwise your answer is too big by $P_{\\mathrm{atm}} A$."
},
{
  "id": "flu-hydrostatic-paradox",
  "group": "u8",
  "prereqs": [
    "flu-hydrostatic"
  ],
  "source": "CED 8.2",
  "title": "Why the container's shape does not matter",
  "analogy": "A wide bucket and a thin test tube, both filled to the same line, have the same pressure at the bottom. The bucket holds far more water. It also has far more floor to spread it over, and the two effects cancel exactly.",
  "plain": "Look at $P = P_0 + \\rho g h$ again and notice what is missing: there is no area, no volume, no width, no mass of fluid. Pressure at a point in a static fluid depends only on the depth, the density and $g$.\n\nSo a swimming pool and a drinking straw filled to the same depth with the same liquid have the same pressure at the bottom. This is often called the hydrostatic paradox, and it is only paradoxical if you were secretly thinking \"more water above me means more pressure\". More water above you does mean more weight above you — but that weight is also spread over more area, and pressure is weight per area. The extra cancels.\n\nThe other half of the surprise: the total downward **force** on the bottom is $F = PA$, and that *does* depend on the area. Same pressure, ten times the floor, ten times the force. Pressure and force are different questions and a stem will ask you for one of them specifically.",
  "depth": "The strange case is a vessel whose walls are not vertical. Take a container with a wide flat base and a narrow neck, like a funnel standing on its wide end. Fill it to depth $h$. The pressure at the base is $\\rho g h$ (gauge), so the force the water exerts down on the base is $\\rho g h A_{\\text{base}}$ — and that can be much **larger** than the weight of the water actually in the vessel.\n\nNothing is broken. Do a free-body diagram on the water. Forces on it are: gravity down, the base pushing up, and the sloping walls pushing on it. Because the walls slope, the force they exert perpendicular to their own surface has a downward component. The water is in equilibrium, so up must equal down, and the walls' downward push makes up exactly the difference. The extra force on the base is transmitted through the walls, not conjured out of nothing.\n\nFlip the vessel — a wide top narrowing to a small base — and the walls push *up* on the water instead, and the force on the base comes out smaller than the weight of the water. Only for straight vertical walls does force on the base equal the weight of the fluid. That is the special case, not the rule.",
  "formula": "$P$ at depth $h$: depends on $\\rho$, $g$, $h$ only.\n$F$ on the base: $F = P A_{\\mathrm{base}}$ — depends on the area too.\nStraight vertical walls only:  $F_{\\mathrm{base}} = $ weight of fluid.\nSloping walls:  $F_{\\mathrm{base}} \\ne$ weight of fluid (walls carry the rest).",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "Two open vessels are filled with water to a depth of 0.80 m. Vessel A is a straight cylinder of base area 0.020 m². Vessel B has a base area of 0.20 m² for its lowest 0.20 m and then narrows to a neck of area 0.020 m² for the remaining 0.60 m. Compare the gauge pressure at the base of each, the downward force on each base, and the weight of water in vessel B.",
    "given": "$h = 0.80$ m for both; $\\rho = 1000$ kg/m³; $g = 9.8$ m/s²; A: $A = 0.020$ m². B: base 0.20 m² for 0.20 m, neck 0.020 m² for 0.60 m.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Gauge pressure at the base of each: $P = \\rho g h = 1000 \\times 9.8 \\times 0.80 = 7840$ Pa. Identical.",
        "why": "The equation contains no area or volume term, so the two shapes cannot give different answers. Doing this step first stops you from being led by the picture."
      },
      {
        "do": "Force on A's base: $F = PA = 7840 \\times 0.020 = 157$ N. Force on B's base: $F = 7840 \\times 0.20 = 1568$ N.",
        "why": "Force needs the area, and B's base is ten times A's. Same pressure, ten times the force — this is where the shape finally enters."
      },
      {
        "do": "Volume of water in B: $0.20 \\times 0.20 + 0.020 \\times 0.60 = 0.040 + 0.012 = 0.052$ m³. Mass $= \\rho V = 52$ kg, weight $= 52 \\times 9.8 = 510$ N.",
        "why": "Computing the weight separately is the only way to see the paradox. It has to be done from the geometry, since no equation gives it."
      },
      {
        "do": "Compare: the base of B is pushed down on with 1568 N while the water in it weighs only 510 N. The missing 1058 N is the downward component of the force the sloping shoulder exerts on the water.",
        "why": "The water is in equilibrium, so the total downward force on it (weight plus the walls' downward push) must equal the base's upward push of 1568 N. That equation is what identifies where the extra comes from."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "Both bases are at 7840 Pa gauge. Force on A's base 157 N; on B's base 1568 N. The water in B weighs only 510 N — the sloping shoulder supplies the other 1058 N downward.",
    "check": "Run the same reasoning on vessel A, which has straight vertical walls and so must show no paradox. Its water volume is $0.020 \\times 0.80 = 0.016$ m³, mass 16 kg, weight 157 N — equal to the 157 N force on its base, to the digits carried. The method therefore reproduces the case where the answer is known independently, which it would not do if the pressure or the area had been mishandled."
  },
  "trap": "Believing a wider container has higher pressure at the same depth. It does not; it has a larger *force* on a larger base. The reverse error is just as common: assuming the force on the base always equals the weight of the fluid. That is only true for straight vertical walls."
},
{
  "id": "flu-pascal-hydraulic",
  "group": "u8",
  "prereqs": [
    "flu-pressure",
    "flu-hydrostatic-paradox"
  ],
  "source": "CED 8.2",
  "title": "The hydraulic lift: force multiplied, work not",
  "plain": "Connect two vertical cylinders at the bottom with a pipe, fill the whole thing with an incompressible fluid, and put a piston in each. Because the two pistons are at the same height in the same connected fluid, the pressure at each is the same. Same pressure, and $F = PA$, so the piston with the bigger area feels the bigger force:\n\n$\\frac{F_1}{A_1} = \\frac{F_2}{A_2}$,  so  $F_2 = F_1 \\frac{A_2}{A_1}$.\n\nMake the second piston forty times the area and a 50 N push becomes a 2000 N lift. This is how a garage jack raises a car with one hand.\n\nIt is not free. The fluid is incompressible, so whatever volume the small piston pushes down must appear under the big one. The small piston travels a long way and the big one barely moves — by exactly the same factor of forty. Multiply force by 40, divide distance by 40, and the work is unchanged.",
  "depth": "Nothing here is new physics. The equal-pressure claim is the last line of [[flu-hydrostatic]]: two points at the same depth in the same connected fluid are at the same pressure. The force conversion is $F = PA$ from [[flu-pressure]]. The distance relation is incompressibility: $A_1 d_1 = A_2 d_2$, the same statement as [[flu-continuity]] with volume instead of volume-per-second.\n\nPut those together and the work follows:\n\n$W_2 = F_2 d_2 = \\left(F_1 \\frac{A_2}{A_1}\\right)\\left(d_1 \\frac{A_1}{A_2}\\right) = F_1 d_1 = W_1$.\n\nThe area ratio appears once multiplying and once dividing, so it cancels. The machine gives out exactly the energy you put in, which is what conservation of energy requires; a device that returned more would let you build a perpetual motion machine out of a jack and a bucket. Real jacks return less, because real fluids have viscosity and real seals have friction.\n\nOn the equation sheet: **nothing in this paragraph is printed there**. $F_1/A_1 = F_2/A_2$ is not a sheet line, and neither is $A_1 d_1 = A_2 d_2$. You are expected to reconstruct both from $P = F_{\\perp}/A$, which *is* on the sheet, plus the fact that pressure is equal at equal depths. Practise deriving it; on the day you will not be handed it.",
  "formula": "Same height, same connected fluid  ->  same pressure:\n$$\\frac{F_1}{A_1} = \\frac{F_2}{A_2}$$\nIncompressible, so equal volumes:  $A_1 d_1 = A_2 d_2$\nTherefore:  $F_1 d_1 = F_2 d_2$   (work in = work out)\nNEITHER of these is on the equation sheet — derive them from $P = F_{\\perp}/A$.",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A hydraulic lift has a small piston of area $2.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ m² and a large piston of area $8.0 \\times 10^{-2}$ m², at the same height. You push down on the small piston with 50 N. (a) What upward force does the large piston exert? (b) If the small piston descends 0.60 m, how far does the large one rise? (c) Compare the work done at each piston.",
    "given": "$A_1 = 2.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ m²; $A_2 = 8.0 \\times 10^{-2}$ m²; $F_1 = 50$ N; $d_1 = 0.60$ m; pistons at the same height; ideal fluid.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Area ratio: $A_2/A_1 = 8.0 \\times 10^{-2} / 2.0 \\times 10^{-3} = 40$.",
        "why": "Computing the ratio once, up front, means it appears as a clean factor of 40 in every later step and the cancellation in part (c) becomes visible rather than buried in decimals."
      },
      {
        "do": "(a) Same height and same connected fluid, so the pressure at both pistons is equal: $F_1/A_1 = F_2/A_2$, giving $F_2 = 50 \\times 40 = 2000$ N.",
        "why": "The 'same height' phrase is what licenses equal pressure. If the pistons were at different heights you would need a $\\rho g h$ correction between them, so this is a condition to check, not a formality."
      },
      {
        "do": "(b) The fluid is incompressible, so the volume pushed out of one cylinder appears in the other: $A_1 d_1 = A_2 d_2$, giving $d_2 = 0.60/40 = 0.015$ m.",
        "why": "This is where the price is paid, and the reason is a property of the fluid rather than of the machine. Volume, not distance, is the conserved thing."
      },
      {
        "do": "(c) $W_1 = F_1 d_1 = 50 \\times 0.60 = 30$ J. $W_2 = F_2 d_2 = 2000 \\times 0.015 = 30$ J. Equal.",
        "why": "Work is force times distance along the motion, and the two factors of 40 are inverse. The equality is required by conservation of energy, so a disagreement would signal an arithmetic error, not a discovery."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "(a) 2000 N. (b) 0.015 m, or 1.5 cm. (c) 30 J at each — the lift multiplies force by 40 and does not create energy.",
    "check": "Check the volume independently of the work. Volume displaced at the small piston: $2.0 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 0.60 = 1.2 \\times 10^{-3}$ m³. Volume gained at the large piston: $8.0 \\times 10^{-2} \\times 0.015 = 1.2 \\times 10^{-3}$ m³. They match, so the distance in (b) is consistent with incompressibility. Also note that 2000 N supports a mass of about 204 kg, the right order of magnitude for a jack lifting a corner of a car, not a whole one."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Why is it *force* that gets multiplied and not energy? Because pressure is force per area, so handing the fluid a bigger area is handing it a bigger force, for free. Distance is fixed separately by how much fluid there is to move, and that is the constraint energy conservation enters through. A machine can trade the two but cannot beat their product.",
  "trap": "Thinking the lift creates energy, or answering (c) with 'forty times as much work out'. The other common slip is using the *radius* ratio where the *area* ratio is wanted: if a piston's radius is 4 times larger, its area is 16 times larger, and the force multiplication is 16, not 4."
},
{
  "id": "flu-buoyancy",
  "group": "u8",
  "prereqs": [
    "flu-hydrostatic",
    "flu-density"
  ],
  "source": "CED 8.3",
  "title": "Buoyancy, derived from a pressure difference",
  "plain": "Put an object in a fluid and the fluid pushes on every face of it. The pushes on the sides cancel by symmetry. But the bottom of the object is deeper than the top, and deeper means higher pressure, so the upward push on the bottom beats the downward push on the top. What is left over is a net upward force. That is the **buoyant force**. It is not a new kind of force — it is the sum of the ordinary contact forces the fluid exerts, and it points up because pressure rises with depth.\n\nWork out the size and it comes to something clean: the buoyant force equals the weight of the fluid the object pushed out of the way.\n\n$F_b = \\rho V g$\n\nHere $\\rho$ is the density of the **fluid**, not the object, and $V$ is the volume of fluid **displaced** — the submerged volume of the object, which for a fully submerged object is its whole volume and for a floating one is only the part below the waterline.",
  "depth": "The derivation is worth doing once, because it explains every surprising property of $F_b$. Take a rectangular block of horizontal cross-section $A$ with its top face at depth $h_1$ and its bottom face at depth $h_2$. The fluid pushes down on the top with $(P_0 + \\rho g h_1)A$ and up on the bottom with $(P_0 + \\rho g h_2)A$. Subtract:\n\n$F_b = \\rho g (h_2 - h_1) A = \\rho g V$,\n\nsince $(h_2 - h_1)A$ is the block's volume. Three things fall out at once. $P_0$ cancels, so the buoyant force does not depend on the atmosphere. The depths appear only as a *difference*, so the force does not depend on how deep the object is. And the object's own mass and material never entered at any point — only the fluid's density and the displaced volume.\n\n$F_b = \\rho V g$ **is on the equation sheet**, with $\\rho$ = density and $V$ = volume in the sheet's own variable list. The sheet does not tell you *whose* density and *which* volume; that is the part you have to know, and it is where most marks are lost.\n\nTreat buoyancy as one force among others on a normal free-body diagram. A block held under water has weight down, buoyancy up, and tension or a normal force making up the difference; $\\sum F = ma$ applies exactly as always. This unit is Newton's laws with a new force on the diagram, which is what the topic title 'Fluids and Newton's Laws' is telling you.",
  "formula": "$$F_b = \\rho V g$$\n$\\rho$ = density of the FLUID\n$V$ = volume of fluid DISPLACED (= submerged volume of the object)\nDerivation:  $F_b = (P_{\\mathrm{bottom}} - P_{\\mathrm{top}})A = \\rho g \\Delta h A$\nApparent weight of a submerged object:  $F_{\\mathrm{apparent}} = mg - F_b$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A solid cube of side 0.10 m is fully submerged in water, held so that its top face is 0.30 m below the surface. Using pressures on the top and bottom faces, find the net upward force the water exerts on it. Then show it equals $\\rho V g$.",
    "given": "cube side 0.10 m; top face at $h_1 = 0.30$ m, bottom at $h_2 = 0.40$ m; $\\rho = 1000$ kg/m³; $g = 9.8$ m/s²; $P_0 = 1.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Face area $A = 0.10 \\times 0.10 = 1.0 \\times 10^{-2}$ m². Bottom face depth $= 0.30 + 0.10 = 0.40$ m.",
        "why": "The bottom is one side-length deeper than the top. Getting this offset right is the only geometry in the problem, and it is what sets the size of the answer."
      },
      {
        "do": "$P_{\\text{top}} = 1.0 \\times 10^{5} + 1000 \\times 9.8 \\times 0.30 = 1.0294 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa. Downward force on top $= P_{\\text{top}} A = 1029.4$ N.",
        "why": "Pressure converts to force by $F = PA$, and the direction is set by the surface: fluid always pushes into a surface, so on the top face that is downward."
      },
      {
        "do": "$P_{\\text{bottom}} = 1.0 \\times 10^{5} + 1000 \\times 9.8 \\times 0.40 = 1.0392 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa. Upward force on bottom $= 1039.2$ N.",
        "why": "Same reasoning, opposite face, so the push is upward. The two absolute pressures are nearly equal, which is why the subtraction must be done carefully rather than rounded early."
      },
      {
        "do": "Side faces: each vertical face has an identical opposite face at the same depths, so their horizontal forces cancel in pairs.",
        "why": "Stating this is not padding — it is the reason the answer is purely vertical, and a question can ask for it explicitly."
      },
      {
        "do": "Net upward $= 1039.2 - 1029.4 = 9.8$ N. Compare $\\rho V g = 1000 \\times (0.10)^3 \\times 9.8 = 1000 \\times 1.0 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 9.8 = 9.8$ N.",
        "why": "The agreement is the point of the exercise: the sheet formula is not an extra law, it is this subtraction already done in symbols."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "9.8 N upward, matching $F_b = \\rho V g = 9.8$ N.",
    "check": "An invariance check rather than a re-substitution. Redo the pressures with the cube much deeper — top at 1.30 m, bottom at 1.40 m. Each pressure rises by $1000 \\times 9.8 \\times 1.0 = 9800$ Pa, so each force rises by 98 N, and the *difference* is untouched: still 9.8 N. The buoyant force is therefore independent of depth, exactly as the symbolic derivation said, and a solution that produced a depth-dependent answer would be wrong somewhere."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Why is the answer the weight of the *displaced fluid* rather than something about the object? Imagine scooping the object out and letting fluid fill the hole. That parcel of fluid sits there in equilibrium, so the surrounding fluid must be pushing up on it with exactly its own weight. The surrounding fluid cannot tell what is in the hole, so it pushes on the object with the same force. Shape, material and mass of the occupant are irrelevant by construction.",
  "trap": "Putting the object's density into $F_b = \\rho V g$. The $\\rho$ is the fluid's. Equally common: using the object's total volume when it is only partly submerged, or believing that a submerged object gets more buoyant force as it goes deeper. In an incompressible fluid it does not — only the pressure *difference* across the object matters, and that does not change with depth."
},
{
  "id": "flu-floating",
  "group": "u8",
  "prereqs": [
    "flu-buoyancy"
  ],
  "source": "CED 8.3",
  "title": "Floating, sinking, and how much sticks out",
  "plain": "A fully submerged object has two vertical forces on it: its weight $\\rho_o V g$ down and the buoyant force $\\rho_f V g$ up, with the same $V$. Which wins is decided entirely by which density is larger.\n\n- $\\rho_o > \\rho_f$: weight wins, the object sinks.\n- $\\rho_o < \\rho_f$: buoyancy wins, the object rises until part of it is out of the fluid.\n- $\\rho_o = \\rho_f$: the forces balance anywhere, and it hangs where it is put.\n\nOnce floating, the object settles at the depth where the two forces balance. It is not fully submerged any more, so the displaced volume is only the part below the surface, and that is what sets the waterline.",
  "depth": "For a floating body in equilibrium the buoyant force equals the weight:\n\n$\\rho_f V_{\\text{sub}} g = \\rho_o V_{\\text{obj}} g$,\n\nand cancelling $g$ gives the fraction submerged:\n\n$\\frac{V_{\\text{sub}}}{V_{\\text{obj}}} = \\frac{\\rho_o}{\\rho_f}$.\n\nThat ratio is worth holding in your head, but it is **not on the equation sheet**. Rebuild it each time from $F_b = \\rho V g$ (which is) plus $\\sum F = 0$. Doing so also protects you from getting it upside down, which is the single commonest error in this topic — if you derive it, the denominators can only land one way.\n\nThe result is a pure ratio of densities, so it does not depend on how big the object is. A small block and a large block of the same wood float with the same fraction underwater. It also explains why an object floats higher in a denser fluid: raise $\\rho_f$ and the fraction submerged falls.\n\nTwo related quantities. The **apparent weight** of a submerged object hanging from a scale is $mg - F_b$, since the scale only supplies what buoyancy does not. And a hollow object — a boat, a submarine with empty tanks — has a low *overall* density even when made of dense material, because the enclosed air counts toward $V_{\\text{obj}}$ and adds almost nothing to $m$. Flood the tanks and the overall density rises past the water's, and the submarine sinks.",
  "formula": "Floating equilibrium:  $\\rho_f V_{\\mathrm{sub}} g = \\rho_o V_{\\mathrm{obj}} g$\n$$\\frac{V_{\\mathrm{sub}}}{V_{\\mathrm{obj}}} = \\frac{\\rho_o}{\\rho_f}$$\n(NOT on the equation sheet — derive it from $F_b = \\rho V g$ and $\\sum F = 0$)\nApparent weight when submerged:  $mg - F_b$",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "A block of wood of density 650 kg/m³ and volume $2.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ m³ floats in fresh water of density 1000 kg/m³. (a) What fraction of its volume is below the surface? (b) What is the buoyant force on it? (c) The same block is moved to seawater of density 1025 kg/m³. Does it float higher or lower, and by how much does the submerged fraction change?",
    "given": "$\\rho_o = 650$ kg/m³; $V_{\\mathrm{obj}} = 2.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ m³; $\\rho_{\\text{fresh}} = 1000$ kg/m³; $\\rho_{\\text{sea}} = 1025$ kg/m³; $g = 9.8$ m/s².",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Write the equilibrium condition rather than a formula: the block floats at rest, so $\\sum F = 0$ and $F_b = mg$.",
        "why": "Starting from Newton's first law rather than from a remembered ratio means the ratio comes out the right way up. The block is at rest — that is the physical fact the whole problem hangs on."
      },
      {
        "do": "Substitute both sides: $\\rho_f V_{\\mathrm{sub}} g = \\rho_o V_{\\mathrm{obj}} g$, so $V_{\\mathrm{sub}}/V_{\\mathrm{obj}} = \\rho_o/\\rho_f = 650/1000 = 0.65$.",
        "why": "Both the weight and the buoyant force are of the form $\\rho V g$; writing them the same way makes the $g$ cancel and leaves a pure ratio, which is why the answer has no units."
      },
      {
        "do": "(b) $V_{\\mathrm{sub}} = 0.65 \\times 2.0 \\times 10^{-3} = 1.3 \\times 10^{-3}$ m³, so $F_b = 1000 \\times 1.3 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 9.8 = 12.7$ N.",
        "why": "The displaced volume is the submerged part only, not the whole block. Using the whole block here is the standard way to get a buoyant force that exceeds the weight and makes the block accelerate upward out of the water."
      },
      {
        "do": "(c) In seawater: $V_{\\mathrm{sub}}/V_{\\mathrm{obj}} = 650/1025 = 0.634$. Smaller fraction, so the block floats higher, by about 1.6 percentage points of its volume.",
        "why": "Only $\\rho_f$ changed, and it is in the denominator, so the direction of the change can be read off before any arithmetic. Doing the number afterwards confirms the reasoning rather than replacing it."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "(a) 0.65, i.e. 65% submerged. (b) 12.7 N. (c) It floats slightly higher, 63.4% submerged rather than 65%.",
    "check": "Two limiting cases the formula must survive. If $\\rho_o = \\rho_f$ the fraction is 1 — just fully submerged and neutrally buoyant, which is right. If $\\rho_o > \\rho_f$ the fraction exceeds 1, which is impossible, and the impossibility is the formula's way of saying the object sinks instead. Both behave correctly, so the ratio is not inverted. Separately, part (b)'s 12.7 N should equal the block's weight $\\rho_o V_{\\mathrm{obj}} g = 650 \\times 2.0 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 9.8 = 12.7$ N — computed from the object's side rather than the fluid's, and it matches."
  },
  "trap": "Inverting the ratio and writing $\\rho_f/\\rho_o$, which for wood in water gives 1.54 — a fraction greater than one, which should be an instant alarm. The other trap is comparing masses instead of densities: a large light object can be far more massive than a small dense one and still float. And for part-submerged objects, $V$ in $F_b = \\rho V g$ is the submerged volume, never the total."
},
{
  "id": "flu-continuity",
  "group": "u8",
  "prereqs": [
    "flu-fluid-structure",
    "flu-pressure"
  ],
  "source": "CED 8.4",
  "title": "Continuity: narrow pipe, fast flow",
  "analogy": "Four lanes of traffic merging into one. If no cars are allowed to park or vanish, the same number must pass each point per minute, so the cars in the single lane have to be moving faster.",
  "plain": "Take a pipe that is completely full of an incompressible fluid, open at both ends, and steady flow. Whatever mass enters per second has to leave per second — there is nowhere in the middle for it to accumulate, because the fluid cannot be compressed and the pipe cannot hold more.\n\nThe volume passing a point per second is the cross-sectional area times the speed, $V/t = Av$. Setting that equal at two points along the pipe gives the **continuity equation**:\n\n$A_1 v_1 = A_2 v_2$.\n\nRead it as a trade: where the pipe is narrow, the fluid must be fast. Squeeze the end of a garden hose and the water shoots further, not because you added energy but because you shrank $A$.\n\nWatch the geometry. For a circular pipe $A = \\pi r^2$, so halving the radius quarters the area and therefore quadruples the speed. The relation is with area, not with radius or diameter.",
  "depth": "$A_1 v_1 = A_2 v_2$ **is on the equation sheet**. The volume flow rate $V/t = Av$ is *not* — the CED lists it as a derived equation, meaning you are expected to be able to produce it. It is easy to produce: in a time $t$ the fluid at a cross-section moves a distance $vt$, sweeping out a cylinder of volume $Avt$, so the volume per unit time is $Av$.\n\nWhat makes the fluid speed up? A pressure difference. A fluid element entering the narrowing section has higher pressure behind it than in front, so the net force on it is forward and Newton's second law accelerates it. This is the content of the topic's first idea — fluid flows when the pressure at one place differs from the pressure at another — and it is the bridge to [[flu-bernoulli]], which quantifies how much the pressure has to drop.\n\nThe conditions matter. Continuity in this form needs the fluid incompressible, the flow steady, and the pipe completely filled. The exam's stated conventions grant you the ideal fluid and the filled pipe by default; steadiness is an assumption you are making, and the conventions do not mention it. A pipe that is only half full, or a gas being compressed as it flows, breaks it — and in that case the conserved quantity is mass flow rate $\\rho A v$, with the density allowed to differ at the two points.",
  "formula": "$$A_1 v_1 = A_2 v_2$$   (on the equation sheet)\nVolume flow rate:  $\\frac{V}{t} = Av$   (derived — NOT on the sheet)\nCircular pipe:  $A = \\pi r^2$, so halving $r$ multiplies $v$ by 4\nConditions: incompressible, steady, pipe completely filled.",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "Water flows steadily through a horizontal pipe of radius 0.040 m at 1.5 m/s. The pipe narrows to a radius of 0.020 m. (a) Find the speed in the narrow section. (b) Find the volume flow rate. (c) How long does it take to fill a 0.030 m³ tank from this pipe?",
    "given": "$r_1 = 0.040$ m; $v_1 = 1.5$ m/s; $r_2 = 0.020$ m; tank volume 0.030 m³; water is ideal and the pipe is full.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Areas: $A_1 = \\pi (0.040)^2 = 5.03 \\times 10^{-3}$ m², $A_2 = \\pi (0.020)^2 = 1.26 \\times 10^{-3}$ m².",
        "why": "Continuity is a statement about areas. Going straight from the radii to a speed ratio is where the factor-of-two error creeps in, so convert to areas explicitly."
      },
      {
        "do": "(a) $A_1 v_1 = A_2 v_2$, so $v_2 = v_1 (A_1/A_2) = 1.5 \\times 4 = 6.0$ m/s.",
        "why": "The area ratio is exactly 4 because the radius halved and area goes as $r^2$. Using the ratio rather than the two decimals keeps the exact factor visible."
      },
      {
        "do": "(b) $V/t = A_1 v_1 = 5.03 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 1.5 = 7.5 \\times 10^{-3}$ m³/s.",
        "why": "Either section can be used — that is what continuity asserts — so choosing the wide one where both numbers were given avoids compounding a rounded value."
      },
      {
        "do": "(c) $t = V / (V/t) = 0.030 / (7.5 \\times 10^{-3}) = 4.0$ s.",
        "why": "The flow rate is constant in time (steady flow), so filling time is just total volume over rate; no kinematics is needed."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "(a) 6.0 m/s. (b) $7.5 \\times 10^{-3}$ m³/s. (c) 4.0 s.",
    "check": "A functional-dependence check that does not reuse the arithmetic: the radius was halved, so the area is quartered, so the speed must be exactly four times larger. $6.0 = 4 \\times 1.5$, so part (a) is consistent with the scaling and not merely with a calculator. And the units in (b): m² $\\times$ m/s = m³/s, which is a volume per time, as a flow rate must be."
  },
  "rejectedAlternative": "The tempting wrong model is that a narrow pipe restricts the flow, so less water gets through and it moves more slowly. That is a statement about a pipe with a *fixed pressure source* and real viscosity, where narrowing genuinely reduces the total throughput. In the ideal steady-flow problem the throughput is fixed by the requirement that nothing accumulates, and the only free variable left is the speed.",
  "trap": "Using the diameter or radius ratio where the area ratio belongs. Doubling the radius divides the speed by 4, not by 2. Also, continuity by itself says nothing about pressure — do not conclude the narrow section has higher pressure because the fluid is being squeezed. It is the other way round; that is [[flu-bernoulli]]."
},
{
  "id": "flu-bernoulli",
  "group": "u8",
  "prereqs": [
    "flu-continuity",
    "flu-hydrostatic"
  ],
  "source": "CED 8.4",
  "title": "Bernoulli's equation as energy per unit volume",
  "plain": "Bernoulli's equation says that along a streamline in an ideal fluid, this combination is the same everywhere:\n\n$P_1 + \\rho g y_1 + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v_1^2 = P_2 + \\rho g y_2 + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v_2^2$.\n\nIt is conservation of energy, written per unit volume. Take the ordinary energy statement $\\frac{1}{2}mv^2 + mgy$ = constant and divide every term by the volume: $m/V$ is the density, so $mgy/V$ becomes $\\rho g y$ and $\\frac{1}{2}mv^2/V$ becomes $\\frac{1}{2}\\rho v^2$. The extra $P$ term is the work per unit volume that the surrounding fluid does pushing the parcel along.\n\nThe consequence people remember: at the same height, where the fluid moves faster, the pressure is **lower**. Faster does not mean pushier. The three terms share a fixed total, so raising the speed term must lower one of the others.",
  "depth": "The full equation **is on the equation sheet**, exactly in the form above. Torricelli's result, $v = \\sqrt{2g\\Delta y}$, is **not** — the CED lists it as derived, so you are expected to get it from Bernoulli. Do that now, since it is a standard exam move.\n\nTake a large open tank with a small hole a height $\\Delta y$ below the surface. Point 1 is the top surface, point 2 is the hole. Both are open to the atmosphere, so $P_1 = P_2$ and the pressure terms cancel. The tank is wide compared with the hole, so by continuity the surface descends far more slowly than the jet leaves and $v_1 \\approx 0$. What is left is $\\rho g y_1 = \\rho g y_2 + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v_2^2$, and $\\rho$ cancels, giving $v = \\sqrt{2g\\Delta y}$ — the same speed an object would reach falling freely through $\\Delta y$, which is a strong hint that this was energy conservation all along.\n\nBernoulli's conditions are strict and are examined: the flow must be **steady**, the fluid **incompressible**, and there must be **no viscosity**, and the two points must lie on the same streamline. Real flows that are turbulent, or through a long narrow tube where drag matters, lose energy, and the sum falls from point 1 to point 2 rather than staying equal. The exam's stated conventions assume ideal fluids unless a question says otherwise, so the usual job is to notice when a question *has* said otherwise.\n\nTwo special cases are worth naming, because most problems are one of them. At constant height the $\\rho g y$ terms cancel and you get the pure pressure-speed trade. At zero speed everywhere the $\\frac{1}{2}\\rho v^2$ terms vanish and the equation collapses to $P_1 = P_2 + \\rho g (y_2 - y_1)$, which is [[flu-hydrostatic]]. Bernoulli contains the static case, so they cannot contradict each other.",
  "formula": "$$P_1 + \\rho g y_1 + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v_1^2 = P_2 + \\rho g y_2 + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v_2^2$$\n(on the equation sheet; every term is an energy per unit volume, in Pa)\nSame height:  $P_1 + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v_1^2 = P_2 + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v_2^2$\nTorricelli (derived, NOT on the sheet):  $v = \\sqrt{2 g \\Delta y}$\nConditions: steady, incompressible, non-viscous, same streamline.",
  "worked": {
    "prompt": "Water of density 1000 kg/m³ flows steadily through a horizontal pipe. In the wide section the speed is 2.0 m/s and the absolute pressure is $1.80 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa. The pipe narrows so that the cross-sectional area is one third of its original value. Find the speed and the absolute pressure in the narrow section.",
    "given": "$\\rho = 1000$ kg/m³; $v_1 = 2.0$ m/s; $P_1 = 1.80 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa; $A_2 = A_1/3$; horizontal pipe; ideal fluid.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "do": "Use continuity first: $v_2 = v_1 (A_1/A_2) = 2.0 \\times 3 = 6.0$ m/s.",
        "why": "Bernoulli's equation contains two unknowns here, $v_2$ and $P_2$, so it cannot be solved alone. Continuity supplies one of them and comes from mass conservation, which is independent of the energy statement."
      },
      {
        "do": "The pipe is horizontal, so $y_1 = y_2$ and the $\\rho g y$ terms are identical on both sides and cancel.",
        "why": "Cancelling them explicitly is safer than setting $y = 0$, because it shows the result does not depend on where the height origin was put."
      },
      {
        "do": "$P_2 = P_1 + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho (v_1^2 - v_2^2) = 1.80 \\times 10^{5} + 500 \\times (4.0 - 36) $.",
        "why": "Rearranging symbolically before substituting keeps the sign visible: $v_2 > v_1$ makes the bracket negative, so the pressure must fall. A numeric-first approach hides that and invites a sign slip."
      },
      {
        "do": "$= 1.80 \\times 10^{5} - 1.6 \\times 10^{4} = 1.64 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa.",
        "why": "Note the pressures are absolute throughout. Bernoulli's $P$ term is an energy density and only absolute pressure represents it correctly."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$v_2 = 6.0$ m/s; $P_2 = 1.64 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa — the pressure drops by $1.6 \\times 10^{4}$ Pa where the fluid speeds up.",
    "check": "Convert the pressure drop into a height, which is an independent physical quantity. A drop of $1.6 \\times 10^{4}$ Pa corresponds to a water column of $h = \\Delta P/(\\rho g) = 1.6 \\times 10^{4}/(1000 \\times 9.8) = 1.6$ m. So if you stood open vertical tubes in the two sections, the water would sit about 1.6 m lower in the narrow one — a visible, plausible amount for a pipe carrying water at a few metres per second, and not the tens of metres a factor-of-ten error would give. The direction also passes: pressure fell where speed rose."
  },
  "whyThisChoice": "Why include a pressure term at all, when energy conservation for a ball needs only kinetic and potential? Because a fluid parcel is not isolated — the fluid behind it does work on it as it moves forward, and the fluid ahead does negative work. Per unit volume, that net work is exactly the pressure difference. So $P$ is not a third kind of energy; it is the bookkeeping for the work done by the rest of the fluid on the parcel you chose.",
  "trap": "Applying Bernoulli where the conditions fail — turbulent flow, a viscous fluid, unsteady flow, or two points not on the same streamline — and getting an answer that looks fine. The other trap is the intuition that fast-moving fluid has high pressure. It has high *kinetic energy density*; the pressure term is what paid for it. And do not use Bernoulli when only mass conservation is needed: if a question asks only for a speed from areas, that is [[flu-continuity]] and Bernoulli will add unknowns you cannot solve for."
}
];

