{
  "id": "c-flu-50",
  "conceptId": "flu-bernoulli",
  "type": "concept",
  "front": "State Bernoulli's equation and say what each term means physically.",
  "back": "P_1 + rho g y_1 + (1/2) rho v_1^2 = P_2 + rho g y_2 + (1/2) rho v_2^2. Each term is an energy per unit volume: rho g y is gravitational potential energy per volume, (1/2) rho v^2 is kinetic energy per volume, and P is the work per unit volume done by the surrounding fluid. It is on the equation sheet."
},
{
  "id": "c-flu-51",
  "conceptId": "flu-bernoulli",
  "type": "concept",
  "front": "In a horizontal pipe, the fluid speeds up. What happens to the pressure, and why?",
  "back": "It falls. The three terms share a fixed total and the height terms cancel, so raising the kinetic term must lower the pressure term."
},
{
  "id": "c-flu-52",
  "conceptId": "flu-bernoulli",
  "type": "concept",
  "front": "Derive Torricelli's result for a jet leaving a hole a depth dy below the surface of a wide open tank.",
  "back": "Both surface and hole are open to the air, so the P terms cancel. The tank is wide, so by continuity the surface descends negligibly slowly and v_top is about zero. Bernoulli then gives rho g dy = (1/2) rho v^2, and rho cancels: v = sqrt(2 g dy). It is a derived equation and is NOT on the sheet."
},
{
  "id": "c-flu-53",
  "conceptId": "flu-bernoulli",
  "type": "concept",
  "front": "Torricelli's speed is the same as what other familiar result, and why is that a good sign?",
  "back": "It is the speed of an object dropped from rest through the same height, sqrt(2 g dy). The match is expected, because Bernoulli's equation is conservation of energy and the falling object is the same energy statement with no fluid attached."
},
{
  "id": "c-flu-54",
  "conceptId": "flu-bernoulli",
  "type": "concept",
  "front": "Name the four conditions under which Bernoulli's equation may be applied.",
  "back": "Steady flow, incompressible fluid, no viscosity, and the two points must lie on the same streamline."
},
{
  "id": "c-flu-55",
  "conceptId": "flu-bernoulli",
  "type": "choice",
  "front": "Fast-moving fluid pushes harder, so it has higher pressure — what is wrong with this?",
  "back": "It confuses kinetic energy density with pressure. The fluid does have more kinetic energy per unit volume, and the pressure term is what paid for it, so the pressure is lower. The mistaken version contradicts Bernoulli's fixed total and would predict the wrong sign for every constriction problem."
},
{
  "id": "c-flu-56",
  "conceptId": "flu-hydrostatic",
  "type": "concept",
  "front": "Show that Bernoulli's equation contains the hydrostatic relation.",
  "back": "Set both speeds to zero in Bernoulli's equation. The kinetic terms vanish and what remains is P_1 = P_2 + rho g (y_2 - y_1), which is P = P_0 + rho g h with h measured downward. So the two are not rival formulas: the hydrostatic relation is the zero-speed case."
}
