  "prompt": "For each of the three motion graphs — position–time, velocity–time, acceleration–time — say what the slope means and what the area means, and say which of those six statements are actually useful.",
  "modelAnswer": "Slope of position–time is velocity; slope of velocity–time is acceleration. Area under velocity–time is displacement; area under acceleration–time is change in velocity. The remaining two are not used: the slope of an acceleration–time graph is not analysed quantitatively in AP Physics 1, and the area under a position–time graph has no standard physical meaning. So slopes run down the chain, position to velocity to acceleration, and areas run back up it. On a curve, 'slope' means the slope of the tangent at the instant in question, while the slope of a chord is the average over that interval.",
