  "modelAnswer": "(a) $T_s = 2\\pi\\sqrt{m/k} = 2\\pi\\sqrt{0.80/50} = 2\\pi\\sqrt{0.016} = 2\\pi(0.1265) = 0.795$ s, so about 0.79 s. Then $f = 1/T = 1.26$ Hz. (b) Set $T_p = T_s$: $2\\pi\\sqrt{\u2113/g} = 0.795$, so $\u2113 = gT^2/(4\\pi^2) = (9.8)(0.795)^2/39.48 = 6.19/39.48 = 0.157$ m, about 0.16 m. (c) Neither period changes. Amplitude appears in neither $T_s = 2\\pi\\sqrt{m/k}$ nor $T_p = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\u2113/g}$; for the spring this is exact for an ideal spring, and for the pendulum it holds provided the doubled amplitude is still a small angle. What does change is the energy of each system and the maximum speed.",
