  "id": "q-osc-04",
  "tier": 2,
  "prompt": "A 0.50 kg block on a frictionless horizontal surface is attached to a spring with $k = 32$ N/m and released from rest 0.15 m from equilibrium. (a) Calculate the total mechanical energy of the system. (b) Calculate the block's maximum speed. (c) Calculate its speed when it is 0.090 m from equilibrium. (d) Explain why you may not use $v^2 = v_0^2 + 2a\\Delta x$ for part (c), even though that equation is on the provided equation sheet.",
  "modelAnswer": "(a) At release the block is at rest at $\\Delta x = A = 0.15$ m, so all the energy is elastic potential: $E = \\tfrac{1}{2}kA^2 = \\tfrac{1}{2}(32)(0.15)^2 = 0.36$ J. (b) At equilibrium all of it is kinetic: $\\tfrac{1}{2}mv_{\\max}^2 = 0.36$ J, so $v_{\\max} = \\sqrt{2(0.36)/0.50} = \\sqrt{1.44} = 1.2$ m/s. (c) At $x = 0.090$ m, $U_s = \\tfrac{1}{2}(32)(0.090)^2 = 0.1296$ J, so $K = 0.36 - 0.1296 = 0.2304$ J and $v = \\sqrt{2(0.2304)/0.50} = \\sqrt{0.9216} = 0.96$ m/s. (d) That equation is derived on the assumption that the acceleration is constant. In SHM $a_x = -(k/m)x$, so the acceleration changes continuously as the block moves, and there is no single value of $a$ to substitute. Energy conservation carries no such assumption and relates speed to position directly.",
