  "prompt": "A rectangular barge has a flat bottom of area 12 m² and vertical sides. Loaded, its total mass is 9.0 x 10^3 kg. It floats in fresh water of density 1000 kg/m³. Take g = 9.8 m/s². (a) Find the buoyant force on the barge. (b) Find the depth to which the bottom sits below the waterline. (c) Cargo of mass 1.2 x 10^3 kg is removed. Find the new depth.",
  "modelAnswer": "(a) The barge floats at rest, so the net force is zero and the buoyant force equals the weight: F_b = mg = 9.0 x 10^3 x 9.8 = 8.82 x 10^4 N.\n\n(b) F_b = rho V_sub g, and the sides are vertical so V_sub = A d with A = 12 m² and d the depth of the bottom. Then d = F_b/(rho g A) = 8.82 x 10^4 / (1000 x 9.8 x 12) = 0.75 m.\n\n(c) New mass 7.8 x 10^3 kg. The same route gives d = m/(rho A) = 7.8 x 10^3 / (1000 x 12) = 0.65 m. The barge rises by 0.10 m.\n\nNote that in the compact form d = m/(rho A) the g has cancelled, so the floating depth does not depend on gravity.",
