  "modelAnswer": "**(a)** All of the rod's weight can be taken as acting at its centre of mass, which is at the middle of a uniform rod, a distance $L/2$ from the axle. The force is perpendicular to the rod at the instant of release, so the lever arm is the full $L/2$ and\n\n$$\\tau = Mg\\left(\\frac{L}{2}\\right)$$\n\nThe axle's force acts at the axis, so its lever arm is zero and it contributes no torque — which is why taking the axle as the axis is the useful choice. Then\n\n$$\\alpha = \\frac{\\tau}{I} = \\frac{Mg L/2}{\\frac{1}{3}ML^2} = \\frac{3g}{2L}$$\n\n**(b)** Torque is not the right tool here, because $\\tau$ changes as the rod swings down — the lever arm shrinks — so $\\alpha$ is not constant and the rotational kinematic equations do not apply. Energy does apply.\n\nTake the rod and Earth as the system. The axle is frictionless and the point where it acts does not move, so no energy enters or leaves. The centre of mass falls $L/2$, and at the vertical the rod is rotating about a fixed axle so all of its kinetic energy is $\\frac{1}{2}I\\omega^2$:\n\n$$Mg\\left(\\frac{L}{2}\\right) = \\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}ML^2\\right)\\omega^2$$\n\n$$\\frac{gL}{2} = \\frac{L^2\\omega^2}{6} \\to \\omega = \\sqrt{\\frac{3g}{L}}$$\n\nThe mass cancels, which is the expected behaviour: as with a falling object, doubling the mass doubles both the energy released and the inertia.\n\n**(c)** $\\omega = \\sqrt{\\frac{3(10)}{1.2}} = \\sqrt{25} = 5.0$ rad/s. The free end is a distance $L$ from the axis, so $v = L\\omega = (1.2)(5.0) = 6.0$ m/s.\n\n**(d)** The student is incorrect. The centre of mass is at $r = L/2$, so its speed at the vertical is $v_{cm} = \\omega\\left(\\frac{L}{2}\\right) = (5.0)(0.60) = 3.0$ m/s. An object dropped from rest through $L/2$ arrives at $\\sqrt{2g(L/2)} = \\sqrt{(10)(1.2)} \\approx 3.5$ m/s. The rod's centre of mass is slower.\n\nThe reason is that the rod is not a point. Its total kinetic energy is the translational kinetic energy of its centre of mass plus the rotational kinetic energy about that centre of mass, and both terms are non-zero. The falling object has only the first term. Since both start from rest and release the same potential energy per unit mass, the rod has to split what it releases, and its centre of mass ends up with less than the falling object's share.",
