  "modelAnswer": "**(a)** With friction negligible, only two forces act on the cart: the gravitational force exerted by Earth, straight down, and the normal force exerted by the track, perpendicular to the incline surface. Nothing pushes the cart up the incline — the push ended at $t = 0$. Drawing a third arrow along the direction of motion is the most common way to lose this point.\n\n**(b)** Choose axes with one axis parallel to the incline, because the acceleration is along the incline and this makes one component of the acceleration zero. The gravitational force has a component $mg\\sin\\theta$ down the slope and $mg\\cos\\theta$ into the surface. Perpendicular to the surface the cart does not accelerate, so $F_N = mg\\cos\\theta$. Parallel to it,\n\n$$-mg\\sin\\theta = ma \\to a = g\\sin\\theta \\text{ down the incline}$$\n\nThe mass cancels, so the 0.50 kg is not needed. From the equation sheet's table of common angles, the sine of 30° is $1/2$, so $a = (10)(0.50) = 5.0$ m/s² directed down the incline.\n\n**(c)** The acceleration is constant in both magnitude and direction for the whole trip — up, at the top, and back down — because the forces never change. So the velocity graph is one straight line of slope $-5.0$ m/s², from $+4.0$ m/s at $t = 0$, through zero at $t = 4.0/5.0 = 0.80$ s, to $-4.0$ m/s at $t = 1.6$ s. The line does not kink at the top; only the velocity passes through zero there, not the acceleration.\n\n**(d)** From the graph, the distance travelled up the incline is the area of the triangle between the line and the time axis from 0 to 0.80 s:\n\n$$\\Delta x = \\frac{1}{2}(0.80)(4.0) = 1.6 \\text{ m}$$\n\nFrom kinematics, using the equation that has no time in it:\n\n$$0 = (4.0)^2 + 2(-5.0)\\Delta x \\to \\Delta x = \\frac{16}{10} = 1.6 \\text{ m}$$\n\nThe two agree, which they must — the area rule and the kinematic equation are two readings of the same constant-acceleration model, not two independent physical claims. They would disagree only if the graph had been drawn with the wrong slope or intercept.\n\n**(e)** The intercept at $t = 0$ stays at $+4.0$ m/s, because the launch speed is unchanged. The line becomes steeper: the sine of 53° is $4/5$, so $a = (10)(0.80) = 8.0$ m/s². The cart now reaches zero velocity at $t = 4.0/8.0 = 0.50$ s instead of 0.80 s, and returns to its start at 1.0 s. The triangle is narrower, so the maximum distance falls to $\\frac{1}{2}(0.50)(4.0) = 1.0$ m — the cart does not get as far up the steeper track, even though it left at the same speed.",
