  "modelAnswer": "**(a)** Take the two carts together as the system. The track's friction is negligible and the normal and gravitational forces are vertical, so there is no net external force along the track and the system's total momentum is constant:\n\n$$m_A v_0 = (m_A + m_B)v_f \\to v_f = \\frac{m_A v_0}{m_A + m_B}$$\n\n$$v_f = \\frac{(0.60)(3.0)}{0.90} = 2.0 \\text{ m/s to the right}$$\n\nMomentum is the right tool and energy is not, because the carts stick together — which tells you at the outset that kinetic energy is not conserved.\n\n**(b)** Before the collision, $p_A = (0.60)(3.0) = 1.8$ kg·m/s and $p_B = 0$. After, $p_A = (0.60)(2.0) = 1.2$ kg·m/s and $p_B = (0.30)(2.0) = 0.60$ kg·m/s. So A's curve steps down by 0.60 and B's steps up by 0.60 over the same 0.050 s. The total is 1.8 kg·m/s before, during and after — a flat line. That flat line is the whole content of the conservation statement: momentum leaves A at exactly the rate it arrives at B.\n\n**(c)** Apply the impulse–momentum theorem to cart B on its own. The only significant horizontal force on B is the one A exerts:\n\n$$F_{avg} = \\frac{\\Delta p_B}{\\Delta t} = \\frac{0.60 - 0}{0.050} = 12 \\text{ N to the right}$$\n\nThe force on A by B is the third-law partner of that force: a pulse of the same shape and duration, magnitude 12 N, directed to the left, so it is drawn as the mirror image of B's pulse below the time axis. The areas under the two pulses are equal in magnitude and opposite in sign, which is the same fact as the flat total-momentum line in part (b).\n\n**(d)** $K_i = \\frac{1}{2}(0.60)(3.0)^2 = 2.7$ J. $K_f = \\frac{1}{2}(0.90)(2.0)^2 = 1.8$ J. The collision is not elastic: 0.9 J of kinetic energy has been transformed into other forms, mostly thermal energy and sound in the carts' coupling. Momentum was still conserved — the two conservation questions are separate, and answering one does not answer the other.\n\n**(e)** With a much larger $m_B$, the final speed is smaller: the numerator of $v_f = \\frac{m_A v_0}{m_A + m_B}$ does not change but the denominator grows, so $v_f \\to 0$ in the limit of a very large $m_B$. The fraction of kinetic energy retained falls too. Substituting $v_f$ into $K_f = \\frac{1}{2}(m_A + m_B)v_f^2$ gives\n\n$$\\frac{K_f}{K_i} = \\frac{m_A}{m_A + m_B}$$\n\nwhich is $2/3$ for the carts as given, and approaches zero as $m_B$ grows. Physically, a light cart striking a very heavy stationary one barely moves it, and almost all of the original kinetic energy is transformed — which is why a car crashing into a bridge pier is so much worse than the same car striking an identical parked car.",
