  "prompt": "**Design.** A student has a set of objects of known mass, an ideal spring of unknown spring constant, a stand, a metre stick, and a stopwatch. Design an experiment to determine how the period of a vertical object–spring oscillator depends on the mass of the hanging object.\n\n(a) Describe an experimental procedure the student could follow. Include the quantities to be measured, the equipment used to measure each one, the quantity that is varied, the quantities held constant, and any steps taken to reduce the uncertainty in the measurements.\n\n(b) Describe how the collected data could be analysed graphically to determine the spring constant of the spring. State what would be plotted on each axis and how the spring constant would be obtained from the graph.\n\n**Analysis.** A second student investigates a simple pendulum instead, timing 20 complete oscillations at each of five lengths and dividing to obtain the period $T$ for each length $L$. The angular displacement is kept below 10° throughout. The results are:\n\n- $L = 0.200$ m, $T = 0.90$ s\n- $L = 0.400$ m, $T = 1.27$ s\n- $L = 0.600$ m, $T = 1.55$ s\n- $L = 0.800$ m, $T = 1.80$ s\n- $L = 1.000$ m, $T = 2.01$ s\n\n(c) Plot the data on a graph whose axes are chosen so that the relationship is expected to be linear. Label both axes with quantity, unit and scale, plot the transformed data, and draw a best-fit line.\n\n(d) Use the slope of your best-fit line to calculate an experimental value for the acceleration due to gravity, and identify one source of experimental uncertainty in the second student's procedure, stating how it would affect the measured periods.",
