  "modelAnswer": "**(a)** Hang the spring vertically from the stand and attach an object of known mass to its lower end. Let it come to rest at its new equilibrium position and mark that position on the metre stick. Pull the object down a small, fixed distance below equilibrium — say 2 cm, measured with the metre stick — and release it from rest.\n\nStart the stopwatch as the object passes the equilibrium mark moving downward, count 20 complete oscillations, and stop the watch as it passes the same mark moving downward on the twentieth. Divide the total time by 20 to get the period. Repeat three times for the same mass and average.\n\nThen change to a different known mass and repeat, for at least five masses spread over as wide a range as the spring allows. Keep the same spring and the same release displacement every time, so mass is the only thing that changes.\n\nTiming 20 oscillations rather than one matters because the reaction-time error at the start and the stop is the same size regardless of how long the interval is, so spreading it over 20 periods divides its effect on the period by 20.\n\n**(b)** The period of an object–spring oscillator is $T_s = 2\\pi\\sqrt{m/k}$, which is on the equation sheet. Squaring gives\n\n$$T^2 = \\frac{4\\pi^2}{k}m$$\n\nSo plot $T^2$ on the vertical axis against $m$ on the horizontal axis. If the model holds, the points fall on a straight line through the origin with slope $\\frac{4\\pi^2}{k}$, and the spring constant is $k = \\frac{4\\pi^2}{\\text{slope}}$. Plotting $T$ against $m$ instead gives a curve, from which no slope means anything — the transformation is the point of the exercise.\n\n**(c)** The same trick applies to the pendulum. From $T_p = 2\\pi\\sqrt{L/g}$,\n\n$$T^2 = \\frac{4\\pi^2}{g}L$$\n\nso plot $T^2$ against $L$. The transformed values are:\n\n- $L = 0.200$ m, $T^2 = 0.81$ s²\n- $L = 0.400$ m, $T^2 = 1.61$ s²\n- $L = 0.600$ m, $T^2 = 2.40$ s²\n- $L = 0.800$ m, $T^2 = 3.24$ s²\n- $L = 1.000$ m, $T^2 = 4.04$ s²\n\nVertical axis $T^2$ in s² from 0 to about 4.5; horizontal axis $L$ in m from 0 to 1.0. The points lie close to a straight line through the origin; draw one best-fit line, not a dot-to-dot.\n\n**(d)** Taking the slope between two widely separated points on the drawn line:\n\n$$\\text{slope} = \\frac{4.04 - 0.81}{1.000 - 0.200} \\approx 4.04 \\text{ s}^2\\text{/m}$$\n\n$$g = \\frac{4\\pi^2}{\\text{slope}} = \\frac{39.5}{4.04} \\approx 9.8 \\text{ m/s}^2$$\n\nwhich matches the value of $9.8$ m/s² given on the equation sheet.\n\nOne source of uncertainty: the student starts and stops the stopwatch by hand, so each timing carries a reaction-time error of order 0.2 s. Because that error is on the total time rather than on each oscillation, timing 20 oscillations reduces its effect on $T$ to about 0.01 s — small, but it is random, so it scatters points on either side of the line rather than tilting the line. A systematic error would be worse: if the length were measured to the top of the bob instead of to its centre of mass, every $L$ would be too small by the same amount, the line would no longer pass through the origin, and the slope would be slightly wrong.",
