  "modelAnswer": "**(a)** Tie a string to the first object and hang it from the spring scale. Record the reading with the object in air; that is its weight $W$. Now lower the object until it is completely under the water without touching the bottom or sides of the beaker, and record the new reading $F$.\n\nThe buoyant force is $F_b = W - F$: the scale now supports less of the object's weight because the water supports the rest. Repeat for each object, so the submerged volume varies over as wide a range as the objects allow, while the liquid stays the same water throughout and every object is fully submerged so that the displaced volume equals the object's own volume. Take each reading twice and average.\n\n**(b)** Plot $F_b$ on the vertical axis against the known volume $V$ on the horizontal axis. If the buoyant force really is proportional to volume, the points fall on a straight line that passes through the origin — the origin matters, because a line with a non-zero intercept would be a different relationship even if it looked straight. From $F_b = \\rho V g$ the slope is $\\rho g$, so the water's density comes out as $\\rho = \\frac{\\text{slope}}{g}$.\n\n**(c)** Plot $F$ (N) on the vertical axis against $d$ (m) on the horizontal. The readings only span 1.61 N to 1.92 N, so a vertical scale running from 1.5 N to 2.0 N shows the trend clearly, where a scale starting at zero would squeeze all five points into a flat-looking band. Horizontal axis 0 to 0.100 m. The points lie close to a straight line of negative slope; draw one best-fit line.\n\n**(d)** Only the submerged part of the cylinder displaces liquid, so the submerged volume is $Ad$ and\n\n$$F = W - \\rho g A d$$\n\nThis is linear in $d$ with slope $-\\rho g A$ and intercept $W$. Taking the slope between the ends of the drawn line:\n\n$$\\text{slope} = \\frac{1.61 - 1.92}{0.100 - 0.020} \\approx -3.9 \\text{ N/m}$$\n\n$$\\rho = \\frac{|\\text{slope}|}{gA} = \\frac{3.9}{(9.8)(4.0 \\times 10^{-4})} \\approx 1.0 \\times 10^3 \\text{ kg/m}^3$$\n\nAn independent check: extrapolating the line back to $d = 0$ should give the 2.00 N weight in air, and it does, which is evidence that the model and the data agree about more than just the slope.\n\nOne source of uncertainty: the liquid surface rises as the cylinder is lowered in, because the cylinder displaces liquid into a beaker of finite width. If $d$ is measured from the original surface level rather than the current one, every depth is too small, so the slope magnitude — and therefore the calculated density — comes out too large. This is a systematic error, so repeating trials will not remove it; using a much wider container would.",
