  "prompt": "A student sits on a stool that can rotate about a vertical axis with negligible friction. The student holds a dumbbell in each hand with both arms extended horizontally, and is set rotating with angular speed $\\omega_i$. The student then pulls both dumbbells in toward the chest. Let $I_i$ be the rotational inertia of the student–dumbbells system with the arms extended and $I_f$ its rotational inertia with the arms pulled in, where $I_f < I_i$.\n\n(a) Make a claim about how the angular speed of the system changes when the student pulls the dumbbells in, and justify your claim.\n\n(b) Derive an expression for the ratio of the system's final rotational kinetic energy to its initial rotational kinetic energy, in terms of $I_i$ and $I_f$ only. State whether the rotational kinetic energy increases, decreases, or stays the same.\n\n(c) Your answers to (a) and (b) describe a system whose angular momentum does not change but whose kinetic energy does. Explain how both can be true at once, and predict what happens to the angular speed and the kinetic energy if the student slowly lets the dumbbells move back out to the original positions.",
