  "modelAnswer": "**(a)** The angular speed increases.\n\nTake the student, the stool and the dumbbells as one system, and take the vertical axis of the stool as the axis. The stool turns with negligible friction, so no frictional torque acts about that axis. The gravitational forces and the force from the stool's support act along or through the axis, so they exert no torque about it either. With no net external torque, the system's angular momentum about the axis is constant.\n\nPulling the dumbbells inward moves mass closer to the axis, and rotational inertia depends on how far the mass sits from the axis. So $I$ decreases. For $I\\omega$ to stay the same while $I$ falls, $\\omega$ must rise.\n\n**(b)** Angular momentum is constant:\n\n$$I_i\\omega_i = I_f\\omega_f \\to \\omega_f = \\frac{I_i}{I_f}\\omega_i$$\n\nThe rotational kinetic energies are $K_i = \\frac{1}{2}I_i\\omega_i^2$ and $K_f = \\frac{1}{2}I_f\\omega_f^2$. Substituting for $\\omega_f$:\n\n$$K_f = \\frac{1}{2}I_f\\left(\\frac{I_i}{I_f}\\omega_i\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{I_i^2}{I_f}\\omega_i^2$$\n\n$$\\frac{K_f}{K_i} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{I_i^2}{I_f}\\omega_i^2}{\\frac{1}{2}I_i\\omega_i^2} = \\frac{I_i}{I_f}$$\n\nThe same result falls out faster by writing $K = \\frac{1}{2}I\\omega^2 = \\frac{(I\\omega)^2}{2I} = \\frac{L^2}{2I}$: with $L$ fixed, $K$ is inversely proportional to $I$. Since $I_f < I_i$, the ratio exceeds 1 and the rotational kinetic energy increases.\n\n**(c)** Angular momentum and energy answer to different conditions, so there is no contradiction in one being constant while the other is not. Angular momentum about the axis changes only if an external torque acts about that axis, and none does. Energy changes if work is done, and work is done: the student's arms pull inward on each dumbbell while the dumbbell moves inward, so the force and the displacement point the same way and the work is positive. That force is directed at the axis, so it produces no torque — which is exactly why it can add energy without adding angular momentum.\n\nIf the student now lets the dumbbells drift slowly back out to the original positions, the rotational inertia returns to $I_i$ while the angular momentum is still unchanged, so $\\omega$ falls back to $\\omega_i$ and the kinetic energy falls back to $K_i$. This time the arms exert an inward force while the dumbbells move outward, so the work is negative and the energy the student added is returned to the student's body. The whole cycle leaves the rotation exactly where it started, which is the check that the reasoning is consistent.",
